[$\mathcal{F}$ is a $w^{\ast }$-algebra thus one can define the spectrum of
elements, spectral expansions and spectral measures. Positive elements have
a positive spectrum.]

\subsubsection{CAR/Clifford $c^{\ast }$-algebra}

Using the $\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}$ functional if it exists we can define a
complex Hilbert (Hilbert-Schmidt type) space, $\mathcal{A}_{HS}\equiv 
\mathcal{A}_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}$, with inner product given by 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. A\right\vert B\right\rangle _{HS}=\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
\left\{ A^{\dagger }B\right\}
\end{equation}%
and thus Hilbert space norm 
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert A\right\Vert _{\mathcal{HS}}=\left( \mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{
A^{\dagger }A\right\} \right) ^{\frac{1}{2}}.
\end{equation}%
(Other positive linear functionals belonging to $\mathcal{A}^{d}$ produce in
general different Hilbert spaces through the GNS construction). The limit of
the $\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}$ functional of a sequence of operators depends on
the topology, $\tau ,$ used and may be infinite e.g. 
\begin{equation}
\tau \text{-}\lim\limits_{j\rightarrow \infty }\left\{ \mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
\left\{ A_{j}^{\dagger }A_{j}\right\} =\infty \right\} ,
\end{equation}%
as not all operators belong to $\mathcal{A}_{HS}\equiv \mathcal{B}_{2}\left(
\Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) \right) .$

We can use this Hilbert space to define an $c^{\ast }$-operator norm,
(generated by the left regular representation), $\left\Vert A\right\Vert _{%
\mathcal{A}},$ on the $\ast $-algebra $\mathcal{A}_{finite}$ by 
\begin{align}
\left\Vert A\right\Vert _{\mathcal{A}}& =\sup\limits_{X\in \mathcal{A}%
_{HS}}\left\{ \frac{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ \left( AX\right) ^{\dagger
}AX\right\} }{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ X^{\dagger }X\right\} }\right\}
=\sup\limits_{\left\Vert X\right\Vert _{\mathcal{HS}}=1}\left\{ \limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ \left( AX\right) ^{\dagger }AX\right\} \right\}
=\sup\limits_{\left\Vert X\right\Vert _{\mathcal{HS}}=1}\left\{ \limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ X^{\dagger }A^{\dagger }AX\right\} \right\} =\sup\limits_{\left\Vert
X\right\Vert _{\mathcal{HS}}=1}\left\{ \limfunc{tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger
}AXX^{\dagger }\right\} \right\} \\
& =\sup\limits_{D\in \mathcal{S}_{\mathcal{A}}}\left\{ \limfunc{tr}\left\{
A^{\dagger }AD\right\} \right\} ,
\end{align}%
where $\mathcal{S}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ is the convex set of normal
states of $\mathcal{A}.$

The closure of $\mathcal{A}_{finite}$ wrt the $\left\Vert {}\right\Vert _{%
\mathcal{A}}$ norm produces a $c^{\ast }$-algebra $\mathcal{A}^{c},$ that
has a normalized trace $\limfunc{tr}$ (though observe that not all elements
of $\mathcal{A}^{c}$ have finite trace). One should note that the existance
of the normalized trace $\limfunc{tr}$ is not essential for the
definition/charactrization of $\mathcal{A}^{c}$ (as one only needs a norm
not an inner product)$,$ it is however essential for the definition of $%
\mathcal{A}_{HS}.$ Note that the $c^{\ast }$-algebra of bounded operators $%
\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{A}_{HS}\right) $ acting in $\mathcal{A}_{HS}$ 
\underline{\emph{contains}} the space of operators produced by the action of
left multiplication. In fact $\dim \left\{ \mathcal{A}\right\} =\dim \left\{ 
\mathcal{A}_{HS}\right\} $ and $\dim \left\{ \mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{A}%
_{HS}\right) \right\} =\left( \dim \left\{ \mathcal{A}\right\} \right) ^{2}$.

The closure of $\mathcal{A}_{finite}$ in the strong operator topology
induced by $\mathcal{A}_{HS}$ and the \emph{left regular representation}
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\lim\limits_{j\rightarrow \infty }\left\{ \mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{
X^{\dagger }\left( A-A_{j}\right) ^{\dagger }\left( A-A_{j}\right) X\right\}
\right\} \forall X=0\equiv s\text{-}\lim\limits_{j}A_{j}=A
\end{equation}%
is a $w^{\ast }$-algebra, $\mathcal{A}^{w},$ called the enveloping $w^{\ast
} $-algebra of the $c^{\ast }$-algebra $\mathcal{A}^{c}$ and $\mathcal{A}%
^{w}\supseteq \mathcal{A}^{c}.$ The existance of HS-normalized elements
(i.e. amplitudes and their associated density operators) in the infinite
case is very significant as the $\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}$-HS\ limit leads to a
type $II_{1}$ Von Neumann Algebra $\mathcal{A}^{\prime \prime }$, where 
\emph{normal} atomic states do not exist at least in the sense of one
dimensional projectors.

The norm, $\left\Vert {}\right\Vert _{\mathcal{A}},$ can be \emph{extended}
to general linear operators $T\in \mathcal{L}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $
acting in $\mathcal{A}$ 
\begin{equation}
T:\mathcal{A}\longrightarrow \mathcal{A}
\end{equation}%
(not just multiplication operators) by%
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert T\right\Vert _{\mathcal{A}}=\sup\limits_{\left\Vert X\right\Vert
_{\mathcal{HS}}=1}\left\{ \limfunc{tr}\left\{ T\left( X\right) ^{\dagger
}T\left( X\right) \right\} \right\} ,
\end{equation}%
and even further by 
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert T\right\Vert _{\mathcal{L}\left( \mathcal{A}\right)
}=\sup\limits_{\left\Vert X\right\Vert _{\mathcal{A}}=1}\left\{ \limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ T\left( X\right) ^{\dagger }T\left( X\right) \right\} \right\} .
\end{equation}%
This allows one to define the space of bounded operators, $\mathcal{B}\left( 
\mathcal{A}\right) ,$ acting in $\mathcal{A}$ by%
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) =\left\{ A\left\vert \left\Vert
T\right\Vert _{\mathcal{L}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) }<\infty \right.
\right\} .
\end{equation}

A question one needs to be answered is whether $\mathcal{A}^{w}$ is closed
in the topology induced by the norm $\left\Vert {}\right\Vert _{\mathcal{A}}$%
? An answer can be deduced by the following:

\begin{lemma}
Every Banach space is a closed subspace of its double dual (which by
definition is a closed Banach space) i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}^{dd}\supseteq \mathcal{A},
\end{equation}%
the Banach space is reflexive if the two spaces are equal and if the space
is not reflexive then $\mathcal{A}$ is a closed subspace of $\mathcal{A}%
^{dd}.$ From below 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}^{w}=\left( \mathcal{A}^{c}\right) ^{dd}\supseteq \mathcal{A}^{c}
\end{equation}%
and the Banach space norms in $\mathcal{A}^{w}$ and $\mathcal{A}^{c}$ are
the same.
\end{lemma}

\subsubsection{Characterization of $w^{\ast }$-algebras}

A $w^{\ast }$-algebra is a $c^{\ast }$-algebra $\mathcal{A}$ characterized
by the strong operator closure property \emph{or equivalently} by

\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{the algebraic property} 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}^{w}=\left( \mathcal{A}^{w}\right) ^{\prime\prime}.
\end{equation}
One must define in which set the commutant operation $^{\prime}$ takes
place. It seems that $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{A}_{HS}\right) $ would be a
good candidate.

It is obvious that in general 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}^{\prime\prime}\supseteq\mathcal{A}
\end{equation}
so the $w^{\ast}$-algebra case is a special case where equality holds,

\item a $c^{\ast }$-algebra that has a predual $\mathcal{A}_{d},$which is a
Banach space.
\end{enumerate}

A link between $w^{\ast }$ and $c^{\ast }$ algebras is that if $\mathcal{A}$
is a \emph{non-degenerate} $c^{\ast }$-algebra then its double dual $%
\mathcal{A}^{dd}$ is a $w^{\ast }$-algebra. In general 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}^{dd}\supseteq \mathcal{A}.
\end{equation}

Thus 
\begin{equation}
\left( \mathcal{A}^{dd}\right) ^{\prime \prime }=\mathcal{A}^{dd}
\end{equation}%
where $\mathcal{A}$ is a $c^{\ast }$-algebra, showing that $\mathcal{A}^{dd}$
is a $w^{\ast }$-algebra.

$\lceil $ It is always true that 
\begin{align}
\mathcal{A}^{\prime \prime \prime }& =\mathcal{A}^{\prime } \\
\mathcal{A}^{\prime \prime \prime \prime }& =\mathcal{A}^{\prime \prime }
\end{align}%
so $\mathcal{A}^{\prime \prime },$ the double commutant of $\mathcal{A},$ is
always a $w^{\ast }$-algebra. However in general 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}^{ddd}\neq \mathcal{A}^{d}.
\end{equation}%
$\rfloor $

The \emph{Sherman--Takeda theorem} states that if $\mathcal{A}$ is a $%
c^{\ast }$-algebra then its double dual is a $w^{\ast }$-algebra, and any
homomorphism of $\mathcal{A}$ to a $w^{\ast }$-algebra factors through its
double dual. The theorem was announced by \emph{Sherman} (1950) and proved
by \emph{Takeda} (1954). The double dual of $\mathcal{A}$ is called the
universal enveloping $w^{\ast }$-algebra of $\mathcal{A}$. By the
Sherman--Takeda theorem, the double dual of a $c^{\ast }$-algebra $\mathcal{A%
}$, $\mathcal{A}^{dd}$, can be identified with $\mathcal{A}^{\prime \prime }$
as Banach spaces i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}^{\prime \prime }\overset{Banach}{=}\mathcal{A}^{dd}.
\end{equation}%
$\lceil $As an example 
\begin{align}
\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}\right) ^{\prime }& =\left\{ I\right\} \\
\left\{ I\right\} ^{\prime }& =\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}\right) ^{\prime
\prime }=\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}\right) ,
\end{align}%
where the commutators are taken in $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}\right) $
and 
\begin{eqnarray}
\mathcal{B}_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}\right) ^{d} &=&\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H%
}\right) \\
\mathcal{B}_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}\right) &=&\mathcal{B}_{0}\left( \mathcal{H}%
\right) ^{d}
\end{eqnarray}

Thus%
\begin{align}
\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}\right) & =\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}\right)
^{dd}\text{ (thefore reflexive)} \\
\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}\right) ^{d}& =\mathcal{B}_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}%
\right) ^{dd}\supseteq \mathcal{B}_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}\right) .
\end{align}%
Dixmier has shown 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}\right) =\mathcal{B}_{0}\left( \mathcal{H}%
\right) ^{dd},
\end{equation}%
where 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{B}_{0}\left( \mathcal{H}\right) =\overline{\mathcal{B}_{00}\left( 
\mathcal{H}\right) }=\overline{\mathcal{B}_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}\right) }=%
\overline{\mathcal{B}_{2}\left( \mathcal{H}\right) }
\end{equation}%
and closures are taken in operator norm $\left\Vert {}\right\Vert _{\mathcal{%
B}\left( \mathcal{H}\right) }$-topology. $\mathcal{B}_{0}\left( \mathcal{H}%
\right) $ is a $c^{\ast }$-algebra, but not a $w^{\ast }$-algebra, $\mathcal{%
B}\left( \mathcal{H}\right) $ is the enveloping $w^{\ast }$-algebra of $%
\mathcal{B}_{0}\left( \mathcal{H}\right) \rfloor $

The double commutant $\left( \mathcal{A}^{c}\right) ^{\prime\prime}$ is a $%
w^{\ast}$-algebra $\mathcal{A}^{w},$ which in general does not have a
normalized trace and is the closure of $\mathcal{A}_{finite}$ in the strong,
weak, ultra strong and ultraweak topologies in $\mathcal{A}_{finite}$
induced by $\mathcal{A}_{HS}$. It is also closed in the norm topology that
is the same in $\mathcal{A}^{w}$ and $\mathcal{A}^{c}.$

$\lceil $Another example, this time commutative, is given by 
\begin{equation}
L_{\infty }\left( \mathbb{R},\lambda \right) =L_{1}\left( \mathbb{R},\lambda
\right) ^{d}
\end{equation}%
and%
\begin{equation}
L_{\infty }\left( \mathbb{R},\lambda \right) ^{d}=L_{1}\left( \mathbb{R}%
,\lambda \right) ^{dd}=M\left( \mathbb{R},\lambda \right) \supset
L_{1}\left( \mathbb{R},\lambda \right) ,
\end{equation}%
where $M\left( \mathbb{R},\lambda \right) $ is the space of measures on $%
\mathbb{R}.$ Note that $L_{1}\left( \mathbb{R},\lambda \right) $ is not an
algebra nor is it reflexive.$\rfloor $

\section{Type $I_{\infty}$ Von Neumann Algebra $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}%
_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $}

Consider the standard infinite dimensional fermionic Fock space 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}=\limfunc{linspan}\left\{ \left. \left\vert \Psi
_{j}\right\rangle \right\vert ;1\leq j\leq 2^{r}\right\} =\wedge \left( 
\mathcal{H}^{1}\right) ,
\end{equation}%
where $\left\{ \left. \left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \right\vert ;1\leq
j\leq 2^{r}\right\} $ is a conb. The space $\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}$ is
also an exterior algebra, when equipped with the standard antisymmetric
tensor product $"\wedge "$ 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \wedge \left\vert \Psi _{k}\right\rangle
\equiv \left\vert \Psi _{j}\wedge \Psi _{k}\right\rangle \in \mathcal{H}_{%
\mathcal{F}},
\end{equation}%
note that in general 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \wedge \left\vert \Psi _{k}\right\rangle
\neq \alpha \left\vert \Psi _{k}\right\rangle \wedge \left\vert \Psi
_{j}\right\rangle ;\alpha \in \mathbb{C}
\end{equation}%
as $\Psi _{j}$ and $\Psi _{k}$ are not in general homogeneous.

$\lceil $%
\begin{align}
\Psi _{j}\wedge \Psi _{l}& =\left( \sum\limits_{0\leq p\leq
r}\sum\limits_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{p}\leq r}\Psi _{jj_{1}\cdots
j_{p}}\psi _{j_{1}}\wedge \cdots \wedge \psi _{j_{p}}\right) \wedge \left(
\sum\limits_{0\leq q\leq r}\sum\limits_{1\leq l_{1}<\cdots <l_{q}\leq r}\Psi
_{ll_{1}\cdots l_{q}}\psi _{l_{1}}\wedge \cdots \wedge \psi _{l_{q}}\right)
\\
& =\sum\limits_{\substack{ 0\leq p\leq r  \\ 0\leq q\leq r}}\sum\limits 
_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{p}\leq r  \\ 1\leq l_{1}<\cdots
<l_{q}\leq r}}\Psi _{jj_{1}\cdots j_{p}}\Psi _{ll_{1}\cdots l_{q}}\psi
_{j_{1}}\wedge \cdots \wedge \psi _{j_{p}}\wedge \psi _{l_{1}}\wedge \cdots
\wedge \psi _{l_{q}}
\end{align}%
$\rfloor $

The Banach space of bounded operators defined in $\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}$ 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) =\mathbb{C}\text{-}%
\limfunc{linspan}\left\{ \left. \left( \left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi _{k}\right\vert \right) \right\vert ;1\leq j,k\leq
2^{r}\right\} ,
\end{equation}%
with the closure taken in the operator norm, is a $w^{\ast }$-algebra, which
can also be equiped with a product $\boxtimes ,$ $($box product$),$ \emph{%
different from the composition\thinspace \thinspace }$\equiv \emph{operator}%
\,$\emph{product \textquotedblleft }$\circ \text{\emph{\textquotedblleft }},$
defined by 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{k}\right\vert
\boxtimes \left\vert \Psi _{l}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{m}\right\vert \equiv \left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{k}\right\vert \bullet \left\vert \Psi _{l}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{m}\right\vert =\left\vert \Psi _{j}\wedge \Psi _{l}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi _{k}\wedge \Psi _{m}\right\vert .
\end{equation}%
$\left( \text{Grueb defines this as "}\bullet ",\text{ but we have shown
that }\boxtimes \text{ can be extended to }\bullet \text{and hence }%
\boxtimes \,\equiv \bullet \right) .$ The operator product, "$\circ ",$ is
defined by 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{k}\right\vert \circ
\left\vert \Psi _{l}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{m}\right\vert
=\left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{k}\right\vert
\left\vert \Psi _{l}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{m}\right\vert .
\end{equation}

The convex set of States on the algebra $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{%
\mathcal{F}}\right) $ are defined by 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{S}\left( \mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right)
=\left\{ \left. f\left( I_{\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) }\right) =1\right\vert f:\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) \rightarrow \mathbb{C},f\left( \alpha A+\beta B\right) =\alpha
f\left( A\right) +\beta f\left( B\right) \right\} ,f\geq 0,
\end{equation}%
and normal states 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{S}\left( \mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right)
\supseteq \mathcal{S}_{normal}\left( \mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{%
F}}\right) \right)
\end{equation}%
by%
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{S}_{normal}\left( \mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) \right) =\left\{ \left. f\left( A\right) =\func{Tr}\left\{
AD_{f}\right\} \right\vert D_{f}\in \mathcal{B}_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}_{%
\mathcal{F}}\right) ,D_{f}\geq 0,\func{Tr}\left\{ D_{f}\right\} =1\right\} .
\end{equation}%
The norm in $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $ is given
by 
\begin{align}
\left\Vert A\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right)
}^{2}& =\sup\limits_{D_{f}\in \mathcal{S}\left( \mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}%
_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right) }\left\{ \func{Tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger
}AD_{f}\right\} \right\} =\sup\limits_{D_{f}\in \limfunc{ext}\left\{ 
\mathcal{S}\left( \mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right)
\right\} }\left\{ \func{Tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger }AD_{f}\right\} \right\}
=\sup\limits_{D_{f}=D_{f}^{2}}\left\{ \func{Tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger
}AD_{f}\right\} \right\} \\
& =\sup\limits_{D_{f}=D_{f}^{2}}\left\{ \func{Tr}\left\{ U\alpha ^{\dagger
}\alpha U^{\dagger }D_{f}\right\} \right\}
=\sup\limits_{D_{f}=D_{f}^{2}}\left\{ \func{Tr}\left\{ \alpha ^{\dagger
}\alpha D_{f}\right\} \right\} =\sup\limits_{D_{f}=D_{f}^{2}}\left\{ \func{Tr%
}\left\{ \left\vert \alpha \right\vert ^{2}D_{f}\right\} \right\} =\rho
\left( \left\vert \alpha \right\vert ^{2}\right) =\rho \left( \left\vert
A\right\vert ^{2}\right) ,
\end{align}%
where the spectral radius $\rho $ is defined by 
\begin{equation}
\rho \left( A\right) =\sup \left\{ \left. \left\vert \lambda \right\vert
\right\vert \lambda \in \sigma \left( A\right) \right\} .
\end{equation}%
If $A$ is self adjoint $\sigma \left( A\right) \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ giving 
\begin{gather}
\left\Vert A\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right)
}^{2}=\left\Vert A^{2}\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F%
}}\right) }=\rho \left( \alpha \right) ^{2} \\
\Downarrow \\
\left\Vert A\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right)
}=\left\vert \rho \left( \alpha \right) \right\vert .
\end{gather}

A normalized trace cannot be defined on all of $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}%
_{\mathcal{F}}\right) ,$ as the limit of normalized trace finite dimensional
operator spaces leads to a Type $II_{1}$ $w^{\ast }$-algebra that contain no
atomic projectors and 
\begin{equation}
\func{Tr}\left\{ I_{\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right)
}\right\} =\infty .
\end{equation}

$\lceil$ As $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $ is a $%
w^{\ast}$-algebra one has 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) =\mathcal{B}\left( 
\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) ^{\prime\prime}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) =\mathcal{B}_{1}\left( 
\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) d
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) ^{d}=\mathcal{B}%
_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) ^{dd}\supseteq \mathcal{B}%
_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) .
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) ^{d}=\mathcal{B}%
_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) ^{dd}
\end{equation}%
$\rfloor $

\section{GNS\ Construction}

The GNS\ construction produces $c^{\ast }$-algebra representations $\Pi _{f}$
associated with states $f\in \mathcal{S}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) \subset 
\mathcal{A}^{d}$ on a Hilbert space $\Phi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right)
\equiv \mathcal{H}_{f}$ also associated with the state $f.$ The
representation $\Pi _{f}$ may or may not be surjective or injective in $%
\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{f}\right) .$ The representations $\Pi _{f}$
can be extended to the universal enveloping $w^{\ast }$-algebra $\mathcal{A}%
^{dd}\supseteq \mathcal{A}$ producing $w^{\ast }$-algebra representations $%
\widetilde{\Pi _{f}}$ 
\begin{equation}
\widetilde{\Pi _{f}}:\mathcal{A}^{dd}\rightarrow \Pi _{f}\left( \mathcal{H}%
_{f}\right) ^{\prime \prime }.
\end{equation}%
The universal enveloping $w^{\ast }$-algebra $\mathcal{A}^{dd}$ is a direct
integral of all representations.

$\lceil $Two representations $\Pi _{f}$ and $\Pi _{g}$ are \emph{%
quasi-equivalent (physically equivalent) }if and only if there exists a\emph{%
\ }$w^{\ast }$-algebra isomorphism $\alpha $%
\begin{equation}
\alpha :\Pi _{f}\left( A\right) ^{\prime \prime }\rightarrow \Pi _{g}\left(
A\right) ^{\prime \prime }.
\end{equation}%
Two factor representations are quasi-equivalent if and only if they are
disjoint.$\rfloor $

A state, $f\in \mathcal{S}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) ,$ on a $c^{\ast }$%
-algebra is defined by a linear functional that has the properties 
\begin{align}
f& :\mathcal{A}\longrightarrow \mathbb{C} \\
f\left( A\right) & =\overline{f\left( A^{\dagger }\right) } \\
f\left( \alpha A+\beta B\right) & =\alpha f\left( A\right) +\beta f\left(
B\right) \\
f\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) & =1 \\
A& \geq 0\Rightarrow f\left( A\right) \geq 0.
\end{align}%
($A\geq 0\Longleftrightarrow A=QQ^{\dagger }$ for some $Q,$ these $Q^{\prime
}s$ are obviously non unique as $QU,$ where $UU^{\dagger }=I_{\mathcal{A}}$
also give the same $QQ^{\dagger }.$ In fact consider the positive square
root of $QQ^{\dagger }$ i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert Q^{\dagger }\right\vert =\left( QQ^{\dagger }\right) ^{\frac{1}{2}%
}
\end{equation}%
giving 
\begin{equation}
QQ^{\dagger }=\left\vert Q^{\dagger }\right\vert ^{2}.
\end{equation}%
)$.$

$\lceil $%
\begin{equation}
QQ^{\dagger }=QU\left( QU\right) ^{\dagger }
\end{equation}%
$\rfloor $

A $c^{\ast }$-algebra $\mathcal{A}$ is a $w^{\ast }$-algebra if it is a dual
Banach space of a Banach space $\mathcal{A}_{\ast }$ i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}=\left( \mathcal{A}_{d}\right) ^{d},
\end{equation}%
where $\mathcal{A}_{d}$ is called the \emph{predual} of $\mathcal{A}.$ This
shows that if $\mathcal{A}$ is a $c^{\ast }$-algebra then $\mathcal{A}^{dd}$ 
$\supseteq \mathcal{A}$ is a $w^{\ast }$-algebra, called the enveloping $%
w^{\ast }$-algebra of the $c^{\ast }$-algebra $\mathcal{A},$ which is very
large and contains all representations of all $w^{\ast }$-algebras produced
from $\mathcal{A}$ i.e. all representations produced from states of $%
\mathcal{A},$ thus must be in general a direct sum (integral) of many sub $%
w^{\ast }$-algebras and hence not simple. The predual, $\mathcal{A}_{d},$ of 
$\mathcal{A}^{dd}$ might \emph{or might not} be contained in $\mathcal{A}.$ 
\emph{Normal States }are \emph{contained in and generate} the predual.

If $\mathcal{A}$ has a, possibly unbounded, sesquilinear form 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. {}\right\vert \right\rangle :\mathcal{A}\times \mathcal{A%
}\rightarrow \mathbb{C}
\end{equation}

\begin{enumerate}
\item One can define \emph{weights,} (generalized linear functionals e.g.
can have the value $\infty $), belonging to $\mathcal{A}^{d}$ by 
\begin{equation}
f_{A}\left( B\right) =\left\langle \left. A^{\dagger }\right\vert
B\right\rangle \,\forall B\in \mathcal{A}.
\end{equation}

\item One can define a closed subspace $\mathcal{V}$ of $\mathcal{A}$ by 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{V}=\left\{ \left. A\in \mathcal{A}\right\vert \left\langle \left.
A\right\vert A\right\rangle <\infty \right\} ,
\end{equation}%
on which the restriction of the weight is a bounded linear functional.

\item This produces a map that includes $\mathcal{V}\subseteq \mathcal{A}$
in its dual $\mathcal{W}\subseteq \mathcal{A}^{d}$ 
\begin{equation}
\Upsilon :\mathcal{V}\rightarrow \mathcal{W},
\end{equation}%
where 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{W}=\left\{ \left. f_{A}\in \mathcal{A}^{d}\right\vert f_{A}\left(
B\right) <\infty \,\forall B\in \mathcal{A}\right\} .
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate}

The set $\left\{ f_{A}\right\} $ of all $f_{A}$ that produce such bounded
linear functionals form a closed (in the dual Banach space norm) subspace, $%
\mathcal{W},$ of $\mathcal{A}^{d}.$ The corresponding set $\left\{ A\right\} 
$ of all $A$ that produce such bounded linear functionals is a subspace, $%
\mathcal{V},$ of $\mathcal{A},$ which is \emph{NOT in general CLOSED }in the 
$c^{\ast }$-algebra norm. The subspace $\mathcal{W}\subseteq \mathcal{A}^{d}$
is the predual, $\mathcal{A}_{d},$ of $\mathcal{A}$ and \emph{can be
identified with} $\mathcal{V}\subseteq \mathcal{A}.$

If $\mathcal{A}$ has a trace defined on it then one can define a bounded
linear functional $f_{A}\in \mathcal{A}^{d}$ on $\mathcal{A}$ by 
\begin{equation}
f_{A}\left( B\right) =\func{Tr}\left\{ BA^{\dagger }\right\} <\infty
\,\forall B\in \mathcal{A}.
\end{equation}

It is always true that $\mathcal{A}^{dd}\supseteq $ $\mathcal{A}$ further if 
$\mathcal{A}$ has a trace defined on it then a state is called \emph{Normal }%
if there exists a density operator $D_{f}\in \mathcal{A},D_{f}\geq 0$ and $%
\func{Tr}\left\{ D_{f}\right\} =1$, such that 
\begin{equation}
f\left( A\right) =\func{Tr}\left\{ AD_{f}\right\} .
\end{equation}%
In the case of a type $II_{1}$ VnA the infinite dimensional CAR\ algebra,
which is an infinite Clifford algebra, normalized density operators and
amplitudes exist, but they cannot be atomic.

$\lceil $The trace $\limfunc{tr}$ is a special example of a linear
functional on $\mathcal{A}$, one can consider more general linear
functionals and define a Hilbert spaces $\mathcal{A}_{f}$ with inner product 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. A\right\vert B\right\rangle _{f}=f\left( A^{\dagger
}B\right)
\end{equation}%
defined by 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}_{f}=\left. \mathcal{A}\right/ \ker \left\{ f\right\} ,
\end{equation}%
where 
\begin{equation}
\ker \left\{ f\right\} =\left\{ \left. f\left( A^{\dagger }A\right)
=0\right\vert A\in \mathcal{A}\right\}
\end{equation}%
Of course 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}_{f}=\Phi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) \equiv \mathcal{H}_{f}.
\end{equation}%
The GNS$\ $representation $\Pi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ of a $c^{\ast
}$-algebra $\mathcal{A}$ on a Hilbert space $\mathcal{H}_{f}=\Phi _{f}\left( 
\mathcal{A}\right) $ generated by the state $f$ is given as above. One can
then consider the universal representation that is the direct sum (integral)
of all representations associated with all states$\rfloor $

The GNS$\ $representation $\Pi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ of a $c^{\ast
}$-algebra $\mathcal{A}$ and Hilbert space $\mathcal{H}_{f}=\Phi _{f}\left( 
\mathcal{A}\right) $ generated by the state $f$ has the property $\Pi
_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) \subseteq \mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}%
_{f}\right) ,$ equality can only be achieved if $\Pi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}%
\right) $ is of type $I.$ In general it is properely contained in $\mathcal{B%
}\left( \mathcal{H}_{f}\right) $ (as a sub $w^{\ast }$-algebra). Atomic
states $\left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle
\left\langle \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert $ always
exist in $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{f}\right) $ \emph{but they are not}
always in $\Pi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) .$ One always has 
\begin{equation}
f\left( A\right) =\left\langle \left. \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\vert \Pi _{f}\left( A\right) \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle _{f}=\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{f}\left( A\right) \left\vert \Phi
_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{f}\left(
I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \right\} =f_{\Pi _{f}}\left( \Pi
_{f}\left( A\right) \right) ,
\end{equation}%
where $f_{\Pi _{f}}$is the induced state belonging to $\mathcal{S}\left( 
\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{f}\right) \right) .$ If 
\begin{equation}
f_{\Pi _{f}}\left( \Pi _{f}\left( A\right) \right) =\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi
_{f}\left( A\right) \mathfrak{d}_{f}\right\} =\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi
_{f}\left( A\right) \Pi _{f}\left( D_{f}\right) \right\} ,
\end{equation}%
where 
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{d}_{f}\in \mathcal{B}_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}_{f}\right) ,
\end{equation}%
then $f$ is a \emph{normal state.} Even if $\left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\vert \notin \Pi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ then $f$ can
still be a normal state though not atomic.

$\lceil $If $f$ is a normal state of $\mathcal{A}$ and $\mathcal{A}$
contains its predual then 
\begin{equation}
f\left( A\right) =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ AD_{f}\right\}
\end{equation}%
and $\Pi _{f}\left( D_{f}\right) \in \Pi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $
giving 
\begin{equation}
f\left( A\right) =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ AD_{f}\right\} =\left\langle \left.
\Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Pi _{f}\left( A\right)
\Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{f}=\left\langle
\left. \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Phi _{f}\left(
A\right) \right\rangle _{f}=\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{f}\left( A\right)
\left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle
\Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \right\} .
\end{equation}%
This gives%
\begin{align}
& \func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \Pi _{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}\right) \right\} \\
& =\frac{\left\langle \left. \Phi _{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\vert \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \Pi _{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}\right) \Phi _{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}}{\left\langle \left. \Phi
_{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Phi _{D_{f}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}}%
+\sum_{1\leq p\leq 2r}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{p}\leq 2r}\frac{%
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{D_{f}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right)
\right\vert \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \Pi _{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}\right) \Phi _{D_{f}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots
x_{j_{p}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}}{%
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{D_{f}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right)
\right\vert \Phi _{D_{f}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}} \\
& =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ AD_{f}^{2}\right\} +\sum_{1\leq p\leq
2r}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{p}\leq 2r}\frac{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{
\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right) ^{\dagger }AD_{f}^{2}x_{j_{1}}\cdots
x_{j_{p}}\right\} }{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ \left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots
x_{j_{p}}\right) ^{\dagger }\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right) \right\} 
}=2^{2r}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ A^{\dagger }D_{f}^{2}\right\} \neq 
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ AD_{f}\right\} .
\end{align}%
One can compare $\Phi _{f}\left( D_{f}\right) $ to $\Phi _{f}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) $ 
\begin{eqnarray}
&&\left\langle \left. \left( \Phi _{f}\left( D_{f}\right) -\Phi _{f}\left(
I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right) \right\vert \left( \Phi _{f}\left(
D_{f}\right) -\Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right) \right\rangle
_{f} \\
&=&\left\langle \left. \Phi _{f}\left( D_{f}\right) \right\vert \Phi
_{f}\left( D_{f}\right) \right\rangle _{f}-\left\langle \left. \Phi
_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Phi _{f}\left( D_{f}\right)
\right\rangle _{f}-\left\langle \left. \Phi _{f}\left( D_{f}\right)
\right\vert \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle
_{f}+\left\langle \left. \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert
\Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{f} \\
&=&f\left( D_{f}^{3}\right) -2f\left( D_{f}^{2}\right) +f\left( D_{f}\right)
\\
&=&\limfunc{tr}\left\{ D_{f}^{3}\right\} -2\limfunc{tr}\left\{
D_{f}^{2}\right\} +\limfunc{tr}\left\{ D_{f}\right\} .
\end{eqnarray}%
Thus if $D_{f}$ is \emph{idempotent then one has the vector identity} 
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f}\left( D_{f}\right) =\Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) ,
\end{equation}%
however there is no\emph{\ operator identity i.e.} 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{f}\left( D_{f}\right) \neq \left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\vert ,
\end{equation}%
as 
\begin{eqnarray}
&&\sup\limits_{D\in \mathcal{S}_{\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{f}\right)
}}\left\{ \func{Tr}\left\{ \left( \overset{}{\Pi _{f}\left( D_{f}\right)
-\left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle
\left\langle \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert }\right)
\left( \overset{}{\Pi _{f}\left( D_{f}\right) -\left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\vert }\right) D\right\} \right\} = \\
&&\sup\limits_{D\in \mathcal{S}_{\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{f}\right)
}}\left\{ \func{Tr}\left( 
\begin{array}{c}
\overset{}{\Pi _{f}\left( D_{f}\right) \Pi _{f}\left( D_{f}\right)
-\left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle
\left\langle \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Pi
_{f}\left( D_{f}\right) -\Pi _{f}\left( D_{f}\right) }\left\vert \Phi
_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{f}\left(
I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \\ 
+\left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle
\left\langle \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \left\vert
\Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi
_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert%
\end{array}%
\right) D\right\} \\
&=&\sup\limits_{D\in \mathcal{S}_{\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{f}\right)
}}\left\{ \func{Tr}\left\{ \left( \Pi _{f}\left( D_{f}^{2}\right)
-\left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle
\left\langle \Phi _{f}\left( D_{f}\right) \right\vert -\left\vert \Phi
_{f}\left( D_{f}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{f}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert +\left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\vert \right) D\right\} \right\} \\
&=&\sup\limits_{D\in \mathcal{S}_{\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{f}\right)
}}\left\{ \func{Tr}\left\{ \left( \Pi _{f}\left( D_{f}\right) -\left\vert
\Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi
_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert -\left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\vert +\left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\vert \right) D\right\} \right\} \\
&=&\sup\limits_{D\in \mathcal{S}_{\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{f}\right)
}}\left\{ \func{Tr}\left\{ \left( \Pi _{f}\left( D_{f}\right) -\left\vert
\Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi
_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \right) D\right\} \right\}
\end{eqnarray}%
does not equal zero in general.

\subsubsection{Folium}

$f$ is a $\Pi _{f}$ -normal state if $\exists \Delta _{f}\in \Pi _{f}\left( 
\mathcal{A}\right) $ and $\Delta _{f}\in \mathcal{B}_{1}\left( \Phi
_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) \right) $ such that 
\begin{equation}
f\left( A\right) =\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{f}\left( A\right) \Delta
_{f}\right\}
\end{equation}

\begin{definition}
The set of all $\Pi _{f}$ -normal states is a norm closed subset $\mathcal{S}%
\left( \mathcal{A}\right) \subset \mathcal{A}^{\ast }$ called the \emph{%
Folium }of the representation $\Pi _{f}.$
\end{definition}

The folium condition classifies $\mathcal{S}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ into
eqivalence classes. States in the same folium class are related by 
\begin{equation}
f_{B}\left( A\right) =f\left( B^{\dagger }AB\right) ,\quad \mathcal{\forall }%
B\in \mathcal{A},\mathcal{\quad \forall }A\in \mathcal{A}.
\end{equation}%
The group of unitary elements, $U\left( \mathcal{A}\right) ,$ are a subset
of $\mathcal{A}$ and produce a classification of a given folium into
unitarily equivalent and non equivalent elements i.e. 
\begin{equation}
f_{X}\sim f_{Y}\Longleftrightarrow f_{Y}\left( A\right) =f_{X}\left(
UAU^{\dagger }\right) \mathcal{\quad \forall }A\in \mathcal{A}
\end{equation}

$\lceil $%
\begin{equation}
f_{B}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) =f\left( B^{\dagger }B\right) =1
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
f_{B}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) =f\left( U^{\dagger }U\right) =f\left(
UU^{\dagger }\right) =f\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) =1;U\in \mathcal{U}%
\left( \mathcal{A}\right)
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
f_{B}\left( A\right) =f\left( B^{\dagger }AB\right) =\left\langle \left.
\Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Pi _{f}\left( B\right)
^{\dagger }\Pi _{f}\left( A\right) \Pi _{f}\left( B\right) \Phi _{f}\left(
I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{f}=\left\langle \left. \Phi
_{f}\left( B\right) \right\vert \Pi _{f}\left( A\right) \Phi _{f}\left(
B\right) \right\rangle _{f}
\end{equation}%
If $f$ is a normal state then 
\begin{equation}
f\left( A\right) =\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{f}\left( A\right) \Delta
_{f}\right\}
\end{equation}%
then 
\begin{equation}
f_{B}\left( A\right) =\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{f}\left( B\right) ^{\dagger
}\Pi _{f}\left( A\right) \Pi _{f}\left( B\right) \Delta _{f}\right\} =\func{%
Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{f}\left( A\right) \Pi _{f}\left( B\right) \Delta _{f}\Pi
_{f}\left( B\right) ^{\dagger }\right\} =\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{f}\left(
B^{\dagger }AB\right) \Delta _{f}\right\} =f\left( B^{\dagger }AB\right) ,
\end{equation}%
where 
\begin{equation}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \Delta _{f}\Pi _{f}\left( B\right) ^{\dagger }\Pi
_{f}\left( B\right) \right\} =\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{f}\left( B\right)
^{\dagger }\Pi _{f}\left( B\right) \Delta _{f}\right\} =f\left( B^{\dagger
}B\right) =1.
\end{equation}%
Thus%
\begin{equation}
\func{Tr}\left\{ Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger }\Pi _{f}\left( B\right) ^{\dagger }\Pi
_{f}\left( B\right) \right\} =\func{Tr}\left\{ Q_{f}^{\dagger }\Pi
_{f}\left( B\right) ^{\dagger }\Pi _{f}\left( B\right) Q_{f}\right\} =1,
\end{equation}%
where 
\begin{equation}
\Delta _{f}=Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger }.
\end{equation}%
E.g. in the vacuum Fock representation where $\left\{ a^{\dagger },a\right\} 
$ are creation and annihilation operators.%
\begin{equation}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \Delta _{f}a^{\dagger }a\right\} =\func{Tr}\left\{
a^{\dagger }a\Delta _{f}\right\} =1,
\end{equation}%
$\rfloor $

States are \emph{quasi-equivalent }if and only if they belong to the same
folium.$\rfloor $

\begin{definition}
States are \emph{faithful} if 
\begin{equation}
\text{for }A\geq 0\text{ then }f\left( A\right) =0\,\Longleftrightarrow A=0,
\end{equation}
\end{definition}

In particular the $\limfunc{tr}$ functional (normalized trace) is a faithful
state i.e. 
\begin{equation}
A\geq 0\text{ and }\limfunc{tr}\left\{ A\right\} =0\Longleftrightarrow A=0,
\end{equation}%
this is not the same as representations being faithful.

$\lceil $Non faithful states are more common e.g.%
\begin{eqnarray}
&&f_{\left\vert \Psi \right\rangle \left\langle \Psi \right\vert }\left(
c_{1}\left\vert \Phi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{1}\right\vert
+c_{2}\left\vert \Phi _{2}\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{2}\right\vert
\right) \\
&=&\dim \left\{ \mathcal{A}\right\} \limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left(
c_{1}\left\vert \Phi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{1}\right\vert
+c_{2}\left\vert \Phi _{2}\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{2}\right\vert
\right) ^{\dagger }\left\vert \Psi \right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
\right\vert \right\} =0;c_{1},c_{2}\in \mathbb{R}^{+},c_{1}+c_{2}=1,
\end{eqnarray}%
when 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. \Psi \right\vert \Phi _{1}\right\rangle =\left\langle
\left. \Psi \right\vert \Phi _{2}\right\rangle =0.
\end{equation}%
$\rfloor $

\begin{definition}
States are \emph{extreme }$\equiv \emph{pure}$ if they cannot be expressed
as convex sums of other states.
\end{definition}

States define Hilbert spaces, $\Phi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) ,$ by the
GNS construction%
\begin{align}
\Phi _{f}& :\mathcal{A}\longrightarrow \mathcal{H}_{f} \\
\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f}\right\} & =\left\{ \left. \xi \right\vert f\left( \xi
^{\dagger }\xi \right) =0\right\} \\
\Phi _{f}\left( A+\xi \right) & =\Phi _{f}\left( A\right) \\
\Phi _{f}\left( \alpha A+\beta B\right) & =\alpha \Phi _{f}\left( A\right)
+\beta \Phi _{f}\left( B\right) \\
\left\Vert \Phi _{f}\left( A\right) \right\Vert _{\mathcal{H}_{f}}& =\left(
f\left( A^{\dagger }A\right) \right) ^{\frac{1}{2}}\overset{\text{normal
state}}{=}\left( \limfunc{tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger }AD_{f}\right\} \right) ^{%
\frac{1}{2}} \\
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{f}\left( A\right) \right\vert \Phi _{f}\left(
B\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{f}}& =f\left( A^{\dagger }B\right) 
\overset{\text{normal state}}{=}\limfunc{tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger
}BD_{f}\right\} .
\end{align}%
Hence 
\begin{equation}
\xi \in \ker \left\{ \Phi _{f}\right\} \Longleftrightarrow \left\Vert \Phi
_{f}\left( \xi \right) \right\Vert _{\mathcal{H}_{f}}^{2}=f\left( \xi
^{\dagger }\xi \right) \overset{\text{normal state}}{=}\limfunc{tr}\left\{
\xi ^{\dagger }\xi D_{f}\right\} =0\equiv \Phi _{f}\left( \xi \right) =0.
\end{equation}%
One can observe 
\begin{equation}
\xi \in \ker \left\{ \Phi _{f}\right\} \Rightarrow \left\langle \left. \Phi
_{f}\left( \xi \right) \right\vert \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{f}}=f\left( \xi ^{\dagger }\right) =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ \xi ^{\dagger }D_{f}\right\} =0
\end{equation}%
and 
\begin{equation}
\xi \in \ker \left\{ \Phi _{f}\right\} \Rightarrow \left\langle \left. \Phi
_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right\vert \Phi _{f}\left( \xi \right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{f}}=f\left( \xi \right) =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
\xi D_{f}\right\} =0,
\end{equation}%
\emph{but be especially cognizant that } 
\begin{equation}
\xi \in \ker \left\{ \Phi _{f}\right\} \nRightarrow \xi ^{\dagger }\in \ker
\left\{ \Phi _{f}\right\} \text{ i.e. }\nRightarrow f\left( \xi \xi
^{\dagger }\right) =0.
\end{equation}%
$\lceil $An example of this is 
\begin{equation}
f\left( a^{\dagger }a\right) =0=f\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}-aa^{\dagger }\right)
\Rightarrow f\left( aa^{\dagger }\right) =1.
\end{equation}%
$\rfloor $

If a state $f$ is faithful then 
\begin{equation}
\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f}\right\} =0.
\end{equation}

One should note that

\begin{enumerate}
\item 
\begin{equation}
f\left( \xi^{\dagger}\right) =f\left( \xi\right) =0\nRightarrow\xi\in
\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f}\right\} ,\text{ nor }\xi^{\dagger}\in\ker\left\{
\Phi_{f}\right\} ,
\end{equation}

\item 
\begin{equation*}
0=f\left( \xi^{\dagger}\xi\right) =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \xi^{\dagger }\xi
D_{f}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \xi D_{f}\xi^{\dagger }\right\} =\limfunc{%
tr}\left\{ D_{f}\xi^{\dagger}\xi\right\} \Leftrightarrow\xi\in\ker\left\{
\Phi_{f}\right\} \Leftrightarrow\xi D_{f}\xi^{\dagger}=0,
\end{equation*}

\item 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \xi^{\dagger}\xi D_{f}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
\xi^{\dagger}\xi Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
Q_{f}^{\dagger}\xi^{\dagger}\xi Q_{f}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left(
\xi Q_{f}\right) ^{\dagger}\xi Q_{f}\right\} ,
\end{equation}
thus 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \xi^{\dagger}\xi D_{f}\right\} =0\Leftrightarrow \xi
Q_{f}=0,
\end{equation}
as $\limfunc{tr}$ is faithful.
\end{enumerate}

$\lceil $When considering vacuum state amplitudes $Q_{f}\in \ker \left\{
\Phi _{f}\right\} .$ One can observe that $\Phi _{f}\left( Q_{f}\right) =0$
Iff $Q_{f}$ and $Q_{f}^{\dagger }$ are nilpotent as 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{f}\left( Q_{f}\right) \right\vert \Phi _{f}\left(
Q_{f}\right) \right\rangle =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q_{f}^{\dagger
}Q_{f}Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger }\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
Q_{f}Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger }Q_{f}^{\dagger }\right\} =0
\end{equation}%
giving%
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f}\left( Q_{f}\right) =0\Leftrightarrow Q_{f}^{2}\Leftrightarrow
Q_{f}^{\dagger 2}=0.
\end{equation}%
If $\Phi _{f}\left( Q_{f}^{\dagger }\right) =0$ then the state $%
Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger }$ is also zero as 
\begin{gather}
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{f}\left( Q_{f}^{\dagger }\right) \right\vert \Phi
_{f}\left( Q_{f}^{\dagger }\right) \right\rangle =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger }Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger }\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
Q_{f}^{\dagger }Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger }Q_{f}\right\} =0 \\
\Updownarrow \\
\Phi _{f}\left( Q_{f}^{\dagger }\right) =0\Leftrightarrow Q_{f}^{\dagger
}Q_{f}=Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger }=0,
\end{gather}%
so the situation is trivial if both $Q_{f}$ and $Q_{f}^{\dagger }$ are
nilpotent and belong to $\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f}\right\} .$

$\rfloor$

Representations of $\mathcal{A}$ can then be constructed (GNS construction)
by 
\begin{align}
\Pi _{f}& :\mathcal{A}\hookrightarrow \mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}%
_{f}\right) \\
\Pi _{f}\left( A\right) \Phi _{f}\left( X\right) & =\Phi _{f}\left( AX\right)
\\
\left\Vert \Pi _{f}\left( A\right) \right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H%
}_{f}\right) }^{2}& =\sup\limits_{\Phi _{f}\left( X\right) \in \mathcal{H}%
_{f}}\left\{ \frac{\left\langle \left. \Phi _{f}\left( X\right) \right\vert
\Pi _{f}\left( A\right) ^{\dagger }\Pi _{f}\left( A\right) \Phi _{f}\left(
X\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{f}}}{\left\langle \left. \Phi
_{f}\left( X\right) \right\vert \Phi _{f}\left( X\right) \right\rangle _{%
\mathcal{H}_{f}}}\right\} \\
& =\sup\limits_{\Phi _{f}\left( X\right) \in \mathcal{H}_{f}}\left\{ \frac{%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ X^{\dagger }A^{\dagger }AXD_{f}\right\} }{\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ X^{\dagger }XD_{f}\right\} }\right\} \\
& =\sup\limits_{\Phi _{f}\left( X\right) \in \mathcal{H}_{f}}\left\{
\left\vert \func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{f}\left( A\right) ^{\dagger }\Pi
_{f}\left( A\right) \frac{\left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( X\right) \right\rangle
\left\langle \Phi _{f}\left( X\right) \right\vert }{\left\langle \left. \Phi
_{f}\left( X\right) \right\vert \Phi _{f}\left( X\right) \right\rangle _{%
\mathcal{H}_{f}}}\right\} \right\vert \right\} \\
& =\sup\limits_{\Phi _{f}\left( X\right) \in \mathcal{H}_{f}}\left\{
\left\vert \frac{\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{f}\left( A\right) ^{\dagger }\Pi
_{f}\left( A\right) \left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( X\right) \right\rangle
\left\langle \Phi _{f}\left( X\right) \right\vert \right\} }{\func{Tr}%
\left\{ \left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( X\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi
_{f}\left( X\right) \right\vert \right\} }\right\vert \right\} ,
\end{align}%
where $\Pi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ \emph{is in general a }$c^{\ast }$%
-\emph{algebra} \emph{contained in} $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}%
_{f}\right) .$ As $\Pi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ is contained in $%
\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{f}\right) $ not all elements of $\mathcal{B}%
\left( \mathcal{H}_{f}\right) $ are contained in $\Pi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}%
\right) ^{\prime \prime }$ e.g. if $\Pi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right)
^{\prime \prime }$ is a type $III$ Von Neumann algebra than it contains 
\emph{no finite} dimensional projectors, thus no atomic projections and 
\emph{no pure states}. However the action of $\Pi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}%
\right) ^{\prime \prime }$ is defined on all vectors in $\mathcal{H}_{f}$ $%
\, $e.g. 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. v\right\vert \Pi _{f}\left( A\right) v\right\rangle _{%
\mathcal{H}_{f}}\in \mathbb{R};\Pi _{f}\left( A\right) =\Pi _{f}\left(
A\right) ^{\dagger }
\end{equation}%
is always a finite expectation value of the self adjoint operator $\Pi
_{f}\left( A\right) $ even though 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert v\right\rangle \left\langle v\right\vert \notin \Pi _{f}\left( 
\mathcal{A}\right) .
\end{equation}%
\emph{In particular} it is possible that the projector onto the generating
state 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle
\Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \notin \Pi _{f}\left( 
\mathcal{A}\right) .
\end{equation}%
A state, $f,$ is \emph{normal} if $\exists D_{f}\in \Pi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A%
}\right) ^{\prime \prime }$ such that 
\begin{equation}
f\left( A\right) =\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{f}\left( A\right) D_{f}\right\}
;\,D_{f}\in \Pi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) ^{\prime \prime },\func{Tr}%
\left\{ D_{f}\right\} =1
\end{equation}%
so \emph{it is possible} that \emph{no normal} states exist in the folium of 
$f$ and $\Pi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) ^{\prime \prime }.$

$\lceil$It is always true that 
\begin{equation}
f\left( A\right) =\left\langle \left. \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\vert \Pi_{f}\left( A\right) \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle ,
\end{equation}
however it is possible that 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle
\Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \notin\Pi_{f}\left( 
\mathcal{A}\right) ^{\prime\prime}.
\end{equation}
In particular if $\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) ^{\prime\prime}$ is type $%
II$ or $III$ then no atomic projections exist belonging to $\Pi_{f}\left( 
\mathcal{A}\right) ^{\prime\prime}$, so $\left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\vert \notin\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right)
^{\prime\prime}.\rfloor$

\begin{definition}
The vector $\Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) $ is a \emph{cyclic}
vector for $\Pi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ (and $f$ is a cyclic state)
if 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}_{f}=\overline{\left\{ \left. \Pi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right)
\Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert A\in \mathcal{A}\right\} }%
.
\end{equation}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
If 
\begin{equation}
\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f}\right\} =\left\{ 0\right\}
\end{equation}%
then $f$ is also \emph{seperating, }which also implies\emph{\ }that $f$ is
faithful\emph{. }
\end{definition}

A vector $v$ is \emph{cyclic} for a subalgebra $\mathcal{A}_{sub}$ if 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}=\overline{\left\{ \left. Av\right\vert A\in \mathcal{A}%
_{sub}\right\} }.
\end{equation}%
A vector $v$ is \emph{seperating} for a subalgebra $\mathcal{A}_{sub}$ if
the only solution to 
\begin{equation}
Av=0;\text{ }\forall A\in \mathcal{A}_{sub}
\end{equation}%
is 
\begin{equation}
A=0.
\end{equation}%
In terms of a GNS representation \emph{seperating} is equivalent to $\Pi
_{f} $ being faithful i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{f}\left( A\right) \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) =0\forall
A\Longleftrightarrow \Pi _{f}\left( A\right) =0.
\end{equation}

$\lceil $A representation $\Pi _{f}$ is faithful $\limfunc{Iff}$ 
\begin{equation}
\ker \left\{ \Pi _{f}\right\} =0.
\end{equation}%
In this case $\Pi _{f}$ is injective i.e. $1-1$ so there is a unique element 
$A\in \mathcal{A}$ corresponding to $\Pi _{f}\left( A\right) \in \mathcal{B}%
\left( \Phi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) \right) $ and 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{f}^{-1}:\Pi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) \subseteq \mathcal{B}\left(
\Phi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) \right) \rightarrow \mathcal{A}
\end{equation}%
is well defined. In particular $\Pi _{f}$ and $\Pi _{f}^{-1}$ are positive
maps i.e. they map positive elements onto positive elements as they $c^{\ast
}$-algebra morphisms.

However if $\Pi _{f}$ is not faithful $\Pi _{f}^{-1}$ is not defined but its
preimage $\Pi _{f}^{\text{-}1}\left( \Pi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right)
\right) $ is defined and given by 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{f}^{-1}\left( \Pi _{f}\left( A\right) \right) =\left\{ \left. A+\xi
\right\vert \xi \in \ker \left\{ \Pi _{f}\right\} ,A\in \mathcal{A}\right\}
\end{equation}%
and \emph{non positive} elements can belong to the preimage of as 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{f}^{-1}\left( \Pi _{f}\left( A^{\dagger }A\right) \right) =A^{\dagger
}A+\xi
\end{equation}%
and $\xi $ could be \emph{any} operator belonging to $\ker \left\{ \Pi
_{f}\right\} $, even non normal. \emph{Thus the preimage of the positive
cone of }$\Pi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $\emph{\ can contain nonpositive
elements.}

The positive cone of $\mathcal{A},$however, is mapped onto the positive cone
of $\Pi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ even if $\Pi _{f}$ is non faithful
as 
\begin{eqnarray}
\left( A+\xi \right) ^{\dagger }\left( A+\xi \right) &=&A^{\dagger }A+\xi
^{\dagger }A+A^{\dagger }\xi +\xi ^{\dagger }\xi \\
\Pi _{f}\left( \left( A+\xi \right) ^{\dagger }\left( A+\xi \right) \right)
&=&\Pi _{f}\left( A^{\dagger }A\right) =\Pi _{f}\left( A\right) ^{\dagger
}\Pi _{f}\left( A\right) ,
\end{eqnarray}%
where $\xi $ $\in \ker \left\{ \Pi _{f}\right\} \rfloor $

\bigskip One can observe that $\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f}\right\} $ is a \emph{%
left ideal} in $\mathcal{A},$ $\left( \text{\emph{which is not in general
self adjoint}}\right) ,$ as 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{f}\left( A\right) \Phi _{f}\left( \xi \right) =\Phi _{f}\left( A\xi
\right) =0\text{ if }\xi \in \ker \left\{ \Phi _{f}\right\}
\end{equation}%
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
A\xi \in \ker \left\{ \Phi _{f}\right\} \text{ if }\xi \in \ker \left\{ \Phi
_{f}\right\} .
\end{equation}%
In particular this shows that $\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f}\right\} $ is closed
under multiplication and as it is trivially closed under addition (as $\Phi
_{f}$ is linear) thus \underline{\emph{it is a non self adjoint subalgebra.}}

If the state $f$ is faithful i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f}\right\} =\left\{ 0\right\} ,
\end{equation}
then the condition that the vector $\Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) $
is seperating for $\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{f}\left( A\right) \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) =\Phi
_{f}\left( A\right) =0\,\forall A\in\mathcal{A}
\end{equation}
implies that 
\begin{equation}
A=0.
\end{equation}
This shows that if 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{f}\left( A-B\right) \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) =\Phi
_{f}\left( A-B\right) =0
\end{equation}
and if $f$ is faithful then 
\begin{equation}
A-B=0\equiv A=B
\end{equation}
and thus $\Pi_{f}$ is faithful i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\ker\left\{ \Pi_{f}\right\} =\left\{ 0\right\} .
\end{equation}
\emph{The converse is not true} i.e. $\Pi_{f}$ being faithful$\,\nRightarrow
f$ is faithful. \emph{\ }$f$\emph{\ is a pure state Iff }$\Pi_{f}$\emph{\ is
irreducible}, pure states are in general non faithful but irreducible
representations are usually faithful. \emph{A positive linear functional is
called pure if the GNS representation is irreducible.}

The representation $\Pi _{f}$ is faithful if 
\begin{equation}
\ker \left\{ \Pi _{f}\right\} =\left\{ 0\right\}
\end{equation}%
i.e. $\Pi _{f}$ is injective. The kernel of the representation, $\ker
\left\{ \Pi _{f}\right\} ,$ is a self adjoint $c^{\ast }$-subalgebra. If $%
A\in \ker \left\{ \Pi _{f}\right\} $ one has the following:

\begin{enumerate}
\item 
\begin{gather}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ B^{\dagger}A^{\dagger}ABQ_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger }\right\}
=0\forall B\in\mathcal{A} \\
\Updownarrow \\
ABQ_{f}=0\forall B\in\mathcal{A},
\end{gather}
Hence

\item 
\begin{align}
AQ_{f} & =0 \\
A\left( I-B\right) Q_{f} & =0\Leftrightarrow AQ_{f}=ABQ_{f}\,\forall B\in%
\mathcal{A} \\
& \Rightarrow0=ABQ_{f}\,\forall B\in\mathcal{A},
\end{align}
and

\item 
\begin{equation}
AQ_{f}^{\dagger}Q_{f}=A^{\dagger}Q_{f}^{\dagger}Q_{f}=Q_{f}^{%
\dagger}Q_{f}A=Q_{f}^{\dagger}Q_{f}A^{\dagger}=0.
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate}

$\lceil $Any operator, $B$ $\in $ $\mathcal{A}$ can be expanded in normal
form as 
\begin{eqnarray}
B &=&\beta _{0}I_{\mathcal{A}}+\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq r}\beta
_{j_{1}\cdots j_{n}}b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger
}+\sum_{1\leq k_{1}<\cdots <k_{m}\leq r}\beta _{k_{1}\cdots
k_{m}}b_{k_{1}}\cdots b_{k_{m}} \\
&&+\sum_{1\leq m,n\leq r}\sum_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq r 
\\ 1\leq k_{1}<\cdots <k_{m}\leq r}}\beta _{j_{1}\cdots j_{n}k_{1}\cdots
k_{m}}b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }b_{k_{1}}\cdots
b_{k_{m}}
\end{eqnarray}%
giving%
\begin{align}
Bb_{l_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots b_{l_{p}}^{\dagger }Q_{b}& =\left( \beta _{0}I_{%
\mathcal{A}}+\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq r}\beta _{j_{1}\cdots
j_{n}}b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }+\sum_{1\leq
k_{1}<\cdots <k_{m}\leq r}\beta _{k_{1}\cdots k_{m}}b_{k_{1}}\cdots
b_{k_{m}}\right. \\
& \left. +\sum_{1\leq m,n\leq r}\sum_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots
<j_{n}\leq r  \\ 1\leq k_{1}<\cdots <k_{m}\leq r}}\beta _{j_{1}\cdots
j_{n}k_{1}\cdots k_{m}}b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger
}b_{k_{1}}\cdots b_{k_{m}}\right) b_{l_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
b_{l_{p}}^{\dagger }b_{1}\cdots b_{r}
\end{align}%
and antinormal form as%
\begin{eqnarray}
B &=&\beta _{a0}I_{\mathcal{A}}+\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq r}\beta
_{aj_{1}\cdots j_{n}}b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger
}+\sum_{1\leq k_{1}<\cdots <k_{m}\leq r}\beta _{ak_{1}\cdots
k_{m}}b_{k_{1}}\cdots b_{k_{m}} \\
&&+\sum_{1\leq m,n\leq r}\sum_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq r 
\\ 1\leq k_{1}<\cdots <k_{m}\leq r}}\beta _{ak_{1}\cdots k_{m}j_{1}\cdots
j_{n}}b_{k_{1}}\cdots b_{k_{m}}b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger
}
\end{eqnarray}%
giving%
\begin{align}
Bb_{l_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots b_{l_{p}}^{\dagger }Q_{b}& =\left( \beta _{a0}I_{%
\mathcal{A}}+\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq r}\beta _{aj_{1}\cdots
j_{n}}b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }+\sum_{1\leq
k_{1}<\cdots <k_{m}\leq r}\widetilde{\beta }_{ak_{1}\cdots
k_{m}}b_{k_{1}}\cdots b_{k_{m}}\right. \\
& \left. +\sum_{1\leq m,n\leq r}\sum_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots
<j_{n}\leq r  \\ 1\leq k_{1}<\cdots <k_{m}\leq r}}\beta _{ak_{1}\cdots
k_{m}j_{1}\cdots j_{n}}b_{k_{1}}\cdots b_{k_{m}}b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }\right) \left( b_{l_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
b_{l_{p}}^{\dagger }b_{1}\cdots b_{r}\right) ,
\end{align}%
where%
\begin{equation}
Q_{b}=b_{1}\cdots b_{r}
\end{equation}%
and 
\begin{equation}
b_{l_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots b_{l_{p}}^{\dagger }b_{1}\cdots
b_{r}=b_{l_{1}}^{\dagger }b_{l_{1}}\cdots b_{l_{p}}^{\dagger
}b_{l_{p}}\tprod\limits_{1\leq q\neq l_{1},\cdots ,l_{p}\leq r}b_{q}.
\end{equation}%
$\rfloor $

\begin{example}
Let $r=1$ then 
\begin{eqnarray}
\text{bases of vector space} &=&\left\{ \left\vert \phi \right\rangle
,\left\vert 1\right\rangle \right\} \  \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger }\right\} &=&\frac{1}{2}\func{Tr}%
\left\{ b_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger }\right\} =\frac{1}{2}
\end{eqnarray}%
and%
\begin{equation}
D_{f_{b}}=Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger }=2b_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger }=2h_{1},
\end{equation}%
where 
\begin{align}
Q_{b}& =\sqrt{2}b \\
Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger }& =2bb^{\dagger } \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ 2bb^{\dagger }\right\} & =1=\limfunc{tr}\left\{
2b^{\dagger }b\right\} \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ bb^{\dagger }+b^{\dagger }b\right\} & =1=\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ I_{\mathcal{A}}\right\} \\
\left( bb^{\dagger }\right) \left( b^{\dagger }b\right) & =0.
\end{align}%
The state $D_{f_{b}}$ is not idempotent as 
\begin{equation}
D_{f_{b}}^{2}=2D_{f_{b}}
\end{equation}%
and it is not faithful as 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger }AQ_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger }\right\}
=0\nRightarrow A=0.
\end{equation}%
In particular 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left( b^{\dagger }b\right) \left( 2bb^{\dagger }\right)
\right\} =0
\end{equation}%
but 
\begin{equation}
b^{\dagger }b\neq 0.
\end{equation}
\end{example}

\begin{remark}
In general one has the following 
\begin{align}
\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f_{b}}\right\} & =\left\{ \left. f_{b}\left( q^{\dagger
}q\right) =0\right\vert q\in \mathcal{A}\right\} \\
\Phi _{f_{b}}\left( q\right) & =0 \\
f_{b}\left( q^{\dagger }q\right) & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ q^{\dagger
}qD_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ q^{\dagger }qQ_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger
}\right\} =0\Leftrightarrow Q_{b}^{\dagger }q^{\dagger }=qQ_{b}=0 \\
\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f_{b}}\right\} & =\ker \left\{ R_{Q_{b}}\right\} =\ker
\left\{ L_{Q_{b}^{\dagger }}\right\} ^{\dagger }=\ker \left\{
R_{Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger }}\right\} =\ker \left\{ L_{Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger
}}\right\} ^{\dagger },
\end{align}
\end{remark}

\begin{example}
continued: 
\begin{equation}
\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f_{b}}\right\} =\left\{ b_{1},b_{1}^{\dagger
}b_{1}\right\} =\left\{ b_{1},n_{b1}\right\} ,
\end{equation}%
hence%
\begin{equation}
\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f_{b}}\right\} ^{c}=\left\{ I_{\mathcal{A}%
},b_{1}^{\dagger }\right\} ,
\end{equation}%
and%
\begin{align}
\ker \left\{ R_{Q_{b}}\right\} & =\ker \left\{ R_{b_{1}}\right\} =\left\{
\left. qb_{1}=0\right\vert q\in \mathcal{A}\right\} =\left\{
b_{1},n_{b1}\right\} \\
\ker \left\{ L_{Q_{b}^{\dagger }}\right\} & =\ker \left\{ L_{b_{1}^{\dagger
}}\right\} =\left\{ \left. b_{1}^{\dagger }q=0\right\vert q\in \mathcal{A}%
\right\} =\left\{ b_{1},n_{b1}\right\} ^{\dagger }=\left\{ b_{1}^{\dagger
},n_{b1}\right\} \\
\ker \left\{ L_{Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger }}\right\} & =\ker \left\{
L_{b_{1}^{\dagger }b_{1}}\right\} =\left\{ \left. qb_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger
}=0\right\vert q\in \mathcal{A}\right\} =\left\{ b_{1},n_{b1}\right\} \\
\ker \left\{ R_{Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger }}\right\} & =\ker \left\{
R_{b_{1}^{\dagger }b_{1}}\right\} =\left\{ \left. b_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger
}q=0\right\vert q\in \mathcal{A}\right\} =\left\{ b_{1}^{\dagger
},n_{b1}\right\} .
\end{align}%
The kernel condition gives 
\begin{align}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{1}^{\dagger }b_{1}D_{f_{b}}\right\} & =0 \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ n_{b1}D_{f_{b}}\right\} & =0
\end{align}%
and%
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{1}^{\dagger }D_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
b_{1}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =0.
\end{equation}
\end{example}

\begin{remark}
If $\exists A$ such that 
\begin{gather}
A:\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f_{b}}\right\} ^{c}\rightarrow \ker \left\{ \Phi
_{f_{b}}\right\} ;\text{where }A\in \mathcal{A} \\
\text{then }AB\in \ker \left\{ \Phi _{f_{b}}\right\} ,\forall B\in \ker
\left\{ \Phi _{f_{b}}\right\} ^{c} \\
AB\in \ker \left\{ \Phi _{f_{b}}\right\} ,\forall B\in \ker \left\{ \Phi
_{f_{b}}\right\} \text{ is always true as }\ker \left\{ \Phi
_{f_{b}}\right\} \text{ is a left ideal} \\
\Pi _{f_{b}}\left( A\right) \Phi _{f_{b}}\left( B\right) =\Phi
_{f_{b}}\left( AB\right) =0\forall B
\end{gather}%
then 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{f_{b}}\left( A\right) =0.
\end{equation}%
If $Aq\in \ker \left\{ \Phi _{f_{b}}\right\} $ $\forall q\in \mathcal{A}$
then $\Pi _{f_{b}}\left( A\right) =0$ and 
\begin{gather}
f_{b}\left( q^{\dagger }A^{\dagger }Aq\right) =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left(
Aq\right) ^{\dagger }AqD_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left(
Aq\right) ^{\dagger }AqQ_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger }\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
\left( AqQ_{b}\right) ^{\dagger }\left( AqQ_{b}\right) \right\} =0 \\
\Updownarrow \\
AqQ_{b}=0\forall q\in \mathcal{A}
\end{gather}%
i.e 
\begin{equation}
Aq\in \ker \left\{ \Phi _{f_{b}}\right\} =\ker \left\{ L_{Q_{b}}\right\}
=\ker \left\{ L_{Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger }}\right\} =\ker \left\{
R_{Q_{b}^{\dagger }}\right\} =\ker \left\{ R_{Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger }}\right\}
,
\end{equation}%
which is confirmed by%
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left( AqQ_{b}\right) ^{\dagger }\left( AqQ_{b}\right)
\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q_{b}^{\dagger }q^{\dagger }A^{\dagger
}AqQ_{b}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ q^{\dagger }A^{\dagger
}AqQ_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger }\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger
}q^{\dagger }A^{\dagger }Aq\right\} .
\end{equation}%
If $\limfunc{Range}\left\{ A\right\} $ defined by 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{Range}\left\{ A\right\} =\left\{ \left. AB\right\vert B\in \mathcal{%
A}\right\} ,
\end{equation}%
is contained in the null space of $R_{Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger }}=\ker \left\{
R_{Q_{b}^{\dagger }}\right\} =\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f_{b}}\right\} $ then $\Pi
_{f_{b}}\left( A\right) =0.$

$\lceil $%
\begin{align}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger }AQ_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger }\right\} &
=0\Leftrightarrow AQ_{b}=0 \\
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{f_{b}}\left( A\right) \right\vert \Phi
_{f_{b}}\left( A\right) \right\rangle _{f_{b}}& =\left\langle \left. \Pi
_{f_{b}}\left( A\right) \Phi _{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\vert \Pi _{f_{b}}\left( A\right) \Phi _{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\rangle _{f_{b}} \\
& =\left\langle \left. \Phi _{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\vert \Pi _{f_{b}}\left( A\right) ^{\dagger }\Pi _{f_{b}}\left(
A\right) \Phi _{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{f_{b}}=%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger }AQ_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger }\right\} .
\end{align}%
$\rfloor $
\end{remark}

\begin{example}
Continued: 
\begin{equation}
D_{f_{b}}=Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger }=2b_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger }=2h_{b1},
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f_{b}}\right\} =\left\{ b_{1},b_{1}^{\dagger
}b_{1}\right\} =\left\{ b_{1},n_{b1}\right\}
\end{equation}%
and 
\begin{equation}
\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f_{b}}\right\} ^{c}=\left\{ I_{\mathcal{A}%
},b_{1}^{\dagger }\right\} .
\end{equation}%
\begin{gather}
\text{If }L_{A}:\left\{ I_{\mathcal{A}},b_{1}^{\dagger }\right\} \rightarrow
\left\{ b_{1},b_{1}^{\dagger }b_{1}\right\} \\
\text{then }AB\in \ker \left\{ \Phi _{f_{b}}\right\} ,\forall B\in \ker
\left\{ \Phi _{f_{b}}\right\} ^{c}
\end{gather}%
then 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{f_{b}}\left( A\right) =0.
\end{equation}%
Let 
\begin{equation}
A=\beta _{0}I_{\mathcal{A}}+\beta _{1}b_{1}^{\dagger }+\beta
_{11}b_{1}^{\dagger }b_{1}
\end{equation}%
then 
\begin{align}
\left( \beta _{0}I_{\mathcal{A}}+\beta _{1}b_{1}^{\dagger }+\beta
_{11}b_{1}^{\dagger }b_{1}\right) I_{\mathcal{A}}& =\beta _{0}I_{\mathcal{A}%
}+\beta _{1}b_{1}^{\dagger }+\beta _{11}b_{1}^{\dagger }b_{1} \\
\left( \beta _{0}I_{\mathcal{A}}+\beta _{1}b_{1}^{\dagger }+\beta
_{11}b_{1}^{\dagger }b_{1}\right) b_{1}^{\dagger }& =\beta
_{0}b_{1}^{\dagger }+\beta _{11}b_{1}^{\dagger }=\left( \beta _{0}+\beta
_{11}\right) b_{1}^{\dagger }
\end{align}%
and%
\begin{equation}
L_{A}:\left\{ I_{\mathcal{A}},b_{1}^{\dagger }\right\} \nrightarrow \left\{
b_{1},b_{1}^{\dagger }b_{1}\right\} ,
\end{equation}%
so $\Pi _{f_{b}}$ is injective $\equiv $ faithful. This is confirmed by 
\begin{align}
\mathcal{H}_{f_{b}}& =\left\{ \Phi _{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
,\Phi _{f_{b}}\left( b_{1}^{\dagger }\right) \right\} \\
\Pi _{f_{b}}\left( b_{1}\right) \Phi _{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) &
=\Phi _{f_{b}}\left( b_{1}\right) =0 \\
\Pi _{f_{b}}\left( b_{1}\right) \Phi _{f_{b}}\left( b_{1}^{\dagger }\right)
& =\Phi _{f_{b}}\left( b_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger }\right) \neq 0 \\
\Pi _{f_{b}}\left( b_{1}^{\dagger }b_{1}\right) \Phi _{f_{b}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) & =\Phi _{f_{b}}\left( b_{1}^{\dagger }b_{1}\right) =0 \\
\Pi _{f_{b}}\left( b_{1}^{\dagger }b_{1}\right) \Phi _{f_{b}}\left(
b_{1}^{\dagger }\right) & =\Phi _{f_{b}}\left( b_{1}^{\dagger
}b_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger }\right) =\Phi _{f_{b}}\left( b_{1}^{\dagger }\right)
\neq 0 \\
\Pi _{f_{b}}\left( b_{1}^{\dagger }\right) \Phi _{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) & =\Phi _{f_{b}}\left( b_{1}^{\dagger }\right) \neq 0 \\
\Pi _{f_{b}}\left( b_{1}^{\dagger }\right) \Phi _{f_{b}}\left(
b_{1}^{\dagger }\right) & =\Phi _{f_{b}}\left( b_{1}^{\dagger
}b_{1}^{\dagger }\right) =0 \\
\Pi _{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \Phi _{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) & =\Phi _{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \neq 0 \\
\Pi _{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \Phi _{f_{b}}\left( b_{1}^{\dagger
}\right) & =\Phi _{f_{b}}\left( b_{1}^{\dagger }\right) \neq 0.
\end{align}%
$\lceil $%
\begin{align}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger }b_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger
}b_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger }\right\} & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger
}\right\} \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger }b_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger
}b_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger }b_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger }\right\} & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
b_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger }\right\}
\end{align}%
$\rfloor \lceil $%
\begin{align}
\dim \left\{ \Phi _{f_{b}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) \right\} & =2 \\
\dim \left\{ \Pi _{f_{b}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) \right\} & =4=\dim
\left\{ \mathcal{B}\left( \Phi _{f_{b}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) \right)
\right\} \\
\Pi _{f_{b}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) & =\mathcal{B}\left( \Phi
_{f_{b}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) \right)
\end{align}%
$\rfloor $
\end{example}

\begin{example}
\bigskip Let $r=2$ then the state is given by 
\begin{eqnarray}
\text{bases of vector space} &=&\left\{ \left\vert \phi \right\rangle
,\left\vert 1\right\rangle ,\left\vert 2\right\rangle ,\left\vert
12\right\rangle \right\} \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger }\right\} &=&\frac{1}{4}\func{Tr}%
\left\{ b_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger }\right\} =\frac{1}{2}
\end{eqnarray}%
and 
\begin{eqnarray}
D_{f_{b}} &=&Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger }=2b_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger }=2h_{b1}\  \\
Q_{b}^{\dagger } &=&\sqrt{2}b_{1}^{\dagger }.
\end{eqnarray}%
The kernal of $\Phi _{f_{b}}$ has the properties 
\begin{align}
\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f_{b}}\right\} & =\left\{ \left. f_{b}\left( q^{\dagger
}q\right) =0\right\vert q\in \mathcal{A}\right\} \\
\Phi _{f_{b}}\left( q\right) & =0 \\
f_{b}\left( q^{\dagger }q\right) & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ q^{\dagger
}qD_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ q^{\dagger }qQ_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger
}\right\} =0\Leftrightarrow Q_{b}^{\dagger }q^{\dagger }=qQ_{b}=0 \\
\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f_{b}}\right\} & =\ker \left\{ R_{Q_{b}}\right\} =\ker
\left\{ L_{Q_{b}^{\dagger }}\right\} ^{\dagger }=\ker \left\{
R_{Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger }}\right\} =\ker \left\{ L_{Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger
}}\right\} ^{\dagger },
\end{align}%
and 
\begin{align}
\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f_{b}}\right\} & =\left\{
b_{1},b_{2},b_{1}b_{2},b_{1}^{\dagger }b_{1},b_{2}^{\dagger
}b_{2},b_{2}^{\dagger }b_{1},b_{1}^{\dagger }b_{2},b_{2}^{\dagger
}b_{1}^{\dagger }b_{2},b_{2}^{\dagger }b_{1}^{\dagger }b_{1},b_{1}^{\dagger
}b_{1}b_{2},b_{2}^{\dagger }b_{1}b_{2},b_{1}^{\dagger }b_{2}^{\dagger
}b_{2}b_{1}\right\} \\
& =\left\{ b_{1},b_{2},b_{1}b_{2},n_{b1},n_{b2},b_{2}^{\dagger
}b_{1},b_{1}^{\dagger }b_{2},n_{b1}b_{2},n_{b2}b_{1},n_{b1}b_{2}^{\dagger
},n_{b2}b_{1}^{\dagger },n_{b1}n_{b2}\right\}
\end{align}%
and its compliment by 
\begin{equation}
\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f_{b}}\right\} ^{c}=\left\{ I_{\mathcal{A}%
},b_{1}^{\dagger },b_{2}^{\dagger },b_{1}^{\dagger }b_{2}^{\dagger }\right\}
.
\end{equation}%
$A$ acts by left multiplication i.e. $L_{A}$ 
\begin{eqnarray}
L_{A} &:&\left\{ I_{\mathcal{A}},b_{1}^{\dagger },b_{2}^{\dagger
},b_{1}^{\dagger }b_{2}^{\dagger }\right\} \\
&\rightarrow &\left\{ b_{1},b_{2},b_{1}b_{2},b_{1}^{\dagger
}b_{1},b_{2}^{\dagger }b_{2},b_{2}^{\dagger }b_{1},b_{1}^{\dagger
}b_{2},b_{2}^{\dagger }b_{1}^{\dagger }b_{2},b_{2}^{\dagger }b_{1}^{\dagger
}b_{1},b_{1}^{\dagger }b_{1}b_{2},b_{2}^{\dagger }b_{1}b_{2},b_{1}^{\dagger
}b_{2}^{\dagger }b_{2}b_{1}\right\} \\
\text{then }AB &\in &\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f_{b}}\right\} ,\forall B\in \ker
\left\{ \Phi _{f_{b}}\right\} ^{c}
\end{eqnarray}%
then 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{f_{b}}\left( A\right) =0.
\end{equation}%
Various kernals are given by 
\begin{align}
& \ker \left\{ R_{Q_{b}}\right\} =\ker \left\{ R_{b_{1}b_{2}}\right\} \\
& =\left\{ \left. qb_{1}b_{2}=0\right\vert q\in \mathcal{A}\right\} =\left\{
b_{1},b_{2},b_{1}b_{2},n_{b1},n_{b2},b_{2}^{\dagger }b_{1},b_{1}^{\dagger
}b_{2},n_{b1}b_{2},n_{b2}b_{1},n_{b1}b_{2}^{\dagger },n_{b2}b_{1}^{\dagger
},n_{b1}n_{b2}\right\} \\
& \ker \left\{ L_{Q_{b}^{\dagger }}\right\} =\ker \left\{ L_{b_{1}^{\dagger
}b_{2}^{\dagger }}\right\} \\
& =\left\{ \left. b_{1}^{\dagger }b_{2}^{\dagger }q=0\right\vert q\in 
\mathcal{A}\right\} =\left\{
b_{1},b_{2},b_{1}b_{2},n_{b1},n_{b2},b_{2}^{\dagger }b_{1},b_{1}^{\dagger
}b_{2},n_{b1}b_{2},n_{b2}b_{1},n_{b1}b_{2}^{\dagger },n_{b2}b_{1}^{\dagger
},n_{b1}n_{b2}\right\} ^{\dagger } \\
& \ker \left\{ L_{Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger }}\right\} =\ker \left\{
L_{b_{1}b_{2}b_{2}^{\dagger }b_{1}^{\dagger }}\right\} \\
& =\left\{ \left. qb_{1}b_{2}b_{2}^{\dagger }b_{1}^{\dagger }=0\right\vert
q\in \mathcal{A}\right\} =\left\{
b_{1},b_{2},b_{1}b_{2},n_{b1},n_{b2},b_{2}^{\dagger }b_{1},b_{1}^{\dagger
}b_{2},n_{b1}b_{2},n_{b2}b_{1},n_{b1}b_{2}^{\dagger },n_{b2}b_{1}^{\dagger
},n_{b1}n_{b2}\right\} \\
& \ker \left\{ R_{Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger }}\right\} =\ker \left\{
R_{b_{1}b_{2}b_{2}^{\dagger }b_{1}^{\dagger }}\right\} \\
& =\left\{ \left. b_{1}b_{2}b_{2}^{\dagger }b_{1}^{\dagger }q=0\right\vert
q\in \mathcal{A}\right\} =\left\{
b_{1},b_{2},b_{1}b_{2},n_{b1},n_{b2},b_{2}^{\dagger }b_{1},b_{1}^{\dagger
}b_{2},n_{b1}b_{2},n_{b2}b_{1},n_{b1}b_{2}^{\dagger },n_{b2}b_{1}^{\dagger
},n_{b1}n_{b2}\right\} ^{\dagger }.
\end{align}%
The kernel condition gives 
\begin{align}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{1}^{\dagger }b_{1}D_{f_{b}}\right\} & =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ b_{2}^{\dagger }b_{2}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
b_{1}^{\dagger }b_{2}^{\dagger }b_{1}b_{2}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =0 \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ n_{b1}D_{f_{b}}\right\} & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
n_{b2}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ n_{b1}n_{b2}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ n_{b1}b_{2}^{\dagger }D_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ n_{b2}b_{1}^{\dagger }D_{f_{b}}\right\} =0
\end{align}%
and%
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{1}^{\dagger }D_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
b_{1}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =0,
\end{equation}%
while $\mathbf{b}$-Guage invariance gives 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{2}b_{1}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
b_{1}^{\dagger }b_{2}^{\dagger }D_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
b_{2}^{\dagger }b_{1}b_{2}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
b_{1}^{\dagger }b_{1}b_{2}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =0.
\end{equation}%
If $Aq\in \ker \left\{ \Phi _{f_{b}}\right\} $ $\forall q\in \mathcal{A}$
then $\Pi _{f_{b}}\left( A\right) =0$ and 
\begin{gather}
f_{b}\left( q^{\dagger }A^{\dagger }Aq\right) =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left(
Aq\right) ^{\dagger }AqD_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left(
Aq\right) ^{\dagger }AqQ_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger }\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
\left( AqQ_{b}\right) ^{\dagger }\left( AqQ_{b}\right) \right\} =0 \\
\Updownarrow \\
AqQ_{b}=0\forall q\in \mathcal{A}
\end{gather}%
i.e 
\begin{equation}
Aq\in \ker \left\{ \Phi _{f_{b}}\right\} =\ker \left\{ L_{Q_{b}}\right\}
=\ker \left\{ L_{Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger }}\right\} =\ker \left\{
R_{Q_{b}^{\dagger }}\right\} =\ker \left\{ R_{Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger }}\right\}
,
\end{equation}%
which is confirmed by%
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left( AqQ_{b}\right) ^{\dagger }\left( AqQ_{b}\right)
\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q_{b}^{\dagger }q^{\dagger }A^{\dagger
}AqQ_{b}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ q^{\dagger }A^{\dagger
}AqQ_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger }\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger
}q^{\dagger }A^{\dagger }Aq\right\} .
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{Range}\left\{ A\right\} =\left\{ \left. AB\right\vert B\in \mathcal{%
A}\right\} .
\end{equation}%
If $\exists A$ such that 
\begin{gather}
A:\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f_{b}}\right\} ^{c}\rightarrow \ker \left\{ \Phi
_{f_{b}}\right\} ;A\in \mathcal{A}\text{ ,\qquad i.e.}\,\text{\thinspace }%
AB\in \ker \left\{ \Phi _{f_{b}}\right\} ,\forall B\in \ker \left\{ \Phi
_{f_{b}}\right\} ^{c} \\
(AB\in \ker \left\{ \Phi _{f_{b}}\right\} ,\forall B\in \ker \left\{ \Phi
_{f_{b}}\right\} \text{ is always true as }\ker \left\{ \Phi
_{f_{b}}\right\} \text{ is a left ideal)} \\
\Pi _{f_{b}}\left( A\right) \Phi _{f_{b}}\left( B\right) =\Phi
_{f_{b}}\left( AB\right) =0\forall B
\end{gather}%
then 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{f_{b}}\left( A\right) =0.
\end{equation}%
Thus we only need to consider operators belonging to $\ker \left\{ \Phi
_{f_{b}}\right\} ^{c}$ as $\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f_{b}}\right\} $ is a left
ideal and ask if 
\begin{equation}
A\left( \beta _{0}I_{\mathcal{A}}+\beta _{1}b_{1}^{\dagger }+\beta
_{2}b_{2}^{\dagger }+\beta _{12}b_{1}^{\dagger }b_{2}^{\dagger }\right) \in
\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f_{b}}\right\}
\end{equation}%
if $A\neq 0.$

$\lceil $%
\begin{align}
\dim \left\{ \Phi _{f_{b}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) \right\} & =4 \\
\dim \left\{ \Pi _{f_{b}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) \right\} & =16=\dim
\left\{ \mathcal{B}\left( \Phi _{f_{b}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) \right)
\right\} \\
\Pi _{f_{b}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) & =\mathcal{B}\left( \Phi
_{f_{b}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) \right)
\end{align}%
$\rfloor $
\end{example}

\begin{remark}
$\Pi _{f_{b}}$ is faithful if 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{f_{b}}\left( A\right) \Phi _{f_{b}}\left( b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }\right) =0\Rightarrow A=0;1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq
r;0\leq n\leq r,
\end{equation}%
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f_{b}}\left( Ab_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }\right)
=0\Rightarrow A=0;1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq r;0\leq n\leq r.
\end{equation}
\end{remark}

\subsection{Concrete Example of $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) $}

Let a fock representation $\mathcal{F}=\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{%
\mathcal{F}}\right) ,$ where 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}=\tbigwedge \left( \mathcal{H}^{1}\right)
=\tbigoplus\limits_{0\leq n\leq \infty }\tbigwedge\nolimits^{n}\mathcal{H}%
^{1}
\end{equation}%
and 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{F}=\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) =\tbigwedge
\left( \mathcal{F}_{1}\right) .
\end{equation}

Consider the GNS representation of $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F%
}}\right) $ (a type $I_{r}$ VnA) produced by a pure normal state $\left\vert
\Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert $ wrt $\func{Tr},$
where 
\begin{align}
f\left( A\right) & =\left\langle A\right\rangle _{\left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert }=\func{Tr}\left\{
A\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert \right\}
\\
f\left( I_{\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}}\right) & =\left\langle I_{\mathcal{H}_{%
\mathcal{F}}}\right\rangle _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
\Psi _{1}\right\vert }=\func{Tr}\left\{ I_{\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}}\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
\right\} =1 \\
\func{Tr}\left\{ I_{\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}}\right\} & =\dim \left\{ 
\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right\} .
\end{align}

\begin{remark}
If $r=\infty $ then there does not exist a normalized trace defined on all
of $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) ,$so \underline{\emph{%
it is not}}\emph{\ }a Clifford algebra even though it is a CAR algebra. A
normalized trace is defined on $\mathcal{B}_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F%
}}\right) ,$ but strong and operator norm limits of sums and products of the
second quantized generators are not contained in $\mathcal{B}_{1}\left( 
\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) .$
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
$\mathcal{B}_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $ is not a $c^{\ast
} $-algebra.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
The representation $\Pi _{\limfunc{tr}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ on $\Phi
_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) ,$ where $\mathcal{A}$ is an $%
\infty $ dimensional CAR/Clifford algebra must be properely contained in $%
\mathcal{B}\left( \Phi _{\limfunc{tr}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) \right) .$
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
The GNS\ Hilbert space $\Phi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ \emph{may or
may not be seperable. }Hovever \emph{if }the $c^{\ast }$-algebra $\mathcal{A}
$ is generated by a finite or countable infinite number of elements then $%
\Phi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ is \emph{seperable}.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}
$\mathcal{B}_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $and $\mathcal{B}%
_{2}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $ are $\ast $-algebras \emph{but
not} $c^{\ast }$-algebras and do not contain $\left\{ \left.
\tprod\limits_{0\leq j\leq p}a_{j}^{\dagger }\tprod\limits_{0\leq k\leq
q}a_{k}\right\vert 0\leq p,q\leq \infty ,\right\} .$
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
\begin{enumerate}
\item 
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert A\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}_{2}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) }^{2}=\func{Tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger }A\right\} =\func{Tr}\left\{
\tint \left\vert \alpha \right\vert ^{2}P_{A}\left( \alpha \right) d\alpha
\right\} =\tint \left\vert \alpha \right\vert ^{2}d\alpha
\end{equation}%
but%
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert A^{\dagger }A\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}_{2}\left( \mathcal{H}_{%
\mathcal{F}}\right) }^{2}=\func{Tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger }AA^{\dagger
}A\right\} =\func{Tr}\left\{ \tint \left\vert \alpha \right\vert
^{4}P_{A}\left( \alpha \right) d\alpha \right\} =\tint \left\vert \alpha
\right\vert ^{4}P_{A}\left( \alpha \right) d\alpha \quad {\LARGE \neq \,}%
\left\Vert A\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}_{2}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) }^{4}=\left( \tint \left\vert \alpha \right\vert ^{2}d\alpha
\right) ^{2}.
\end{equation}

\item 
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert A\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) }=\func{Tr}\left\{ \left\vert A^{\dagger }A\right\vert ^{\frac{1}{2}%
}\right\} =\func{Tr}\left\{ \tint \left\vert \alpha \right\vert P_{A}\left(
\alpha \right) d\alpha \right\} =\tint \left\vert \alpha \right\vert d\alpha
\end{equation}%
but%
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert A^{\dagger }A\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}_{%
\mathcal{F}}\right) }^{2}=\func{Tr}\left\{ \left\vert A^{\dagger
}AA^{\dagger }A\right\vert ^{\frac{1}{2}}\right\} =\func{Tr}\left\{ \tint
\left\vert \alpha \right\vert ^{2}P_{A}\left( \alpha \right) d\alpha
\right\} =\tint \left\vert \alpha \right\vert ^{2}d\alpha \quad {\LARGE \neq
\,}\left\Vert A\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) }^{2}=\left( \tint \left\vert \alpha \right\vert P_{A}\left( \alpha
\right) d\alpha \right) ^{2}.
\end{equation}

\begin{enumerate}
\item 
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert I_{\mathcal{F}}\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}_{2}\left( \mathcal{H}_{%
\mathcal{F}}\right) }=\left\Vert I_{\mathcal{F}}\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}%
_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) }=\infty
\end{equation}

\item 
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger }a_{k_{q}}\cdots
a_{k_{1}}\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}_{2}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) }=\left\Vert a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger
}\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}_{2}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right)
}=\left\Vert a_{k_{q}}\cdots a_{k_{1}}\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}_{2}\left( 
\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) }=\infty
\end{equation}

\item 
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger }a_{k_{q}}\cdots
a_{k_{1}}\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) }=\left\Vert a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger
}\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right)
}=\left\Vert a_{k_{q}}\cdots a_{k_{1}}\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}_{1}\left( 
\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) }=\infty
\end{equation}

\item 
\begin{equation}
1\leq p,q\leq \infty ,1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{p}\leq \infty ,1\leq
k_{1}<\cdots <k_{q}\leq \infty .
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate}
\end{enumerate}

One has the following values for the norms of products of second quantized
operators:

\begin{enumerate}
\item 
\begin{gather}
\left\Vert a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger }a_{k_{q}}\cdots
a_{k_{1}}\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}_{2}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) }=\func{Tr}\left\{ \left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger }a_{k_{q}}\cdots a_{k_{1}}\right) ^{\dagger }\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger }a_{k_{q}}\cdots
a_{k_{1}}\right) \right\} \\
=\func{Tr}\left\{ a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{k_{q}}^{\dagger
}a_{j_{p}}\cdots a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger
}a_{k_{q}}\cdots a_{k_{1}}\right\} =\func{Tr}\left\{ a_{k_{q}}\cdots
a_{k_{1}}a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{k_{p}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{q}}\cdots
a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger }\right\} \\
=\func{Tr}\left\{ h_{k_{1}}\cdots h_{k_{q}}h_{j_{1}}\cdots h_{j_{p}}\right\}
=\func{Tr}\left\{ h_{j_{1}}\cdots \cdots h_{j_{p\cap q}}\right\} =2^{r-p\cap
q},
\end{gather}

\item 
\begin{eqnarray}
&&\left\Vert a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger }\right\Vert _{%
\mathcal{B}_{2}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) }=\func{Tr}\left\{
\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger }\right) ^{\dagger
}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger }\right) \right\} \\
&=&\func{Tr}\left\{ a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{k_{q}}^{\dagger
}a_{k_{q}}\cdots a_{k_{1}}\right\} =\func{Tr}\left\{ h_{k_{1}}\cdots
h_{k_{q}}\right\} =2^{r-q},
\end{eqnarray}%
and

\item 
\begin{eqnarray}
&&\left\Vert a_{k_{q}}\cdots a_{k_{1}}\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}_{2}\left( 
\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) }=\func{Tr}\left\{ \left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger }a_{k_{q}}\cdots
a_{k_{1}}\right) ^{\dagger }\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger }a_{k_{q}}\cdots a_{k_{1}}\right) \right\} \\
&=&\func{Tr}\left\{ a_{j_{p}}\cdots a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger }\right\} =\func{Tr}\left\{ h_{j_{1}}\cdots
h_{j_{p}}\right\} =2^{r-p}.
\end{eqnarray}%
$\lceil $%
\begin{eqnarray}
&&\func{Tr}\left\{ a_{1}^{\dagger }a_{1}a_{1}^{\dagger }a_{1}\right\} =\func{%
Tr}\left\{ n_{1}n_{1}\right\} =\func{Tr}\left\{ n_{1}\right\} =\sum_{0\leq
N\leq r}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{N}\leq r}\left\langle \varphi
_{j_{1}}\cdots \varphi _{j_{N}}\right\vert a_{1}^{\dagger }a_{1}\left\vert
\varphi _{j_{1}}\cdots \varphi _{j_{N}}\right\rangle =\sum_{0\leq N\leq r-1}%
\binom{r-1}{N}=2^{r-1} \\
&&\func{Tr}\left\{ a_{1}a_{1}^{\dagger }a_{1}a_{1}^{\dagger }\right\} =\func{%
Tr}\left\{ h_{1}h_{1}\right\} =\func{Tr}\left\{ h_{1}\right\} =\func{Tr}%
\left\{ I_{\mathcal{F}}-a_{1}^{\dagger }a_{1}\right\}
=2^{r}-2^{r-1}=2^{r-1}\left( 2-1\right) =2^{r-1} \\
&&\sum_{1\leq N\leq r}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{N}\leq r}\left\langle
\varphi _{j_{1}}\cdots \varphi _{j_{N}}\right\vert a_{1}^{\dagger
}a_{1}\left\vert \varphi _{j_{1}}\cdots \varphi _{j_{N}}\right\rangle
=\sum_{1\leq N\leq r-1}\binom{r-1}{N}=2^{r-1}=\sum_{1\leq N\leq r-1}\binom{%
r-1}{N}=2^{r-1}
\end{eqnarray}%
$r=1$%
\begin{eqnarray}
\dim \left\{ \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right\} &=&2^{1}=1 \\
\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}} &=&\limfunc{linspan}\left\{ \left\vert \phi
\right\rangle ,\left\vert \varphi _{1}\right\rangle \right\}
\end{eqnarray}%
\begin{equation}
\func{Tr}\left\{ h_{1}\right\} =2^{0}=2-1=1
\end{equation}%
$r=2$%
\begin{eqnarray}
\dim \left\{ \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right\} &=&2^{2}=4 \\
\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}} &=&\limfunc{linspan}\left\{ \left\vert \phi
\right\rangle ,\left\vert \varphi _{1}\right\rangle ,\left\vert \varphi
_{2}\right\rangle ,\left\vert \varphi _{1}\varphi _{2}\right\rangle \right\}
\end{eqnarray}%
\begin{eqnarray}
\func{Tr}\left\{ h_{1}\right\} &=&2^{1}=4-2=2 \\
\func{Tr}\left\{ h_{2}\right\} &=&2^{1}=4-2=2
\end{eqnarray}%
$r=3$%
\begin{eqnarray}
\dim \left\{ \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right\} &=&2^{3}=8 \\
\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}} &=&\limfunc{linspan}\left\{ \left\vert \phi
\right\rangle ,\left\vert \varphi _{1}\right\rangle ,\left\vert \varphi
_{2}\right\rangle ,\left\vert \varphi _{3}\right\rangle ,\left\vert \varphi
_{1}\varphi _{2}\right\rangle ,\left\vert \varphi _{1}\varphi
_{3}\right\rangle ,\left\vert \varphi _{2}\varphi _{3}\right\rangle
,\left\vert \varphi _{1}\varphi _{2}\varphi _{3}\right\rangle \right\}
\end{eqnarray}%
\begin{eqnarray}
\func{Tr}\left\{ h_{1}\right\} &=&2^{2}=8-4=4 \\
\func{Tr}\left\{ h_{2}\right\} &=&2^{2}-4=4 \\
\func{Tr}\left\{ h_{3}\right\} &=&2^{2}-4=4.
\end{eqnarray}%
\begin{equation*}
\func{Tr}\left\{ a_{1}a_{1}^{\dagger }a_{2}a_{2}^{\dagger }\right\} =\func{Tr%
}\left\{ h_{1}h_{2}\right\} =\sum_{1\leq N\leq r}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots
<j_{N}\leq r}\left\langle \varphi _{j_{1}}\cdots \varphi _{j_{N}}\right\vert
a_{1}a_{1}^{\dagger }a_{2}a_{2}^{\dagger }\left\vert \varphi _{j_{1}}\cdots
\varphi _{j_{N}}\right\rangle =2^{r-2}
\end{equation*}%
\begin{equation}
\func{Tr}\left\{ h_{j}h_{k}\right\} =2;1\leq j<k\leq 2
\end{equation}%
$\rfloor $
\end{enumerate}

If $r\rightarrow \infty $ and one does not allow infinite products of second
quantized operators then 
\begin{equation}
\lim\limits_{r\rightarrow \infty }2^{r-\kappa }=\infty ,
\end{equation}

where 
\begin{equation}
\kappa =p,q,p\cap q<\infty ,
\end{equation}%
as Fock space $\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}$ is by definition of the completion
of \emph{finite sequences of vectors wrt to direct sum norm. }
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
$\mathcal{B}_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $ and $\mathcal{B}%
_{2}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $ are not closed subspaces of $%
\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $ wrt the operator norm $%
\left\Vert {}\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) }.$
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
$\mathcal{B}_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $, $\mathcal{B}%
_{2}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $ and $\mathcal{B}_{0}\left( 
\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $ are two sided $\ast $-ideals in $\mathcal{%
B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $. $\mathcal{B}_{1}\left( \mathcal{%
H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $ and $\mathcal{B}_{2}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) $ are not closed wrt $\left\Vert {}\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}\left( 
\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) },$ but $\mathcal{B}_{0}\left( \mathcal{H}_{%
\mathcal{F}}\right) $ is. The closure of $\mathcal{B}_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}_{%
\mathcal{F}}\right) $ and $\mathcal{B}_{2}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) $ wrt $\left\Vert {}\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{%
\mathcal{F}}\right) }$ is $\mathcal{B}_{0}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) .$
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
The spectrum $\sigma \left( A\right) $ of a compact operator $A$ is discrete
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\sigma \left( A\right) =\left\{ \left. z_{j}\in \mathbb{C}\right\vert \left(
z_{j}I_{\mathcal{F}}-A\right) ^{-1}\notin \mathcal{B}\left( \left( \mathcal{H%
}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right) ,1\leq j\leq \infty \right\}
\end{equation}
and its singular values of a compact operator by $A$ 
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{s}\left( A\right) =\left\{ \left. \left\vert x_{j}^{\frac{1}{2}%
}\right\vert \in \mathbb{R}\right\vert \left( x_{j}I_{\mathcal{F}%
}-A^{\dagger }A\right) ^{-1}\notin \mathcal{B}\left( \left( \mathcal{H}_{%
\mathcal{F}}\right) \right) ;x_{j}\geq 0,1\leq j\leq \infty \right\} .
\end{equation}%
Note that $A$ may or may not be a normal operator or even diagonalizable.
\end{remark}

\begin{example}
Let 
\begin{equation}
A=\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
1 & 1 \\ 
0 & 0%
\end{array}%
\right)
\end{equation}%
then 
\begin{equation}
\sigma \left( A\right) =\left\{ 1,0\right\}
\end{equation}%
and 
\begin{eqnarray}
\mathfrak{s}\left( A\right) &=&\sigma \left( \left( A^{\dagger }A\right) ^{%
\frac{1}{2}}\right) =\sigma \left( \left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
1 & 1 \\ 
1 & 1%
\end{array}%
\right) \right) =\left\{ \left. \left\vert x\right\vert \right\vert \det
\left\{ \left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
x-1 & 1 \\ 
1 & x-1%
\end{array}%
\right) \right\} =0\right\} =\left\{ \left. \left\vert x\right\vert
\right\vert \left( x-1\right) \left( x-1\right) -1=0\right\} \\
&=&\left\{ \left. \left\vert x\right\vert \right\vert x^{2}-2x=0\right\}
=\left\{ 2,0\right\} .
\end{eqnarray}
\end{example}

\begin{remark}
If $A$ is a compact normal operator then 
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{s}\left( A\right) =\left\{ \left. \left\vert \alpha
_{j}\right\vert \right\vert \alpha _{j}\in \sigma \left( A\right) ,1\leq
j\leq \infty \right\} .
\end{equation}
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
As Trace Class and Hilbert-Scmidt operators are compact their squared norms $%
\left\Vert {}\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) }^{2}$ and $\left\Vert {}\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}_{2}\left( 
\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) }^{2}$ and thus have discrete spectra their
norms are given by 
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert A\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}_{2}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) }^{2}=\func{Tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger }A\right\} =\sum_{1\leq j\leq
2^{r}}\left\vert \alpha _{j}\right\vert ^{2}
\end{equation}%
and 
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert A\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) }^{2}=\left( \func{Tr}\left\{ \left\vert A^{\dagger }A\right\vert ^{%
\frac{1}{2}}\right\} \right) ^{2}=\left( \sum_{1\leq j\leq 2^{r}}\left\vert
\alpha _{j}\right\vert \right) ^{2}.
\end{equation}%
This shows that 
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert A\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) }^{2}-\left\Vert A\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}_{2}\left( \mathcal{H}_{%
\mathcal{F}}\right) }^{2}=\left( \sum_{1\leq j\leq 2^{r}}\left\vert \alpha
_{j}\right\vert \right) ^{2}-\sum_{1\leq j\leq 2^{r}}\left\vert \alpha
_{j}\right\vert ^{2}\geq 0
\end{equation}%
$\lceil $%
\begin{equation}
\left( \left\vert \alpha _{1}\right\vert +\left\vert \alpha _{2}\right\vert
\right) ^{2}-\left\vert \alpha _{1}\right\vert ^{2}-\left\vert \alpha
_{2}\right\vert ^{2}=\left\vert \alpha _{1}\right\vert ^{2}+\left\vert
\alpha _{2}\right\vert ^{2}+2\left\vert \alpha _{1}\right\vert \left\vert
\alpha _{2}\right\vert -\left\vert \alpha _{1}\right\vert ^{2}-\left\vert
\alpha _{2}\right\vert ^{2}=2\left\vert \alpha _{1}\right\vert \left\vert
\alpha _{2}\right\vert \geq 0
\end{equation}%
$\rfloor $ and thus 
\begin{gather}
\left\Vert {}\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) }\geq \left\Vert {}\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}_{2}\left( \mathcal{H}_{%
\mathcal{F}}\right) } \\
\Downarrow \\
\mathcal{B}_{0}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \supseteq \mathcal{B}%
_{2}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) ,
\end{gather}%
which can be extended to 
\begin{gather}
\left\Vert {}\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) }\geq \left\Vert {}\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}_{2}\left( \mathcal{H}_{%
\mathcal{F}}\right) }\geq \left\Vert {}\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}\left( 
\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) } \\
\Downarrow \\
\mathcal{B}_{0}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \supseteq \mathcal{B}%
_{2}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \supseteq \mathcal{B}_{1}\left( 
\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) .
\end{gather}
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
The ideals $\mathcal{B}_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $, $%
\mathcal{B}_{2}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $ and $\mathcal{B}%
_{0}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $ are in general \emph{not
unital.}
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
Products of Hilbert-Scmidt operators are trace class operators i.e 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{B}_{2}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \circ \mathcal{B}%
_{2}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \subseteq \mathcal{B}_{1}\left( 
\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) .
\end{equation}
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
One can extend this classification of compact operators by considering
Schatten Classes/2-sided Ideals $\mathcal{B}_{p}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{%
F}}\right) ,1\leq p<\infty $%
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{B}_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \subseteq \mathcal{B}%
_{2}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \subseteq \cdots \subseteq 
\mathcal{B}_{p}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \cdots \subseteq 
\mathcal{B}_{0}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \subseteq \mathcal{B}%
\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \equiv \mathcal{B}_{\infty }\left( 
\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) ,
\end{equation}%
where the norms are defined by 
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert A\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}_{p}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) }=\left\Vert A\right\Vert _{l_{p}\left( \mathfrak{s}\left( A\right)
\right) }=\left( \sum_{1\leq j\leq 2^{r}}\left\vert \alpha _{j}\right\vert
^{p}\right) ^{\frac{1}{p}}.
\end{equation}%
These various ideals are related by 
\begin{eqnarray}
\mathcal{B}_{p}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) ^{\ast } &=&\mathcal{B%
}_{q}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) ;\frac{1}{p}+\frac{1}{q}=1 \\
\mathcal{B}_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) ^{\ast } &=&\mathcal{B%
}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \equiv \mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}%
_{\mathcal{F}}\right) _{\ast } \\
\mathcal{B}_{0}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) ^{\ast } &=&\mathcal{B%
}_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right)
\end{eqnarray}%
and 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) ^{\ast }=\mathcal{B}%
_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \oplus _{\mathcal{B}_{1}\left( 
\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) }\mathcal{B}_{0}\left( \mathcal{H}_{%
\mathcal{F}}\right) ^{\bot }.
\end{equation}
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
Finite rank operators $\mathcal{B}_{00}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) $ are finite linear combinations of Ket-Bra operators. The closure
of $\mathcal{B}_{00}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $ in the various
norms produce the corresponding spaces except in the case of the operator
norm which produces the compact operators.
\end{remark}

\subsubsection{GNS\ Representation of $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{%
\mathcal{F}}\right) $}

\emph{It is important to note that }%
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
}\left( I_{\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}}\right) =\Phi _{\left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert \right)
\end{equation}%
as 
\begin{eqnarray}
\left\Vert \Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\left( I_{\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}}\right) -\Phi
_{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
}\left( \left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
\right) \right\Vert _{\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}}^{2} &=&\left\Vert \Phi
_{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
}\left( I_{\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}}-\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert \right) \right\Vert _{\mathcal{H}_{%
\mathcal{F}}}^{2}=\func{Tr}\left\{ \left( I_{\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}}-\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
\right) ^{2}\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert \right\} \\
&=&\func{Tr}\left\{ \left( I_{\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}}-\left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert \right) \left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert \right\} =\left\langle
\left. \Psi _{1}\right\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}}-\left\langle \left. \Psi _{1}\right\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle _{%
\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}}=0.
\end{eqnarray}%
and%
\begin{eqnarray}
&&\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
\Psi _{1}\right\vert }\left( I_{\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}}\right)
\right\vert \Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert \right) \right\rangle _{\Phi \left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) }=\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert }\left( I_{\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}}\right) \right\vert \Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
\Psi _{1}\right\vert }\left( I_{\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}}\right)
\right\rangle _{\Phi \left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) } \\
&=&\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
\Psi _{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
\Psi _{1}\right\vert \right) \right\vert \Phi _{\left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert \right) \right\rangle
_{\Phi \left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) }=\func{Tr}\left\{ \left\vert
\Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert \right\} =1
\end{eqnarray}%
However in $\mathcal{B}_{2}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $ they
have different scalar products i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{j}\right\vert \left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert \right\} =0;2\leq j\leq 2^{r},
\end{equation}%
while 
\begin{equation}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{j}\right\vert I_{\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}}\right\} =1;2\leq j\leq 2^{r}.
\end{equation}

The kernal 
\begin{equation}
\ker \left\{ \Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\right\} =\left\{ A\in \mathcal{F}\left\vert \func{Tr}%
\left\{ A^{\dagger }A\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert \right\} =0\right. \right\}
\end{equation}%
is given by linear combinations of the operators $\left\{ \left. \left\vert
\Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{k}\right\vert \right\vert 2\leq
j\leq 2^{r},1\leq k\leq 2^{r}\right\} ,$ as 
\begin{align}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{j}\right\vert \left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert \right\} & =0=\func{Tr}\left\{ \left\vert \Psi
_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{k}\right\vert \left\vert \Psi
_{k}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{j}\right\vert \left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert \right\} \\
& =\func{Tr}\left\{ \left( \left\vert \Psi _{k}\right\rangle \left\langle
\Psi _{j}\right\vert \right) ^{\dagger }\left( \left\vert \Psi
_{k}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{j}\right\vert \right) \left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert \right\} ;2\leq j\leq
2^{r},1\leq k\leq 2^{r}
\end{align}%
$\lceil $%
\begin{equation}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert \left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{j}\right\vert \left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert \right\} =0;\,2\leq j\leq 2^{r}
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{j}\right\vert \left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert \left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert \right\} =1;\,1\leq j\leq 2^{r}.
\end{equation}%
$\rfloor $

Hence%
\begin{equation}
\ker \left\{ \Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\right\} =\limfunc{linspan}\left\{ \left. \left\vert \Psi
_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{k}\right\vert \right\vert ;2\leq
j,k\leq 2^{r}\right\} ,\left\{ \left. \left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi _{j}\right\vert \right\vert ;2\leq j\leq 2^{r}\right\}
\end{equation}%
giving 
\begin{equation}
\dim \left\{ \ker \left\{ \Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert }\right\} \right\}
=2^{2r}-2^{r}=2^{r}\left( 2^{r}-1\right)
\end{equation}%
and 
\begin{equation}
\dim \left\{ \Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\right\} =2^{r}=\dim \left\{ \mathcal{H}_{F}\right\} .
\end{equation}%
$\lceil \dim \left\{ \mathcal{H}^{1}\right\} =r=2\Rightarrow \dim \left\{
\tbigwedge \left( \mathcal{H}^{1}\right) \right\} =2^{2}=4$%
\begin{gather}
\ker \left\{ \Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\right\} \\
\shortparallel
\end{gather}%
\begin{eqnarray}
\left\{ \left. \left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{k}\right\vert \right\vert ;2\leq j,k\leq 2^{2}\right\} &=&\left\vert \Psi
_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{2}\right\vert ,\left\vert \Psi
_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{3}\right\vert ,\left\vert \Psi
_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{4}\right\vert ,\left\vert \Psi
_{3}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{2}\right\vert ,\left\vert \Psi
_{3}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{3}\right\vert ,\left\vert \Psi
_{3}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{4}\right\vert ,\left\vert \Psi
_{4}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{2}\right\vert ,\left\vert \Psi
_{4}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{3}\right\vert ,\left\vert \Psi
_{4}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{4}\right\vert \\
\left\{ \left. \left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{j}\right\vert \right\vert ;2\leq j\leq 2^{2}\right\} &=&\left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{2}\right\vert ,\left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{3}\right\vert ,\left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{4}\right\vert
\end{eqnarray}%
\begin{equation}
\dim \left\{ \ker \left\{ \Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert }\right\} \right\} =2^{4}-2^{2}=16-4=12
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\ker \left\{ \Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\right\} ^{\bot }=\left\{ \left. \left\vert \Psi
_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert \right\vert ;1\leq j\leq
2^{2}\right\} =\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert ,\left\vert \Psi _{2}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert ,\left\vert \Psi _{3}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert ,\left\vert \Psi _{4}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\dim \left\{ \ker \left\{ \Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert }\right\} ^{\bot }\right\} =4
\end{equation}%
\begin{eqnarray}
\dim \left\{ \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right\} &=&\dim \left\{ \tbigwedge
\left( \mathcal{H}^{1}\right) \right\} =2^{2}=4 \\
\dim \left\{ \mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right\}
&=&2^{4}=16
\end{eqnarray}%
$\rfloor $

\paragraph{Isotropy Group of $\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
\Psi _{1}\right\vert $}

The isotropy group of $\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert $ is $U\left( 2^{r-1}\right) \times U\left( 1\right) $
showing that the set of all atomic projectors belonging to $\mathcal{B}%
\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $ is the coherent state manifold 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\frac{U\left( 2^{r}\right) }{U\left( 1\right) \times U\left(
2^{r-1}\right) }}\left( \left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert \right) =\left\{ \left. \left\vert \Psi \right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi \right\vert \right\vert \Psi \in \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
},\left\Vert \Psi \right\Vert _{\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}}=1\right\}
\end{equation}%
as 
\begin{equation}
U\left( 2^{r}\right) =\frac{U\left( 2^{r}\right) }{U\left( 1\right) \times
U\left( 2^{r-1}\right) }\left( U\left( 1\right) \times U\left(
2^{r-1}\right) \right) .
\end{equation}%
In general 
\begin{eqnarray}
&&\widehat{\exp \left\{ i\tsum\limits_{1\leq j,k\leq 2^{r}}\lambda
_{jk}\left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{k}\right\vert
\right\} }\left( A\right) \\
&=&\widehat{\exp \left\{ i\tsum\limits_{2\leq j\leq 2^{r}}\left( \eta
_{j1}\left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
+\eta _{1j}\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{j}\right\vert \right) \right\} \exp \left\{ i\eta _{11}\left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert \right\} \exp \left\{
i\tsum\limits_{2\leq j,k\leq 2^{r}}\lambda _{jk}\left\vert \Psi
_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{k}\right\vert \right\} }\left(
A\right) .
\end{eqnarray}%
and the action on the projector $\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert $ by 
\begin{eqnarray}
&&\widehat{\exp \left\{ i\tsum\limits_{1\leq j\leq 2^{r}}\left( \lambda
_{j1}\left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
+\lambda _{1j}\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{j}\right\vert \right) \right\} \exp \left\{ i\tsum\limits_{1\leq j\leq
2^{r}}\eta _{11}\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert \right\} \exp \left\{ i\tsum\limits_{2\leq j,k\leq
2^{r}}\lambda _{jk}\left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{k}\right\vert \right\} }\left( \left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert \right) \\
&=&\widehat{\exp \left\{ i\tsum\limits_{1\leq j\leq 2^{r}}\left( \lambda
_{j1}\left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
+\lambda _{1j}\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{j}\right\vert \right) \right\} \exp \left\{ i\tsum\limits_{1\leq j\leq
2^{r}}\eta _{11}\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert \right\} }\left( \left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert \right) \\
&=&\widehat{\exp \left\{ i\tsum\limits_{1\leq j\leq 2^{r}}\left( \lambda
_{j1}\left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
+\lambda _{1j}\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{j}\right\vert \right) \right\} }\left( \left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert \right) .
\end{eqnarray}

One can observe that 
\begin{eqnarray}
\left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
&=&\left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert \\
\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
\left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert &=&0 \\
\left[ \left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
,\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{j}\right\vert \right]
&=&\left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
\end{eqnarray}%
and%
\begin{eqnarray}
\left[ \left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
,\left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{k}\right\vert \right]
&=&0;\,2\leq j,k\leq 2^{r} \\
\left[ \left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
,\left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{k}\right\vert \right]
&=&0;\,2\leq j,k\leq 2^{r} \\
\left[ \left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{l}\right\vert
,\left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{k}\right\vert \right]
&=&0;\,2\leq j,k\leq 2^{r} \\
\left[ \left\vert \Psi _{l}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
,\left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{k}\right\vert \right]
&=&0;\,2\leq j,k\leq 2^{r},1\leq l\leq 2^{r}.
\end{eqnarray}

The compliment of $\ker \left\{ \Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert }\right\} $ in $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{%
H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $ is 
\begin{equation}
\ker \left\{ \Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\right\} ^{c}=\limfunc{linspan}\left\{ \left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert ,\left\{ \left\vert \Psi
_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert ;2\leq j\leq
2^{r}\right\} \right\} =\limfunc{linspan}\left\{ I_{\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}},\left\{ \left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert ;2\leq j\leq 2^{r}\right\} \right\} \cong \mathcal{H}_{%
\mathcal{F}}
\end{equation}

One should note that $\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert $ can be factored into the following positive products of
nilpotent partial isometries 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
=\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{j}\right\vert
\left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert =\left(
\left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert \right)
^{\dagger }\left( \left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert \right) ;1\leq j\leq 2^{r},
\end{equation}%
where 
\begin{equation}
\left( \left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{j}\right\vert
\right) ^{2}=0;2\leq j\leq 2^{r}.
\end{equation}%
The reverse products are 
\begin{equation}
\left( \left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
\right) \left( \left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert \right) ^{\dagger }=\left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert \left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi _{j}\right\vert =\left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi _{j}\right\vert
\end{equation}%
so $\left\{ \left. \left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert \right\vert 2\leq j\leq 2^{r}\right\} $ are partial
isometries, with initial subspace $\left\{ \left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle
\right\} $ and final subspaces $\left\{ \left. \left\vert \Psi
_{j}\right\rangle \right\vert 2\leq j\leq 2^{r}\right\} .$

In general $\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
\Psi_{1}\right\vert $ can be factored into the following positive products
as 
\begin{equation}
\left( \left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi\right\vert \right)
\left( \left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi\right\vert \right)
^{\dagger}=\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi\right\vert
\left\vert \Psi\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert ,
\end{equation}
which are not in general nilpotent as 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi\right\vert \left\vert
\Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi\right\vert \neq0
\end{equation}
unless $\Psi_{1}\bot\Psi.$ The reverse products in these cases are 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \Psi\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert \left\vert
\Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi\right\vert =\left\vert \Psi
\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi\right\vert ,
\end{equation}
so $\left\{ \left. \left\vert \Psi\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert \right\vert \Psi\neq\Psi_{1}\right\} $ are still partial
isometries.

One can prove that the representation $\Pi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert }$ is faithful i.e.

If 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
}\left( A\right) =0\Leftrightarrow A=0
\end{equation}%
by observing that if 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
}\left( A\right) \Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert \right) =0;1\leq j\leq 2^{r},
\end{equation}%
which gives 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
}\left( \sum\limits_{1\leq p,q\leq 2^{r}}A_{pq}\left\vert \Psi
_{p}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{q}\right\vert \right) \Phi
_{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
}\left( \left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
\right) =0;1\leq j\leq 2^{r},
\end{equation}%
then%
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
}\left( \sum\limits_{1\leq p,q\leq 2^{r}}A_{pq}\left\vert \Psi
_{p}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{q}\right\vert \left\vert \Psi
_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert \right) =0;1\leq j\leq
2^{r},
\end{equation}%
and finally%
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
}\left( \sum\limits_{1\leq p\leq 2^{r}}A_{pj}\left\vert \Psi
_{p}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert \right) =0;1\leq j\leq
2^{r}.
\end{equation}%
$\lceil $%
\begin{align}
\Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
}\left( \sum\limits_{1\leq p\leq 3}A_{p1}\left\vert \Psi _{p}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert \right) & =\sum\limits_{1\leq p\leq
3}A_{p1}\Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert \Psi _{p}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert \right) =0\Leftrightarrow A_{p1}=0,1\leq p\leq 3 \\
\Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
}\left( \sum\limits_{1\leq p\leq 3}A_{p2}\left\vert \Psi _{p}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert \right) & =\sum\limits_{1\leq p\leq
3}A_{p2}\Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert \Psi _{p}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert \right) =0\Leftrightarrow A_{p2}=0,1\leq p\leq 3 \\
\Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
}\left( \sum\limits_{1\leq p\leq 3}A_{p3}\left\vert \Psi _{p}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert \right) & =\sum\limits_{1\leq p\leq
3}A_{p3}\Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert \Psi _{p}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert \right) =0\Leftrightarrow A_{p3}=0,1\leq p\leq 3.
\end{align}%
$\rfloor $

Thus 
\begin{equation}
\Phi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert
}\left( \sum\limits_{1\leq p\leq2^{r}}A_{pj}\left\vert \Psi_{p}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert \right) =0\Leftrightarrow\sum\limits_{1\leq
p\leq2^{r}}A_{pj}\left\vert \Psi_{p}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert \in\ker\left\{ \Phi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert }\right\} ;1\leq j\leq2^{r},
\end{equation}
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\sum\limits_{1\leq p\leq2^{r}}A_{pj}\left\vert \Psi_{p}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert \in\limfunc{linspan}\left\{ \left.
\left\vert \Psi_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{k}\right\vert
\right\vert ;2\leq j,k\leq2^{r}\right\} ,\left\{ \left. \left\vert
\Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{j}\right\vert \right\vert ;2\leq
j\leq 2^{r}\right\} ,\text{ }
\end{equation}
i.e.%
\begin{equation}
\sum\limits_{1\leq p\leq2^{r}}A_{pj}\left\vert \Psi_{p}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert =\sum\limits_{2\leq
p,q\leq2^{r}}A_{pq}\left\vert \Psi_{p}\right\rangle \left\langle
\Psi_{q}\right\vert +\sum\limits_{2\leq q\leq2^{r}}A_{1q}\left\vert
\Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{q}\right\vert ;1\leq j\leq2^{r},
\end{equation}
which shows that 
\begin{equation}
A=0.
\end{equation}
$\lceil$%
\begin{equation*}
A\left\vert \Psi_{p}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{q}\right\vert
\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{j}\right\vert
=0\forall\left\vert \Psi_{p}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{q}\right\vert
,q\neq1\in \mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right)
\end{equation*}
$\rfloor$

The GNS\ Hilbert space generated by $\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert $ from the Type $I_{\infty }$ VnA $%
\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) ,$ where $\mathcal{H}_{%
\mathcal{F}}=\limfunc{linspan}\left\{ \left. \left\vert \Psi
_{j}\right\rangle \right\vert ;1\leq j\leq 2^{r}\right\} $ is given by 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}_{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }=\limfunc{linspan}\left\{ \left. \Phi _{\left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert \Psi
_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert \right) \right\vert
;1\leq j\leq 2^{r}\right\} \cong \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}.
\end{equation}

\begin{remark}
One can observe that as 
\begin{equation}
\left\{ \left. \left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{j}\right\vert \right\vert 2\leq j\leq 2^{r}\right\} \in \ker \left\{ \Phi
_{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
}\right\}
\end{equation}%
one has that 
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
}\left( \left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
\right) =\Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) .
\end{equation}
\end{remark}

One also has the creation and annihilation relationships 
\begin{eqnarray}
&&\Pi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
}\left( \left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
\right) \left\vert \Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
\Psi _{1}\right\vert }\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right\rangle
=\left\vert \Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert \right) \right\rangle \\
&&\Pi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
}\left( \left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{j}\right\vert
\right) \left\vert \Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
\Psi _{1}\right\vert }\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right\rangle =0 \\
&&2\leq j\leq 2^{r},
\end{eqnarray}%
showing that $\Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) =$ $\Phi _{\left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert \right) $ is a vacuum
type vector.

\begin{lemma}
\begin{equation}
\Pi \ _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
}\left( \left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
\right) =\left\vert \Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
\Psi _{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
\Psi _{1}\right\vert \right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{\left\vert
\Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert
\Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert \right) \right\vert
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
\begin{eqnarray}
&&\Pi \ _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert \right) \left\vert \Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert \right) \right\rangle =\left\vert \Phi
_{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
}\left( \left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
\right) \right\rangle \delta _{j1} \\
&&\left( \left\vert \Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
\Psi _{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
\Psi _{1}\right\vert \right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{\left\vert
\Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert
\Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert \right) \right\vert
\right) \left\vert \Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
\Psi _{1}\right\vert }\left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert \right\rangle =\left\vert \Phi _{\left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert \right) \right\rangle
\delta _{j1} \\
&&2\leq j\leq 2^{r}.
\end{eqnarray}
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The representations of the bra-kets not in the kernal \emph{do not satisfy
the CAR\ nor CCR} as 
\begin{eqnarray}
\Pi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
}\left( \left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
\right) \Pi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert \Psi _{k}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert \right) \delta _{k1} \\
\Pi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
}\left( \left\vert \Psi _{k}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
\right) \Pi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert \Psi _{k}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert \right) \delta _{j1} \\
\Pi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
}\left( \left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
\right) \Pi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{k}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi _{k}\right\vert \right) \\
\Pi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
}\left( \left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{k}\right\vert
\right) \Pi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert \right) \delta _{jk},
\end{eqnarray}
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
The complete set of bra-kets $\left\{ \left. \left\vert \Psi
_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{k}\right\vert \right\vert ;1\leq
j,k\leq 2^{r}\right\} $ and their representations $\left\{ \left. \Pi
_{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
}\left( \left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{k}\right\vert
\right) \right\vert ;1\leq j,k\leq 2^{r}\right\} $ generate the Lie algebra $%
gl\left( 2^{r},\mathbb{C}\right) $.
\end{remark}

\paragraph{Zero Grade Vectors}

The algebra $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $ has the
operator products $\boxtimes $ and more generally $\bullet $ inherited from
the exterior algebra $\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}$, where 
\begin{eqnarray}
\left\vert \Psi _{a}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{b}\right\vert \cdot
\left\vert \Psi _{c}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{d}\right\vert
&=&\left\vert \Psi _{a}\wedge _{\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}}\Psi
_{c}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{b}\wedge _{\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}}\Psi _{d}\right\vert , \\
\left\vert \Psi _{a}\right\rangle \wedge _{\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}}\left\vert \Psi _{b}\right\rangle &\in &\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
};\,\left\vert \Psi _{a}\right\rangle ,\left\vert \Psi _{b}\right\rangle \in 
\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}
\end{eqnarray}%
and $\wedge _{\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}}$ is the exterior product in $%
\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}$. It also has the Clifford exterior product "$%
\wedge _{\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) }\equiv \wedge _{%
\mathcal{F}}$" and the operator $c^{\ast }$-algebra composition product "$%
\circ $", leading to the $\wedge _{\Pi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert }\left( \mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{%
\mathcal{F}}\right) \right) }$product in the representations generated by
the hilbert space 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}_{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }=\limfunc{linspan}\left\{ \left. \Phi _{\left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert \Psi
_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert \right) \right\vert
;1\leq j\leq 2^{r}\right\} \cong \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}.
\end{equation}

\begin{remark}
The representation $\Pi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
\Psi _{1}\right\vert }$ is a $c^{\ast }$-algebra representation as well as
Clifford algebra representation, while $\Phi _{\left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert }$ is a vector space and
a Grassmann algebra representation.
\end{remark}

The "$\wedge _{\Pi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\left( \mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right)
\right) }$" product has the following properties wrt $\Pi _{\left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert }$ and $\Phi
_{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert }$:

\begin{enumerate}
\item 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
}\left( A\wedge _{\mathcal{F}}B\right) =\Pi _{\left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert }\left( A\right) \wedge
_{\Pi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
}\left( \mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right) }\Pi
_{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
}\left( B\right)
\end{equation}

\item 
\begin{equation}
\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\wedge _{\mathcal{F}}\right) B=B\,\forall B\in 
\mathcal{F}
\end{equation}

\item 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
}\left( A\wedge _{\mathcal{F}}B\right) \Phi _{\left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert }\left( I_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) =\Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\left( A\wedge _{\mathcal{F}}B\right) =\Phi _{\left\vert
\Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert }\left( A\right)
\wedge _{\Pi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\left( \mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right)
\right) }\Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\left( B\right)
\end{equation}

\item 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\wedge _{\mathcal{F}}B\right) =\Pi _{\left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert }\left( I_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) \wedge _{\Pi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\left( \mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right)
\right) }\Pi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\left( B\right) =\Pi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert }\left( B\right)
\end{equation}%
Thus 
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \wedge _{\Pi _{\left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert }\left( \mathcal{B}%
\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right) }\Phi _{\left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert }\left( B\right) =\Phi
_{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
}\left( B\right) \,\forall B\in \mathcal{F}
\end{equation}%
showing that $\Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $ is a \emph{grade zero
vector in the exterior algebra }$\Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert }\left( \mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{%
\mathcal{F}}\right) \right) $.
\end{enumerate}

$\lceil $%
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\wedge _{\mathcal{F}}B\right) \Phi _{\left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert }\left( I_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) =\Phi _{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\wedge _{\mathcal{F}}B\right) =\Phi
_{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \wedge _{\Pi _{\left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert }\left( \mathcal{B}%
\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right) }\Phi _{\left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert }\left( B\right) =\Phi
_{\left\vert \Psi _{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
}\left( B\right)
\end{equation}%
$\rfloor $

All finite dimensional $w^{\ast }$-algebras are type $I_{n}$ and are
isomorphic to $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}\right) ,$where $\dim \left\{ 
\mathcal{H}\right\} =n<\infty ,$ it is the limiting process that produces
different types of $w^{\ast }$-algebras. The limiting process \emph{involves
states and dimension}. In particular the limit of the CAR algebra with
normalized trace is a $w^{\ast }$-algebra of type $II_{1}.$

\subsubsection{Vacuum States in General CAR algebras $\mathcal{A}$}

$\lceil $ The transformation form the non self adjoint second quantized
basis to the self adjoint basis for $r=1$ is 
\begin{align}
& K\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
a^{\dagger } & a%
\end{array}%
\right) =\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
a^{\dagger } & a%
\end{array}%
\right) \mathbb{K}=\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
a^{\dagger } & a%
\end{array}%
\right) \left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} & \frac{i}{\sqrt{2}} \\ 
\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} & \frac{-i}{\sqrt{2}}%
\end{array}%
\right) =\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
x_{1} & x_{2}%
\end{array}%
\right) \\
& K^{\dagger }\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
x_{1} & x_{2}%
\end{array}%
\right) =\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
x_{1} & x_{2}%
\end{array}%
\right) \mathbb{K}^{\dagger }=\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
x_{1} & x_{2}%
\end{array}%
\right) \left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} & \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \\ 
\frac{-i}{\sqrt{2}} & \frac{i}{\sqrt{2}}%
\end{array}%
\right) =\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
a^{\dagger } & a%
\end{array}%
\right)
\end{align}%
giving 
\begin{align}
& n=a^{\dagger }a=\frac{1}{2}\left( x_{1}-ix_{2}\right) \left(
x_{1}+ix_{2}\right) =\frac{1}{2}\left(
x_{1}^{2}+x_{2}^{2}-ix_{2}x_{1}+ix_{1}x_{2}\right) \\
& h=aa^{\dagger }=\frac{1}{2}\left( x_{1}+ix_{2}\right) \left(
x_{1}-ix_{2}\right) =\frac{1}{2}\left(
x_{1}^{2}+x_{2}^{2}+ix_{2}x_{1}-ix_{1}x_{2}\right) \\
& n+h=a^{\dagger }a+aa^{\dagger }=I_{\mathcal{A}}=I_{2} \\
& =\frac{1}{2}\left( x_{1}^{2}+x_{2}^{2}-ix_{2}x_{2}+ix_{1}x_{2}\right) +%
\frac{1}{2}\left( x_{1}^{2}+x_{2}^{2}+ix_{2}x_{2}-ix_{1}x_{2}\right)
=x_{1}^{2}+x_{2}^{2}
\end{align}%
\begin{equation}
\Longleftrightarrow x_{1}^{2}=x_{2}^{2}=\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{A}}.
\end{equation}%
$\left( \text{as it is always true }x_{1}^{2}=x_{2}^{2}\right) .$ Thus 
\begin{align}
a^{\dagger }a& =\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{A}}+ix_{1}x_{2} \\
aa^{\dagger }& =\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{A}}-ix_{1}x_{2}
\end{align}%
$\rfloor $

Normal \emph{vacuum states} are states, $D_{f_{b}},$that have the \emph{%
necessary} properties

\begin{enumerate}
\item 
\begin{gather}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ D_{f_{b}}\right\} =1 \\
D_{f_{b}}\geq0\Leftrightarrow D_{f_{b}}=Q_{f_{b}}Q_{f_{b}}^{\dagger} \\
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{b}\left( D_{f_{b}}\right) =\left[ \emph{\mathcal{N}}%
_{b},D_{f_{b}}\right] =0 \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \mathcal{N}_{b}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =0,
\end{gather}
where 
\begin{align}
\emph{\mathcal{N}}_{b} & =\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{j} \\
\left[ \mathcal{N}_{b},b_{j}\right] & =-b_{j}\Longleftrightarrow\left[ 
\mathcal{N}_{b},b_{j}^{\dagger}\right] =b_{j}^{\dagger}.
\end{align}
$\lceil$%
\begin{align}
\left( b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{1}+b_{2}^{\dagger}b_{2}+b_{3}^{\dagger}b_{3}\right)
b_{3} & =b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{1}b_{3}+b_{2}^{\dagger}b_{2}b_{3} \\
-b_{3}\left(
b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{1}+b_{2}^{\dagger}b_{2}+b_{3}^{\dagger}b_{3}\right) &
=-b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{1}b_{3}-b_{2}^{\dagger}b_{2}b_{3}-\left(
1-b_{3}^{\dagger}b_{3}\right)
b_{3}=-b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{1}b_{3}-b_{2}^{\dagger}b_{2}b_{3}-b_{3} \\
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{b}\left( b_{3}\right) & =\left[ \mathcal{N}_{b},b_{3}%
\right] =-b_{3} \\
\left[ b_{3}^{\dagger},\mathcal{N}_{b}\right] & =-b_{3}^{\dagger
}\Longleftrightarrow\left[ \mathcal{N}_{b},b_{3}^{\dagger}\right]
=b_{3}^{\dagger}
\end{align}
$\rfloor$

\begin{remark}
$\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{b}$ is a derrivation i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{b}\left( Q_{f_{b}}Q_{f_{b}}^{\dagger }\right) =%
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{b}\left( Q_{f_{b}}\right) Q_{f_{b}}^{\dagger
}+Q_{f_{b}}\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{b}\left( Q_{f_{b}}^{\dagger }\right)
\end{equation}%
as%
\begin{align}
\left[ A,BC\right] & =ABC-BCA \\
\left[ A,B\right] C+B\left[ A,C\right] & =ABC-BAC+BAC-BCA=ABC-BCA \\
\left[ A,BC\right] & =\left[ A,B\right] C+B\left[ A,C\right] .
\end{align}
\end{remark}

\item 
\begin{equation}
\left[ \emph{\mathcal{N}}_{b},D_{f_{b}}\right] =0\Rightarrow D_{f_{b}}=\eta
_{0}I_{\mathcal{F}}+\tsum\limits_{1\leq n\leq r}\tsum\limits_{\substack{ %
1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq r  \\ 1\leq k_{1}<\cdots <k_{n}\leq r}}\eta
_{j_{1}\cdots j_{n}k_{1}\cdots k_{n}}b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }b_{k_{n}}\cdots b_{k_{1}}.
\end{equation}%
Note that in general%
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \tsum\limits_{1\leq n\leq r}\tsum\limits_{\substack{ %
1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq r  \\ 1\leq k_{1}<\cdots <k_{n}\leq r}}\eta
_{j_{1}\cdots j_{n}k_{1}\cdots k_{n}}b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }b_{k_{n}}\cdots b_{k_{1}}\right\} \neq 0.
\end{equation}

\item $U\left( r\right) $ has unitary (spinor) representations $\mathcal{R}%
_{b}\left( U\left( r\right) \right) $ in $\mathcal{A}$ given by 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{b}\left( U\left( r\right) \right) \left( A\right) =\exp \left\{
i\tsum\limits_{1\leq j,k\leq r}\beta _{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{k}\right\}
\left( A\right) \exp \left\{ -i\tsum\limits_{1\leq j,k\leq r}\beta
_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{k}\right\} ;\beta _{jk}=\overline{\beta }%
_{kj};\forall A\in \mathcal{A}
\end{equation}%
These representations are unitarily equivalent as 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{\left[ b^{\prime }\right] }\left( U\left( r\right) \right) =%
\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{A}}\left( O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}\right) \right) \left( 
\mathcal{R}_{\left[ b\right] }\left( U\left( r\right) \right) \right) ,
\end{equation}%
where $\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{A}}\left( O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}\right) \right) $
is a unitary representation of $O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}\right) $ in $\mathcal{A%
}.$ Clearly as $U\left( r\right) \subset O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}\right) $ 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{\left[ b\right] }\left( U\left( r\right) \right) =\mathcal{R}_{%
\mathcal{A}}\left( U\left( r\right) \right) \left( \mathcal{R}_{\left[ b%
\right] }\left( U\left( r\right) \right) \right) ,
\end{equation}%
where $\mathcal{R}_{\left[ b\right] }$ is an equivalence class of $U\left(
r\right) $ equivalent representations within $\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{A}%
}\left( O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}\right) \right) $.

$\lceil $ One can factor $O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}\right) $ in terms of a coset
decomposition as 
\begin{equation}
O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}\right) =\frac{O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}\right) }{U\left(
r\right) }U\left( r\right) ,
\end{equation}%
which is represented in $\mathcal{A}$ as 
\begin{equation}
\left( \widehat{\exp \left\{ i\tsum\limits_{1\leq j<k\leq r}\left( \alpha
_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{k}^{\dagger }+\overline{\alpha }_{jk}b_{k}^{\dagger
}b_{j}^{\dagger }\right) \right\} }\right) \left( \widehat{\exp \left\{
i\tsum\limits_{1\leq j,k\leq r}\beta _{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{k}\right\} }%
\right)
\end{equation}%
the term 
\begin{equation}
\left( \widehat{\exp \left\{ i\tsum\limits_{1\leq j,k\leq r}\beta
_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{k}\right\} }\right)
\end{equation}%
can be be replaced by any 
\begin{equation}
\left( \widehat{\exp \left\{ i\tsum\limits_{1\leq j,k\leq r}\gamma
_{jk}c_{j}^{\dagger }c_{k}\right\} }\right) \in \mathcal{R}_{\left[ c\right]
}\left( U\left( r\right) \right) .
\end{equation}%
Different equivalence classes $\left[ b\right] \neq \left[ c\right] $ form
unitarily \emph{equivalent} representations of $U\left( r\right) $ in $%
\mathcal{A},$ through transformations belonging to $\mathcal{R}_{\left[ b%
\right] }\left( \frac{O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}\right) }{U\left( r\right) }%
\right) $ or $\mathcal{R}_{\left[ c\right] }\left( \frac{O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R%
}\right) }{U\left( r\right) }\right) .$

\item Different $\mathcal{R}_{\left[ b\right] }\left( U\left( r\right)
\right) $ are associated with different vacuum states leading to different
vacuum vectors in the \emph{GNS Hilbert space} induced by these vacuum
vectors, that can be identified as lowest weight vectors associated with the
representation.

All vacuum states lie on the orbit produced by the action of the coset $%
\mathcal{R}_{\left[ b\right] }\left( \frac{O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}\right) }{%
U\left( r\right) }\right) $ on the vacuum state $D_{f_{b}}.$ This orbit
forms a Homogeneous manifold (Symmetric space, K\"{a}hler manifold) and are
coherent states.

\item $U\left( r\right) $-invariance%
\begin{gather}
\widehat{\exp \left\{ i\tsum\limits_{1\leq j,k\leq r}\beta
_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{k}\right\} }\left( D_{f_{b}}\right) =\exp \left\{
i\tsum\limits_{1\leq j,k\leq r}\beta _{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{k}\right\}
\left( D_{f_{b}}\right) \exp \left\{ -i\tsum\limits_{1\leq j,k\leq r}\beta
_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{k}\right\} =D_{f_{b}};\beta _{jk}=\overline{\beta }%
_{kj} \\
\left[ b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{k},D_{f_{b}}\right] =0.
\end{gather}%
These conditions can be strengthened by observing that $\mathbf{\beta }$ is
hermitian and thus it can always be diagonalized by a unitary $r\times r$
matrix $\mathbb{V}$ to give%
\begin{equation}
\beta _{jk}=\tsum\limits_{1\leq l\leq r}\overline{V}_{lj}\gamma _{ll}V_{lk}.
\end{equation}%
This shows that 
\begin{align}
\tsum\limits_{1\leq j,k\leq r}\beta _{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{k}&
=\tsum\limits_{1\leq j,k\leq r}\tsum\limits_{1\leq l\leq r}\overline{V}%
_{lj}\gamma _{ll}V_{lk}b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{k}=\tsum\limits_{1\leq l\leq
r}\gamma _{ll}\tsum\limits_{1\leq j,k\leq r}\overline{V}_{lj}b_{j}^{\dagger
}b_{k}V_{lk}=\tsum\limits_{1\leq l\leq r}\gamma _{ll}c_{l}^{\dagger }c_{l} \\
c_{l}^{\dagger }& =\tsum\limits_{1\leq j\leq r}\overline{V}%
_{lj}b_{j}^{\dagger };\quad c_{l}=\tsum\limits_{1\leq k\leq r}V_{lk}b_{k},
\end{align}%
and hence%
\begin{gather}
\exp \left\{ i\tsum\limits_{1\leq j,k\leq r}\beta _{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger
}b_{k}\right\} \left( D_{f_{b}}\right) \exp \left\{ -i\tsum\limits_{1\leq
j,k\leq r}\beta _{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{k}\right\} =D_{f_{b}} \\
\Updownarrow \\
\exp \left\{ i\tsum\limits_{1\leq l\leq r}\gamma _{ll}c_{l}^{\dagger
}c_{l}\right\} \left( D_{f_{b}}\right) \exp \left\{ -i\tsum\limits_{1\leq
l\leq r}\gamma _{ll}c_{l}^{\dagger }c_{l}\right\} =D_{f_{b}}.
\end{gather}

Hence if 
\begin{equation}
\left[ b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{j},D_{f_{b}}\right] =0;1\leq j\leq r
\end{equation}
for a basis set $\left\{ \left. b_{j}^{\dagger}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq
r\right\} $ then $D_{f_{b}}$ is $U\left( r\right) $-invariant. This further
shows that 
\begin{equation}
\left[ \tprod \limits_{1\leq j\leq r}n_{bj},D_{f_{b}}\right] =\left[
n_{bj_{1}}\cdots n_{bj_{m}},D_{f_{b}}\right] =0;1\leq j_{1},\cdots,j_{m}\leq
r.
\end{equation}
$\lceil$As%
\begin{align}
\left[ D_{f_{b}},n_{bj_{1}}\cdots n_{bj_{m}}\right] & =\left[
D_{f_{b}},n_{bj_{1}}\right] n_{bj_{2}}\cdots n_{bj_{m}}+n_{bj_{1}}\left[
D_{f_{b}},n_{bj_{2}}\cdots n_{bj_{m}}\right] \\
& =\left[ D_{f_{b}},n_{bj_{1}}\right] n_{bj_{2}}\cdots
n_{bj_{m}}+n_{bj_{1}}\left( \left[ D_{f_{b}},n_{bj_{2}}\right]
n_{bj_{3}}\cdots n_{bj_{m}}+n_{bj_{2}}\left[ D_{f_{b}},n_{bj_{3}}\cdots
n_{bj_{m}}\right] \right) \\
& =\left[ D_{f_{b}},n_{bj_{1}}\right] n_{bj_{2}}\cdots n_{bj_{m}}+n_{bj_{1}}%
\left[ D_{f_{b}},n_{bj_{2}}\right] n_{bj_{3}}\cdots
n_{bj_{m}}+\cdots+n_{bj_{1}}\cdots n_{bj_{m-1}}\left[ D_{f_{b}},n_{bj_{n}}%
\right] .
\end{align}
$\rfloor$

As $\left\{ \left. n_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq r\right\} $ is set of
commuting orthogonal (however, not orthogonal to each other) projections
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\left[ n_{j},n_{k}\right] =0,n_{j}=n_{j}^{\dagger };1\leq j,k\leq r
\end{equation}%
$D_{f_{b}}$ \emph{can be expanded in terms of these projectors as (may not
be true in general but only for vacuum states) }%
\begin{equation}
D_{f_{b}}=\eta _{0}I_{\mathcal{F}}+\tsum\limits_{1\leq n\leq
r}\tsum\limits_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq r}\eta _{j_{1}\cdots
j_{n}}n_{bj_{1}}\cdots n_{bj_{n}},
\end{equation}%
which is consistent with the representation of vacuum states given by 
\begin{align}
D_{f_{b}}& =\tprod\limits_{1\leq n\leq r}\left( 2h_{bn}\right)
=\tprod\limits_{1\leq n\leq r}2\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}-\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{%
F}}+ix_{n}x_{n+r}\right) =\tprod\limits_{1\leq n\leq r}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}%
}+2ix_{j}x_{j+r}\right) \\
& =I_{\mathcal{F}}+\tsum\limits_{1\leq n\leq r}\left( 2i\right)
^{n}\tsum\limits_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq r}x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\cdots
x_{j_{n}}x_{j_{n}+r} \\
& =\tprod\limits_{1\leq n\leq r}2\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}-n_{bj}\right)
=2^{n}I_{\mathcal{F}}+\tsum\limits_{1\leq n\leq r}2^{n}\left( -1\right)
^{n}\tsum\limits_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq r}n_{bj_{1}}\cdots n_{bj_{n}}
\end{align}%
obtained elsewhere, which shows 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ D_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
\tprod\limits_{1\leq n\leq r}\left( 2h_{bn}\right) \right\} =2^{n}\limfunc{tr%
}\left\{ \tprod\limits_{1\leq n\leq r}\left( h_{bn}\right) \right\} =1,\quad
1\leq r\leq \infty ,
\end{equation}%
as 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ I_{\mathcal{F}}\right\} =1
\end{equation}%
and 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\cdots x_{j_{n}}x_{j_{n}+r}\right\}
=0;1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq r,1\leq n\leq r.
\end{equation}%
The projectors $\left\{ \left. n_{bj},h_{bj}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq
r\right\} $ are not normalized, while the density operators $\left\{ \left.
2n_{bj},2h_{bj}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq r\right\} $ are normalized but not
projectors. The operators $\left\{ \left. n_{bj}-h_{bj}\right\vert 1\leq
j\leq r\right\} $ are unitary in $\mathcal{A}$ i.e 
\begin{equation}
\left( n_{bj}-h_{bj}\right) ^{\dagger }\left( n_{bj}-h_{bj}\right) =\left(
n_{bj}-h_{bj}\right) \left( n_{bj}-h_{bj}\right) ^{\dagger }=I_{\mathcal{A}};%
\text{ }1\leq j\leq r
\end{equation}%
and thus are contained in $\mathcal{U}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) \equiv
U\left( 2^{r}\right) .$

As $r\rightarrow \infty $ one must consider infinite products of projectors
i.e. the sequences 
\begin{equation}
\left\{ 2h_{b1},\cdots ,\tprod\limits_{1\leq n\leq r}\left( 2h_{bn}\right)
,\cdots \right\}
\end{equation}%
and their limits 
\begin{equation}
\lim\limits_{r\rightarrow \infty }\tprod\limits_{1\leq n\leq r}\left(
2h_{bn}\right) .
\end{equation}%
As 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \lim\limits_{r\rightarrow \infty }\tprod\limits_{1\leq n\leq
r}\left( 2h_{bn}\right) \right\rangle _{0}=I_{\mathcal{F}};1\leq n\leq \infty
\end{equation}%
one trivially obtains%
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \lim\limits_{r\rightarrow \infty }\tprod\limits_{1\leq
n\leq r}\left( 2h_{bn}\right) \right\} =\left\langle \left\langle
\lim\limits_{r\rightarrow \infty }\tprod\limits_{1\leq n\leq r}\left(
2h_{bn}\right) \right\rangle \right\rangle _{0}=1.
\end{equation}

The identity 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \phi _{b}\right\rangle \left\langle \phi _{b}\right\vert
=\tprod\limits_{1\leq n\leq r}h_{bn}
\end{equation}%
\emph{only exists if} $\mathcal{A}$ acts on an Hilbert space where $%
\left\vert \phi _{b}\right\rangle $ exists. If no action on a Hilbert is
defined the notation is meaningless. \emph{The self adjoint idempotents }$%
\left\{ n_{b1},\cdots ,n_{br}\right\} \equiv \left\{ h_{b1},\cdots
,h_{br}\right\} $\emph{\ always exist in }$\mathcal{A}$\emph{\ and have the
property }%
\begin{equation}
n_{bj_{1}}\cdots n_{bj_{p}}\tprod\limits_{1\leq n\leq r}\left(
2h_{bn}\right) =0;1\leq p\leq r
\end{equation}%
\emph{and define vacuum states, }$f_{b},$\emph{\ that have density operators 
}$\tprod\limits_{1\leq n\leq r}\left( 2h_{bn}\right) $\emph{\ in }$\mathcal{A%
}.$ \emph{However} \emph{vacuum vectors, }$\left\vert \phi _{b}\right\rangle
=\Phi _{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) ,$\emph{\ only exist in the GNS\
Hilbert space }$H_{f_{b}}.$

The density operators $\tprod\limits_{1\leq n\leq r}\left( 2h_{bn}\right) $
can be decomposed into the product of normalized amplitudes 
\begin{equation}
Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger },
\end{equation}%
where 
\begin{equation}
Q_{b}=\tprod\limits_{1\leq j\leq r}\left( \sqrt{2}b_{j}\right) ,
\end{equation}%
\emph{that also have the vacuum property} 
\begin{equation}
n_{bj_{1}}\cdots n_{bj_{p}}Q_{b}=0;1\leq p\leq r.
\end{equation}%
One should observe that the amplitude $Q_{b}$ is not grade zero, unlike the
GNS\ vacuum vector $\left\vert \phi _{b}\right\rangle .$

$\lceil $One might think that if the expansions of $D_{f_{b}}$ contained
multiples of $I_{\mathcal{F}}$ then it could not be annihilated by all $%
\left\{ n_{bj_{1}}\cdots n_{bj_{n}}\right\} ,$ that this is not true is
shown by 
\begin{align}
h_{b1}h_{b2}& =\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}-n_{b1}\right) \left( I_{\mathcal{F}%
}-n_{b2}\right) =I_{\mathcal{A}}-n_{b1}-n_{b2}+n_{b1}n_{b2} \\
n_{b1}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}-n_{b1}-n_{b2}+n_{b1}n_{b2}\right) &
=n_{b1}-n_{b1}-n_{b1}n_{b2}+n_{b1}n_{b2}=0.
\end{align}%
\begin{gather}
n_{bj}=\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{A}}+ix_{j}x_{j+r} \\
n_{b1}n_{b2}=\left( \frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{A}}+ix_{1}x_{3}\right) \left( 
\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{A}}+ix_{2}x_{4}\right) =\frac{1}{4}I_{\mathcal{A}%
}+ix_{1}x_{3}+ix_{2}x_{2+r}-x_{1}x_{1+r}x_{2}x_{4} \\
\left\langle n_{b1}n_{b2}\right\rangle _{0}=\frac{1}{4}I_{\mathcal{A}} \\
\left\langle \left\langle n_{b1}n_{b2}\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0}=%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left( 2n_{b1}\right) \left( 2n_{b2}\right) \right\} =1
\end{gather}%
$\rfloor $

\emph{Thus it seems that }$U\left( r\right) $\emph{-invariance of the state }%
$f_{b}$\emph{\ is equivalent to all of the vacuum conditions in fact it is a 
}\underline{\emph{necessary and sufficient condition.}}

\item 
\begin{gather}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{p_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots b_{p_{n}}^{\dagger
}b_{q_{m}}\cdots b_{q_{1}}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
b_{p_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots b_{p_{n}}^{\dagger }D_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr%
}\left\{ b_{q_{m}}\cdots b_{q_{1}}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =0 \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{q_{m}}\cdots b_{q_{1}}b_{p_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
b_{p_{n}}^{\dagger }D_{f_{b}}\right\} =\delta _{mn}\delta _{\left(
q_{1}\cdots q_{n}\right) \left( p_{1}\cdots p_{n}\right) }, \\
1\leq p_{1}<\cdots <p_{n}\leq r,1\leq q_{1}<\cdots <q_{m}\leq r,1\leq
m,n\leq r, \\
\delta _{\left( q_{1}\cdots q_{n}\right) \left( p_{1}\cdots p_{n}\right)
}=\left\{ 
\begin{array}{c}
\left( -1\right) ^{\pi \left( \sigma \right) }\text{ if }\sigma \left\{
q_{1},\cdots ,q_{n}\right\} =\left\{ p_{1},\cdots ,p_{n}\right\} \\ 
0\text{ otherwise.}%
\end{array}%
\right. .
\end{gather}

If $f_{b}$ is a normal vacuum state then in particular%
\begin{gather}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{j}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =0=\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{j}Q_{f_{b}}Q_{f_{b}}^{\dagger }\right\} =\limfunc{%
tr}\left\{ b_{j}Q_{f_{b}}Q_{f_{b}}^{\dagger }b_{j}^{\dagger }\right\} =%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{j}Q_{f_{b}}\left( b_{j}Q_{f_{b}}\right) ^{\dagger
}\right\} \Leftrightarrow b_{j}Q_{f_{b}}=0 \\
\text{i.e. }\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{j}D_{f_{b}}\right\}
=0\Leftrightarrow b_{j}Q_{f_{b}}=0.
\end{gather}%
Using 
\begin{equation}
b_{j}b_{j}^{\dagger }=I_{\mathcal{A}}-b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{j}
\end{equation}%
one obtains%
\begin{gather}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{j}b_{j}^{\dagger }D_{f_{b}}\right\} =1=\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ b_{j}b_{j}^{\dagger }Q_{f_{b}}Q_{f_{b}}^{\dagger }\right\} =\limfunc{%
tr}\left\{ b_{j}^{\dagger }Q_{f_{b}}Q_{f_{b}}^{\dagger }b_{j}\right\} \\
=\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{j}^{\dagger }Q_{f_{b}}\left( b_{j}^{\dagger
}Q_{f_{b}}\right) ^{\dagger }\right\} =\left\Vert b_{j}^{\dagger
}Q_{f_{b}}\right\Vert _{HS}=1\Rightarrow b_{j}^{\dagger }Q_{f_{b}}\neq 0%
\text{ and is in fact HS-normalized},
\end{gather}%
while%
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert b_{j}^{\dagger }\right\Vert _{HS}^{2}=\left\Vert b_{j}\right\Vert
_{HS}^{2}=\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{j}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ b_{j}b_{j}^{\dagger }\right\} =\frac{1}{2}.
\end{equation}%
$\lceil $Even though $\left\{ b_{j}^{\dagger }\right\} $ are not normalized
wrt $\left\Vert {}\right\Vert _{HS}$ they form $\left\Vert {}\right\Vert
_{HS}$-normalized amplitudes $b_{j}^{\dagger }Q_{f_{b}}$ when acting on the $%
\left\Vert {}\right\Vert _{HS}$-normalized vacuum amplitudes$\rfloor $

Another way to obtain $\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{j}^{\dagger }D_{f_{b}}\right\}
=0$ follows from 
\begin{align}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{j}^{\dagger }D_{f_{b}}\right\} & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ %
\left[ \mathcal{N}_{b},b_{j}^{\dagger }\right] D_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr%
}\left\{ \left( \mathcal{N}_{b}b_{j}^{\dagger }-b_{j}^{\dagger }\mathcal{N}%
_{b}\right) D_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left( \mathcal{N}%
_{b}b_{j}^{\dagger }D_{f_{b}}-b_{j}^{\dagger }\mathcal{N}_{b}D_{f_{b}}%
\right) \right\} \\
& =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left( D_{f_{b}}\mathcal{N}_{b}b_{j}^{\dagger }-%
\mathcal{N}_{b}D_{f_{b}}b_{j}^{\dagger }\right) \right\} =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ \left[ D_{f_{b}},\mathcal{N}_{b}\right] b_{j}^{\dagger }\right\} =0%
\text{ as }\left[ \mathcal{N}_{b},D_{f_{b}}\right] =0 \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{j}^{\dagger }D_{f_{b}}\right\} & =0\Leftrightarrow 
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{j}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =0.
\end{align}

\item Diffferential generating relationships: 
\begin{equation}
\left\{ \exp \left( \tsum\limits_{1\leq j\leq r}\beta _{j}b_{j}^{\dagger
}b_{j}\right) \right\} =\left\{ \tprod\limits_{1\leq j\leq r}e^{\beta
_{j}b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{j}}\right\}
\end{equation}%
\begin{align}
& \left. \frac{d}{d\beta _{j}}\left\{ \tprod\limits_{1\leq j\leq r}e^{\beta
_{j}b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{j}}\right\} \right\vert _{\mathbf{\beta }=\mathbf{0}}=%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{j}\right\} \\
& \left. \frac{d^{2}}{d\beta _{j}d\beta _{k}}\left\{ \tprod\limits_{1\leq
j\leq r}e^{\beta _{j}b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{j}}\right\} \right\vert _{\mathbf{%
\beta }=\mathbf{0}}=\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{j}b_{k}^{\dagger
}b_{k}\right\}
\end{align}%
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\exp \left( i\tsum\limits_{1\leq j\leq r}\beta _{j}b_{j}^{\dagger
}b_{j}\right) }\left( D_{f_{b}}\right) =\tprod\limits_{1\leq j\leq
r}e^{i\beta _{j}b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{j}}D_{f_{b}}\tprod\limits_{1\leq k\leq
r}e^{-i\beta _{k}b_{k}^{\dagger }b_{k}}
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\left. \frac{d}{d\beta _{j}}\left\{ \tprod\limits_{1\leq j\leq r}e^{i\beta
_{j}b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{j}}D_{f_{b}}\tprod\limits_{1\leq k\leq r}e^{-i\beta
_{k}b_{k}^{\dagger }b_{k}}\right\} \right\vert _{\mathbf{\beta }=\mathbf{0}%
}=ib_{j}^{\dagger }b_{j}D_{f_{b}}-iD_{f_{b}}b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{j}=i\left[
b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{j},D_{f_{b}}\right]
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\exp \left( i\tsum\limits_{1\leq j\leq r}\beta _{j}b_{j}^{\dagger
}b_{j}\right) D_{f_{b}}=\tprod\limits_{1\leq j\leq r}e^{i\beta
_{j}b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{j}}D_{f_{b}}
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\left. \frac{d}{d\beta _{j}}\left\{ \tprod\limits_{1\leq j\leq r}e^{i\beta
_{j}b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{j}}D_{f_{b}}\right\} \right\vert _{\mathbf{\beta }=%
\mathbf{0}}=ib_{j}^{\dagger }b_{j}D_{f_{b}}=ib_{j}^{\dagger }b_{j}D_{f_{b}}
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\left[ \mathcal{N}_{b},b_{j}^{\dagger }\right] =b_{j}^{\dagger
}\Leftrightarrow \left[ \mathcal{N}_{b},b_{j}\right] =-b_{j}
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\left( \mathcal{N}_{b}A-A\mathcal{N}_{b}\right) D_{f_{b}}=\mathcal{N}%
_{b}AD_{f_{b}}-A\mathcal{N}_{b}D_{f_{b}}=\mathcal{N}_{b}AD_{f_{b}}+AD_{f_{b}}%
\mathcal{N}_{b}
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left[ \mathcal{N}_{b},A\right] D_{f_{b}}\right\} =-%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left[ \mathcal{N}_{b},D_{f_{b}}\right] A\right\}
\end{equation}

The same substitution gives 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{j_{1}}\cdots b_{j_{n}}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =0
\end{equation}%
and%
\begin{align}
\widehat{\exp \left( i\tsum\limits_{1\leq j\leq r}\beta _{j}b_{j}^{\dagger
}b_{j}\right) }\left( D_{f_{b}}\right) & =\tprod\limits_{1\leq j\leq
r}e^{i\beta _{j}b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{j}}D_{f_{b}}\tprod\limits_{1\leq k\leq
r}e^{-i\beta _{k}b_{k}^{\dagger }b_{k}} \\
& \Updownarrow \\
\tprod\limits_{1\leq j\leq r}e^{i\beta _{j}b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{j}}D_{f_{b}}&
=D_{f_{b}}\tprod\limits_{1\leq j\leq r}e^{i\beta _{j}b_{j}^{\dagger
}b_{j}}\equiv \left[ \tprod\limits_{1\leq j\leq r}e^{i\beta
_{j}b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{j}},D_{f_{b}}\right] =0.
\end{align}

\item Pure states in $\mathcal{A}$ are defined as extreme points of the
convex set $\mathcal{S}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) ,$ normal states are
defined by topological and/or algebraic conditions. Extremity does not
translate to idempotency wrt the normalized trace $\limfunc{tr}.$
\end{enumerate}

\subsubsection{Bases for $\mathcal{A}$}

Let $r=1$ the dimension of $\mathcal{A}_{2}=2=2r$. The underlying Fock space
has dimension 
\begin{equation}
2^{1}=2
\end{equation}%
and the number of operator basis vectors is 
\begin{equation}
2^{2}=4,
\end{equation}%
however the number of linearly independent normal form operators 
\begin{equation}
a_{1}^{\dagger },a_{1},a_{1}^{\dagger }a_{1}
\end{equation}%
\emph{is only three}. Thus we must include one non normal form operator, $%
a_{1}a_{1}^{\dagger },$ in the basis or, more conveinently, the identity
operator $I_{\mathcal{A}}.$

$\lceil I_{\mathcal{A}}$ \emph{cannot be expanded} in terms of normally
ordered basis elements as 
\begin{equation}
I_{\mathcal{A}}\left\vert \phi\right\rangle =\left\vert \phi\right\rangle
\neq\left( \alpha_{1}a_{1}^{\dagger}+\overline{\alpha}_{1}a_{1}+\alpha
_{12}a_{1}^{\dagger}a_{1}\right) \left\vert \phi\right\rangle =\alpha
_{1}\left\vert \varphi_{1}\right\rangle .
\end{equation}
$\rfloor$

Let $r=2$ the the dimension of $\mathcal{A}_{2}=4=2r$. The underlying Fock
space has dimension 
\begin{equation}
2^{2}=4
\end{equation}%
and the number of operator basis vectors is 
\begin{equation}
2^{4}=16,
\end{equation}%
however the number of linearly independent normal form operators 
\begin{align}
& a_{1}^{\dagger },a_{2}^{\dagger },a_{1},a_{2}, \\
& a_{1}^{\dagger }a_{1},a_{1}^{\dagger }a_{2},a_{2}^{\dagger
}a_{1},a_{2}^{\dagger }a_{2}, \\
& a_{1}^{\dagger }a_{2}^{\dagger },a_{1}a_{2}, \\
& a_{1}^{\dagger }a_{2}^{\dagger }a_{1},a_{1}^{\dagger }a_{2}^{\dagger
}a_{2},a_{1}^{\dagger }a_{1}a_{2},a_{2}^{\dagger }a_{1}a_{2}, \\
& a_{1}^{\dagger }a_{2}^{\dagger }a_{2}a_{1},
\end{align}%
\emph{is fifteen} and again the identity operator 
\begin{equation}
I_{\mathcal{A}}
\end{equation}%
must be adjoined to obtain a full basis, which has cardenality 
\begin{equation}
2^{2r}=2^{2\times 2}=2^{4}=16.
\end{equation}%
Any operator can be expanded terms of the linearly independent normally
ordered basis as 
\begin{align}
A& ={\LARGE a}_{\left( A\right) 00}I_{\mathcal{A}}+{\LARGE a}_{\left(
A\right) 10}a_{1}^{\dagger }+{\LARGE a}\left( A\right) _{20}a_{2}^{\dagger }+%
{\LARGE a}\left( A\right) _{01}a_{1}+{\LARGE a}\left( A\right) _{02}a_{2}+%
{\LARGE a}\left( A\right) _{11}a_{1}^{\dagger }a_{1} \\
& +{\LARGE a}\left( A\right) _{12}a_{1}^{\dagger }a_{2}+{\LARGE a}\left(
A\right) _{21}a_{2}^{\dagger }a_{1}+{\LARGE a}\left( A\right)
_{22}a_{2}^{\dagger }a_{2}+{\LARGE a}\left( A\right) _{\left( 12\right)
0}a_{1}^{\dagger }a_{2}^{\dagger } \\
& +{\LARGE a}\left( A\right) _{0\left( 12\right) }a_{1}a_{2}+{\LARGE a}%
\left( A\right) _{\left( 12\right) 1}a_{1}^{\dagger }a_{2}^{\dagger }a_{1}+%
{\LARGE a}\left( A\right) _{\left( 12\right) 2}a_{1}^{\dagger
}a_{2}^{\dagger }a_{2}+{\LARGE a}\left( A\right) _{1\left( 12\right)
}a_{1}^{\dagger }a_{1}a_{2}+{\LARGE a}\left( A\right) _{\left( 21\right)
2}a_{2}^{\dagger }a_{1}a_{2}+{\LARGE a}\left( A\right) _{\left( 12\right)
\left( 21\right) }a_{1}^{\dagger }a_{2}^{\dagger }a_{2}a_{1}.
\end{align}

\subsubsection{Self Adjoint Bases}

Let 
\begin{equation}
\left\{ I_{\mathcal{A}},\left\{ \left. x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{n}}\right\vert
1\leq n\leq 2r,1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq 2r\right\} \right\}
\end{equation}%
be a self adjoint basis for $\mathcal{A}$ then the asociated metric matrix
for $\Phi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ is given by 
\begin{gather}
\left\{ \left. \left\langle \left. \Phi _{f}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots
x_{j_{n}}\right) \right\vert \Phi _{f}\left( x_{k_{1}}\cdots
x_{k_{m}}\right) \right\rangle =f\left( \left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots
x_{j_{n}}\right) ^{t}x_{k_{1}}\cdots x_{k_{m}}\right) \right\vert \right. \\
\left. 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq 2r,1\leq k_{1}<\cdots <k_{m}\leq
2r,1\leq m,n\leq 2r\right\} \\
\left\{ \left. \left\langle \left. \Phi _{f}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots
x_{j_{n}}\right) \right\vert \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle =f\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{n}}\right) \right\vert 1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq 2r,1\leq n\leq 2r\right\} \\
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Phi
_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle =f\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) .
\end{gather}

If $\Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) $ is orthogonal to all $\left\{
\Phi _{f}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{n}}\right) \right\} $ then 
\begin{equation}
f\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{n}}\right) =0\quad \forall x_{j_{1}}\cdots
x_{j_{n}},1\leq n\leq 2r
\end{equation}%
i.e. $f$ is zero accept on $\mathcal{F}_{0}.$

\subsubsection{Non Self Adjoint Bases}

Let 
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{c}
\left\{ \left\{ \left. a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger
}a_{k_{m}}\cdots a_{k_{1}}\right\vert 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq r,1\leq
k_{1}<\cdots <k_{m}\leq r,1\leq m,n\leq r\right\} \right. \\ 
\left. \left\{ \left. a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger
}\right\vert 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq r\right\} ,\left\{ \left.
a_{k_{m}}^{\left. {}\right. }\cdots a_{k_{1}}\right\vert 1\leq k_{1}<\cdots
<k_{m}\leq r\right\} ,I_{\mathcal{A}}\right\}%
\end{array}%
\end{equation}%
be a non self adjoint basis for $\mathcal{A}$ then the associated metric
matrix for $\Phi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ is given by 
\begin{gather}
\left\{ \left. \left\langle \left. \Phi _{f}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}\cdots a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }a_{k_{m}}\cdots a_{k_{1}}\right) \right\vert
\Phi _{f}\left( a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{p_{n^{\prime }}}^{\dagger
}a_{q_{m^{\prime }}}\cdots a_{q_{1}}\right) \right\rangle =f\left( \left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }a_{k_{m}}\cdots
a_{k_{1}}\right) ^{\dagger }a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{p_{n^{\prime
}}}^{\dagger }a_{q_{m^{\prime }}}\cdots a_{q_{1}}\right) \right\vert \right.
\\
\left. 
\begin{array}{c}
1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq r,1\leq k_{1}<\cdots <k_{m}\leq r,1\leq
m,n\leq r,1\leq p_{1}<\cdots <p_{n^{\prime }}\leq r, \\ 
1\leq q_{1}<\cdots <q_{m^{\prime }}\leq r,1\leq m^{\prime },n^{\prime }\leq r%
\end{array}%
\right\}
\end{gather}%
\begin{align}
& \left\{ \left. \left\langle \left. \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\vert \Phi _{f}\left( a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{p_{n^{\prime
}}}^{\dagger }a_{q_{m^{\prime }}}\cdots a_{q_{1}}\right) \right\rangle
=f\left( a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{p_{n^{\prime }}}^{\dagger
}a_{q_{m^{\prime }}}\cdots a_{q_{1}}\right) \right\vert \right. \\
& \left. 1\leq p_{1}<\cdots <p_{n^{\prime }}\leq r,1\leq q_{1}<\cdots
<q_{m^{\prime }}\leq r,1\leq m^{\prime },n^{\prime }\leq r\right\}
\end{align}%
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Phi
_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle =f\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right)
\end{equation}

\emph{The identity }$I_{\mathcal{A}}$\emph{\ is linearly independent of the
normally ordered non self adjoint basis as} \emph{it cannot be expressed as
the stong limit of normally ordered bases elements i.e.}

\begin{equation}
I_{\mathcal{A}}\neq s-\lim\left\{ \sum_{1\leq n,m\leq r}\tsum _{\substack{ %
1\leq j_{1},\cdots,j_{n}\leq r  \\ 1\leq k_{1},\cdots,k_{m}\leq r}}%
\alpha_{j_{1}\cdots j_{n}k_{1}\cdots k_{m}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}a_{k_{1}}\cdots a_{k_{m}}\right\} .
\end{equation}
This is shown by the $r=1$ and $r=2$ examples in the preceding and by
observing that linear combinations of operators in normal order must
annihilate the corresponding vacuum vector.

\paragraph{Quasi Free Vacuum States}

If $\Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) $ is orthogonal \emph{to all of
the normally ordered basis elements } $\left\{ \Phi_{f}\left(
a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger}a_{q_{m}}\cdots
a_{q_{1}}\right) ,\Phi_{f}\left( a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger}\right) ,\Phi_{f}\left( a_{q_{m}}\cdots a_{q_{1}}\right)
\right\} $ then 
\begin{equation}
f\left( a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger}a_{q_{m}}\cdots
a_{q_{1}}\right) =f\left( a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger
}\right) =f\left( a_{q_{m}}\cdots a_{q_{1}}\right) =0
\end{equation}
and if the state is a normal state 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left( a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger
}a_{q_{m}}\cdots a_{q_{1}}D_{f}\right) =\limfunc{tr}\left(
a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger}D_{f}\right) =\limfunc{tr}%
\left( a_{q_{m}}\cdots a_{q_{1}}D_{f}\right) =0
\end{equation}
for the range of subscripts%
\begin{equation}
1\leq p_{1}<\cdots<p_{n}\leq r,1\leq q_{1}<\cdots<q_{m}\leq r,1\leq m,n\leq
r.
\end{equation}

\emph{Quasi free vacuum states, }$f_{b},$\emph{\ are gauge invariant for a
specific gauge, }$\widehat{\emph{\mathcal{N}_{b}}}$\emph{\ i.e. }%
\begin{equation}
f_{b}\left( e^{i\widehat{\emph{\mathcal{N}}_{b}}}\left( a\right) \right)
=f_{b}\left( a\right) \,\forall a\in \mathcal{A},
\end{equation}%
which\emph{\ are charaterized by }%
\begin{gather}
f_{b}\left( b_{p_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots b_{p_{n}}^{\dagger }b_{q_{m}}\cdots
b_{q_{1}}\right) =f_{b}\left( b_{p_{1}}\cdots b_{p_{n}}\right) =f_{b}\left(
b_{p_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots b_{p_{n}}^{\dagger }\right) =0, \\
f_{b}\left( b_{p_{n}}\cdots b_{p_{1}}b_{p_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
b_{p_{n}}^{\dagger }\right) =1\text{ } \\
1\leq p_{1}<\cdots <p_{n}\leq r,1\leq n\leq r,1\leq q_{1}<\cdots <q_{m}\leq
r,1\leq m\leq r,
\end{gather}%
where $\left\{ b_{1}^{\dagger },\cdots ,b_{r}^{\dagger }\right\} $ are
canonical transforms of $\left\{ a_{1}^{\dagger },\cdots ,a_{r}^{\dagger
}\right\} $ and $f_{b}$ is the corresponding vacuum state. \emph{Thus} $\Phi
_{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) $\emph{\ is orthogonal to} $\left\{
\Phi _{f_{b}}\left( b_{p_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots b_{p_{n}}^{\dagger
}b_{q_{m}}\cdots b_{q_{1}}\right) ,\Phi _{f_{b}}\left( b_{p_{1}}^{\dagger
}\cdots b_{p_{n}}^{\dagger }\right) ,\Phi _{f_{b}}\left( b_{q_{m}}\cdots
b_{q_{1}}\right) ;1\leq p_{1}<\cdots <p_{n}\leq r,1\leq q_{1}<\cdots
<q_{m}\leq r,1\leq m,n\leq r\right\} $.

Vacuum states are left invariant by $U\left( r\right) $ through the spinor
(adjoint) representation i.e.%
\begin{equation}
f_{b}\left( \widehat{u}^{\dagger }\left( A\right) \right) =f_{b}\left(
A\right) \,\forall A\in \mathcal{A},\forall \widehat{u}\in U\left( r\right) ,
\end{equation}%
where 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{u}^{\dagger }\left( A\right) =u^{\dagger }au
\end{equation}%
and 
\begin{equation}
u=\exp \left\{ i\tsum\limits_{1\leq j,k\leq r}\lambda _{bjk}b_{j}^{\dagger
}b_{k}\right\} ,\lambda _{bjk}=\overline{\lambda }_{bkj}.
\end{equation}%
In particular this implies gauge invariance as $U\left( 1\right) \subset
U\left( r\right) $. For normal states invariance to $\mathcal{R}_{b}\left(
U\left( r\right) \right) $ this gives 
\begin{equation}
\left[ D_{f_{b}},b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{k}\right] =0
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left( u^{\dagger }auD_{f_{b}}\right) =\limfunc{tr}\left(
aD_{f_{b}}\right) \,\forall a\in \mathcal{A},\forall \widehat{u}\in U\left(
r\right) ,
\end{equation}%
and%
\begin{equation}
uD_{f_{b}}u^{\dagger }=D_{f_{b}},\forall \widehat{u}\in U\left( r\right) .
\end{equation}%
If $u\in SU\left( r\right) $ then $\func{Tr}\left\{ \mathbf{\lambda }%
_{b}\right\} =0.$

\begin{theorem}
$f_{b}$ is a vacuum state Iff%
\begin{equation}
f_{b}\left( \widehat{u}\left( A\right) \right) =f_{b}\left( A\right)
\,\,\forall \widehat{u}\in \mathcal{R}_{b}\left( U\left( r\right) \right)
,\forall A\in \mathcal{A},
\end{equation}

\begin{enumerate}
\item Then 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\emph{\mathcal{N}}}_{b}\left( \left\vert \phi _{b}\right\rangle
\left\langle \phi _{b}\right\vert \right) =\left[ \emph{\mathcal{N}}%
_{b},\left\vert \phi _{b}\right\rangle \left\langle \phi _{b}\right\vert %
\right] =0,
\end{equation}%
where 
\begin{equation}
\emph{\mathcal{N}}_{b}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{j}
\end{equation}

\item and%
\begin{gather}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{p_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots b_{p_{n}}^{\dagger
}b_{q_{m}}\cdots b_{q_{1}}\left\vert \phi _{b}\right\rangle \left\langle
\phi _{b}\right\vert \right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{p_{1}}^{\dagger
}\cdots b_{p_{n}}^{\dagger }\left\vert \phi _{b}\right\rangle \left\langle
\phi _{b}\right\vert \right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{q_{m}}\cdots
b_{q_{1}}\left\vert \phi _{b}\right\rangle \left\langle \phi _{b}\right\vert
\right\} =0, \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{p_{n}}\cdots b_{p_{1}}b_{p_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
b_{p_{n}}^{\dagger }\left\vert \phi _{b}\right\rangle \left\langle \phi
_{b}\right\vert \right\} =1\text{ } \\
1\leq p_{1}<\cdots <p_{n}\leq r,1\leq n\leq r;\,1\leq q_{1}<\cdots
<q_{m}\leq r,1\leq m\leq r.
\end{gather}
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}
\begin{align}
1 & \Rightarrow2 \\
3 & \Rightarrow2
\end{align}
\end{corollary}

$\lceil$Two different representations of $U\left( r\right) $ do not have the
same vacuum states, even though they are unitarily equivalent i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\exp\left\{ i\tsum \limits_{1\leq j,k\leq
r}\lambda_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{k}\right\} \left\vert \phi_{b}\right\rangle
\left\langle \phi_{b}\right\vert \left( \exp\left\{ i\tsum \limits_{1\leq
j,k\leq r}\lambda_{bk}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{k}\right\} \right)
^{\dagger}=\left\vert \phi_{b}\right\rangle \left\langle \phi_{b}\right\vert
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\exp\left\{ i\tsum \limits_{1\leq j,k\leq
r}\lambda_{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{k}\right\} \left\vert \phi_{b}\right\rangle
\left\langle \phi_{b}\right\vert \left( \exp\left\{ i\tsum \limits_{1\leq
j,k\leq r}\lambda_{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{k}\right\} \right)
^{\dagger}\neq\left\vert \phi_{b}\right\rangle \left\langle
\phi_{b}\right\vert
\end{equation}
$\rfloor$

\subsubsection{$\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ is a $c^{\ast}$-algebra
and $\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) ^{\prime\prime}$ is the (enveloping)
vNA generated by $\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) .$}

As $\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) ^{\prime\prime}$ is a vNA it is a dual
Banach space i.e. the dual of $\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) _{\ast
}^{\prime\prime},$which is the predual of $\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right)
^{\prime\prime}.$ \emph{Note that} preduals are not in general algebras. In
the case of a vNA $\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) ^{\prime\prime}$ that
does not have any normal states we can have the following situation for $%
l\in\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) ^{\prime\prime}$%
\begin{equation}
l\left( a\right) =\func{Tr}\left\{ aD_{l}\right\} ;D_{l}\in\Pi _{f}\left( 
\mathcal{A}\right) _{\ast}^{\prime\prime}\subset\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}%
_{f}\right) \text{ and}\notin\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right)
^{\prime\prime},
\end{equation}
it is even possible 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) ^{\prime\prime}\cap\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}%
\right) _{\ast}^{\prime\prime}=\phi.
\end{equation}

We now concentrate on the representation generated by the state $\limfunc{tr}
$ with density operator $I_{\mathcal{A}}$ 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left( A\right) =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ AI_{\mathcal{A}}\right\}
\end{equation}%
produced by the Hilbert Space $\mathcal{A}_{HS}\equiv \Phi _{\limfunc{tr}%
}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ i.e. 
\begin{align}
\Phi _{\limfunc{tr}}& :\mathcal{A\longrightarrow H}_{\limfunc{tr}}\equiv 
\mathcal{A}_{HS} \\
\ker \left\{ \Phi _{\limfunc{tr}}\right\} & =\left\{ \left. \xi \right\vert 
\limfunc{tr}\left( \xi ^{\dagger }\xi \right) =0\right\} \Rightarrow \xi =0
\\
\left\Vert \Phi _{\limfunc{tr}}\left( A\right) \right\Vert _{\mathcal{H}_{%
\limfunc{tr}}}& =\left( \limfunc{tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger }A\right\} \right) ^{%
\frac{1}{2}} \\
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\limfunc{tr}}\left( A\right) \right\vert \Phi _{%
\limfunc{tr}}\left( B\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{\limfunc{tr}}}& =%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger }B\right\} .
\end{align}%
The representation 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{\limfunc{tr}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) \subseteq \mathcal{B}\left( 
\mathcal{A}_{HS}\right)
\end{equation}%
and is a faithful representation. A representation is faithful if 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{f}\left( A\right) =0\Rightarrow A=0,
\end{equation}%
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\ker \left\{ \Pi _{f}\right\} =0,
\end{equation}%
thus a $c^{\ast }$-algebra isomorphism. If $f$ is faithful so is $\Pi _{f}$
(i.e. this is a sufficient condition), however $\Pi _{f}$ can be faithful
even if $f$ is not (so it is not a neccesary condition).

The metric matrix associated with a state $f\in\mathcal{A}^{d}$ is 
\begin{align}
f\left( \left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right) ^{\dagger}\left(
x_{k_{1}}\cdots x_{k_{q}}\right) \right) & =\left\langle \left.
\Phi_{f}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right) \right\vert \Phi_{f}\left(
x_{k_{1}}\cdots x_{k_{q}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{f}} \\
0 & \leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p}\leq2r,0\leq k_{1}<\cdots<k_{p}\leq2r;0\leq
p,q\leq2r.
\end{align}
In particular the basis $\left\{ \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) ,\left\{ \left. \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left(
x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right) \right\vert 1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p}\leq2r\right\} ;1\leq p\leq2r\right\} $ is orthogonal i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots
x_{j_{p}}\right) \right\vert \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left(
x_{k_{1}}\cdots x_{k_{q}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}\mathit{\limfunc{%
tr}}}=\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ \left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right)
^{\dagger}\left( x_{k_{1}}\cdots x_{k_{q}}\right) \right\} =g_{j_{1}\cdots
j_{p}}\delta_{j_{1}\cdots j_{p};k_{1}\cdots k_{q};}
\end{equation}
as the metric matrix is diagonal.

Any polynomial $\ast $-algebra (that has a non infinite trace) contains
positive elements that have unit trace i.e. density operators, so that if a
state $f\in \mathcal{A}^{d}$ is normal then 
\begin{equation}
f\left( A\right) =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ AD_{f}\right\} =\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ D_{f}A\right\}
\end{equation}%
and%
\begin{align}
f\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right) & =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{
x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}D_{f}\right\} \\
f\left( A\right) & \geq 0\text{ if }A\geq 0 \\
f\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) & =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ I_{\mathcal{A}%
}D_{f}\right\} =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ D_{f}\right\} =1.
\end{align}%
Note that 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \right\vert
\Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}=\left\Vert A\right\Vert _{\mathcal{H}\mathit{\limfunc{%
tr}}}^{2}=\left\Vert A\right\Vert _{HS}^{2}=\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{
A^{\dagger }A\right\}
\end{equation}%
and%
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert I_{\mathcal{A}}\right\Vert _{HS}^{2}=1.
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Connection Between Traces}

$f_{D_{f}}$ and $f_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}$ are normal states on a Clifford/CAR
algebra wrt $\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}$ Iff 
\begin{align}
f_{D_{f}}\left( A\right) & =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ AD_{f}\right\} \\
f_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( A\right) & =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ A\right\}
\end{align}%
and%
\begin{align}
f_{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) & =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ I_{%
\mathcal{A}}D_{f}\right\} =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ D_{f}\right\} =1 \\
f_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( D_{f}\right) & =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ I_{%
\mathcal{A}}D_{f}\right\} =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ D_{f}\right\} =1.
\end{align}%
Normality can be also be expressed in terms of ultra $\equiv \sigma $ weak
continuity of a linear functional $f$ and supremums of operator filters.

$\lceil $The GNS representation, $\Pi _{f_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}}\equiv \Pi _{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}},$ of $\mathcal{A}$ has the property

\begin{equation}
\Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) =P_{\mathcal{V}},
\end{equation}%
where $P_{\mathcal{V}}$ is an orthogonal projector belonging to $\mathcal{B}%
\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\right) ,$ and in general infinite
dimensional. The representation $\Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}$ is faithful
thus $\Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}^{-1}$ exists on $\Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr%
}}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $, ($\Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}$ is injective
but may not be surjective)$.$ The value of $A$ in the state $f_{I_{\mathcal{A%
}}}$ is given by 
\begin{eqnarray}
&&f_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( A\right) =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ A\right\}
=\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\vert \Pi _{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( A\right) \Phi _{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}}=\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left(
A\right) \left\vert \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \right\} \\
&&\overset{\text{as }f_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\text{is normal }}{=}\func{Tr}%
\left\{ \Pi _{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( A\right) \mathfrak{D}_{\mathit{\limfunc{%
tr}}}\right\} =\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( A\right) 
\mathfrak{Q}_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\mathfrak{Q}_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
}^{\dagger }\right\} =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ A\Pi _{I_{\mathcal{A}%
}}^{-1}\left( \mathfrak{Q}_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\right) \Pi _{I_{\mathcal{A%
}}}^{-1}\left( \mathfrak{Q}_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}^{\dagger }\right)
\right\} =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ AQ_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}Q_{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}}^{\dagger }\right\} .
\end{eqnarray}%
Thus 
\begin{equation}
Q_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}Q_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}^{\dagger }=I_{\mathcal{A}}
\end{equation}%
and $Q_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}$ must unitary and 
\begin{equation}
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ I_{\mathcal{A}}\right\} =1.
\end{equation}

States, $D_{X},$ in the same folium as $f_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}$ $\equiv f_{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}$ are given by 
\begin{eqnarray}
&&f_{X}\left( A\right) =f_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( X^{\dagger }AX\right) =%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ X^{\dagger }AX\right\} =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
\left\{ AXX^{\dagger }\right\} =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ AD_{X}\right\}
\\
&=&\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\vert \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( X^{\dagger
}AX\right) \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}}=\left\langle \left.
\Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( X\right) \right\vert \Pi _{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( X\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}}=\left\langle \left. \Pi
_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( X\right) \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left(
I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left(
A\right) \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( X\right) \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{%
tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}}} \\
&=&\left\langle \left. \mathfrak{X}\Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) 
\mathfrak{X}\Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}}=\func{Tr}_{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}}\left\{ \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \left\vert
\Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( X\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi
_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( X\right) \right\vert \right\} =\func{Tr}_{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left\{ \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( X^{\dagger
}AX\right) \left\vert \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \right\} \\
&=&\left\langle \left. \Phi _{X}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert
\Pi _{X}\left( A\right) \Phi _{X}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle
_{\mathcal{H}_{X}}=\func{Tr}_{X}\left\{ \Pi _{X}\left( A\right) \left\vert
\Phi _{X}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi
_{X}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \right\} =\func{Tr}_{X}\left\{
\Pi _{X}\left( A\right) \mathfrak{D}_{X}\right\} =\func{Tr}_{X}\left\{ \Pi
_{X}\left( A\right) \mathfrak{XX}^{\dagger }\right\} \\
&=&\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ A\Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}^{-1}\left( 
\mathfrak{XX}^{\dagger }\right) \right\} =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{
AXX^{\dagger }\right\} .
\end{eqnarray}%
Let $\mathfrak{Y}\in \mathcal{B}_{1+}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}%
}}\right) $ with $\func{Tr}\left\{ \mathfrak{Y}\right\} =1,$ but $\notin \Pi
_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ then one can define a
state $\in \mathcal{S}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ by 
\begin{equation}
f_{\mathfrak{Y}}\left( A\right) =\left\langle \left. \mathfrak{Y}\Phi _{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Pi _{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \mathfrak{Y}\Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}%
}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}}}=\func{Tr}_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left\{ \Pi _{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \left\vert \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( 
\mathfrak{Y}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
}\left( \mathfrak{Y}\right) \right\vert \right\} =\func{Tr}_{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}}\left\{ \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \mathfrak{%
YY}^{\dagger }\right\}
\end{equation}%
then $\mathfrak{YY}^{\dagger }$ is in the GNS\ folium of $\mathit{\limfunc{tr%
}}$ but cannot be expressed as $f_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( Y^{\dagger
}AY\right) $ as $\Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}^{-1}\left( \mathfrak{Y}\right) 
$ $\nexists $ in $\mathcal{A}.$

$\lceil $%
\begin{equation}
\func{Tr}_{X}\left\{ \Pi _{X}\left( A\right) \left\vert \Phi _{X}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{X}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\vert \right\} =\func{Tr}_{X}\left\{ P_{\mathcal{V}}\Pi
_{X}\left( A\right) P_{\mathcal{V}}\mathfrak{D}_{X}\right\}
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{D}_{X}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq \infty }\alpha _{j}\left\vert
v_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle v_{j}\right\vert
\end{equation}%
$\rfloor $

$\lceil $ Consider $\mathcal{A}=M\left( 2,\mathbb{C}\right) $ the following
ket-bra and tensor product relationships are important 
\begin{eqnarray}
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \otimes \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
&=&\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \equiv
\left\vert \left( 
\begin{array}{c}
1 \\ 
0%
\end{array}%
\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \left( 
\begin{array}{c}
1 \\ 
0%
\end{array}%
\right) \right\vert =\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
1 & 0%
\end{array}%
\right) \left( 
\begin{array}{c}
1 \\ 
0%
\end{array}%
\right) =\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
1 & 0 \\ 
0 & 0%
\end{array}%
\right) \\
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \otimes \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle
&=&\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \equiv
\left\vert \left( 
\begin{array}{c}
1 \\ 
0%
\end{array}%
\right) \right\rangle \left\vert \left( 
\begin{array}{c}
1 \\ 
0%
\end{array}%
\right) \right\rangle =\left\vert \left( 
\begin{array}{c}
1 \\ 
0%
\end{array}%
\right) \otimes \left( 
\begin{array}{c}
1 \\ 
0%
\end{array}%
\right) \right\rangle =\left( 
\begin{array}{c}
1 \\ 
0%
\end{array}%
\right) \left( 
\begin{array}{c}
1 \\ 
0%
\end{array}%
\right) =\left( 
\begin{array}{c}
1 \\ 
0 \\ 
0 \\ 
0%
\end{array}%
\right)
\end{eqnarray}%
where we have used the isomorphism 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert e_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{k}\right\vert \sim \left\vert
e_{j}\right\rangle \left\vert e_{k}\right\rangle \neq \left\vert
e_{k}\right\rangle \left\vert e_{j}\right\rangle
\end{equation}%
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}\right) \cong \mathcal{H\otimes H}^{d}\cong 
\mathcal{H\otimes H}.
\end{equation}%
One can consider the mixed faithful state 
\begin{equation}
f_{D}\left( A\right) =\func{Tr}_{\mathcal{A}}\left\{ A\left( \frac{1}{4}%
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert +\frac{3}{4}%
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) \right\}
,
\end{equation}%
where 
\begin{equation}
f_{D}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) =\func{Tr}_{\mathcal{A}}\left\{ I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\left( \frac{1}{4}\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert +\frac{3}{4}\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \right) \right\} =\func{Tr}_{\mathcal{A}}\left\{ \frac{1}{4}%
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert +\frac{3}{4}%
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right\} =1,
\end{equation}%
to generate a GNS\ representation.

The values of this state on the basis $\left\{ \left. \left\vert
e_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{k}\right\vert \right\vert 1\leq j,k\leq
2\right\} $ using its GNS\ representation are given by 
\begin{eqnarray}
f_{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) &=&\left\langle \left. \Phi _{D}\left( I_{2}\right) \right\vert \Pi
_{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) \Phi _{D}\left( I_{2}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}%
_{D}}=\left\langle \left. \Phi _{D}\left( I_{2}\right) \right\vert \Phi
_{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{D}} \\
&=&\func{Tr}_{\mathcal{A}}\left\{ \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \left( \frac{1}{4}\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{1}\right\vert +\frac{3}{4}\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) \right\} =\frac{1}{4}
\end{eqnarray}%
\begin{eqnarray}
f_{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) &=&\left\langle \left. \Phi _{D}\left( I_{2}\right) \right\vert \Pi
_{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) \Phi _{D}\left( I_{2}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}%
_{D}}=\left\langle \left. \Phi _{D}\left( I_{2}\right) \right\vert \Phi
_{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{D}} \\
&=&\func{Tr}_{\mathcal{A}}\left\{ \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \left( \frac{1}{4}\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{2}\right\vert +\frac{3}{4}\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) \right\} =\frac{3}{4}
\end{eqnarray}%
\begin{equation*}
f_{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) =\left\langle \left. \Phi _{D}\left( I_{2}\right) \right\vert \Pi
_{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) \Phi _{D}\left( I_{2}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}%
_{D}}=\left\langle \left. \Phi _{D}\left( I_{2}\right) \right\vert \Phi
_{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{D}}=\func{Tr}_{\mathcal{A}}\left\{
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \left( \frac{1}{4%
}\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert +\frac{3}{4}%
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) \right\}
=0
\end{equation*}%
\begin{equation*}
f_{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) =\left\langle \left. \Phi _{D}\left( I_{2}\right) \right\vert \Pi
_{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) \Phi _{D}\left( I_{2}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}%
_{D}}=\left\langle \left. \Phi _{D}\left( I_{2}\right) \right\vert \Phi
_{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{D}}=\func{Tr}_{\mathcal{A}}\left\{
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \left( \frac{1}{4%
}\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert +\frac{3}{4}%
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) \right\}
=0
\end{equation*}

The norms of the images of the basis vectors under the GNS\ map $\Phi _{D}$
are%
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) \right\vert \Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert
e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) \right\rangle _{%
\mathcal{H}_{D}}=f_{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) =f_{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right) =\frac{1}{4}
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) \right\vert \Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert
e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) \right\rangle _{%
\mathcal{H}_{D}}=f_{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) =f_{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \right) =\frac{3}{4}
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) \right\vert \Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert
e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) \right\rangle _{%
\mathcal{H}_{D}}=f_{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) =f_{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right) =\frac{1}{4}
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) \right\vert \Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert
e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) \right\rangle _{%
\mathcal{H}_{D}}=f_{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) =f_{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \right) =\frac{3}{4}.
\end{equation}%
The images themselves are 
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) =F_{1}=\frac{1}{2}\left\vert \mathfrak{f}_{1}\right\rangle
\rightarrow \mathcal{R}_{f}\left( F_{1}\right) =\frac{1}{2}\left( 
\begin{array}{c}
1 \\ 
0 \\ 
0 \\ 
0%
\end{array}%
\right)
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) =F_{4}=\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\left\vert \mathfrak{f}_{4}\right\rangle
\rightarrow \mathcal{R}_{f}\left( F_{4}\right) =\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\left( 
\begin{array}{c}
0 \\ 
0 \\ 
0 \\ 
1%
\end{array}%
\right)
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) =F_{2}=\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\left\vert \mathfrak{f}_{2}\right\rangle
\rightarrow \mathcal{R}_{f}\left( F_{2}\right) =\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\left( 
\begin{array}{c}
0 \\ 
1 \\ 
0 \\ 
0%
\end{array}%
\right)
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) =F_{3}=\frac{1}{2}\left\vert \mathfrak{f}_{3}\right\rangle
\rightarrow \mathcal{R}_{f}\left( F_{3}\right) =\frac{1}{2}\left( 
\begin{array}{c}
0 \\ 
0 \\ 
1 \\ 
0%
\end{array}%
\right) ,
\end{equation}%
thus $\Phi _{D}$ is not an isometry, though it is an isomorphism as $\ker
\left\{ \Phi _{D}\right\} =\phi $ and it is surjective$.$

The image of the identity is 
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{D}\left( I_{2}\right) =\Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{1}\right\vert +\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \right) =F_{1}+F_{4}=\frac{1}{2}\left\vert \mathfrak{f}%
_{1}\right\rangle +\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\left\vert \mathfrak{f}%
_{4}\right\rangle \overset{\mathcal{R}_{\mathfrak{f}}}{\rightarrow }\frac{1}{%
2}\left( 
\begin{array}{c}
1 \\ 
0 \\ 
0 \\ 
0%
\end{array}%
\right) +\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\left( 
\begin{array}{c}
0 \\ 
0 \\ 
0 \\ 
1%
\end{array}%
\right) =\left( 
\begin{array}{c}
\frac{1}{2} \\ 
0 \\ 
0 \\ 
\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}%
\end{array}%
\right) ,
\end{equation}%
giving 
\begin{eqnarray}
&&\left\vert \Phi _{D}\left( I_{2}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi
_{D}\left( I_{2}\right) \right\vert =\left( \frac{1}{2}\left\vert \mathfrak{f%
}_{1}\right\rangle +\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\left\vert \mathfrak{f}%
_{4}\right\rangle \right) \left( \frac{1}{2}\left\langle \mathfrak{f}%
_{1}\right\vert +\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\left\langle \mathfrak{f}_{4}\right\vert
\right) =\frac{1}{4}\left\vert \mathfrak{f}_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle 
\mathfrak{f}_{1}\right\vert +\frac{\sqrt{3}}{4}\left\vert \mathfrak{f}%
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \mathfrak{f}_{4}\right\vert +\frac{\sqrt{3}}{4%
}\left\vert \mathfrak{f}_{4}\right\rangle \left\langle \mathfrak{f}%
_{1}\right\vert +\frac{3}{4}\left\vert \mathfrak{f}_{2}\right\rangle
\left\langle \mathfrak{f}_{2}\right\vert \\
&&\overset{\mathcal{R}_{\mathfrak{f}}}{\rightarrow }\left( 
\begin{array}{c}
\frac{1}{2} \\ 
0 \\ 
0 \\ 
\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}%
\end{array}%
\right) \left( 
\begin{array}{cccc}
\frac{1}{2} & 0 & 0 & \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}%
\end{array}%
\right) =\left( 
\begin{array}{cccc}
\frac{1}{4} & 0 & 0 & \frac{\sqrt{3}}{4} \\ 
0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\ 
0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\ 
\frac{\sqrt{3}}{4} & 0 & 0 & \frac{3}{4}%
\end{array}%
\right) .
\end{eqnarray}%
The expectation value of any operator $A\in \mathcal{A}$ is given by 
\begin{equation}
f_{D}\left( A\right) =\left\langle \left. \Phi _{D}\left( I_{2}\right)
\right\vert \Pi _{D}\left( A\right) \Phi _{D}\left( I_{2}\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{D}}=\func{Tr}_{\mathcal{A}}\left\{ \mathcal{R}%
_{e}\left( A\right) \left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\ 
0 & \frac{3}{4}%
\end{array}%
\right) \right\} =\func{Tr}_{\Pi _{D}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) }\left\{ 
\mathcal{R}_{\mathfrak{f}}\left( \Pi _{D}\left( A\right) \right) \left( 
\begin{array}{cccc}
\frac{1}{4} & 0 & 0 & \frac{\sqrt{3}}{4} \\ 
0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\ 
0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\ 
\frac{\sqrt{3}}{4} & 0 & 0 & \frac{3}{4}%
\end{array}%
\right) \right\} .
\end{equation}%
The representation $\Pi _{D}$ of $\mathcal{A}$ is given by 
\begin{eqnarray}
\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) \Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) F_{1}=\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert
e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) \frac{1}{2}%
\left\vert \mathfrak{f}_{1}\right\rangle \\
&=&\Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) =\Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right) =F_{1}=\frac{1}{2}\left\vert \mathfrak{f}%
_{1}\right\rangle \\
\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) \Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) F_{3}=\Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert
e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \left\vert
e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) =\Phi _{D}\left(
0\right) =0 \\
\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) \Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) F_{2}=\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert
e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}%
\left\vert \mathfrak{f}_{2}\right\rangle \\
&=&\Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) =\Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \right) =F_{2}=\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\left\vert \mathfrak{f}%
_{2}\right\rangle \\
\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) \Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) F_{4}=\Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert
e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \left\vert
e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) =\Phi _{D}\left(
0\right) =0
\end{eqnarray}

\bigskip 
\begin{eqnarray}
\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) \Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) F_{1}=\Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert
e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \left\vert
e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) =0 \\
\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) \Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) F_{3}=\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert
e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) \frac{1}{2}%
\left\vert \mathfrak{f}_{3}\right\rangle \\
&=&\Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) =\Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right) =F_{1}=\frac{1}{2}\left\vert \mathfrak{f}%
_{1}\right\rangle \\
\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) \Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) F_{2}=\Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert
e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \left\vert
e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) =0 \\
\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) \Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) F_{4}=\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert
e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}%
\left\vert \mathfrak{f}_{4}\right\rangle \\
&=&\Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) =\Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \right) =F_{2}=\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\left\vert \mathfrak{f}%
_{2}\right\rangle
\end{eqnarray}

\bigskip 
\begin{eqnarray}
\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) \Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) F_{1}=\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert
e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) \frac{1}{2}%
\left\vert \mathfrak{f}_{1}\right\rangle =\Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert
e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \left\vert
e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) \\
&=&\Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right) =F_{3}=\frac{1}{2}\left\vert \mathfrak{f}%
_{3}\right\rangle \\
\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) \Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) F_{3}=\Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert
e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \left\vert
e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) =\Phi _{D}\left(
0\right) =0 \\
\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) \Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) F_{2}=\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert
e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}%
\left\vert \mathfrak{f}_{2}\right\rangle =\Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert
e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \left\vert
e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) \\
&=&\Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \right) =F_{4}=\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\left\vert \mathfrak{f}%
_{4}\right\rangle \\
\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) \Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) F_{4}=\Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert
e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \left\vert
e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) =\Phi _{D}\left(
0\right) =0
\end{eqnarray}

\bigskip 
\begin{eqnarray}
\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) \Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) F_{1}=\Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert
e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \left\vert
e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) =0 \\
\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) \Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) F_{3}=\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert
e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) \frac{1}{2}%
\left\vert \mathfrak{f}_{3}\right\rangle =\Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert
e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \left\vert
e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) \\
&=&\Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right) =F_{3}=\frac{1}{2}\left\vert \mathfrak{f}%
_{3}\right\rangle \\
\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) \Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) F_{2}=\Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert
e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \left\vert
e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) =0 \\
\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) \Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) F_{4}=\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert
e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}%
\left\vert \mathfrak{f}_{4}\right\rangle =\Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert
e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \left\vert
e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) \\
&=&\Phi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \right) =F_{4}=\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\left\vert \mathfrak{f}%
_{4}\right\rangle
\end{eqnarray}

The states $\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert $
and $\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert $ give the
orthogonal GNS$\ $Hilbert subspaces $\mathcal{H}_{\left\vert
e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }$ and $\mathcal{H}%
_{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }$ with
orthonormal bases $\left\{ \Phi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right) ,\Phi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right) \right\} $ and $\left\{ \Phi _{\left\vert
e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert
e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) ,\Phi _{\left\vert
e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert
e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) \right\} $
respectively as can observed by considering the normalizations 
\begin{eqnarray}
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right) \right\vert \Phi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) \right\rangle _{\left\vert
e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert } &=&\func{Tr}_{\mathcal{A}%
}\left\{ \left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right)
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right\} =1 \\
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \right) \right\vert \Phi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) \right\rangle _{\left\vert
e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert } &=&\func{Tr}_{\mathcal{A}%
}\left\{ \left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right)
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right\} =0 \\
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right) \right\vert \Phi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) \right\rangle _{\left\vert
e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert } &=&\func{Tr}_{\mathcal{A}%
}\left\{ \left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right)
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right\} =1 \\
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right) \right\vert \Phi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) \right\rangle _{\left\vert
e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert } &=&\func{Tr}_{\mathcal{A}%
}\left\{ \left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right)
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right\} =0,
\end{eqnarray}%
so that 
\begin{eqnarray}
\Phi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) &=&%
\widetilde{F}_{1} \\
\Phi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) &=&0 \\
\Phi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) &=&%
\widetilde{F}_{3} \\
\Phi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) &=&0,
\end{eqnarray}%
showing that 
\begin{equation}
\ker \left\{ \Phi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\right\} =\left\{ \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{2}\right\vert ,\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \right\}
\end{equation}%
and 
\begin{eqnarray}
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right) \right\vert \Phi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) \right\rangle _{\left\vert
e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert } &=&\func{Tr}_{\mathcal{A}%
}\left\{ \left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right)
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right\} =0 \\
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \right) \right\vert \Phi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) \right\rangle _{\left\vert
e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert } &=&\func{Tr}_{\mathcal{A}%
}\left\{ \left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right)
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right\} =1 \\
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right) \right\vert \Phi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) \right\rangle _{\left\vert
e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert } &=&\func{Tr}_{\mathcal{A}%
}\left\{ \left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right)
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right\} =0 \\
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \right) \right\vert \Phi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) \right\rangle _{\left\vert
e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert } &=&\func{Tr}_{\mathcal{A}%
}\left\{ \left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right)
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right\} =1
\end{eqnarray}%
so that 
\begin{eqnarray}
\Phi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) &=&0 \\
\Phi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) &=&%
\widetilde{F}_{2} \\
\Phi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) &=&0 \\
\Phi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) &=&%
\widetilde{F}_{4}
\end{eqnarray}%
showing that 
\begin{equation}
\ker \left\{ \Phi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\right\} =\left\{ \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{1}\right\vert ,\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right\} .
\end{equation}%
The corresponding GNS$\ $representations are given by:

1. $\Pi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }$ in $%
\mathcal{H}_{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }$
by%
\begin{eqnarray}
\Pi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) \Phi
_{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi
_{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) 
\widetilde{F}_{1}=\Phi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) =\Phi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) =%
\widetilde{F}_{1} \\
\Pi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) \Phi
_{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi
_{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) 
\widetilde{F}_{3}=\Phi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) =0
\end{eqnarray}%
\begin{eqnarray}
\Pi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) \Phi
_{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi
_{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) 
\widetilde{F}_{1}=\Phi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) =0 \\
\Pi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) \Phi
_{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi
_{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) 
\widetilde{F}_{3}=\Phi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) =\Phi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) =%
\widetilde{F}_{1}
\end{eqnarray}%
\begin{eqnarray}
\Pi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) \Phi
_{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi
_{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) 
\widetilde{F}_{1}=\Phi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) =\Phi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) =%
\widetilde{F}_{3} \\
\Pi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) \Phi
_{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi
_{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) 
\widetilde{F}_{3}=\Phi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) =0
\end{eqnarray}%
\begin{eqnarray}
\Pi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) \Phi
_{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi
_{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) 
\widetilde{F}_{1}=\Phi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) =0 \\
\Pi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) \Phi
_{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi
_{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) 
\widetilde{F}_{3}=\Phi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) =\Phi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) =%
\widetilde{F}_{3}
\end{eqnarray}

2. $\Pi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }$ in $%
\mathcal{H}_{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }$
by%
\begin{eqnarray}
\Pi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) \Phi
_{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi
_{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) 
\widetilde{F}_{2}=\Phi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) =\Phi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) =%
\widetilde{F}_{2} \\
\Pi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) \Phi
_{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi
_{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) 
\widetilde{F}_{4}=\Phi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) =0
\end{eqnarray}%
\begin{eqnarray}
\Pi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) \Phi
_{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi
_{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) 
\widetilde{F}_{2}=\Phi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) =0 \\
\Pi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) \Phi
_{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi
_{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) 
\widetilde{F}_{4}=\Phi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) =\Phi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) =%
\widetilde{F}_{2}
\end{eqnarray}%
\begin{eqnarray}
\Pi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) \Phi
_{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi
_{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) 
\widetilde{F}_{2}=\Phi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) =\Phi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) =%
\widetilde{F}_{4} \\
\Pi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) \Phi
_{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi
_{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) 
\widetilde{F}_{4}=\Phi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) =0
\end{eqnarray}%
\begin{eqnarray}
\Pi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) \Phi
_{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi
_{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) 
\widetilde{F}_{2}=\Phi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) =0 \\
\Pi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) \Phi
_{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) &=&\Pi
_{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) 
\widetilde{F}_{4}=\Phi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) =\Phi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) =%
\widetilde{F}_{4}.
\end{eqnarray}%
The representation $\Pi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\oplus \Pi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }$ acts on the space 
\begin{eqnarray}
&&\mathbb{C-}\limfunc{linspan}\left\{ \widetilde{F}_{1},\widetilde{F}%
_{3}\right\} \oplus \mathbb{C-}\limfunc{linspan}\left\{ \widetilde{F}_{2},%
\widetilde{F}_{4}\right\} = \\
&&\mathbb{C-}\limfunc{linspan}\left\{ \Phi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) ,\Phi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) \right\} \oplus \mathbb{C-}\limfunc{%
linspan}\left\{ \Phi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \right) ,\Phi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \right) \right\}
\end{eqnarray}%
$\lceil $as these subspaces are disjoint we can always make the
identifications 
\begin{eqnarray}
\widetilde{F}_{1} &=&\left\vert \mathfrak{f}_{1}\right\rangle
\leftrightarrow \left( \widetilde{F}_{1},0\right) \\
\widetilde{F}_{2} &=&\left\vert \mathfrak{f}_{2}\right\rangle
\leftrightarrow \left( 0,\widetilde{F}_{2}\right) \\
\widetilde{F}_{3} &=&\left\vert \mathfrak{f}_{3}\right\rangle
\leftrightarrow \left( \widetilde{F}_{3},0\right) \\
\widetilde{F}_{4} &=&\left\vert \mathfrak{f}_{4}\right\rangle
\leftrightarrow \left( 0,\widetilde{F}_{4}\right)
\end{eqnarray}%
$\rfloor $

and is given by

\begin{enumerate}
\item 
\begin{eqnarray}
\left( \left( \Pi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\oplus \Pi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\right) \left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right) \right) \left( \widetilde{F}_{1},0\right) &=&\left(
\Pi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) 
\widetilde{F}_{1},\Pi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right) 0\right) =\left( \widetilde{F}_{1},0\right) \\
\left( \left( \Pi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\oplus \Pi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\right) \left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right) \right) \left( \widetilde{F}_{3},0\right) &=&\left(
\Pi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) 
\widetilde{F}_{1},\Pi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right) 0\right) =\left( 0,0\right) \\
\left( \left( \Pi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\oplus \Pi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\right) \left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right) \right) \left( 0,\widetilde{F}_{2}\right) &=&\left(
\Pi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) 0,\Pi
_{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) 
\widetilde{F}_{2}\right) =\left( 0,\widetilde{F}_{2}\right) \\
\left( \left( \Pi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\oplus \Pi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\right) \left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right) \right) \left( 0,\widetilde{F}_{4}\right) &=&\left(
\Pi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) 0,\Pi
_{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }\left(
\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert \right) 
\widetilde{F}_{4}\right) =\left( 0,0\right) ,
\end{eqnarray}%
which we can write as 
\begin{eqnarray}
\left( \left( \Pi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\oplus \Pi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\right) \left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right) \right) \widetilde{F}_{1} &=&\widetilde{F}_{1} \\
\left( \left( \Pi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\oplus \Pi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\right) \left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right) \right) \widetilde{F}_{3} &=&0 \\
\left( \left( \Pi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\oplus \Pi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\right) \left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right) \right) \widetilde{F}_{2} &=&\widetilde{F}_{2} \\
\left( \left( \Pi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\oplus \Pi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\right) \left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right) \right) \widetilde{F}_{4} &=&0,
\end{eqnarray}%
and compared to 
\begin{eqnarray}
\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) F_{1} &=&F_{1} \\
\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) F_{3} &=&0 \\
\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) F_{2} &=&F_{2} \\
\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) F_{4} &=&0
\end{eqnarray}

\item similarly 
\begin{eqnarray}
\left( \left( \Pi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\oplus \Pi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\right) \left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \right) \right) \widetilde{F}_{1} &=&0 \\
\left( \left( \Pi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\oplus \Pi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\right) \left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \right) \right) \widetilde{F}_{3} &=&\widetilde{F}_{1} \\
\left( \left( \Pi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\oplus \Pi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\right) \left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \right) \right) \widetilde{F}_{2} &=&0 \\
\left( \left( \Pi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\oplus \Pi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\right) \left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \right) \right) \widetilde{F}_{4} &=&\widetilde{F}_{2},
\end{eqnarray}%
can be compared to 
\begin{eqnarray}
\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) F_{1} &=&0 \\
\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) F_{3} &=&F_{1} \\
\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) F_{2} &=&0 \\
\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) F_{4} &=&F_{2}
\end{eqnarray}

\item and 
\begin{eqnarray}
\left( \left( \Pi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\oplus \Pi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\right) \left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right) \right) \widetilde{F}_{1} &=&\widetilde{F}_{3} \\
\left( \left( \Pi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\oplus \Pi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\right) \left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right) \right) \widetilde{F}_{3} &=&0 \\
\left( \left( \Pi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\oplus \Pi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\right) \left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right) \right) \widetilde{F}_{2} &=&\widetilde{F}_{4} \\
\left( \left( \Pi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\oplus \Pi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\right) \left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert \right) \right) \widetilde{F}_{4} &=&0,
\end{eqnarray}%
which we can compared to 
\begin{eqnarray}
\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) F_{1} &=&F_{3} \\
\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) F_{3} &=&0 \\
\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) F_{2} &=&F_{4} \\
\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert
\right) F_{4} &=&0
\end{eqnarray}

\item and 
\begin{eqnarray}
\left( \left( \Pi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\oplus \Pi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\right) \left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \right) \right) \widetilde{F}_{1} &=&0 \\
\left( \left( \Pi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\oplus \Pi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\right) \left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \right) \right) \widetilde{F}_{3} &=&\widetilde{F}_{3} \\
\left( \left( \Pi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\oplus \Pi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\right) \left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \right) \right) \widetilde{F}_{2} &=&0 \\
\left( \left( \Pi _{\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert }\oplus \Pi _{\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert }\right) \left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \right) \right) \widetilde{F}_{4} &=&\widetilde{F}_{4},
\end{eqnarray}%
which we can compare to 
\begin{eqnarray}
\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) F_{1} &=&0 \\
\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) F_{3} &=&F_{3} \\
\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) F_{2} &=&0 \\
\Pi _{D}\left( \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
\right) F_{4} &=&F_{4}.
\end{eqnarray}%
\emph{Hence the representations }$\Pi _{D}$\emph{\ and }$\Pi _{\left\vert
e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert }\oplus \Pi _{\left\vert
e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert }$\emph{\ are the same!}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{theorem}
The GNS\ representation $\Pi _{f}$ given by the mixed state 
\begin{equation}
f=\sum_{1\leq j\leq m}\alpha _{j}f_{j}
\end{equation}%
formed from the pure states $\left\{ \left. f_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq
m\right\} $ is the direct sum of the representations given by the component
pure states i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{f}=\bigoplus\limits_{1\leq j\leq m}\Pi _{f_{j}}.
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

In the particular in the $2\times 2$ case this means all density operators 
\begin{equation}
\alpha \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{1}\right\vert +\left(
1-\alpha \right) \left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert
;0<\alpha <1
\end{equation}%
give the same GNS representation. This would mean that the normalized trace
state 
\begin{equation}
D=\frac{1}{2}\left( \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert +\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{2}\right\vert \right) =\frac{1}{2}I_{2}
\end{equation}%
would also give the same representation.

The set of representations could be classified by rank i.e rank $=1$ or $2.$
It is trivial to see that within each rank class there is a set of unitary
equivalent representations for each distinct $\alpha $ i.e 
\begin{equation}
D_{\left( 2\right) U}=U\left( \alpha \left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{1}\right\vert +\left( 1-\alpha \right) \left\vert
e_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{2}\right\vert \right) U^{\dagger }=\alpha
\left\vert f_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle f_{1}\right\vert +\left( 1-\alpha
\right) \left\vert f_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle f_{2}\right\vert
\end{equation}%
and%
\begin{equation}
D_{\left( 1\right) U}=U\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{1}\right\vert U^{\dagger }=\left\vert f_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
f_{1}\right\vert ,
\end{equation}%
where 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert f_{j}\right\rangle =U\left\vert e_{j}\right\rangle ,
\end{equation}%
in fact any vector can be produced from $\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle $ by
the action of $U\in U\left( 2\right) $ as 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert e_{2}\right\rangle =U_{12}\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle \text{
for some }U_{12}\in U\left( 2\right) ,
\end{equation}%
as $\left\vert e_{1}\right\rangle $ is cyclic for $U\left( 2\right) .$
Further by the preceeding different $\alpha \in \left( 0,1\right) $ also
produce unitary equivalent representations as do $\alpha \in \left\{
0,1\right\} .$

One can define new states on $\mathcal{A}$ that are in the same folium as $f$
by 
\begin{eqnarray}
f_{v}\left( A\right) &=&\left\langle \left. U_{e}\Phi _{e}\left(
I_{2}\right) \right\vert \Pi _{e_{1}}\left( A\right) U_{e}\Phi _{e}\left(
I_{2}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{e}}\overset{\text{if }\Pi _{e_{1}}%
\text{ is surjective}}{=}\left\langle \left. \Phi _{e}\left( I_{2}\right)
\right\vert \Pi _{e}\left( U\right) ^{\dagger }\Pi _{e_{1}}\left( A\right)
\Pi _{e}\left( U\right) \Phi _{e}\left( I_{2}\right) \right\rangle _{%
\mathcal{H}_{e}} \\
&=&\left\langle \left. \Phi _{e}\left( U\right) \right\vert \Pi
_{e_{1}}\left( A\right) \Phi _{e}\left( U\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}%
_{e}}=\left\langle \left. \Phi _{e}\left( I_{2}\right) \right\vert \Pi
_{e_{1}}\left( U^{\dagger }AU\right) \Phi _{e}\left( I_{2}\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{e}}=f_{e}\left( U^{\dagger }AU\right) .
\end{eqnarray}%
These states produce unitarily equivalent irreducible representations. If $%
\Pi _{e}$ is faithful i.e. injective then $U$ is unique--though there are in
general many $U$ such that 
\begin{equation}
U_{e}\left\vert \Phi _{e}\left( I_{2}\right) \right\rangle _{e}=\left\vert
v\right\rangle _{e}.
\end{equation}%
This construction can be generalized to mixed state $f_{D}$ producing
unitarily equivalent representations for states that have the same rank,
this can be observed by noting the expansion of a general state in terms of
pure$\equiv $extreme ones i.e. 
\begin{equation}
f=\sum_{1\leq j\leq p}\alpha _{j}f_{j}
\end{equation}%
and noting that the representations produced by the extreme states are
unitarily equivalent. The rank classification does not seem to correspond to
the folium classification.

All positive operators are given by 
\begin{equation}
QdQ^{\dagger }=Qd^{\frac{1}{2}}\left( Qd^{\frac{1}{2}}\right) ^{\dagger }
\end{equation}%
for any positive $d$ and any normal state by 
\begin{equation}
D=\frac{1}{\func{Tr}_{\mathcal{A}}\left\{ Qd^{\frac{1}{2}}\left( Qd^{\frac{1%
}{2}}\right) ^{\dagger }\right\} }Qd^{\frac{1}{2}}dQ^{\dagger }.
\end{equation}

The algebra $\mathcal{A}=M\left( 2,\mathbb{C}\right) $ is a complex algebra
that can be generated by the complex span of the self adjoint Pauli
traceless operators $\left\{ \left. \sigma _{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq
3\right\} $ that has a basis $\left\{ I_{2},\left\{ \left. \sigma
_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq 3\right\} \right\} .$This basis generates the
real algebra $\mathcal{A}_{r}\equiv M_{h}\left( 2,\mathbb{C}\right) $ of
self adjoint operators and hermitian matrices. States on $\mathcal{A}_{r}$
are the same as states on $\mathcal{A}.$

$\rfloor $

What is the transformation 
\begin{equation}
T:\mathcal{H}_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\rightarrow \mathcal{H}_{X}
\end{equation}%
given by 
\begin{equation}
T\left( \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \right) =\Phi
_{X}\left( A\right) .
\end{equation}%
so that%
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{X}\left( A\right) \right\vert \Phi _{X}\left(
A\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{X}}=\left\langle \left. T\left( \Phi _{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \right) \right\vert T\left( \Phi _{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}%
_{X}}=\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ A^{\dagger }AXX^{\dagger }\right\} ,
\end{equation}%
while 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \right\vert
\Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}}=\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ A^{\dagger }A\right\} .
\end{equation}%
The amplitudes, $X,$ of the state $D_{X}$ are normalized 
\begin{equation}
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ XX^{\dagger }\right\} =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
\left\{ D_{X}\right\} =1
\end{equation}%
and thus all normalized positive operators produce normal states wrt $%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}$ and are in the same folium. Are $f_{X}$ faithful?
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
f_{X}\left( A^{\dagger }A\right) =f_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( X^{\dagger
}A^{\dagger }AX\right) =0\Longleftrightarrow A=0
\end{equation}%
Obviously 
\begin{equation}
f_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( X^{\dagger }A^{\dagger }AX\right)
=0\Longleftrightarrow AX=0
\end{equation}%
as $f_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}$ is faithful. Let 
\begin{eqnarray}
A &=&n_{1} \\
X &=&h_{1}
\end{eqnarray}%
then 
\begin{equation}
f_{n_{1}}\left( h_{1}\right) =f_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( n_{1}h_{1}\right) =0
\end{equation}%
so $f_{n_{1}}$ is not faithful even though $f_{n_{1}}$ is in the same folium
as $f_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}.$

One should further observe 
\begin{equation}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{X}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \mathfrak{Q}_{X}%
\mathfrak{Q}_{X}^{\dagger }\right\} =\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{X}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \mathfrak{Q}_{X}\mathfrak{Q}_{X}^{\dagger }\right\} =%
\func{Tr}\left\{ P_{\mathcal{V}}\mathfrak{Q}_{X}\mathfrak{Q}_{X}^{\dagger
}\right\} =\func{Tr}\left\{ P_{\mathcal{V}}\mathfrak{D}_{X}\right\} =1,
\end{equation}%
which is consistent with $\mathfrak{D}_{X}$ defining a normal state of $\Pi
_{X}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) .$

The GNS representation, $\Pi _{X},$ of $\mathcal{A}$ has the property 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{X}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) =P_{\mathcal{V}},
\end{equation}%
where $P_{\mathcal{V}}$ is an orthogonal projector belonging to $\mathcal{B}%
\left( \mathcal{H}_{X}\right) $. In particular 
\begin{eqnarray}
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ I_{\mathcal{A}}\right\} &=&\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
\left\{ XX^{\dagger }\right\} =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ D_{X}\right\}
=1=\left\langle \left. \Phi _{X}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert
\Pi _{X}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \Phi _{X}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{X}} \\
&=&\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{X}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \left\vert \Phi
_{X}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{X}\left(
I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \right\} =\func{Tr}\left\{ P_{\mathcal{V}%
}\left\vert \Phi _{X}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle
\left\langle \Phi _{X}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \right\}
\leq \func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{X}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\} =\func{%
Tr}\left\{ P_{\mathcal{V}}\right\} .
\end{eqnarray}

\begin{theorem}
GNS representations generated by vector states in the representation space
are unitarily equivalent i.e. 
\begin{equation}
u^{\dagger }\Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) u=\Pi _{\Psi }\left(
A\right) \,\forall A\in \mathcal{A},
\end{equation}%
where $\Psi $ is a cyclic vector wrt $\Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( 
\mathcal{A}\right) $ and defines a state $f_{\Psi }$ given by 
\begin{equation}
f_{\Psi }\left( A\right) =\left\langle \left. \Psi \right\vert \Pi _{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \Psi \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}}}=\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right)
\left\vert \Psi \right\rangle \left\langle \Psi \right\vert \right\} =\func{%
Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \left\vert \Psi
\right\rangle \left\langle \Upsilon \right\vert \left\vert \Upsilon
\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi \right\vert \right\} =\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \left\vert \Psi \right\rangle
\left\langle \Upsilon \right\vert \left\vert \Psi \right\rangle \left\langle
\Upsilon \right\vert ^{\dagger }\right\} .
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}=\overline{\left\{ \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{%
tr}}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\} }=\overline{\left\{ \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) \Psi \right\} }
\end{equation}%
thus $\exists $ a unitary transformation $u\in \mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}%
_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\right) $ such that 
\begin{equation}
\Psi =u\Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) .
\end{equation}%
The state $f_{\Psi }$ generates a GNS\ representation 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{\Psi }\left( A\right) \Phi _{\Psi }\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) =\Phi
_{\Psi }\left( A\right)
\end{equation}%
such that 
\begin{equation}
f_{\Psi }\left( A\right) =\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\Psi }\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Pi _{\Psi }\left( A\right) \Phi _{\Psi
}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{\Psi }}.
\end{equation}%
As 
\begin{equation}
f_{\Psi }\left( A\right) =\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\Psi }\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Pi _{\Psi }\left( A\right) \Phi _{\Psi
}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{\Psi
}}=\left\langle \left. \Psi \right\vert \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left(
A\right) \Psi \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}}\forall
A\in \mathcal{A}
\end{equation}%
and $\left\langle \left. {}\right\vert \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{\Psi
}}=\left\langle \left. {}\right\vert \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}}}$ one concludes 
\begin{equation}
\Psi =\Phi _{\Psi }\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) =u\Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr%
}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\end{equation}%
giving 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\Psi }\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert
\Pi _{\Psi }\left( A\right) \Phi _{\Psi }\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{\Psi }}=\left\langle \left. u\Phi _{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Pi _{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) u\Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
}}=\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\vert u^{\dagger }\Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right)
u\Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{%
\mathcal{H}_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}}
\end{equation}%
and 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\Psi }\left( X\right) \right\vert \Pi _{\Psi
}\left( A\right) \Phi _{\Psi }\left( X\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}%
_{\Psi }}=\left\langle \left. u\Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( X\right)
\right\vert \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) u\Phi _{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}}\left( X\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{\mathit{\limfunc{%
tr}}}}=\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( X\right)
\right\vert u^{\dagger }\Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) u\Phi _{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( X\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}}}\forall X\in \mathcal{A}.
\end{equation}%
Thus 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{\Psi }\left( A\right) =u^{\dagger }\Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left(
A\right) u.
\end{equation}

\emph{Note it is possible that neither } 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \Psi \right\rangle \left\langle \Psi \right\vert =u\left\vert
\Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle
\left\langle \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\vert u^{\dagger }\text{ nor }\left\vert \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert
\end{equation}
\emph{belong to} the representations $\Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( 
\mathcal{A}\right) =\left( u\Pi _{\Psi }u^{\dagger }\right) \left( \mathcal{A%
}\right) .$

As 
\begin{equation}
f_{\Psi }\left( A\right) =\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\Psi }\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Pi _{\Psi }\left( A\right) \Phi _{\Psi
}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{\Psi
}}=\left\langle \left. \Psi \right\vert \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left(
A\right) \Psi \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
}}=\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\vert u^{\dagger }\Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right)
u\Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{%
\mathcal{H}_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}}
\end{equation}%
and $\Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}$ is faithful one has 
\begin{equation}
f_{\Psi }\left( A\right) =\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
}\left( v\right) ^{\dagger }\Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \Pi
_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( v\right) \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left(
I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
}}=\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\vert \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( v^{\dagger
}Av\right) \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}}=f_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
}\left( v^{\dagger }Av\right) ,
\end{equation}%
where 
\begin{equation}
v^{\dagger }v=vv^{\dagger }=I_{\mathcal{A}}
\end{equation}%
thus one can see that the states $f_{\Psi }$ and $f_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}$
belong to the same folium.$\rfloor $
\end{proof}

If $f_{D_{f}}$ and $f_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}$ are normal states one can consider $%
\Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}\right) \equiv \Pi _{I_{\mathcal{A}%
}}\left( D_{f}\right) ,\Pi _{D_{f}}\left( D_{f}\right) $, $\Pi
_{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) ,\left\vert \Phi _{D_{f}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{D_{f}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert $ and $\left\vert \Phi _{I_{\mathcal{A}%
}}\left( D_{f}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{I_{\mathcal{A}%
}}\left( D_{f}\right) \right\vert $ these last two operators $\in \Pi
_{D_{f}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ or $\Pi _{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( 
\mathcal{A}\right) $ if $\Pi _{D_{f}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ or $\Pi
_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ are a type $I$
representations. (Can $\Pi _{D_{f}}\left( D_{f}\right) =0?).$ In particular 
\begin{align}
\Pi _{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( D_{f}\right) \Phi _{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) & =\Phi _{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( D_{f}\right) \\
\Pi _{D_{f}}\left( D_{f}\right) \Phi _{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) &
=\Phi _{D_{f}}\left( D_{f}\right) .
\end{align}%
The values of the states $f_{D_{f}}$ and $f_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}$ can be
expressed in terms of cyclic vector expectation values as 
\begin{align}
f_{D_{f}}\left( A\right) & =\left\langle \left. \Phi _{D_{f}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Pi _{D_{f}}\left( A\right) \Phi
_{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{D_{f}}}=%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ AD_{f}\right\} \\
f_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( A\right) & =\left\langle \left. \Phi _{I_{\mathcal{%
A}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Pi _{D_{f}}\left( A\right)
\Phi _{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{%
\mathcal{H}_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}}=\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ A\right\} .
\end{align}%
The GNS inner products are be expressed as 
\begin{align}
f_{D_{f}}\left( A^{\dagger }B\right) & =\left\langle \left. \Phi
_{D_{f}}\left( A\right) \right\vert \Phi _{D_{f}}\left( B\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{D_{f}}}=\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{D_{f}}\left(
A\right) ^{\dagger }\Pi _{D_{f}}\left( B\right) \left\vert \Phi
_{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi
_{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \right\} =\mathit{\limfunc{%
tr}}\left\{ A^{\dagger }BD_{f}\right\} \\
f_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( A^{\dagger }B\right) & =\left\langle \left. \Phi
_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( A\right) \right\vert \Phi _{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left(
B\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}}=\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi
_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( A\right) ^{\dagger }\Pi _{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left(
B\right) \left\vert \Phi _{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\vert \right\} =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ A^{\dagger
}B\right\}
\end{align}%
The connection between the two GNS\ inner products is given by 
\begin{align}
f_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( A^{\dagger }D_{f}B\right) & =\fbox{$\left\langle
\left. \Phi _{I}{}_{\mathcal{A}}\left( A\right) \right\vert \Pi _{I}{}_{%
\mathcal{A}}\left( D_{f}\right) \Phi _{I}{}_{\mathcal{A}}\left( B\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}}{}_{I}{}_{\mathcal{A}}$=$\left\langle \left.
\Phi _{D}{}_{f}\left( B^{\dagger }\right) \right\vert \Phi _{D}{}_{f}\left(
A^{\dagger }\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{D_{f}}}\ $}=\overline{%
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{D_{f}}\left( A^{\dagger }\right) \right\vert \Phi
_{D_{f}}\left( B^{\dagger }\right) \right\rangle }_{\mathcal{H}_{D_{f}}} \\
& =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ A^{\dagger }D_{f}B\right\} =\mathit{\limfunc{%
tr}}\left\{ BA^{\dagger }D_{f}\right\} =\overline{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
\left\{ D_{f}AB^{\dagger }\right\} }=\overline{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{
AB^{\dagger }D_{f}\right\} }.
\end{align}%
\begin{align}
f_{D_{f}}\left( A\right) & =\left\langle \left. \Phi _{D_{f}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right)
\Phi _{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}%
_{D_{f}}}=\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ AD_{f}\right\} \\
f_{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) & =\left\langle \left. \Phi
_{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \Phi _{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{D_{f}}}=\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{
D_{f}\right\} =1 \\
f_{D_{f}}\left( D_{f}\right) & =\left\langle \left. \Phi _{D_{f}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left(
D_{f}\right) \Phi _{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{%
\mathcal{H}_{D_{f}}}=\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ D_{f}^{2}\right\} .
\end{align}%
The representation spaces $\mathcal{H}_{D_{f}}$ and $\mathcal{H}_{I_{%
\mathcal{A}}}$always have the $\func{Tr}$ functional defined giving

\begin{enumerate}
\item 
\begin{eqnarray}
f_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( D_{f}\right) &=&\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}\right) \left\vert \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \right\}
=\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\vert \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}}=\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{
D_{f}\right\} \\
&=&\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\vert \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}\right) \Phi _{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{%
H}_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}}=1
\end{eqnarray}

\item 
\begin{equation}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi_{D_{f}}\left( D_{f}\right) \right\} =\left\langle
\left. \Phi_{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert
\Pi_{D_{f}}\left( D_{f}\right) \Phi_{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{D_{f}}}+\cdots=2^{2r}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
\left\{ D_{f}^{2}\right\}
\end{equation}

\item 
\begin{align}
& \func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{D_{f}}\left( A\right) ^{\dagger }\Pi _{D_{f}}\left(
D_{f}\right) \right\} \\
& =\frac{\left\langle \left. \Phi _{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\vert \Pi _{D_{f}}\left( A\right) ^{\dagger }\Pi _{D_{f}}\left(
D_{f}\right) \Phi _{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{%
\mathcal{H}_{D_{f}}}}{\left\langle \left. \Phi _{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\vert \Phi _{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{D_{f}}}} \\
& +\sum_{1\leq p\leq 2r}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{p}\leq 2r}\frac{%
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{D_{f}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right)
\right\vert \Pi _{D_{f}}\left( A\right) ^{\dagger }\Pi _{D_{f}}\left(
D_{f}\right) \Phi _{D_{f}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{D_{f}}}}{\left\langle \left. \Phi
_{D_{f}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right) \right\vert \Phi
_{D_{f}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}%
_{D_{f}}}} \\
& =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ A^{\dagger }D_{f}^{2}\right\} +\sum_{1\leq
p\leq 2r}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{p}\leq 2r}\frac{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
\left\{ \left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right) ^{\dagger }A^{\dagger
}D_{f}^{2}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right) \right\} }{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ \left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right) ^{\dagger
}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right) \right\} }=2^{2r}\mathit{\limfunc{tr%
}}\left\{ A^{\dagger }D_{f}^{2}\right\}
\end{align}

\item 
\begin{align}
& \func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( A\right) ^{\dagger }\Pi _{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}\right) \right\} \\
& =\frac{\left\langle \left. \Phi _{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\vert \Pi _{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( A\right) ^{\dagger }\Pi _{I_{%
\mathcal{A}}}\left( D_{f}\right) \Phi _{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}}{\left\langle \left. \Phi
_{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Phi _{D_{f}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}} \\
& +\sum_{1\leq p\leq 2r}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{p}\leq 2r}\frac{%
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{D_{f}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right)
\right\vert \Pi _{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( A\right) ^{\dagger }\Pi _{I_{%
\mathcal{A}}}\left( D_{f}\right) \Phi _{D_{f}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots
x_{j_{p}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}}{%
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{D_{f}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right)
\right\vert \Phi _{D_{f}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}} \\
& =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ A^{\dagger }D_{f}\right\} +\sum_{1\leq p\leq
2r}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{p}\leq 2r}\frac{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{
\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right) ^{\dagger }A^{\dagger }D_{f}\left(
x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right) \right\} }{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{
\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right) ^{\dagger }\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots
x_{j_{p}}\right) \right\} }=2^{2r}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ A^{\dagger
}D_{f}\right\}
\end{align}

\item 
\begin{equation}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}\right) \right\}
=2^{2r}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ D_{f}\right\} =2^{2r}.
\end{equation}
$\rfloor$
\end{enumerate}

The connection between $\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}$ on $\mathcal{A}^{c}$ and $%
\func{Tr}$ on $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\right)
\equiv \mathcal{B}\left( \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( \mathcal{A}%
^{c}\right) \right) $ is given by 
\begin{align}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \right\} & =%
\frac{\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\vert \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \Phi _{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{%
H}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}}{\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}\mathit{\limfunc{tr%
}}}} \\
& +\sum_{1\leq p\leq 2r}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{p}\leq 2r}\frac{%
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots
x_{j_{p}}\right) \right\vert \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right)
\Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}}{\left\langle \left. \Phi
_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right) \right\vert
\Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}} \\
& =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ A\right\} +\sum_{1\leq p\leq 2r}\sum_{1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots <j_{p}\leq 2r}\frac{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ \left(
x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right) ^{\dagger }A\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots
x_{j_{p}}\right) \right\} }{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ \left(
x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right) ^{\dagger }\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots
x_{j_{p}}\right) \right\} } \\
& =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ A\right\} +\sum_{1\leq p\leq 2r}\sum_{1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots <j_{p}\leq 2r}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ A\right\} \\
& =2^{2r}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ A\right\} ,
\end{align}%
where 
\begin{eqnarray}
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ A\right\} &=&\frac{\left\langle \left. \Phi _{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Pi _{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}}}{%
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\vert \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}}} \\
&=&\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \left\vert
\Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle
\left\langle \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\vert \right\} =\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
}\left( A\right) \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}}.
\end{eqnarray}%
$\lceil $%
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\vert \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}=1
\end{equation}%
$\rfloor $

This shows in particular that 
\begin{equation}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\} =2^{2r}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ I_{\mathcal{A}}\right\} =2^{2r}
\end{equation}
and if $r=\infty$ 
\begin{equation}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\} =\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\} =\infty.
\end{equation}

One can introduce a normalized trace in $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\right) $ by 
\begin{equation}
\left. \mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\right\vert _{\mathcal{H}_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
}}\left\{ \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \right\} =\frac{1}{%
2^{2r}}\func{Tr}\left\{ I_{\mathcal{H}_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}}\right\} =%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ A\right\} .
\end{equation}
Thus 
\begin{align}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\} & =2^{2r}=2^{2r}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ I_{\mathcal{A}}\right\}
\\
\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}\right) \right\} &
=2^{2r}=2^{2r}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ D_{f}\right\}
\end{align}
$\lceil$%
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) =I_{\mathcal{H}_{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}}
\end{equation}
as can be seen from 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \Phi_{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) =\Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right)
\,\forall A\in\mathcal{A}
\end{equation}

$\rfloor$

and%
\begin{equation}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) ^{\dagger}\Pi_{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}\right) \right\} =\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi_{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A^{\dagger}D_{f}\right) \right\} =2^{2r}\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}\left( A^{\dagger}D_{f}\right) =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left(
A^{\dagger}2^{2r}D_{f}\right) .
\end{equation}
Thus a density operator, $\mathrm{D},$ belonging to $\mathcal{B}\left( \Phi_{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( \mathcal{F}\right) \right) $ and having the
property 
\begin{equation}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \mathrm{D}\right\} =1
\end{equation}
is given by 
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{D}=\frac{1}{2^{2r}}\Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}\right) .
\end{equation}
$\lceil$%
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{D}^{2}=\frac{1}{2^{4r}}\Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left(
D_{f}^{2}\right)
\end{equation}%
\begin{align}
\mathrm{D}^{2} & =\mathrm{D\Rightarrow}\frac{1}{2^{4r}}\Pi _{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}^{2}\right) =\frac{1}{2^{2r}}\Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{%
tr}}}\left( D_{f}\right) \\
2^{2r}\Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}^{2}\right) & =\Pi_{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}\right) \\
\Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}^{2}\right) & =\frac {1}{2^{2r}}\Pi_{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}\right)
\end{align}
$\rfloor$

$\lceil$If $\left\vert \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert $ $\in\Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( 
\mathcal{A}\right) $ then 
\begin{equation*}
\mathrm{D}=\left\vert \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert =\Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( \frac{1}{%
2^{2r}}I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\end{equation*}
and 
\begin{align}
& \func{Tr}\left\{ \left\vert \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{%
A}}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \right\} =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right\} =1 \\
& \mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ AD_{f}\right\} =\func{Tr}\left\{ A\left\vert
\Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle
\left\langle \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\vert \right\} =\left\langle \left. \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left(
I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left(
A\right) \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}=\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
\left\{ A\right\} .
\end{align}
$\rfloor$

$\lceil $ 
\begin{equation}
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left( D_{f}\right) =1
\end{equation}%
no matter the dimension of $\mathcal{A}$ if we define 
\begin{equation}
D_{f}=I_{\mathcal{A}}+\sum_{1\leq j\leq 2r}D_{fj}
\end{equation}%
so that for any finite dimensional subspace 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}_{\mathcal{V}_{ry}}=\tbigwedge \left( \mathcal{V}_{ry}\right) ,
\end{equation}%
where 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{V}_{ry}=\limfunc{linspan}\left\{ y_{1},\cdots ,y_{2r}\right\} ,
\end{equation}%
one has 
\begin{align}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}\right) \right\} & =%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ D_{f}\right\} +\sum_{1\leq p\leq 2r}\sum_{1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots <j_{p}\leq 2r}\frac{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ \left(
y_{j_{1}}\cdots y_{j_{p}}\right) ^{\dagger }D_{f}\left( y_{j_{1}}\cdots
y_{j_{p}}\right) \right\} }{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ \left(
y_{j_{1}}\cdots y_{j_{p}}\right) ^{\dagger }\left( y_{j_{1}}\cdots
y_{j_{p}}\right) \right\} } \\
& =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ D_{f}\right\} +\sum_{1\leq p\leq
2r}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{p}\leq 2r}\frac{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{
D_{f}y_{j_{1}}\cdots y_{j_{p}}y_{j_{p}}^{\dagger }\cdots y_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}\right\} }{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ y_{j_{1}}\cdots
y_{j_{p}}y_{j_{p}}^{\dagger }\cdots y_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right\} } \\
& =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ D_{f}\right\} +\sum_{1\leq p\leq
2r}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{p}\leq 2r}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{
D_{f}\right\} =2^{2r}.
\end{align}%
Giving that 
\begin{equation}
\lim\limits_{r\rightarrow \infty }\left\{ 2^{-2r}\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}\right) \right\} \right\} =1
\end{equation}%
for any sequence of subspaces 
\begin{equation}
\left\{ \mathcal{V}_{ry}\right\} _{r=0,\cdots ,\infty }.
\end{equation}%
If $r=0$ then 
\begin{equation}
D_{f}=I_{\mathcal{A}}
\end{equation}%
and 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{V}_{0y}\cong \mathbb{C}\cong \left\{ \left. zI_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right\vert z\in \mathbb{C}\right\} .
\end{equation}

$\rfloor$

In particular the \emph{central faithful linear} functional $\widetilde{\tau}
$%
\begin{equation}
\widetilde{\tau}:\Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right)
\rightarrow\mathbb{C}
\end{equation}
given by 
\begin{align}
\widetilde{\tau}\left( \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \right) & =%
\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \left\vert \Phi_{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle
\left\langle \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\vert \right\} =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ A\right\} \\
\widetilde{\tau}\left( \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \Pi_{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( B\right) \right) & =\widetilde{\tau}\left( \Pi_{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( B\right) \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left(
A\right) \right) =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ AB\right\} =\mathit{\limfunc{%
tr}}\left\{ BA\right\} \\
& =\left\langle \left. \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\vert \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \Pi_{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}}\left( B\right) \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
}=\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\vert \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( B\right) \Pi _{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}} \\
\widetilde{\tau}\left( \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right) & =\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \left\vert \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \right\} =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
\left\{ I_{\mathcal{A}}\right\}
\end{align}
is a normalized trace on $\Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( \mathcal{A}%
\right) .$ If $r=\infty$ then $\Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( \mathcal{A}%
^{c}\right) $ is $c^{\ast}$-isomorphic to $\mathcal{A}^{c}$ and $\left( \Pi_{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( \mathcal{A}^{c}\right) \right) ^{\prime\prime}$
is a type II$_{1}$ Von Neumann algebra. Of course $\mathcal{B}\left( 
\mathcal{A}_{HS}\right) $ is a $\,$type I$_{r}$ Von Neumann algebra \emph{as
is} $\left( \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( \mathcal{A}^{c}\right) \right)
^{\prime\prime}$ if $r<\infty$. $\widetilde{\tau}$ can be extended to $%
\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{A}_{HS}\right) $, where it cannot be assumed that
it is normalized, finite nor central. See section \cite{CAR Types}

\subsubsection{Convex Set of States}

States $f\in $ $\mathcal{A}^{\ast }$ 
\begin{align}
& f:\mathcal{A\longrightarrow }\mathbb{R} \\
& f\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) =1
\end{align}%
form a convex set $\mathcal{S}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) \subset \mathcal{A}%
^{\ast }$ under convex linear combination 
\begin{equation}
f=\tsum\limits_{1\leq p\leq q}\alpha _{p}f_{p},
\end{equation}%
where 
\begin{align}
& \tsum\limits_{1\leq p\leq q}\alpha _{p}=1 \\
& 0\leq \alpha _{p}\leq 1;1\leq p\leq q \\
f& _{p}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) =1;1\leq p\leq q.
\end{align}%
A state $f$ is an \emph{extreme point} of $\mathcal{S}\left( \mathcal{A}%
\right) $ if 
\begin{equation}
f=\tsum\limits_{1\leq p\leq q}\alpha _{p}f_{p}\Rightarrow \alpha
_{j}=1,\alpha _{k}=1;\alpha _{j}=0,1\leq j\neq k\leq q.
\end{equation}%
If $f$ is a normal state and the algebra has a trace $\tau $ 
\begin{equation}
f\left( A\right) =\tau \left( AD_{f}\right)
\end{equation}%
and normal states form a convex set $\mathcal{S}\left( \mathcal{A}\right)
\subset \mathcal{A}$ so that 
\begin{align}
& \tau \left( D_{f}\right) =1 \\
& D_{f}\geq 0\Leftrightarrow D_{f}=Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger } \\
& D_{f}=\tsum\limits_{1\leq p\leq q}\alpha _{p}D_{f_{p}};D_{f_{p}}\in 
\mathcal{S}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) ,1\leq p\leq q.
\end{align}%
As density operators are postive operators we can write 
\begin{equation}
Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger }=\tsum\limits_{1\leq p\leq q}\alpha
_{p}Q_{f_{p}}Q_{f_{p}}^{\dagger },
\end{equation}%
where $\left\{ Q_{f},Q_{f_{p}}\right\} $ are normalized elements of $%
\mathcal{A}.$ A state $f$ is a \emph{normal} \emph{extreme point} of $%
\mathcal{S}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ if 
\begin{equation}
D_{f}=\tsum\limits_{1\leq p\leq q}\alpha _{p}D_{f_{p}}\Rightarrow \alpha
_{j}=1,\alpha _{k}=1;\alpha _{j}=0,1\leq j\neq k\leq q,
\end{equation}%
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger }=\tsum\limits_{1\leq p\leq q}\alpha
_{p}Q_{f_{p}}Q_{f_{p}}^{\dagger }\Rightarrow \alpha _{j}=1,\alpha
_{k}=1;\alpha _{j}=0,1\leq j\neq k\leq q.
\end{equation}%
If $Q_{f_{p}}Q_{f_{p}}^{\dagger }$ is an orthogonal projector i.e. 
\begin{equation}
Q_{f_{p}}Q_{f_{p}}^{\dagger }Q_{f_{p}}Q_{f_{p}}^{\dagger
}=Q_{f_{p}}Q_{f_{p}}^{\dagger }
\end{equation}%
then $Q_{f_{p}}$ is a partial isometry.

\subsubsection{Identifications}

Do 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}^{w}\,\cong \mathcal{\,\,}\left( \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
}\left( \mathcal{A}^{c}\right) \right) ^{\ast \ast }=\left( \Pi _{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}}\left( \mathcal{A}^{c}\right) \right) ^{\prime \prime }
\end{equation}%
as $w^{\ast }$-algebras? Yes, see \emph{Sherman--Takeda theorem} in the
preceeding.

\subsection{Symmetries and the GNS\ Construction}

\subsubsection{c$^{\ast }$-Algebra Symmetries}

Denote the group of unitary inner automorphism of $\mathcal{A}$ by $\mathcal{%
U}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ where 
\begin{eqnarray}
\widehat{u} &:&\mathcal{A}\rightarrow \mathcal{A} \\
\widehat{u}\left( A\right) &=&uAu^{\dagger } \\
uu^{\dagger } &=&u^{\dagger }u=I_{\mathcal{A}}
\end{eqnarray}
then $A$ is invariant to a subgroup $\mathcal{U}_{G}\left( \mathcal{A}%
\right) $ of $\mathcal{U}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ if 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{u}\left( A\right) =A\text{ }\forall \widehat{u}\in \mathcal{U}%
_{G}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) .
\end{equation}%
Let $f$ be state belonging to $\mathcal{S}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ then
it is invariant to $\mathcal{U}_{G}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ if 
\begin{equation}
f\left( \widehat{u}\left( A\right) \right) =f\left( A\right) \text{ }\forall 
\widehat{u}\in \mathcal{U}_{G}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) ,\forall A\in 
\mathcal{A}.
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Unitary Symmetries}

Unitary symmetries can be implemented in $\mathcal{A}$ by inner $\ast $%
-automorphisms \emph{that are unitary} wrt the $\limfunc{tr}$ inner product
in $\mathcal{A}_{HS}$ i.e. 
\begin{align}
\widehat{u}\left( A\right) & =uAu^{\dagger } \\
\left\langle \left. \widehat{u}\left( A\right) \right\vert \widehat{u}\left(
B\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{A}}& =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ uA^{\dagger
}u^{\dagger }uBu^{\dagger }\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ uA^{\dagger
}Bu^{\dagger }\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger }Bu^{\dagger
}u\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger }B\right\} =\left\langle \left.
A\right\vert B\right\rangle _{\mathcal{A}},
\end{align}%
where 
\begin{equation}
uu^{\dagger }=u^{\dagger }u=I_{\mathcal{A}};u\in \mathcal{A}.
\end{equation}%
$\lceil $One could also consider non unitary symmetries therefore not $\ast $%
-automorphisms 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{g}\left( A\right) =gAg^{-1}=A.
\end{equation}%
and anti-unitary symmetries$\rfloor $

If $A$ is invariant to $\widehat{u}$ i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{u}\left( A\right) =A
\end{equation}%
then $A$ has the symmetry implemented by $\widehat{u}.$ The adjoint $%
\widehat{u}^{\dagger }$ of $\widehat{u}$ wrt $\limfunc{tr},$ which can be
shown as the same as the star operation in $\mathcal{A}$ and is given by 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. \widehat{u}^{\dagger }\left( A\right) \right\vert
B\right\rangle _{\mathcal{A}}=\left\langle \left. A\right\vert \widehat{u}%
\left( B\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{A}}\forall B
\end{equation}%
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \widehat{u}^{\dagger }\left( A\right) B\right\} =%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ AuBu^{\dagger }\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ u^{\dagger
}AuB\right\}
\end{equation}%
so that 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{u}^{\dagger }\left( A\right) =u^{\dagger }Au.
\end{equation}%
As 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{u}^{-1}=\widehat{u}^{\dagger }
\end{equation}%
$\lceil $%
\begin{equation}
\widehat{u}^{\dagger }\left( uAu^{\dagger }\right) =u^{\dagger }uAu^{\dagger
}u=A
\end{equation}%
$\rfloor $

then 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{u}^{\dagger}\left( \widehat{u}\left( A\right) \right) =\widehat{u}%
\left( \widehat{u}^{\dagger}\left( A\right) \right) =A
\end{equation}
so if 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{u}\left( A\right) =A
\end{equation}
then 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{u}^{\dagger}\left( \widehat{u}\left( A\right) \right) =\widehat{u}%
^{\dagger}\left( A\right) =A,
\end{equation}
so if $\widehat{u}$ is a symmetry so is $\widehat{u}^{\dagger}.$

As $\widehat{u}$ is a unitary transformation it is generated by a self
adjoint operator $\widehat{h}$ i.e. 
\begin{align}
\widehat{u}& =\exp \left\{ i\func{adj}\left( h\right) \right\} =\exp \left\{
i\widehat{h}\right\} \\
\func{adj}\left( h\right) \left( \cdot \right) & =\widehat{h}\left( \cdot
\right) =\left[ h,\cdot \right] \\
\widehat{h}& =\widehat{h}^{\dagger };h=h^{\dagger },
\end{align}%
note that $h$ can belong to $\mathcal{A}_{\left( n\right) }$ for any $n\in
\left\{ 0,\cdots ,2r\right\} .$ The adjoint $\widehat{h}^{\dagger }$of $%
\widehat{h}$ is given by 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. \widehat{h}^{\dagger }\left( A\right) \right\vert
B\right\rangle _{\mathcal{A}}=\left\langle \left. \overset{}{A}\right\vert 
\widehat{h}\left( B\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{A}}
\end{equation}%
i.e. 
\begin{eqnarray}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left( \widehat{h}^{\dagger }\left( A\right) \right)
^{\dagger }B\right\} &=&\limfunc{tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger }\widehat{h}\left(
B\right) \right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger }\left[ h,B\right]
\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger }hB-A^{\dagger }Bh\right\} \\
&=&\limfunc{tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger }hB-hA^{\dagger }B\right\} =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ \left[ A^{\dagger },h\right] B\right\} ,
\end{eqnarray}%
so that the self adjoint condition 
\begin{align}
& \left( \widehat{h}^{\dagger }\left( A\right) \right) ^{\dagger }=\left[
A^{\dagger },h\right] \\
& \Rightarrow \widehat{h}^{\dagger }\left( A\right) =\left( \left[
A^{\dagger },h\right] \right) ^{\dagger }=h^{\dagger }A-Ah^{\dagger }=\left[
h,A\right] =\widehat{h}\left( A\right) .
\end{align}%
One should note that as 
\begin{align}
\widehat{u}\left( A\right) & =uAu^{\dagger } \\
\widehat{u}^{\dagger }\left( A\right) & =u^{\dagger }Au \\
\left( \widehat{u}\left( A\right) \right) ^{\dagger }& =uA^{\dagger
}u^{\dagger }=\widehat{u}\left( A^{\dagger }\right) \\
\left( \widehat{u}\left( A^{\dagger }\right) \right) ^{\dagger }&
=uAu^{\dagger }=\widehat{u}\left( A\right)
\end{align}%
one has 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{u}\left( A\right) ^{\dagger }=uA^{\dagger }u^{\dagger }=\widehat{u}%
\left( A^{\dagger }\right) \neq \widehat{u}^{\dagger }\left( A\right) \text{
in general}
\end{equation}%
and 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{h}\left( A\right) ^{\dagger }=-\widehat{h}\left( A^{\dagger
}\right) .
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Projective Symmetries}

$A$ is \emph{projectively invariant} to $\widehat{u}$ if 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{u}\left( A\right) =\exp\left\{ i\func{adj}\left( h\right)
\lambda\right\} \left( A\right) =e^{i\lambda}A;\lambda \in\mathbb{R}.
\end{equation}
If $A$ is projectively invariant to $\widehat{u}$ then 
\begin{align}
\widehat{u}\left( A\right) \left( \widehat{u}\left( A\right) \right)
^{\dagger} & =AA^{\dagger} \\
\left( \widehat{u}\left( A\right) \right) ^{\dagger}\widehat{u}\left(
A\right) & =A^{\dagger}A
\end{align}
and $AA^{\dagger}$ and $A^{\dagger}A$ are invariant to $\widehat{u}$ i.e. 
\begin{align}
\widehat{u}\left( AA^{\dagger}\right) & =uAu^{\dagger}uA^{\dagger
}u^{\dagger}=\widehat{u}\left( A\right) \widehat{u}\left( A^{\dagger
}\right) =e^{i\lambda}Ae^{-i\lambda}A^{\dagger}=AA^{\dagger} \\
\widehat{u}\left( A^{\dagger}A\right) & =uA^{\dagger}u^{\dagger
}uAu^{\dagger}=\widehat{u}\left( A^{\dagger}\right) \widehat{u}\left(
A\right) =e^{-i\lambda}A^{\dagger}e^{i\lambda}A=A^{\dagger}A.
\end{align}
If 
\begin{equation}
Au=uA,\quad\text{i.e. }\left[ A,u\right] =0
\end{equation}
then 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{u}\left( A\right) =uAu^{\dagger}=A
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{h}\left( A\right) =0,
\end{equation}
which is a special case of 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{h}\left( A\right) =\lambda A.
\end{equation}
Other special cases are given by 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{h}\left( A\right) =2n\pi iA;0\leq n\leq\infty,
\end{equation}
which give 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{u}\left( A\right) =uAu^{\dagger}=A.
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Symmetry Adaptation}

A symmetry group, $G,$ is represented in $\mathcal{A}$ by 
\begin{equation}
\Pi :G\rightarrow \mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) ,
\end{equation}%
where $\Pi $ can be outer or inner automorphisms, the inner automorphisms
can be classified into

\begin{itemize}
\item adjoint 
\begin{equation}
\Pi\left( g\right) \left( A\right) =\mathcal{R}\left( g\right) A\mathcal{R}%
\left( g\right) ^{-1}.
\end{equation}

\item adjoint and $\ast$-automorphisms therefore unitary%
\begin{equation}
\Pi\left( g\right) \left( A\right) =\mathcal{R}\left( g\right) A\mathcal{R}%
\left( g\right) ^{\dagger};\mathcal{R}\left( g\right) \mathcal{R}\left(
g\right) ^{\dagger}=I_{F}.
\end{equation}

\item twisted adjoint 
\begin{equation}
\Pi \left( g\right) \left( A\right) =\mathcal{R}\left( g\right) A\mathcal{R}%
\left( g\right) ^{\#};\mathcal{R}\left( g\right) ^{\#}=\left(
\tsum\limits_{0\leq j\leq 2r}\mathcal{R}\left( g\right) \right)
^{\#}=\tsum\limits_{0\leq j\leq 2r}\left( \mathcal{R}\left( g\right) \right)
_{j}^{\#}=\tsum\limits_{0\leq j\leq 2r}\left( -1\right) ^{j}\left( \mathcal{R%
}\left( g\right) \right) _{j}.
\end{equation}

\item antiunitary
\end{itemize}

$\mathcal{F}$ can be decomposed into a direct sum of subspaces invariant to
the action of $O\left( 2r,\mathbb{C}\right) ,O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}\right)
,\ldots,etc.$ In particular to subgroups of these groups. E.g. $SO\left( 3,%
\mathbb{R}\right) $ and $SU\left( 2\right) .$ These groups act in $\mathcal{F%
}_{1}$ thus their action on $\mathcal{F}$ is induced by 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{F}=\tbigwedge \left( \mathcal{F}_{1}\right) =\tbigwedge \left(
\tbigoplus \limits_{1\leq j\leq\nu}\mathcal{F}_{1j}\right) =\tbigwedge
\left( \mathcal{F}_{11}\right) \otimes\cdots\otimes\tbigwedge \left( 
\mathcal{F}_{1\nu}\right) ,
\end{equation}
where $\left\{ \left. \mathcal{F}_{1j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq\nu\right\} $
are invariant subspaces, which are sums of irreducible subspaces, of the
relevant groups.

[Could invariant subspaces define particles/regions/fields that lead to
multiparticle/multifield/multiparameter description?.]

$\lceil O\left( 2r,\mathbb{C}\right) $ and $O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}\right) $
act irreducibly on $\left\{ \left. \mathcal{F}_{n}\right\vert 0\leq n\leq
2r\right\} $ in a unitary fashion i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\Pi \left( O\left( 2r,\mathbb{C}\right) \right) \left( \mathcal{F}%
_{n}\right) =\mathcal{F}_{n},
\end{equation}%
while $\Pi \left( U\left( r,\mathbb{R}\right) \right) $ is reducible 
\begin{align}
& \Pi \left( U\left( r,\mathbb{R}\right) \right) \left( \mathcal{F}%
_{n}\oplus i\mathcal{F}_{n}\right) =\left( \mathcal{F}_{n}\oplus i\mathcal{F}%
_{n}\right) =\Pi \left( U\left( r,\mathbb{R}\right) \right) \left( \mathcal{F%
}_{n0}\right) =\mathcal{F}_{n0} \\
& \Pi \left( U\left( r,\mathbb{R}\right) \right) \left( \mathcal{F}%
_{n}\ominus i\mathcal{F}_{n}\right) =\left( \mathcal{F}_{n}\ominus i\mathcal{%
F}_{n}\right) =\Pi \left( U\left( r,\mathbb{R}\right) \right) \left( 
\mathcal{F}_{0n}\right) =\mathcal{F}_{0n} \\
& \mathcal{F}_{n}=\left( \mathcal{F}_{n}\oplus i\mathcal{F}_{n}\right)
\oplus \left( \mathcal{F}_{n}\ominus i\mathcal{F}_{n}\right) =\mathcal{F}%
_{n0}\oplus \mathcal{F}_{0n}\equiv \mathcal{F}_{n0}\oplus \overline{\mathcal{%
F}_{n0}}\equiv \mathcal{F}_{n0}\oplus \mathcal{F}_{n0}^{\dagger }
\end{align}%
\begin{align}
\mathcal{F}_{10}& =\left\{ \left. x_{j}+ix_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq
r\right\} \\
\mathcal{F}_{01}& =\left\{ \left. x_{j}-ix_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq
r\right\}
\end{align}%
\begin{align}
\Pi \left( Gl\left( r,\mathbb{C}\right) \right) \left( \mathcal{F}_{n}\oplus
i\mathcal{F}_{n}\right) & =\left( \mathcal{F}_{n}\oplus i\mathcal{F}%
_{n}\right) =\Pi \left( Gl\left( r,\mathbb{C}\right) \right) \left( \mathcal{%
F}_{n0}\right) =\mathcal{F}_{n0} \\
\Pi \left( Gl\left( r,\mathbb{C}\right) \right) \left( \mathcal{F}%
_{n}\ominus i\mathcal{F}_{n}\right) & =\left( \mathcal{F}_{n}\ominus i%
\mathcal{F}_{n}\right) =\Pi \left( Gl\left( r,\mathbb{C}\right) \right)
\left( \mathcal{F}_{0n}\right) =\mathcal{F}_{0n}
\end{align}%
$\mathcal{F}_{n}$ is real space under real linear combinations, and complex
under complex linear combinations%
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{F}_{n}=\mathcal{F}_{n}\otimes \mathbb{C}=\mathcal{F}_{n}\oplus i%
\mathcal{F}_{n}
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
V=\left( V\oplus \overline{V}\right) \oplus i\left( V\ominus \overline{V}%
\right)
\end{equation}%
The set 
\begin{equation}
\left\{ v-\sigma _{J}\left( v\right) \right\} \neq V\oplus \sigma _{J}\left(
V\right)
\end{equation}

We can define a conjugation $\sigma _{b}$ by 
\begin{align}
\sigma _{b}& :\mathcal{F}_{1}\rightarrow \mathcal{F}_{1} \\
\sigma _{b}\left( b_{j}\right) & =b_{j}^{\dagger };\,\sigma _{b}\left(
b_{j}^{\dagger }\right) =b_{j};1\leq j\leq r
\end{align}

$\rfloor$

\subsubsection{Well Defined GNS Representations}

A transformation $T\in\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ induces a
transformation belonging to $\mathcal{B}\left( \Phi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}%
\right) \right) $ if 
\begin{equation}
T:\ker\Phi_{f}\rightarrow\ker\Phi_{f}
\end{equation}
i.e. it produces a \emph{well defined operator} $\Pi_{f}\left( T\right) .$

The GNS representation of $\widehat{u}$, $\Pi _{f}\left( \widehat{u}\right)
, $ is well defined if 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{u}:\ker \Phi _{f}\rightarrow \ker \Phi _{f}
\end{equation}%
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
f\left( B^{\dagger }B\right) =f\left( uA^{\dagger }u^{\dagger }uAu^{\dagger
}\right) =f\left( uA^{\dagger }Au^{\dagger }\right) =0\Leftrightarrow
f\left( A^{\dagger }A\right) =0;A,B\in \ker \Phi _{f},
\end{equation}%
where 
\begin{equation}
B=\widehat{u}\left( A\right) .
\end{equation}%
Thus if the state $f$ is normal and the algebra $\mathcal{A}$ has a trace 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ uA^{\dagger }Au^{\dagger }D_{f}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ A^{\dagger }Au^{\dagger }D_{f}u\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
A^{\dagger }AD_{f}\right\} =0.
\end{equation}%
Hence one can see that a \emph{sufficient condition }for $\Pi _{f}\left( 
\widehat{u}\right) $ and its infinitessimal generator $\Pi _{f}\left( 
\widehat{h}\right) $ to be well defined is that $\widehat{u}$ and $\widehat{u%
}^{\dagger }$ are symmetries of $D_{f}$ i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{u}\left( D_{f}\right) ^{\dagger }=u^{\dagger
}D_{f}u=uD_{f}u^{\dagger }=D_{f},
\end{equation}%
which is equivalent to 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{h}\left( D_{f}\right) =\left[ h,D_{f}\right] =0.
\end{equation}%
\emph{Neccesary conditions }for well definedness of $\Pi _{f}\left( \widehat{%
u}\right) $ and $\Pi _{f}\left( \widehat{h}\right) $ are given by 
\begin{equation}
f\left( \left[ h,X\right] ^{\dagger }\left[ h,X\right] \right) =0\text{ if }%
f\left( X^{\dagger }X\right) =0
\end{equation}%
and thus 
\begin{equation}
f\left( \left[ h,X\right] ^{\dagger }\right) =f\left( \left[ h,X\right]
\right) =0.
\end{equation}%
The condition 
\begin{equation}
f\left( \left[ h,X\right] ^{\dagger }\left[ h,X\right] \right) =0\text{ }
\end{equation}%
can be further developed to give%
\begin{equation}
f\left( \left[ h,X\right] ^{\dagger }\left[ h,X\right] \right) =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ \left[ h,X\right] ^{\dagger }\left[ h,X\right] Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger
}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q_{f}^{\dagger }\left[ h,X\right] ^{\dagger }%
\left[ h,X\right] Q_{f}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left( \left[ h,X\right]
Q_{f}\right) ^{\dagger }\left( \left[ h,X\right] Q_{f}\right) \right\} =0,
\end{equation}%
which gives 
\begin{equation}
\left[ h,X\right] Q_{f}=0=hXQ_{f}-XhQ_{f}\Rightarrow hXQ_{f}=XhQ_{f}
\end{equation}%
and thus 
\begin{equation}
\left[ h,X\right] Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger }=\left[ h,X\right] D_{f}=0\quad
\forall X\in \ker \Phi _{f}.
\end{equation}%
The condition 
\begin{equation}
f\left( \left[ h,X\right] \right) =0
\end{equation}%
can be further developed to give

\begin{align}
f\left( \left[ h,X\right] \right) & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left[ h,X\right]
D_{f}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left( hX-Xh\right) D_{f}\right\} =%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left( hXD_{f}-XhD_{f}\right) \right\} \\
& =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left( XD_{f}h-XhD_{f}\right) \right\} =-\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ X\left[ h,D_{f}\right] \right\} =0\quad\forall X\in\ker\Phi_{f}.
\end{align}

\subsubsection{Time Evolution}

The time evolution can be described and characterized by 
\begin{align}
D_{Q}\left( t\right) & =\widehat{u}\left( t\right) \left( D\right) =\widehat{%
u}\left( t\right) \left( QQ^{\dagger}\right) =Q\left( t\right) Q\left(
t\right) ^{\dagger} \\
-i\frac{d}{dt}\left\{ Q\left( t\right) Q\left( t\right) ^{\dagger }\right\}
& =\left( -i\frac{d}{dt}Q\left( t\right) \right) Q\left( t\right)
^{\dagger}+Q\left( t\right) \left( -i\frac{d}{dt}Q\left( t\right)
^{\dagger}\right) \\
& =\left[ H,Q\left( t\right) \right] Q\left( t\right) ^{\dagger }+Q\left(
t\right) \left( \left[ H,Q\left( t\right) ^{\dagger}\right] \right) \\
& =\left[ H,Q\left( t\right) Q\left( t\right) ^{\dagger}\right] .
\end{align}

$D_{Q}\left( t\right) $ is a stationary state if%
\begin{equation}
-i\frac{d}{dt}\left\{ D_{Q}\left( t\right) \right\} =\left[ H,D_{Q}\left(
t\right) \right] =0\text{ i.e. }D_{Q}\left( t\right) =D_{Q}\quad \forall t.
\end{equation}%
The operators $Q\left( t\right) $ and $Q\left( t\right) ^{\dagger }$ are
called \emph{amplitudes} of $D_{Q}\left( t\right) $ and if they are eigen
operators of $\widehat{H}$ i.e. 
\begin{gather}
\widehat{H}\left( Q\left( t\right) \right) =\left[ H,Q\left( t\right) \right]
=EQ\left( t\right) \\
\Updownarrow \\
\widehat{H}\left( Q^{\dagger }\left( t\right) \right) =\left[ H,Q\left(
t\right) ^{\dagger }\right] =-EQ\left( t\right) ^{\dagger }
\end{gather}%
they are \emph{stationary operator amplitudes} of $\widehat{H}$ i.e. 
\begin{align}
\widehat{u}\left( t\right) \left( Q\right) & =e^{\text{-}iEt}Q \\
\widehat{u}\left( t\right) \left( Q^{\dagger }\right) & =e^{iEt}Q^{\dagger },
\end{align}%
which implies that $D_{Q}$ and $D_{Q^{\dagger }}$ are stationary states of $%
\widehat{H}.$ As $Q$ and $Q^{\dagger }$ are eigen operators of the self
adjoint (super) operator $\widehat{H}$ associated with different eigen
values one has the orthogonality relationships 
\begin{align}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ QQ\right\} & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q^{\dagger }Q^{\dagger
}\right\} =0 \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q\left[ H,Q\right] \right\} & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
Q^{\dagger }\left[ H,Q^{\dagger }\right] \right\} =0 \\
& \Updownarrow \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ QHQ-QQH\right\} & =0\Longleftrightarrow \limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ QHQ\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ QQH\right\} \text{ (which is
trivial and always true)}.
\end{align}%
A representation $\Pi _{f}$ is faithful if 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{f}\left( A\right) =0\Longleftrightarrow A=0.
\end{equation}%
If a time evolution is defined by 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{u}\left( t\right) =\exp \left\{ -i\widehat{H}t\right\}
\end{equation}%
then $\Pi _{f}\left( \widehat{u}\right) $ is well defined if 
\begin{equation}
\left[ H,D_{f}\right] =0,
\end{equation}%
i.e. $D_{f}$ is a stationary state of the Hamiltonian $\widehat{H},$ and its
action on $\Phi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ is given by 
\begin{align}
\Pi _{f}\left( \widehat{H}\right) \Phi _{f}\left( A\right) & =\Phi
_{f}\left( \left[ H,A\right] \right) \\
\Pi _{f}\left( \widehat{u}\left( t\right) \right) \Phi _{f}\left( A\right) &
=\Phi _{f}\left( u\left( t\right) Au\left( t\right) ^{\dagger }\right)
\,\forall A\in \mathcal{A}.
\end{align}%
In particular 
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f}\left( \left[ H,D_{f}\right] \right) =0.
\end{equation}%
The inner product in $\Phi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ is given by 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \Phi _{f}\left( A\right) \left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( B\right)
\right. \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{f}}=\limfunc{tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger
}BD\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger }BQQ^{\dagger }\right\} ,
\end{equation}%
where 
\begin{equation}
D_{Q}=QQ^{\dagger }.
\end{equation}%
Then 
\begin{align}
\Pi _{f}\left( \widehat{H}\right) \Phi _{f}\left( Q\right) & =\Phi
_{f}\left( \left[ H,Q\right] \right) =E\Phi _{f}\left( Q\right) \\
\Pi _{f}\left( \widehat{u}\left( t\right) \right) \Phi _{f}\left( Q\right) &
=\Phi _{f}\left( u\left( t\right) Qu\left( t\right) ^{\dagger }\right)
=e^{-iEt}\Phi _{f}\left( Q\right) .
\end{align}%
From the eigenvalue equations and the orthogonality of $Q^{\dagger }$ and $Q$
one has 
\begin{align}
\left\langle \Phi _{f}\left( Q\right) \left\vert \Pi _{f}\left( \widehat{H}%
\right) \Phi _{f}\left( Q\right) \right. \right\rangle _{Q}& =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ Q^{\dagger }\left[ H,Q\right] QQ^{\dagger }\right\} =E\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ Q^{\dagger }QQQ^{\dagger }\right\} \\
\left\langle \Phi _{f}\left( Q^{\dagger }\right) \left\vert \Pi _{f}\left( 
\widehat{H}\right) \Phi _{f}\left( Q^{\dagger }\right) \right. \right\rangle
_{Q}& =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q\left[ H,Q^{\dagger }\right] QQ^{\dagger
}\right\} =-E\limfunc{tr}\left\{ QQ^{\dagger }QQ^{\dagger }\right\}
\end{align}%
\begin{align}
\left\langle \Phi _{f}\left( Q\right) \left\vert \Pi _{f}\left( \widehat{H}%
\right) \Phi _{f}\left( Q\right) \right. \right\rangle _{Q^{\dagger }}& =%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q^{\dagger }\left[ H,Q\right] Q^{\dagger }Q\right\} =E%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q^{\dagger }QQ^{\dagger }Q\right\} \\
\left\langle \Phi _{f}\left( Q^{\dagger }\right) \left\vert \Pi _{f}\left( 
\widehat{H}\right) \Phi _{f}\left( Q^{\dagger }\right) \right. \right\rangle
_{Q^{\dagger }}& =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q\left[ H,Q^{\dagger }\right]
Q^{\dagger }Q\right\} =-E\limfunc{tr}\left\{ QQ^{\dagger }Q^{\dagger
}Q\right\}
\end{align}%
and%
\begin{align}
\left\langle \Phi _{f}\left( Q^{\dagger }\right) \left\vert \Pi _{f}\left( 
\widehat{H}\right) \Phi _{f}\left( Q\right) \right. \right\rangle _{Q}& =%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q\left[ H,Q\right] QQ^{\dagger }\right\} \\
& =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q\left( HQ-QH\right) QQ^{\dagger }\right\} =E\limfunc{%
tr}\left\{ QQQQ^{\dagger }\right\} =0 \\
& \Rightarrow \limfunc{tr}\left\{ HQQQ^{\dagger }Q\right\} =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ HQQ^{\dagger }QQ\right\} \\
\left\langle \Phi _{f}\left( Q\right) \left\vert \Pi _{f}\left( \widehat{H}%
\right) \Phi _{f}\left( Q^{\dagger }\right) \right. \right\rangle _{Q}& =%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q^{\dagger }\left[ H,Q^{\dagger }\right] QQ^{\dagger
}\right\} \\
& =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q^{\dagger }\left( HQ^{\dagger }-Q^{\dagger }H\right)
QQ^{\dagger }\right\} =-E\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q^{\dagger }Q^{\dagger
}QQ^{\dagger }\right\} =0 \\
& \Rightarrow \limfunc{tr}\left\{ HQ^{\dagger }QQ^{\dagger }Q^{\dagger
}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ HQQ^{\dagger }Q^{\dagger }Q^{\dagger }\right\}
\end{align}%
\begin{align}
\left\langle \Phi _{f}\left( Q^{\dagger }\right) \left\vert \Pi _{f}\left( 
\widehat{H}\right) \Phi _{f}\left( Q\right) \right. \right\rangle
_{Q^{\dagger }}& =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q\left[ H,Q\right] Q^{\dagger
}Q\right\} \\
& =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q\left( HQ-QH\right) Q^{\dagger }Q\right\} =E\limfunc{%
tr}\left\{ QQQ^{\dagger }Q\right\} =0 \\
& \Rightarrow \limfunc{tr}\left\{ HQQ^{\dagger }QQ\right\} =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ HQ^{\dagger }QQQ\right\} \\
\left\langle \Phi _{f}\left( Q\right) \left\vert \Pi _{f}\left( \widehat{H}%
\right) \Phi _{f}\left( Q^{\dagger }\right) \right. \right\rangle
_{Q^{\dagger }}& =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q^{\dagger }\left[ H,Q^{\dagger }%
\right] Q^{\dagger }Q\right\} \\
& =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q^{\dagger }\left( HQ^{\dagger }-Q^{\dagger }H\right)
Q^{\dagger }Q\right\} =-E\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q^{\dagger }Q^{\dagger
}Q^{\dagger }Q\right\} =0 \\
& \Rightarrow \limfunc{tr}\left\{ HQ^{\dagger }Q^{\dagger }QQ^{\dagger
}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ HQ^{\dagger }QQ^{\dagger }Q^{\dagger
}\right\} .
\end{align}

$\lceil$%
\begin{align}
\left[ H,Q\left( t\right) \right] Q\left( t\right) ^{\dagger}+Q\left(
t\right) \left[ H,Q\left( t\right) ^{\dagger}\right] & =\left( HQ\left(
t\right) -Q\left( t\right) H\right) Q\left( t\right) ^{\dagger}+Q\left(
t\right) \left( HQ\left( t\right) ^{\dagger}-Q\left( t\right)
^{\dagger}H\right) \\
& =HQ\left( t\right) Q\left( t\right) ^{\dagger}-Q\left( t\right) HQ\left(
t\right) ^{\dagger}+Q\left( t\right) HQ\left( t\right) ^{\dagger}-Q\left(
t\right) Q\left( t\right) ^{\dagger}H \\
& =HQ\left( t\right) Q\left( t\right) ^{\dagger}-Q\left( t\right) Q\left(
t\right) ^{\dagger}H.
\end{align}
$\rfloor$

$\lceil$%
\begin{align}
\frac{d}{dt}\left( e^{At}Xe^{-At}e^{At}Ye^{-At}\right) & =\frac{d\left(
e^{At}Xe^{-At}\right) }{dt}e^{At}Ye^{-At}+e^{At}Xe^{-At}\frac{d\left(
e^{At}Ye^{-At}\right) }{dt} \\
& =\left[ A,X\left( t\right) \right] e^{At}Ye^{-At}+e^{At}Xe^{-At}\left[
A,Y\left( t\right) \right] \\
& =\left[ A,X\left( t\right) \right] Y\left( t\right) +X\left( t\right) %
\left[ A,Y\left( t\right) \right] \\
& =\left( AX\left( t\right) -X\left( t\right) A\right) Y\left( t\right)
+X\left( t\right) \left( AY\left( t\right) -Y\left( t\right) A\right) \\
& =AX\left( t\right) Y\left( t\right) -X\left( t\right) AY\left( t\right)
+X\left( t\right) AY\left( t\right) -X\left( t\right) Y\left( t\right) A \\
& =AX\left( t\right) Y\left( t\right) -X\left( t\right) Y\left( t\right) A=%
\frac{d}{dt}\left( e^{At}XYe^{-At}\right) .
\end{align}

$\rfloor$

Note that $D_{Q}$ and $D_{Q^{\dagger}}$ are eigen vectors of $\widehat{H}$
with eigen values zero.

\subsection{Gauge Group}

The number operator associated with the basis $\mathbf{x}$ is given by 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{N}_{x}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}n_{j}=\mathcal{N}_{x0}+\mathcal{N}_{x2}=%
\frac{r}{2}I_{\mathcal{F}}+i\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}x_{j}x_{j+r}.
\end{equation}%
leading to 
\begin{align}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( \mathcal{N}_{x}\right)
^{\dagger }\frac{1}{2^{2r}}\Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}\right)
\right\} & =\frac{1}{2^{2r}}\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
}\left( \mathcal{N}_{x}D_{f}\right) \right\} =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left( 
\mathcal{N}_{x}D_{f}\right) =\left\langle \left\langle \mathcal{N}%
_{x}D_{f}\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0} \\
& =\frac{1}{2^{2r}}\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left(
\left( \frac{r}{2}I_{\mathcal{F}}+i\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}x_{j}x_{j+r}\right)
D_{f}\right) \right\} \\
& =\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( \left( \frac{r}{2^{2r}%
}I_{\mathcal{F}}+\frac{i}{2^{2r}}\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}x_{j}x_{j+r}\right)
D_{f}\right) \right\} .
\end{align}

The \emph{Gauge} group, $G_{N},$ is defined by its realization 
\begin{equation}
u\left( \theta \right) =e^{i\mathcal{N}_{x}\theta }=\exp \left\{ i\left( 
\frac{r}{2}I_{\mathcal{F}}+i\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}x_{j}x_{j+r}\right) \theta
\right\} ,\theta \in \mathbb{R}
\end{equation}%
and its action on $\mathcal{F}$ is given by 
\begin{equation}
Adj\left( u\left( \theta \right) \right) \left( \cdot \right) =\widehat{%
u\left( \theta \right) }\left( \cdot \right) =\exp \left\{ adj\left( 
\mathcal{N}_{x}\right) \theta \right\} \left( \cdot \right) =\exp \left\{ %
\left[ \mathcal{N}_{x},\cdot \right] \theta \right\} =\exp \left\{ \widehat{%
\mathcal{N}_{x}}\theta \right\} \left( \cdot \right) ,
\end{equation}%
i.e. 
\begin{eqnarray}
\widehat{u\left( \theta \right) }\left( A\right) &=&e^{i\mathcal{N}%
_{x}\theta }Ae^{-i\mathcal{N}_{x}\theta }=\exp \left\{ i\sum_{1\leq j\leq
r}x_{j}x_{j+r}\right\} A\exp \left\{ -i\sum_{1\leq j\leq
r}x_{j}x_{j+r}\right\} \\
&=&\exp \left\{ i\left[ \mathcal{N}_{x},A\right] \theta \right\} =\exp
\left\{ i\left[ \mathcal{N}_{xmaj},A\right] \theta \right\} ,
\end{eqnarray}%
so the generator of $\widehat{u\left( \theta \right) }$ is not unique and
can be in particular either $\widehat{\mathcal{N}_{x}}$ or $\widehat{%
\mathcal{N}_{x}}_{maj}.$

As 
\begin{equation}
\left[ x_{j}x_{k},x_{l}x_{m}\right] =0,\left\{ jk\right\} \neq \left\{
lm\right\} ,
\end{equation}%
but 
\begin{equation}
\left[ x_{j}x_{k},x_{l}x_{m}\right] \neq 0,\left\{ jk\right\} \cap \left\{
lm\right\} =\left\{ j\right\} \text{ or }\left\{ k\right\} ,
\end{equation}%
thus in general 
\begin{equation}
\left[ \mathcal{N}_{x},A\right] =\left[ \mathcal{N}_{xmaj},A\right] \neq 0
\end{equation}%
and 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{u\left( \theta \right) }\left( A\right) \neq A.
\end{equation}%
$\mathcal{F}_{2}$ decomposes into irreducible subspaces of $\widehat{%
\mathcal{N}_{x}}$, $\widehat{\mathcal{N}_{xmaj}}$ and $\widehat{u\left(
\theta \right) }$.

\subsubsection{Properties of the Number Operator}

1. One should observe%
\begin{equation}
\exp \left\{ 2\pi i\mathcal{N}_{x}\right\} =I_{\mathcal{F}},
\end{equation}%
as 
\begin{align}
\exp \left\{ 2\pi i\mathcal{N}_{x}\right\} \left\vert \Psi \right\rangle &
=\exp \left\{ 2\pi i\mathcal{N}_{x}\right\} \sum_{0\leq N\leq \infty
}\left\vert \Psi _{N}\right\rangle =\sum_{0\leq n\leq \infty }e^{2\pi
iN}\left\vert \Psi _{N}\right\rangle =\sum_{0\leq N\leq \infty }\left\{ \cos
\left( 2\pi N\right) +i\sin \left( 2\pi N\right) \right\} \left\vert \Psi
_{N}\right\rangle \\
& =\sum_{0\leq N\leq \infty }\left\{ \cos \left( 2\pi N\right) \right\}
\left\vert \Psi _{N}\right\rangle =\sum_{0\leq N\leq \infty }\left\vert \Psi
_{N}\right\rangle =\left\vert \Psi \right\rangle ,
\end{align}%
even though in general 
\begin{equation}
\exp \left\{ i\mathcal{N}_{x}\theta \right\} \left\vert \Psi \right\rangle
\neq \left\vert \Psi \right\rangle .
\end{equation}

2. The projector onto a $N_{x}-$particle subspace is given by 
\begin{equation}
P_{N_{x}}=\tint\nolimits_{0}^{1}e^{2\pi it\left( \mathcal{N}_{x}-N_{x}I_{%
\mathcal{F}}\right) }dt.
\end{equation}

A complexification $\widehat{J_{xa}}=-i\widehat{\mathcal{N}_{x}}_{a}$ is
given by the adjoint action of $-i\mathcal{N}_{xa}$ on 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{F}_{1}=\mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\oplus \mathbf{a=x}_{1}\oplus \mathbf{x}%
_{2}
\end{equation}%
given by%
\begin{align}
\widehat{J_{xa}}\left( \mathbf{x}_{1}\right) & =\left[ J_{a},\mathbf{x}_{1}%
\right] =-\mathbf{x}_{2} \\
\widehat{J_{xa}}\left( \mathbf{x}_{2}\right) & =\left[ J_{a},\mathbf{x}_{2}%
\right] =\mathbf{x}_{1}
\end{align}
\begin{align}
\widehat{J_{xa}}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\right) & =\left[ J_{a},\mathbf{a%
}^{\dagger }\right] =\mathbf{a}^{\dagger } \\
\widehat{J_{xa}}\left( \mathbf{a}\right) & =\left[ J_{a},\mathbf{a}\right] =-%
\mathbf{a}
\end{align}%
$\lceil $%
\begin{eqnarray}
\widehat{J_{xa}}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\right) &=&\widehat{J_{xa}}%
\left( \mathbf{x}_{1}+i\mathbf{x}_{2}\right) =-\mathbf{x}_{2}+i\mathbf{x}%
_{1}=i\left( \mathbf{x}_{1}+i\mathbf{x}_{2}\right) =i\mathbf{a}^{\dagger } \\
\widehat{J_{xa}}\left( \mathbf{a}\right) &=&\widehat{J_{xa}}\left( \mathbf{x}%
_{1}-i\mathbf{x}_{2}\right) =-\mathbf{x}_{2}-i\mathbf{x}_{1}=-i\left( 
\mathbf{x}_{1}-i\mathbf{x}_{2}\right) =-i\mathbf{a}
\end{eqnarray}%
$\rfloor $

and 
\begin{equation}
\exp \left\{ \theta \widehat{J_{xa}}\right\} \left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger
}\right) =e^{i\theta }\mathbf{a}^{\dagger }.
\end{equation}

\section{Clifford Norms}

By construction and definition of the CAR/Clifford algebra 
\begin{align}
\limfunc{Trace}\left\{ A\right\} & =\left\langle A\right\rangle _{0}=\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}\left( A\right) I_{\mathcal{F}} \\
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left( A\right) & \equiv\left\langle \left\langle
A\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0}\in\mathbb{C} \\
\limfunc{Trace}\left\{ I_{\mathcal{F}}\right\} & =\left\langle I_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right\rangle _{0}=I_{\mathcal{F}} \\
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) & =1
\end{align}
and one can define 
\begin{align}
\left\langle \left. A\right\vert B\right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}} &
=\left\langle \left\langle A^{\dagger}B\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0} \\
\left( \left. A\right\vert B\right) _{\mathcal{F}} & =\left\langle
\left\langle A^{t}B\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0} \\
\left\Vert A\right\Vert _{\mathcal{HS}} & =\left( \left\langle \left.
A\right\vert A\right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}}\right) ^{\frac{1}{2}} \\
\left\Vert A\right\Vert _{\mathcal{TC}} & =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{
\left( A^{\dagger}A\right) ^{\frac{1}{2}}\right\} .
\end{align}

On the Von Neumann closure one has 
\begin{align}
\limfunc{Trace}& :\mathcal{F}\longrightarrow \mathcal{F} \\
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}& :\mathcal{F}\longrightarrow \left[ -\infty ,\infty %
\right] .
\end{align}

\emph{The trace is zero on} 
\begin{equation}
\dbigoplus\limits_{1\leq n\leq 2r}\mathcal{F}_{n}
\end{equation}%
as 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \sum_{1\leq n\leq 2r}A_{n}\right\rangle _{0}=0.
\end{equation}%
The subspaces $\left\{ \mathcal{F}_{n}\right\} $ are orthogonal wrt $%
\left\langle \left. A\right\vert B\right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}}$ i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{F}_{n}\bot \mathcal{F}_{m};0\leq n\neq m\leq 2r,
\end{equation}%
as the \emph{geometric}$\equiv $\emph{Clifford} product between two general
elements $A^{\dagger }$ and $B$ is defined as 
\begin{equation}
A^{\dagger }B=\sum_{0\leq m,n\leq 2r}A_{m}^{\dagger }B_{n},
\end{equation}%
where 
\begin{equation}
A_{m}^{\dagger }B_{n}=\sum_{j=0}^{\min \left( m,n\right) }\left\langle
A_{m}^{\dagger }B_{n}\right\rangle _{\left\vert m-n\right\vert +2j},
\end{equation}%
which can never have a zeroth component if $m\neq n$ giving that 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle A_{m}^{\dagger }B_{n}\right\rangle _{0}=0,\,m\neq n,
\end{equation}%
leading to 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle A^{\dagger }B\right\rangle _{0}=\sum_{0\leq m\leq
2r}\left\langle A_{m}^{\dagger }B_{m}\right\rangle _{0},
\end{equation}%
in particular (\emph{for normalized basis})%
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left\langle A_{1}B_{1}\right\rangle \right\rangle
_{0}=\left\langle \left\langle \sum_{1\leq j,k\leq
2r}A_{1j}B_{1k}x_{j}x_{k}\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0}=\left\langle
\left\langle \sum_{1\leq j\leq 2r}A_{1j}B_{1j}x_{j}^{2}\right\rangle
\right\rangle _{0}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq 2r}A_{1j}B_{1j}.
\end{equation}%
Let 
\begin{align}
A_{p}& =a_{1}\wedge \cdots \wedge a_{p} \\
B_{p}& =b_{1}\wedge \cdots \wedge b_{p}
\end{align}%
then 
\begin{align}
\left\langle \left. A_{p}\right\vert B_{p}\right\rangle & =\left( -1\right)
^{\frac{p\left( p-1\right) }{2}}\left\langle \left. a_{p}\wedge \cdots
\wedge a_{1}\right\vert b_{1}\wedge \cdots \wedge b_{p}\right\rangle =\left(
-1\right) \left\langle ^{\frac{p\left( p-1\right) }{2}}\left\langle \left(
a_{p}\wedge \cdots \wedge a_{1}\right) \left( b_{1}\wedge \cdots \wedge
b_{p}\right) \right\rangle \right\rangle _{0} \\
& =\left( -1\right) ^{\frac{p\left( p-1\right) }{2}}\sum_{\substack{ 1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots <j_{p}\leq 2r  \\ 1\leq k_{1}<\cdots <k_{p}\leq 2r}}%
\left\langle \left\langle a_{pj_{1}}\cdots a_{1j_{1}}x_{j_{p}}\wedge \cdots
\wedge x_{j_{1}}b_{pk_{p}}\cdots b_{1k_{1}}x_{k_{1}}\wedge \cdots \wedge
x_{k_{p}}\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0} \\
& =\left( -1\right) ^{\frac{p\left( p-1\right) }{2}}\sum_{\substack{ 1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots <j_{p}\leq 2r  \\ 1\leq k_{1}<\cdots <k_{p}\leq 2r}}\left\vert 
\begin{array}{ccc}
\left( \left. a_{j_{1}}\right\vert b_{k_{1}}\right) & \cdots & \left( \left.
a_{j_{1}}\right\vert b_{k_{p}}\right) \\ 
\vdots & \vdots & \vdots \\ 
\left( \left. a_{j_{p}}\right\vert b_{k_{1}}\right) & \cdots & \left( \left.
a_{j_{p}}\right\vert b_{k_{p}}\right)%
\end{array}%
\right\vert .
\end{align}%
Note that 
\begin{align}
A_{m}^{\dagger }& =\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{m}\leq 2r}\overline{A}%
_{mj_{1}\cdots j_{m}}x_{j_{m}}\wedge \cdots \wedge x_{j_{1}} \\
B_{m}& =\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{m}\leq 2r}B_{mj_{1}\cdots
j_{m}}x_{j_{1}}\wedge \cdots \wedge x_{j_{m}}
\end{align}%
\begin{align}
\left\langle \left. A_{m}\right\vert B_{m}\right\rangle & =\sum_{\substack{ %
1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{m}\leq 2r  \\ 1\leq k_{1}<\cdots <k_{m}\leq 2r}}%
\overline{A}_{mj_{1}\cdots j_{m}}B_{mk_{1}\cdots k_{m}}\left\langle
\left\langle \left( x_{j_{m}}\wedge \cdots \wedge x_{j_{1}}\right) \left(
x_{k_{1}}\wedge \cdots \wedge x_{k_{m}}\right) \right\rangle \right\rangle
_{0} \\
& =\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{m}\leq 2r}\overline{A}_{mj_{1}\cdots
j_{m}}B_{mj_{1}\cdots j_{m}}
\end{align}%
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left\langle A^{\dagger }B\right\rangle \right\rangle
_{0}=\sum_{0\leq m\leq 2r}\left\langle \left\langle A_{m}^{\dagger
}B_{m}\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0}=\sum_{0\leq m\leq 2r}\sum_{1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots <j_{m}\leq 2r}\overline{A}_{mj_{1}\cdots j_{m}}B_{mj_{1}\cdots
j_{m}}.
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Inner and Scalar Products}

The Clifford inner and scalar products can be expressed in terms of the
trace as 
\begin{align}
\left\langle \left. A\right\vert B\right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}} &
=\left\langle A^{\dagger}B\right\rangle _{0}=\limfunc{Trace}\left\{
A^{\dagger}B\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger}B\right\} I_{\mathcal{F}%
}\in\mathcal{F}_{0} \\
\left( \left. A\right\vert B\right) _{\mathcal{F}} & =\left\langle
A^{t}B\right\rangle _{0}=\limfunc{Trace}\left\{ A^{t}B\right\} =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ A^{t}B\right\} I_{\mathcal{F}}\in\mathcal{F}_{0} \\
\left\langle \left. A\right\vert B\right\rangle & =\overline{\left\langle
\left. B\right\vert A\right\rangle }=\left\langle \left\langle A^{\dagger
}B\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0}=\limfunc{tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger }B\right\}
\in\mathbb{C} \\
\left( \left. A\right\vert B\right) & =\left( \left. B\right\vert A\right)
=\left\langle \left\langle A^{t}B\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0}=\limfunc{tr%
}\left\{ A^{t}B\right\} \in\mathbb{C}.
\end{align}
Other internal products in $\mathcal{F}$ can be defined in terms of these
preceeding products.

\subsection{States $f_{D}$}

States are positive normalized linear functionals defined on $\mathcal{F}$
and if the state is a \emph{normal }state it can be associated with a
density operator $D\in \mathcal{F}$ i.e. 
\begin{equation}
f_{D}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) =1=\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left( D\right)
=\left\langle \left\langle \left. I\right\vert D\right\rangle \right\rangle
_{0}.
\end{equation}%
We can always define $D$ by 
\begin{equation*}
D=\frac{QQ^{\dagger }}{\limfunc{tr}\left\{ QQ^{\dagger }\right\} }.
\end{equation*}

\subsection{Differentials and Directional Derrivatives}

A function $f$ between Banach spaces $X$ and $Y$ 
\begin{equation}
f:U\subset X\longrightarrow Y
\end{equation}%
is differentiable at $x\in U$ if $\exists $ a linear transformation $%
df\left( x\right) \in \mathcal{B}\left( X,Y\right) ,$ the differential $%
df\left( x\right) $ of $f$ at $x,$ such that for all $h\in X$ 
\begin{align}
f\left( x+h\right) -f\left( x\right) -df\left( x\right) & =o\left( h\right)
\\
o\left( h\right) & =\left\Vert h\right\Vert \psi \left( h\right) \\
\psi & :X\longrightarrow Y \\
\lim\limits_{h\rightarrow 0}\psi \left( h\right) & =0\text{ (Banach space
norms).}
\end{align}%
The directional derrivative $\frac{\partial }{\partial h}f\left( x\right) $
of $f$ at $x$ in the direction $h$ is given by 
\begin{align}
& \frac{\partial }{\partial h}f\left( x\right) =\lim\limits_{t\rightarrow
0}\left\{ \frac{f\left( x+th\right) -f\left( x\right) }{t}\right\} =\left. 
\frac{d}{dt}f\left( x+th\right) \right\vert _{t=0} \\
& \overset{\text{If }\emph{df}\left( x\right) \text{ exists}}{=}df\left(
x\right) \left( h\right) =\frac{\partial }{\partial h}f\left( x\right)
=\sum_{1\leq j\leq 2r}\frac{\partial f\left( x\right) }{\partial x_{j}}%
\mathit{h}_{j}\equiv \sum_{1\leq j\leq 2r}\mathit{h}_{j}\frac{\partial }{%
\partial x_{j}}f\left( x\right) \\
h& =\sum_{1\leq j\leq 2r}\mathit{h}_{j}x_{j};\left\{ \left. \mathit{h}%
_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq 2r\right\} \text{ are scalars.}
\end{align}%
The existance of $\left\{ \frac{\partial f\left( x\right) }{\partial h}%
\right\} $ for all $h$ does not imply that $df\left( x\right) $ exists.

The \emph{Dirac Operator}, $\mathfrak{D,}$is defined as 
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{D}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq 2r}x_{j}\frac{\partial }{\partial x_{j}}%
,x_{j}\in \mathcal{F}_{1}
\end{equation}%
where $\mathfrak{D}$ acts on functions $f$ 
\begin{equation}
f:\mathcal{F}\longrightarrow \mathcal{F},
\end{equation}%
so that 
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{D}f\left( x\right) =\sum_{1\leq j\leq 2r}x_{j}\frac{\partial }{%
\partial x_{j}}f\left( x\right) \overset{\text{If }\emph{df}\left( x\right) 
\text{ exists}}{=}\sum_{1\leq j\leq 2r}x_{j}df\left( x\right) \left(
x_{j}\right) \,;x_{j}\in \mathcal{F}_{1},x\in \mathcal{F}.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Expectation Values}

Density and quartic Hamiltonian operators can be expanded in terms of the
generators of $\mathcal{F}$ as 
\begin{equation}
D=I_{\mathcal{F}}+\sum_{1\leq j\leq 2^{2r}}D_{j}=I_{\mathcal{F}%
}+i\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<j_{2}\leq
2r}d_{2j_{1}j_{2}}x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}+\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<j_{2}<j_{3}<j_{4}\leq
2r}d_{4j_{1}j_{2}j_{3}j_{4}}x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}x_{j_{3}}x_{j_{4}}+\cdots
\end{equation}%
and 
\begin{equation}
H=\eta _{0}I_{\mathcal{F}}+i\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<j_{2}\leq 2r}\eta
_{2j_{1}j_{2}}x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}+\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<j_{2}<j_{3}<j_{4}\leq
2r}\eta _{4j_{1}j_{2}j_{3}j_{4}}x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}x_{j_{3}}x_{j_{4}},
\end{equation}%
where all the coefficients are real and one assumes a normalized trace. As $%
\widehat{H}$ generates adjoint time evolution the term $\eta _{0}I_{\mathcal{%
F}}$ is not relevant and thus $\eta _{0}$ can have any arbitary value.

The energy in a state $f_{D}$ is then given by 
\begin{align}
f_{D}\left( H\right) & =f_{D}\left( \eta _{0}I_{\mathcal{F}}+i\sum_{1\leq
j_{1}<j_{2}\leq 2r}\eta _{2j_{1}j_{2}}x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}+\sum_{1\leq
j_{1}<j_{2}<j_{3}<j_{4}\leq 2r}\eta
_{4j_{1}j_{2}j_{3}j_{4}}x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}x_{j_{3}}x_{j_{4}}\right)  \notag
\\
& =\eta _{0}+i\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<j_{2}\leq 2r}\eta _{2j_{1}j_{2}}f_{D}\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}\right) +\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<j_{2}<j_{3}<j_{4}\leq 2r}\eta
_{4j_{1}j_{2}j_{3}j_{4}}f_{D}\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}x_{j_{3}}x_{j_{4}}\right) ,
\end{align}%
whic can always be expressed in the GNS representation asssociated with the
state $f$ as 
\begin{equation}
f_{D}\left( H\right) =\left\langle \Phi _{f_{D}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) \left. \Pi _{f_{D}}\left( H\right) \right\vert \Phi _{f_{D}}\left(
I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right\rangle _{f_{D}}=\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi
_{f_{D}}\left( H\right) \left\vert \Phi _{f_{D}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{f_{D}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) \right\vert \right\}
\end{equation}%
or as 
\begin{align}
f_{D}\left( H\right) & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left\langle
H_{0}D_{0}\right\rangle _{0}\right\} +\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left\langle
H_{2}D_{2}\right\rangle _{0}\right\} +\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left\langle
H_{4}D_{4}\right\rangle _{0}\right\}  \notag \\
& =\eta _{0}+\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<j_{2}\leq 2r}\eta
_{2j_{1}j_{2}}d_{2j_{2}j_{1}}+\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<j_{2}<j_{3}<j_{4}\leq
2r}\eta _{4j_{1}j_{2}j_{3}j_{4}}d_{4j_{1}j_{2}j_{3}j_{4}},
\end{align}%
when $f_{D}$ is a normal state and $\limfunc{tr}$ exists.

$\lceil $The representation of $\Pi _{f_{D}}\left( H\right) $ in the \emph{%
possibly overcomplete basis} $\left\{ \Phi _{f_{D}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) ,\left\{ \left. \Phi _{f_{D}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots
x_{j_{n}}\right) \right\vert 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq 2r,1\leq n\leq
2r\right\} \right\} $ is given by the matrix 
\begin{equation}
\left\{ 
\begin{array}{c}
\left\langle \Phi _{f_{D}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{n}}\right) \left. \Pi
_{f_{D}}\left( H\right) \right\vert \Phi _{f_{D}}\left( x_{k_{1}}\cdots
x_{k_{m}}\right) \right\rangle _{f_{D}};1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq
2r,1\leq k_{1}<\cdots <k_{m}\leq 2r, \\ 
0\leq n\leq 2r,0\leq m\leq 2r%
\end{array}%
\right\} ,
\end{equation}
where the $n,m=0$ basis element is given by $\Phi _{f_{D}}\left( I_{\mathcal{%
F}}\right) .$ These matrix elements are given by 
\begin{gather}
\left\langle \Phi _{f_{D}}\left( x_{k_{1}}\cdots x_{k_{n}}\right) \left. \Pi
_{f_{D}}\left( H\right) \right\vert \Phi _{f_{D}}\left( x_{l_{1}}\cdots
x_{l_{m}}\right) \right\rangle _{f_{D}}=f_{D}\left( x_{k_{n}}\cdots
x_{k_{1}}Hx_{l_{1}}\cdots x_{l_{m}}\right) \\
=f_{D}\left( x_{k_{n}}\cdots x_{k_{1}}\left( \eta _{0}I_{\mathcal{F}%
}+i\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<j_{2}\leq 2r}\eta
_{2j_{1}j_{2}}x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}+\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<j_{2}<j_{3}<j_{4}\leq
2r}\eta _{4j_{1}j_{2}j_{3}j_{4}}x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}x_{j_{3}}x_{j_{4}}\right)
x_{l_{1}}\cdots x_{l_{m}}\right) \\
=\eta _{0}f_{D}\left( x_{k_{n}}\cdots x_{k_{1}}x_{l_{1}}\cdots
x_{l_{m}}\right) +i\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<j_{2}\leq 2r}\eta
_{2j_{1}j_{2}}f_{D}\left( x_{k_{n}}\cdots
x_{k_{1}}x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}x_{l_{1}}\cdots x_{l_{m}}\right) \\
+\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<j_{2}<j_{3}<j_{4}\leq 2r}\eta
_{4j_{1}j_{2}j_{3}j_{4}}f_{D}\left( x_{k_{n}}\cdots
x_{k_{1}}x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}x_{j_{3}}x_{j_{4}}x_{l_{1}}\cdots x_{l_{m}}\right)
\\
1\leq k_{1}<\cdots <k_{n}\leq 2r,1\leq l_{1}<\cdots <l_{m}\leq 2r,0\leq
n\leq 2r,0\leq m\leq 2r.
\end{gather}%
$\rfloor $

In the normal state case $D$ is positive and one has

\begin{align}
& D=QQ^{\dagger }=\sum_{0\leq m,n\leq 2r}Q_{m}Q_{n}^{\dagger }=\sum_{0\leq
m,n\leq 2r}\sum_{j=0}^{\min \left( m,n\right) }\left\langle
Q_{m}Q_{n}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{\left\vert m-n\right\vert +2j} \\
& \left\langle QQ^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{0}=I_{\mathcal{F}}\equiv
\left\langle \left\langle QQ^{\dagger }\right\rangle \right\rangle
_{0}=1\equiv \left\Vert Q_{0}\right\Vert _{HS}=1.
\end{align}%
As $H$ is gauge invariant one only needs to consider $D^{\prime }s$ that are
even, i.e. gauge invariant, then 
\begin{equation}
Q=\sum_{0\leq p\leq r}Q_{2p},
\end{equation}%
otherwise $D$ would contain terms like $Q_{0}Q_{2p+1}^{\dagger }$ etc.

$\lceil 2r=2$ 
\begin{align}
& D=QQ^{\dagger }=\left( Q_{0}+Q_{2}\right) \left( Q_{0}+Q_{2}\right)
^{\dagger } \\
& =Q_{0}Q_{0}^{\dagger }+\left\langle Q_{2}Q_{2}^{\dagger }\right\rangle
_{0}+Q_{0}Q_{2}^{\dagger }+Q_{2}Q_{0}^{\dagger }+\left\langle
Q_{2}Q_{2}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{2}
\end{align}%
$2r=4$ 
\begin{align}
& D=QQ^{\dagger }=\left( Q_{0}+Q_{2}+Q_{4}\right) \left(
Q_{0}+Q_{2}+Q_{4}\right) ^{\dagger } \\
& \left. =\right. Q_{0}Q_{0}^{\dagger }+\left\langle Q_{2}Q_{2}^{\dagger
}\right\rangle _{0}+\left\langle Q_{4}Q_{4}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{0} \\
& +Q_{0}Q_{2}^{\dagger }+Q_{2}Q_{0}^{\dagger }+\left\langle
Q_{2}Q_{4}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{2}+\left\langle Q_{4}Q_{2}^{\dagger
}\right\rangle _{2}+\left\langle Q_{2}Q_{2}^{\dagger }\right\rangle
_{2}+\left\langle Q_{4}Q_{4}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{2} \\
& +Q_{0}Q_{4}^{\dagger }+Q_{4}Q_{0}^{\dagger }+\left\langle
Q_{2}Q_{4}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{4}+\left\langle Q_{4}Q_{2}^{\dagger
}\right\rangle _{4}+\left\langle Q_{2}Q_{2}^{\dagger }\right\rangle
_{4}+\left\langle Q_{4}Q_{4}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{4}
\end{align}%
$2r=6$ 
\begin{align}
& D=QQ^{\dagger }=\left( Q_{0}+Q_{2}+Q_{4}+Q_{6}\right) \left(
Q_{0}+Q_{2}+Q_{4}+Q_{6}\right) ^{\dagger } \\
& \left. =\right. \left\langle Q_{0}Q_{0}^{\dagger }\right\rangle
_{0}+\left\langle Q_{2}Q_{2}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{0}+\left\langle
Q_{4}Q_{4}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{0}+\left\langle Q_{6}Q_{6}^{\dagger
}\right\rangle _{0} \\
& +\left\langle Q_{0}Q_{2}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{2}+\left\langle
Q_{2}Q_{0}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{2}+\left\langle Q_{2}Q_{2}^{\dagger
}\right\rangle _{2}+\left\langle Q_{2}Q_{4}^{\dagger }\right\rangle
_{2}+\left\langle Q_{4}Q_{2}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{2}+\left\langle
Q_{6}Q_{2}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{2}+\left\langle Q_{2}Q_{6}^{\dagger
}\right\rangle _{2} \\
& +\left\langle Q_{4}Q_{4}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{2}+\left\langle
Q_{6}Q_{4}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{2}+\left\langle Q_{4}Q_{6}^{\dagger
}\right\rangle _{2}+\left\langle Q_{6}Q_{6}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{2} \\
& +\left\langle Q_{0}Q_{4}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{4}+\left\langle
Q_{4}Q_{0}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{4}+\left\langle Q_{2}Q_{4}^{\dagger
}\right\rangle _{4}+\left\langle Q_{4}Q_{2}^{\dagger }\right\rangle
_{4}+\left\langle Q_{6}Q_{2}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{4}+\left\langle
Q_{2}Q_{6}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{4} \\
& +\left\langle Q_{6}Q_{4}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{4}+\left\langle
Q_{4}Q_{6}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{4}+\left\langle Q_{2}Q_{2}^{\dagger
}\right\rangle _{4}+\left\langle Q_{4}Q_{4}^{\dagger }\right\rangle
_{4}+\left\langle Q_{6}Q_{6}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{4} \\
& +\left\langle Q_{0}Q_{6}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{6}+\left\langle
Q_{6}Q_{0}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{6}+\left\langle Q_{2}Q_{4}^{\dagger
}\right\rangle _{6}+\left\langle Q_{4}Q_{2}^{\dagger }\right\rangle
_{6}+\left\langle Q_{6}Q_{2}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{6}+\left\langle
Q_{2}Q_{6}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{6}+\left\langle Q_{6}Q_{4}^{\dagger
}\right\rangle _{6} \\
& +\left\langle Q_{4}Q_{6}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{6}+\left\langle
Q_{4}Q_{4}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{6}+\left\langle Q_{6}Q_{6}^{\dagger
}\right\rangle _{6}
\end{align}%
Hence 
\begin{eqnarray}
QQ^{\dagger } &=&\sum_{0\leq p,q\leq 3}\left\langle Q_{2p}Q_{2q}^{\dagger
}\right\rangle _{0}+\sum_{0\leq p,q\leq 3}\left\langle Q_{2p}Q_{2q}^{\dagger
}\right\rangle _{2}+\sum_{0\leq p,q\leq 3}\left\langle Q_{2p}Q_{2q}^{\dagger
}\right\rangle _{4}+\sum_{0\leq p,q\leq 3}\left\langle Q_{2p}Q_{2q}^{\dagger
}\right\rangle _{6} \\
&=&\sum_{0\leq p,q\leq 3}\sum_{j=0}^{\min \left( p,q\right) }\left\langle
Q_{2p}Q_{2q}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{2\left\vert p-q\right\vert +2j}.
\end{eqnarray}%
$\rfloor $

Thus in general 
\begin{equation}
QQ^{\dagger }=\sum_{0\leq p,q\leq r}\sum_{j=0}^{\min \left( p,q\right)
}\left\langle Q_{2p}Q_{2q}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{2\left\vert
p-q\right\vert +2j}
\end{equation}%
and 
\begin{equation}
D=QQ^{\dagger }=\sum_{0\leq j\leq r}D_{2j}=\sum_{0\leq p,q\leq
r}\sum_{j=0}^{\min \left( p,q\right) }\left\langle Q_{2p}Q_{2q}^{\dagger
}\right\rangle _{2\left\vert p-q\right\vert +2j},
\end{equation}%
which gives 
\begin{equation}
D_{2j}=\sum_{\substack{ 0\leq p,q\leq r  \\ \left\vert p-q\right\vert \leq j 
}}\left\langle Q_{2p}Q_{2q}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{2j}.
\end{equation}%
In particular 
\begin{align}
D_{0}& =\sum_{\substack{ 0\leq p,q\leq r  \\ \left\vert p-q\right\vert \leq
0 }}\left\langle Q_{2p}Q_{2q}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{0}=\left\Vert
Q_{0}\right\Vert _{HS}^{2} \\
D_{2}& =\sum_{\substack{ 0\leq p,q\leq r  \\ \left\vert p-q\right\vert \leq
1 }}\left\langle Q_{2p}Q_{2q}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{2} \\
D_{4}& =\sum_{\substack{ 0\leq p,q\leq r  \\ \left\vert p-q\right\vert \leq
2 }}\left\langle Q_{2p}Q_{2q}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{4}.
\end{align}%
As $Q$ is normalized one has 
\begin{align}
\left\langle H\right\rangle _{D}& =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ HD\right\} =\limfunc{%
tr}\left\{ Q^{\dagger }HQ\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ HQQ^{\dagger
}\right\} =\left\langle \left\langle HQQ^{\dagger }\right\rangle
\right\rangle _{0}=\left\langle \left\langle Q^{\dagger }HQ\right\rangle
\right\rangle _{0}=\left\langle Q\left\vert HQ\right. \right\rangle _{%
\mathcal{F}} \\
& \neq \left\langle \widehat{H}\right\rangle _{D}=\limfunc{tr}\left\{ 
\widehat{H}\left( Q\right) Q^{\dagger }\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left(
HQ-QH\right) Q^{\dagger }\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left( HQQ^{\dagger
}-QHQ^{\dagger }\right) \right\} \\
& =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left( HQQ^{\dagger }-HQ^{\dagger }Q\right) \right\}
=E\limfunc{tr}\left\{ QQ^{\dagger }\right\} =\left\langle Q\left\vert 
\widehat{H}\left( Q\right) \right. \right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}}=E.
\end{align}

In particular if 
\begin{equation}
D=\frac{aa^{\dagger }}{\left\langle \left\langle aa^{\dagger }\right\rangle
\right\rangle _{0}},
\end{equation}%
where 
\begin{align}
& \limfunc{tr}\left\{ aa^{\dagger }+a^{\dagger }a\right\} =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ I_{\mathcal{F}}\right\} =1, \\
& \text{i.e. }\limfunc{tr}\left\{ aa^{\dagger }\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
a^{\dagger }a\right\} =\frac{1}{2}=\left\Vert a^{\dagger }\right\Vert
_{HS}^{2}=\left\Vert a\right\Vert _{HS}^{2}
\end{align}%
then 
\begin{align}
\left\langle \widehat{H}\right\rangle _{D}& =E=\frac{\limfunc{tr}\left\{
\left( aHa^{\dagger }-aa^{\dagger }H\right) \right\} }{\limfunc{tr}\left\{
a^{\dagger }a\right\} } \\
& =\frac{\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left( Ha^{\dagger }a-H\left( 1-a^{\dagger
}a\right) \right) \right\} }{\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a^{\dagger }a\right\} }=4%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Ha^{\dagger }a\right\} -2\limfunc{tr}\left\{ H\right\}
=E,
\end{align}%
and 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle H\right\rangle _{D}=\frac{\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Ha^{\dagger
}a\right\} }{\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a^{\dagger }a\right\} }=2\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ Ha^{\dagger }a\right\} ,
\end{equation}%
while 
\begin{align}
& \func{Tr}\left\{ \tprod\nolimits_{\left\vert \phi \right\rangle
\left\langle \phi \right\vert }\left( \widehat{H}\right) \left\vert \overset{%
}{\Phi _{\left\vert \phi \right\rangle \left\langle \phi \right\vert }\left(
a^{\dagger }\right) }\right\rangle \left\langle \overset{}{\Phi _{\left\vert
\phi \right\rangle \left\langle \phi \right\vert }\left( a^{\dagger }\right) 
}\right\vert \right\} =\left\langle \Phi _{\left\vert \phi \right\rangle
\left\langle \phi \right\vert }\left( a^{\dagger }\right) \left\vert
\tprod\nolimits_{\left\vert \phi \right\rangle \left\langle \phi \right\vert
}\left( \widehat{H}\right) \right. \Phi _{\left\vert \phi \right\rangle
\left\langle \phi \right\vert }\left( a^{\dagger }\right) \right\rangle \\
& =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left( aHa^{\dagger }-aa^{\dagger }H\right)
\left\vert \phi \right\rangle \left\langle \phi \right\vert \right\} =E%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ aa^{\dagger }\left\vert \phi \right\rangle \left\langle
\phi \right\vert \right\} =E,
\end{align}%
as 
\begin{equation}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \left\vert \Phi _{\left\vert \phi \right\rangle
\left\langle \phi \right\vert }\left( a^{\dagger }\right) \right\rangle
\left\langle \Phi _{\left\vert \phi \right\rangle \left\langle \phi
\right\vert }\left( a^{\dagger }\right) \right\vert \right\} =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ aa^{\dagger }\left\vert \phi \right\rangle \left\langle \phi
\right\vert \right\} =1.
\end{equation}

The amplitudes $a,a^{\dagger}$ are eigen amplitudes of the Hamiltonian 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{H}\left( X\right) =\left[ aa^{\dagger},X\right]
\end{equation}
giving 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{H}\left( a\right) =\left[ aa^{\dagger},a\right] =aa^{\dagger
}a-aaa^{\dagger}=aa^{\dagger}a=\left( 1-a^{\dagger}a\right) a=a
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{H}\left( a^{\dagger}\right) =-a^{\dagger}.
\end{equation}
The Number operator (Gauge operator) has the effect 
\begin{align}
\widehat{\mathcal{N}_{a}}\left( a^{\dagger}\right) & =\left[ a^{\dagger
}a,a^{\dagger}\right] =a^{\dagger}aa^{\dagger}-a^{\dagger}a^{\dagger
}a=a^{\dagger}\left( 1-a^{\dagger}a\right) =a^{\dagger} \\
\widehat{\mathcal{N}_{a}}\left( a\right) & =\left( -\widehat{\mathcal{N}_{a}}%
\left( a^{\dagger}\right) \right) ^{\dagger}=-a. \\
a^{\dagger}a & =\frac{1}{2}\left( x_{1}-ix_{2}\right) \left(
x_{1}+ix_{2}\right) =\frac{1}{2}\left(
x_{1}^{2}+ix_{1}x_{2}-ix_{2}x_{1}+x_{2}^{2}\right) =I_{\mathcal{F}%
}+ix_{1}x_{2} \\
\widehat{\mathcal{N}_{a}}\left( x_{1}\right) & =\left[ I_{\mathcal{F}%
}+ix_{1}x_{2},x_{1}\right] =\left[ ix_{1}x_{2},x_{1}\right]
=ix_{1}x_{2}x_{1}-ix_{1}x_{1}x_{2}=-\frac{i}{2}x_{2}-\frac{i}{2}x_{2}=-ix_{2}
\\
\widehat{\mathcal{N}_{a}}\left( x_{2}\right) & =\left[ I_{\mathcal{F}%
}+ix_{1}x_{2},x_{2}\right] =\left[ ix_{1}x_{2},x_{2}\right]
=ix_{1}x_{2}x_{2}-ix_{2}x_{1}x_{2}=\frac{i}{2}x_{1}+\frac{i}{2}x_{1}=ix_{1}
\end{align}
and the complexifiction 
\begin{align}
\widehat{J_{x}}\left( x_{1}\right) & =-x_{2} \\
\widehat{J_{x}}\left( x_{2}\right) & =x_{1}.
\end{align}

\subsubsection{Gauge Invariant Solutions}

If one envokes gauge invariance then 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}\left( Q\right) =N_{a}Q,
\end{equation}
where%
\begin{equation}
Q\in\mathcal{F}_{\left[ N_{a}\right] }
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation}
Q_{\left[ N_{a}\right] }=\sum_{\substack{ 0\leq p,q\leq r  \\ p-q=N_{a}}}%
\sum _{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p}\leq r  \\ 1\leq
k_{1}<\cdots<k_{q}\leq r }}q_{pqj_{1}\cdots j_{p}k_{1}\cdots
k_{q}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger}a_{k_{1}}\cdots a_{k_{q}},
\end{equation}
which can be expressed in a Majorana basis as 
\begin{equation}
Q_{\left[ N_{a}\right] }=\sum_{\substack{ 0\leq p,q\leq r  \\ p-q=N_{a}}}%
\sum _{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p}\leq r  \\ 1\leq
k_{1}<\cdots<k_{q}\leq r }}q_{xpqj_{1}\cdots j_{p}k_{1}\cdots
k_{q}}x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}x_{k_{1}}\cdots x_{k_{q}},
\end{equation}
where%
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
x_{j} & x_{j+r}%
\end{array}
\right) \left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
1 & 1 \\ 
-i & i%
\end{array}
\right) =\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
a_{j}^{\dagger} & a_{j}%
\end{array}
\right)
\end{equation}
and thus 
\begin{equation}
Q_{\left[ N_{a}\right] }=\sum_{\substack{ 0\leq p,q\leq r  \\ p-q=N_{a}}}%
\sum _{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p}\leq r  \\ 1\leq
k_{1}<\cdots<k_{q}\leq r }}q_{pqj_{1}\cdots j_{p}k_{1}\cdots k_{q}}\left(
x_{j_{1}}-ix_{j_{1}+r}\right) \cdots\left( x_{j_{p}}-ix_{j_{p}+r}\right)
\left( x_{k_{1}}+ix_{k_{1}+r}\right) \cdots\left(
x_{k_{q}}+ix_{k_{q}+r}\right) .
\end{equation}
$\lceil$%
\begin{equation}
\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
x_{j} & x_{j+r}%
\end{array}
\right) =\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
a_{j}^{\dagger} & a_{j}%
\end{array}
\right) \left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
1 & i \\ 
1 & -i%
\end{array}
\right)
\end{equation}
$\rfloor$

The corresponding density operators are given by 
\begin{align}
D& =QQ^{\dagger }= \\
& \sum_{\substack{ p_{1}-q_{1}=N_{a}  \\ 0\leq p_{1},q_{1}\leq r}}\sum 
_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{p_{1}}\leq r  \\ 1\leq k_{1}<\cdots
<k_{q_{1}}\leq r}}\sum_{\substack{ p_{2}-q_{2}=N_{a}  \\ 0\leq
p_{2},q_{2}\leq r}}\sum_{\substack{ 1\leq l_{1}<\cdots <l_{p_{2}}\leq r  \\ %
1\leq m_{1}<\cdots <m_{q_{2}}\leq r}}q_{p_{1}q_{1}j_{1}\cdots
j_{p_{1}}k_{1}\cdots k_{q_{1}}}\overline{q}_{p_{2}q_{2}l_{1}\cdots
l_{p_{2}}m_{1}\cdots m_{q_{2}}} \\
& a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{p_{2}}}^{\dagger }a_{k_{1}}\cdots
a_{k_{q_{2}}}\left( a_{m_{1}}\cdots a_{m_{q_{2}}}\right) ^{\dagger }\left(
a_{l_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{l_{p_{2}}}^{\dagger }\right) ^{\dagger } \\
& =\sum_{\substack{ p_{1}-q_{1}=N_{a}  \\ 0\leq p_{1},q_{1}\leq r}}\sum 
_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{p_{1}}\leq r  \\ 1\leq k_{1}<\cdots
<k_{q_{1}}\leq r}}\sum_{\substack{ p_{2}-q_{2}=N_{a}  \\ 0\leq
p_{2},q_{2}\leq r}}\sum_{\substack{ 1\leq l_{1}<\cdots <l_{p_{2}}\leq r  \\ %
1\leq m_{1}<\cdots <m_{q_{2}}\leq r}}q_{p_{1}q_{1}j_{1}\cdots
j_{p_{1}}k_{1}\cdots k_{q_{1}}}\overline{q}_{p_{2}q_{2}l_{1}\cdots
l_{p_{2}}m_{1}\cdots m_{q_{2}}} \\
& a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{p_{2}}}^{\dagger }a_{k_{1}}\cdots
a_{k_{q_{2}}}a_{m_{q_{2}}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{m_{1}}^{\dagger
}a_{l_{p_{2}}}\cdots a_{l_{1}}
\end{align}%
In general $Q$ can be expanded as 
\begin{align}
Q& =\sum_{-r\leq N_{a}\leq r}\sum_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots
<j_{\left\vert N_{a}\right\vert }\leq r}}\sum_{0\leq p\leq r}\sum_{\substack{
1\leq m_{1}<\cdots <m_{p}\leq r  \\ 1\leq l_{1}<\cdots <l_{p}\leq r}}%
q_{j_{1}\cdots j_{N_{a}}m_{1}\cdots m_{p}l_{1}\cdots l_{p}}\left[ a\right]
_{j_{1}}\cdots \left[ a\right] _{j_{\left\vert N_{a}\right\vert
}}a_{m_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{m_{p}}^{\dagger }a_{l_{1}}\cdots a_{l_{p}} \\
& =\sum_{-r\leq N_{a}\leq r}Q_{\left[ N_{a}\right] },
\end{align}%
where 
\begin{align}
\left[ a\right] _{j}& =a_{j};N_{a}<0 \\
\left[ a\right] _{j}& =a_{j}^{\dagger };N_{a}>0 \\
\left[ a\right] _{j}& =I_{\mathcal{F}};N_{a}=0.
\end{align}%
The number eigen amplitudes $Q_{\left[ N_{a}\right] }$ can be expressed as 
\begin{align}
Q_{\left[ N_{a}\right] }& =\sum_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots
<j_{\left\vert N_{a}\right\vert }\leq r}}\sum_{0\leq p\leq r}q_{j_{1}\cdots
j_{\left\vert N_{a}\right\vert }m_{1}\cdots m_{p}l_{1}\cdots l_{p}}\left[ a%
\right] _{j_{1}}\cdots \left[ a\right] _{j_{\left\vert N_{a}\right\vert
}}\sum_{\substack{ 1\leq m_{1}<\cdots <m_{p}\leq r  \\ 1\leq l_{1}<\cdots
<l_{p}\leq r}}a_{m_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{m_{p}}^{\dagger }a_{l_{1}}\cdots
a_{l_{p}} \\
& =\sum_{0\leq p\leq r}Q_{\left\{ N_{a},p\right\} }=\sum_{0\leq p\leq r} 
\left[ a\right] _{j_{1}}\cdots \left[ a\right] _{j_{\left\vert
N_{a}\right\vert }}\widetilde{Q}_{\left\{ N_{a},p\right\} },
\end{align}%
where $Q_{\left\{ N_{a},p\right\} }$ and $\widetilde{Q}_{\left\{
N_{a},p\right\} }$ are defined as 
\begin{align}
Q_{\left\{ N_{a},p\right\} }& =\left[ a\right] _{j_{1}}\cdots \left[ a\right]
_{j_{\left\vert N_{a}\right\vert }}\sum_{\substack{ 1\leq m_{1}<\cdots
<m_{p}\leq r  \\ 1\leq l_{1}<\cdots <l_{p}\leq r}}q_{j_{1}\cdots
j_{\left\vert N_{a}\right\vert }m_{1}\cdots m_{p}l_{1}\cdots
l_{p}}a_{m_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{m_{p}}^{\dagger }a_{l_{1}}\cdots
a_{l_{p}} \\
\widetilde{Q}_{\left\{ N_{a},p\right\} }& =\sum_{\substack{ 1\leq
m_{1}<\cdots <m_{p}\leq r  \\ 1\leq l_{1}<\cdots <l_{p}\leq r}}%
q_{j_{1}\cdots j_{\left\vert N_{a}\right\vert }m_{1}\cdots m_{p}l_{1}\cdots
l_{p}}a_{m_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{m_{p}}^{\dagger }a_{l_{1}}\cdots
a_{l_{p}}.
\end{align}%
In particular 
\begin{align}
& Q_{\left\{ N_{a},0\right\} }=\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{\left\vert
N_{a}\right\vert }\leq r}q_{j_{1}\cdots j_{N_{a}}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
a_{j_{N_{a}}}^{\dagger }=\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{N_{a}}\leq
r}q_{j_{1}\cdots j_{N_{a}}}\left( x_{j_{1}}-ix_{j_{1}+r}\right) \cdots
\left( x_{j_{N_{a}}}-ix_{j_{N_{a}}+r}\right) \\
& =\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{N_{a}}\leq r}q_{j_{1}\cdots
j_{N_{a}}}\left\{ 
\begin{array}{c}
x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{N_{a}}}+\left( -i\right) x_{j_{1}}\cdots
x_{j_{N_{a}-1}}x_{j_{1}+r}+\left( -i\right) ^{2}x_{j_{1}}\cdots
x_{j_{N_{a}-2}}x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}+r} \\ 
+\left( -i\right) ^{r-1}x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{N_{a}}+r-1}+\left( -i\right)
^{N_{a}}x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}+r}\cdots x_{j_{N_{a}}+r}%
\end{array}%
\right\} .
\end{align}

$\lceil$Most general form of factored expression is 
\begin{align}
Q_{\left\{ N_{a},0\right\} } & =\left\langle \left( \sum_{1\leq j_{1}\leq
r}q_{1j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}+\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<j_{2}\leq
r}q_{1j_{1}j_{2}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}+\cdots+\sum_{1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots<j_{\left\vert N_{a}\right\vert }\leq r}q_{1j_{1}\cdots
j_{N_{a}}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{j_{N_{a}}}^{\dagger}\right)
\times\cdots\right. \\
& \left. \cdots\times\left( \sum_{1\leq j_{1}\leq
r}q_{N_{a}-1j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}+\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<j_{2}\leq
r}q_{N_{a}-11j_{1}j_{2}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}+\cdots+%
\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{\left\vert N_{a}\right\vert }\leq
r}q_{N_{a}j_{1}\cdots j_{N_{a}-1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{j_{N_{a}-1}}^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle _{N_{a}} \\
& =\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{\left\vert N_{a}\right\vert }\leq
r}q_{j_{1}\cdots j_{N_{a}}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{j_{N_{a}}}^{\dagger}.
\end{align}
Hence 
\begin{equation}
q_{j_{1}\cdots j_{N_{a}}}=q_{j_{1}\cdots j_{N_{a}}}\left( \mathbf{q}_{j_{1}},%
\mathbf{q}_{j_{1}j_{2}},\cdots,\mathbf{q}_{j_{1}j_{2}\cdots
j_{N_{a}-1}}\right) ,
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{q}_{j_{1}\cdots j_{p}}=\left( q_{1j_{1}\cdots
j_{p}},\cdots,q_{\kappa j_{1}\cdots j_{p}}\right) .
\end{equation}
$\rfloor$

$\lceil $%
\begin{align}
\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<j_{2}\leq r}q_{j_{1}j_{2}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }& =\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<j_{2}\leq r}q_{j_{1}j_{2}}\left(
x_{j_{1}}-ix_{j_{1}+r}\right) \left( x_{j_{2}}-ix_{j_{2}+r}\right) \\
& =\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<j_{2}\leq r}q_{j_{1}j_{2}}\left\{
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}-ix_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}}-ix_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}+r}-x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}+r}\right\}
\\
& =\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<j_{2}\leq r}q_{j_{1}j_{2}}\left\{
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}-x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}+r}-i\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}+r}-x_{j_{2}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) \right\}
\end{align}%
\begin{eqnarray}
\sum_{1\leq j_{1}\leq s}\alpha _{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}a_{j_{1}+s}^{\dagger } &=&\sum_{1\leq j_{1}\leq s}\alpha _{j_{1}}\left(
x_{j_{1}}-ix_{j_{1}+r}\right) \left( x_{j_{1}+s}-ix_{j_{1}+r+s}\right) \\
&=&\sum_{1\leq j_{1}\leq s}\alpha _{j_{1}}\left\{
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+s}-x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}+r+s}-i\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r+s}-x_{j_{1}+s}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) \right\}
\end{eqnarray}%
$\rfloor $

These operators produce density operators%
\begin{equation}
Q_{\left\{ N_{a},0\right\} }Q_{\left\{ N_{a},0\right\} }^{\dagger }=\sum 
_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{N_{a}}\leq r  \\ 1\leq k_{1}<\cdots
<k_{N_{a}}\leq r}}q_{j_{1}\cdots j_{N_{a}}}\overline{q}_{k_{1}\cdots
k_{N_{a}}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{N_{a}}}^{\dagger
}a_{k_{N_{a}}}\cdots a_{k_{1}}=\sum\limits_{0\leq n\leq 2r}\left\langle
Q_{\left\{ N_{a},0\right\} }Q_{\left\{ N_{a},0\right\} }^{\dagger
}\right\rangle _{n},
\end{equation}%
where 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle Q_{\left\{ N_{a},0\right\} }Q_{\left\{ N_{a},0\right\}
}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{n}=\sum_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots
<j_{n}\leq r  \\ 1\leq k_{1}<\cdots <k_{n}\leq r}}d_{j_{1}\cdots
j_{n}k_{1}\cdots k_{n}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger
}a_{k_{n}}\cdots a_{k_{1}};0\leq n\leq N_{a}.
\end{equation}%
The only components we are interested in are $n=0,2,4$ i.e. 
\begin{align}
D_{0}& =I_{\mathcal{F}}=\left\langle Q_{\left\{ N_{a},0\right\} }Q_{\left\{
N_{a},0\right\} }^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{0}=\sum_{\substack{ 1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots <j_{\left\vert N_{a}\right\vert }\leq r  \\ 1\leq k_{1}<\cdots
<k_{\left\vert N_{a}\right\vert }\leq r}}q_{j_{1}\cdots j_{N_{a}}}\overline{q%
}_{k_{1}\cdots k_{N_{a}}}\left\langle a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
a_{j_{N_{a}}}^{\dagger }a_{k_{N_{a}}}\cdots a_{k_{1}}\right\rangle _{0} \\
D_{2}& =\sum_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1}\leq r  \\ 1\leq k_{1}\leq r}}%
d_{j_{1}k_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{k_{1}}=\left\langle Q_{\left\{
N_{a},0\right\} }Q_{\left\{ N_{a},0\right\} }^{\dagger }\right\rangle
_{2}=\sum_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{\left\vert N_{a}\right\vert
}\leq r  \\ 1\leq k_{1}<\cdots <k_{\left\vert N_{a}\right\vert }\leq r}}%
q_{j_{1}\cdots j_{N_{a}}}\overline{q}_{k_{1}\cdots k_{N_{a}}}\left\langle
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{N_{a}}}^{\dagger }a_{k_{N_{a}}}\cdots
a_{k_{1}}\right\rangle _{2} \\
D_{4}& =\sum_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1}<j_{2}\leq r  \\ 1\leq k_{1}<k_{2}\leq r 
}}d_{j_{1}j_{2}k_{1}k_{2}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j2}^{\dagger
}a_{k_{2}}a_{k_{1}}=\left\langle Q_{\left\{ N_{a},0\right\} }Q_{\left\{
N_{a},0\right\} }^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{4} \\
& =\sum_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{\left\vert N_{a}\right\vert }\leq
r  \\ 1\leq k_{1}<\cdots <k_{\left\vert N_{a}\right\vert }\leq r}}%
q_{j_{1}\cdots j_{N_{a}}}\overline{q}_{k_{1}\cdots k_{N_{a}}}\left\langle
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{N_{a}}}^{\dagger }a_{k_{N_{a}}}\cdots
a_{k_{1}}\right\rangle _{4}.
\end{align}

\subsubsection{Evolution Equations}

Time dependent operators generated by unitary evolution operators e.g. in
the case of one particle Hamiltonians 
\begin{equation}
\prod_{1\leq j\leq N}a_{j}^{\dagger}\left( t\right) =\widehat{u}_{H}\left(
t\right) \left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq N}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) =\prod_{1\leq
j\leq N}\widehat{u}_{H}\left( t\right) \left( a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) ,
\end{equation}
satisfy the EOM 
\begin{gather}
A\left( t\right) =\widehat{u}_{H}\left( t\right) \left( A\right)
=\exp\left\{ iHt\right\} A\exp\left\{ -iHt\right\} =\exp\left\{ i\func{adj}%
\left( H\right) t\right\} \left( A\right) \\
\Downarrow \\
-i\frac{dA\left( t\right) }{dt}=-\left[ H,A\left( t\right) \right] .
\end{gather}
Hence in particular 
\begin{align}
-i\frac{d}{dt}\left\{ Q\left( t\right) Q\left( t\right) ^{\dagger }\right\}
& =\frac{dQ\left( t\right) }{dt}Q\left( t\right) ^{\dagger }+Q\left(
t\right) \frac{dQ^{\dagger}\left( t\right) }{dt}=\left[ H,Q\left( t\right) %
\right] Q\left( t\right) ^{\dagger}+Q\left( t\right) \left[
H,Q^{\dagger}\left( t\right) \right] \\
& =HQ\left( t\right) Q\left( t\right) ^{\dagger}-Q\left( t\right) HQ\left(
t\right) ^{\dagger}+Q\left( t\right) HQ^{\dagger}\left( t\right) -Q\left(
t\right) Q^{\dagger}\left( t\right) H=\left[ H,Q\left( t\right)
Q^{\dagger}\left( t\right) \right] ,
\end{align}
giving 
\begin{equation}
-i\frac{d}{dt}D\left( t\right) =\left[ H,D\left( t\right) \right] =\widehat{H%
}\left( D\left( t\right) \right) .
\end{equation}
The time evolution of basis elements generated by one particle Hamiltonians
is given by 
\begin{align}
\widehat{u}_{H}\left( t\right) \left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq N}a_{j}^{\dagger
}\right) & =\prod_{1\leq j\leq N}\widehat{u}_{H}\left( t\right) \left(
a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) =\prod_{1\leq j\leq N}a_{j}^{\dagger}\left( t\right)
\\
-i\frac{d}{dt}\left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq N}a_{j}^{\dagger}\left( t\right)
\right) & =\left[ H,\prod_{1\leq j\leq N}a_{j}^{\dagger}\left( t\right) %
\right] \\
-i\frac{d}{dt}\left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq N}a_{j}^{\dagger}\left( t\right)
\right) & =\left[ H,a_{1}^{\dagger}\left( t\right) \right] \left(
\prod_{2\leq j\leq N}a_{j}^{\dagger}\left( t\right) \right)
+a_{1}^{\dagger}\left( t\right) \left[ H,a_{2}^{\dagger}\left( t\right) %
\right] \left( \prod_{3\leq j\leq N}a_{j}^{\dagger}\left( t\right) \right) +
\\
& \cdots+\left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq p}a_{j}^{\dagger}\left( t\right) \right) 
\left[ H,a_{p+1}^{\dagger}\left( t\right) \right] \left( \prod_{p+2\leq
j\leq N}a_{j}^{\dagger}\left( t\right) \right) +\cdots+\left( \prod_{1\leq
j\leq N-1}a_{j}^{\dagger}\left( t\right) \right) \left[ H,a_{N}^{\dagger}%
\left( t\right) \right] \\
& =\left( -i\frac{d}{dt}a_{1}^{\dagger}\left( t\right) \right) \left(
\prod_{2\leq j\leq N}a_{j}^{\dagger}\left( t\right) \right) +\cdots+\left(
\prod_{1\leq j\leq N-1}a_{j}^{\dagger}\left( t\right) \right) \left( -i\frac{%
d}{dt}a_{N}^{\dagger}\left( t\right) \right) .
\end{align}

\subsubsection{Eigen Vector Equations{}}

$Q\in\mathcal{F}$ is an eigen operator of the operator $\widehat{H}:\mathcal{%
F}\rightarrow$ $\mathcal{F}$ if 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{H}\left( Q\right) =\left[ H,Q\right] =EI_{\mathcal{F}}.
\end{equation}
It is \emph{IMPORTANT to note that }%
\begin{align}
\widehat{H}\left( Q_{1}\right) Q_{2} & =\left[ H,Q_{1}\right]
Q_{2}=HQ_{1}Q_{2}-Q_{1}HQ_{2} \\
\widehat{H}\left( Q_{1}Q_{2}\right) & =\left[ H,Q_{1}\right] Q_{2}+Q_{1}%
\left[ H,Q_{2}\right] =HQ_{1}Q_{2}-Q_{1}HQ_{2}+Q_{1}HQ_{2}-Q_{1}Q_{2}H=\left[
H,Q_{1}Q_{2}\right]
\end{align}
thus in general 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{H}\left( Q_{1}\right) Q_{2}\neq\widehat{H}\left( Q_{1}Q_{2}\right) .
\end{equation}

Density Operators $D$ are \emph{Stationary }wrt a unitary time evolution
generated by $H$ if 
\begin{equation}
-i\frac{d}{dt}D\left( t\right) =\left[ H,D\left( t\right) \right] =\widehat{H%
}\left( D\left( t\right) \right) =0,
\end{equation}%
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
D\left( t\right) =D,
\end{equation}%
where 
\begin{equation}
D\left( t\right) =\widehat{u}\left( t\right) \left( D\right) =u\left(
t\right) Du\left( t\right) ^{\dagger }\equiv \exp \left\{ -i\widehat{H}%
t\right\} \left( D\right) .
\end{equation}%
Density operators are positive and normalized i.e. 
\begin{equation}
D=\frac{QQ^{\dagger }}{\left\langle \left\langle QQ^{\dagger }\right\rangle
\right\rangle _{0}}=\frac{QQ^{\dagger }}{\limfunc{tr}\left\{ QQ^{\dagger
}\right\} }=\widetilde{Q}\widetilde{Q}^{\dagger }.
\end{equation}%
$\widetilde{Q}$ are defined up to unitary transformation that change the
phase of $\widetilde{Q}$ i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{v}\left( \widetilde{Q}\right) =v\widetilde{Q}v^{\dagger
}=e^{i\theta }\widetilde{Q},
\end{equation}%
where 
\begin{equation}
v=\exp \left\{ i\eta \right\} ;\quad \eta =\eta ^{\dagger }
\end{equation}%
and that leaves the density operator invariant 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{v}\left( D\right) =v\widetilde{Q}v^{\dagger }v\widetilde{Q}%
^{\dagger }v^{\dagger }=vDv^{\dagger }=e^{i\theta }\widetilde{Q}\widetilde{Q}%
^{\dagger }e^{-i\theta }=D.
\end{equation}%
Thus 
\begin{equation}
\left[ \eta ,D\right] =0
\end{equation}%
and%
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\eta }\left( \widetilde{Q}\right) =\left[ \eta ,\widetilde{Q}\right]
=\theta \widetilde{Q}
\end{equation}%
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\widetilde{Q}\equiv \widetilde{Q}_{\theta }.
\end{equation}%
One can also observe that 
\begin{align}
v\widetilde{Q}& =e^{i\theta }\widetilde{Q}v \\
\left( v\widetilde{Q}\right) ^{\dagger }& =\left( e^{i\theta }\widetilde{Q}%
v\right) ^{\dagger } \\
& \Updownarrow \\
\widetilde{Q}v^{\dagger }& =e^{-i\theta }v^{\dagger }\widetilde{Q}^{\dagger
}.
\end{align}

\begin{theorem}
\begin{gather}
D=\widehat{v}\left( D\right) =v\widetilde{Q}v^{\dagger}v\widetilde{Q}%
^{\dagger}v^{\dagger}=vDv^{\dagger} \\
\Updownarrow \\
\widehat{v}\left( \widetilde{Q}\right) =v\widetilde{Q}v^{\dagger}=e^{i\theta}%
\widetilde{Q}.
\end{gather}
\end{theorem}

This gives the important corollary that shows in particular that time
stationarity is equivalent to an eigen value problem.

\begin{corollary}
\begin{gather}
\widehat{v}\left( \widetilde{Q}\right) =v\widetilde{Q}v^{\dagger}=e^{i\theta}%
\widetilde{Q} \\
\Updownarrow \\
\widehat{\eta}\left( \widetilde{Q}\right) =\theta\widetilde{Q}.
\end{gather}
\end{corollary}

\subsubsection{Optimization Formulation of Eigenvalue Problem}

The variational problem that is associated with time stationarity is 
\begin{equation}
\min\limits_{Q}\left\{ \frac{\left\langle Q\left\vert \widehat{H}\left(
Q\right) \right. \right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}}}{\left\langle Q\left\vert
Q\right. \right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}}}\right\} =\min\limits_{\left\langle
Q\left\vert Q\right. \right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}}=1}\left\{ \left\langle
Q\left\vert \widehat{H}\left( Q\right) \right. \right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}%
}\right\} =\max\limits_{\lambda }\min\limits_{Q}\left\{ \left\langle
Q\left\vert \widehat{H}\left( Q\right) \right. \right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}%
}-\lambda \left( \left\langle Q\left\vert Q\right. \right\rangle _{\mathcal{F%
}}-1\right) \right\} .
\end{equation}%
The sufficient condition for $Q$ to be an energy stationary operator, in the
sense of optimization, with energy $E$ is thus 
\begin{equation}
\left( \widehat{H}-EI_{\mathcal{F}}\right) Q=0,
\end{equation}%
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{H}\left( Q\right) =EQ.
\end{equation}%
This a minimum if 
\begin{equation}
\left( \widehat{H}-EI_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \geq 0
\end{equation}%
otherwise it is stationary.

One should observe%
\begin{align}
\widehat{H}\left( Q^{\dagger}\right) & =HQ^{\dagger}-Q^{\dagger}H \\
\left( \widehat{H}\left( Q\right) \right) ^{\dagger} & =\left( HQ-QH\right)
^{\dagger}=Q^{\dagger}H-HQ^{\dagger}=-\widehat{H}\left( Q^{\dagger}\right)
\end{align}
hence if $E_{k}$ is an eigenvalue associated to eigenoperator $Q_{k},$ $%
-E_{k}$ is an eigenvalue associated to the eigenoperator $Q_{k}^{\dagger}$
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{H}\left( Q^{\dagger}\right) =-\left( \widehat{H}\left( Q\right)
\right) ^{\dagger}=-EQ^{\dagger}.
\end{equation}
One can observe 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{H}\left( Q\right) Q^{\dagger}=EQQ^{\dagger}\equiv ED
\end{equation}
$\lceil$of course 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{H}\left( QQ^{\dagger}\right) =\widehat{H}\left( D\right) =0.
\end{equation}
$\rfloor$

\subsubsection{Evaluation of $\protect\widehat{H}\left( Q\right) $}

The product $HQ$ can be expanded as 
\begin{align}
HQ & =\left( H_{0}+H_{2}+H_{4}\right) Q=H_{0}Q+H_{2}Q+H_{4}Q \\
& =\eta_{0}Q+\sum_{0\leq n\leq2r}\left\langle H_{2}Q\right\rangle
_{n}+\sum_{0\leq n\leq2r}\left\langle H_{4}Q\right\rangle _{n} \\
& =\sum_{0\leq n\leq2r}\left\{ \eta_{0}\left\langle Q\right\rangle
_{n}+\left\langle H_{2}Q\right\rangle _{n}+\left\langle H_{4}Q\right\rangle
_{n}\right\}
\end{align}
The term $H_{2}Q$ has the expansion 
\begin{equation}
H_{2}Q=\sum_{0\leq
n\leq2r}H_{2}Q_{n}=H_{2}Q_{0}+H_{2}Q_{1}+H_{2}Q_{2}+H_{2}Q_{3}+%
\cdots+H_{2}Q_{2r},
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{align}
H_{2}Q_{0} & =q_{0}H_{2}, \\
H_{2}Q_{1} & =\sum_{j=0}^{1}\left\langle H_{2}Q_{1}\right\rangle
_{1+2j}=\left\langle H_{2}Q_{1}\right\rangle _{1}+\left\langle
H_{2}Q_{1}\right\rangle _{3} \\
H_{2}Q_{2} & =\sum_{j=0}^{2}\left\langle H_{2}Q_{2}\right\rangle
_{2j}=\left\langle H_{2}Q_{2}\right\rangle _{0}+\left\langle
H_{2}Q_{2}\right\rangle _{2}+\left\langle H_{2}Q_{2}\right\rangle _{3} \\
H_{2}Q_{3} & =\sum_{j=0}^{2}\left\langle H_{2}Q_{3}\right\rangle
_{1+2j}=\left\langle H_{2}Q_{3}\right\rangle _{1}+\left\langle
H_{2}Q_{3}\right\rangle _{3}+\left\langle H_{2}Q_{3}\right\rangle _{5} \\
H_{2}Q_{4} & =\sum_{j=0}^{2}\left\langle H_{2}Q_{4}\right\rangle
_{2+2j}=\left\langle H_{2}Q_{4}\right\rangle _{2}+\left\langle
H_{2}Q_{4}\right\rangle _{4}+\left\langle H_{2}Q_{4}\right\rangle _{6} \\
& \vdots
\end{align}
The term $H_{4}Q$ has the expansion 
\begin{equation}
H_{4}Q=\sum_{0\leq
n\leq2r}H_{4}Q_{n}=H_{4}Q_{0}+H_{4}Q_{1}+H_{4}Q_{2}+H_{4}Q_{3}+%
\cdots+H_{4}Q_{2r},
\end{equation}
where each term has the expansion 
\begin{align}
H_{4}Q_{0} & =q_{0}H_{4}, \\
H_{4}Q_{1} & =\sum_{j=0}^{1}\left\langle H_{4}Q_{1}\right\rangle
_{3+2j}=\left\langle H_{4}Q_{1}\right\rangle _{3}+\left\langle
H_{4}Q_{1}\right\rangle _{5} \\
H_{4}Q_{2} & =\sum_{j=0}^{2}\left\langle H_{4}Q_{2}\right\rangle
_{2+2j}=\left\langle H_{4}Q_{2}\right\rangle _{2}+\left\langle
H_{4}Q_{2}\right\rangle _{4}+\left\langle H_{4}Q_{2}\right\rangle _{6} \\
H_{4}Q_{3} & =\sum_{j=0}^{3}\left\langle H_{4}Q_{3}\right\rangle
_{1+2j}=\left\langle H_{4}Q_{3}\right\rangle _{1}+\left\langle
H_{4}Q_{3}\right\rangle _{3}+\left\langle H_{4}Q_{3}\right\rangle
_{5}+\left\langle H_{4}Q_{3}\right\rangle _{7} \\
H_{4}Q_{4} & =\sum_{j=0}^{4}\left\langle H_{4}Q_{4}\right\rangle
_{2j}=\left\langle H_{4}Q_{4}\right\rangle _{0}+\left\langle
H_{4}Q_{4}\right\rangle _{2}+\left\langle H_{4}Q_{4}\right\rangle
_{4}+\left\langle H_{4}Q_{4}\right\rangle _{6}+\left\langle
H_{4}Q_{4}\right\rangle _{8} \\
H_{4}Q_{5} & =\sum_{j=0}^{4}\left\langle H_{4}Q_{5}\right\rangle
_{1+2j}=\left\langle H_{4}Q_{5}\right\rangle _{1}+\left\langle
H_{4}Q_{5}\right\rangle _{3}+\left\langle H_{4}Q_{5}\right\rangle
_{5}+\left\langle H_{4}Q_{5}\right\rangle _{7}+\left\langle
H_{4}Q_{5}\right\rangle _{9} \\
H_{4}Q_{6} & =\sum_{j=0}^{4}\left\langle H_{4}Q_{6}\right\rangle
_{2+2j}=\left\langle H_{4}Q_{6}\right\rangle _{2}+\left\langle
H_{4}Q_{6}\right\rangle _{4}+\left\langle H_{4}Q_{6}\right\rangle
_{6}+\left\langle H_{4}Q_{6}\right\rangle _{8}+\left\langle
H_{4}Q_{6}\right\rangle _{10} \\
& \vdots
\end{align}

\section{Gauge Invariance}

In order for the density operators $D$ to be gauge invariant i.e.%
\begin{align}
\widehat{u_{\mathcal{N}_{b}}}\left( \theta \right) \left( D\right) & =D \\
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{b}\left( D\right) & =0
\end{align}%
\begin{equation}
Q\left( \theta \right) =\widehat{u_{\mathcal{N}_{b}}}\left( \theta \right)
Q=e^{iN_{b}\theta }Q,
\end{equation}%
they need to be products of operators, $Q,Q^{\dagger },$ that are stationary
solutions of the constrained optimization problem 
\begin{eqnarray}
\min \left\{ \frac{\left\langle Q\left\vert \widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{b}\left(
Q\right) \right. \right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}}}{\left\langle Q\left\vert
Q\right. \right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}}}\right\} &=&\min\limits_{\left\langle
Q^{\dagger }\left\vert Q\right. \right\rangle =1}\left\{ \left\langle
Q\left\vert \widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{b}\left( Q\right) \right. \right\rangle _{%
\mathcal{F}}\right\} \\
&=&\max\limits_{\lambda }\min\limits_{Q}\left\{ \left\langle Q\left\vert 
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{b}\left( Q\right) \right. \right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}%
}-\lambda \left( \left\langle Q\left\vert Q\right. \right\rangle _{\mathcal{F%
}}-1\right) \right\} ,
\end{eqnarray}%
where 
\begin{align}
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{b}\left( Q\right) & =\left[ \mathcal{N}_{b},Q\right]
\\
\mathcal{N}_{b}& =\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{j}.
\end{align}%
A sufficient condition for $\frac{\left\langle Q\left\vert \widehat{\mathcal{%
N}}_{b}\left( Q\right) \right. \right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}}}{\left\langle
Q\left\vert Q\right. \right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}}}$ to be stationary with
value $N_{b}$ at $Q$ is thus 
\begin{equation}
\left( \widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{b}-N_{b}I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) Q=0,
\end{equation}%
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{b}\left( Q\right) =N_{b}Q.
\end{equation}%
This a minimum if 
\begin{equation}
\left( \widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{b}-N_{b}I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \geq 0,
\end{equation}%
otherwise it is stationary. If $Q$ is eigenoperator with eigenvalue $N_{b}$
then $Q^{\dagger }$ is eigenoperator with eigenvalue $-N_{b},$ so when $%
r=\infty $ the operator $\widehat{\mathcal{N}_{b}}$ is an unbounded self
adjoint operator with a discrete spectra that is unbounded below and above.

It follows trivially that gauge invariance implies that 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left\langle \widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{b}\left( Q\right)
Q^{\dagger}\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0}=N_{b},
\end{equation}
and%
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left\langle \widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{b}\left( Q\left(
\theta\right) \right) Q^{\dagger}\left( \theta\right) \right\rangle
\right\rangle _{0}=N_{b}.
\end{equation}

$\lceil$%
\begin{align}
\left(
a_{1}^{\dagger}a_{1}+a_{2}^{\dagger}a_{2}+a_{3}^{\dagger}a_{3}+a_{4}^{%
\dagger}a_{4}\right) a_{1}^{\dagger} &
=a_{1}^{\dagger}+a_{2}^{\dagger}a_{2}a_{1}^{\dagger}+a_{3}^{%
\dagger}a_{3}a_{1}^{\dagger}+a_{4}^{\dagger}a_{4}a_{1}^{\dagger} \\
a_{1}^{\dagger}\left(
a_{1}^{\dagger}a_{1}+a_{2}^{\dagger}a_{2}+a_{3}^{\dagger}a_{3}+a_{4}^{%
\dagger}a_{4}\right) &
=a_{1}^{\dagger}a_{2}^{\dagger}a_{2}+a_{1}^{\dagger}a_{3}^{%
\dagger}a_{3}+a_{1}^{\dagger}a_{4}^{\dagger}a_{4} \\
\left[ \mathcal{N},a_{1}^{\dagger}\right] & =a_{1}^{\dagger}
\end{align}%
\begin{align}
\left(
a_{1}^{\dagger}a_{1}+a_{2}^{\dagger}a_{2}+a_{3}^{\dagger}a_{3}+a_{4}^{%
\dagger}a_{4}\right) a_{1} &
=a_{2}^{\dagger}a_{2}a_{1}+a_{3}^{\dagger}a_{3}a_{1}+a_{4}^{%
\dagger}a_{4}a_{1} \\
a_{1}\left(
a_{1}^{\dagger}a_{1}+a_{2}^{\dagger}a_{2}+a_{3}^{\dagger}a_{3}+a_{4}^{%
\dagger}a_{4}\right) &
=a_{1}+a_{1}a_{2}^{\dagger}a_{2}+a_{1}a_{3}^{\dagger}a_{3}+a_{1}a_{4}^{%
\dagger}a_{4} \\
\left[ \mathcal{N},a_{1}\right] & =-a_{1}
\end{align}
$\rfloor$

The action of $\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}\equiv \func{adj}\left( \mathcal{N}%
_{a}\right) $ on $\mathcal{F}_{1}$ is given by 
\begin{align}
\func{adj}\left( \mathcal{N}_{a}\right) \left( a_{j}^{\dagger }\right) & =%
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger }\right) =\left[ \mathcal{N}%
_{a},a_{j}^{\dagger }\right] =a_{j}^{\dagger } \\
\func{adj}\left( \mathcal{N}_{a}\right) \left( x_{j}\right) & =\widehat{%
\mathcal{N}}_{a}\left( x_{j}\right) =\left[ \mathcal{N}_{a},x_{j}\right] =%
\left[ \mathcal{N}_{a},\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger
}+a_{j}\right) \right] =\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger
}-a_{j}\right) =x_{j+r} \\
\func{adj}\left( \mathcal{N}_{a}\right) \left( a_{j}\right) & =\widehat{%
\mathcal{N}}_{a}\left( a_{j}\right) =\left[ \mathcal{N}_{a},a_{j}\right]
=-a_{j} \\
\func{adj}\left( \mathcal{N}_{a}\right) \left( x_{j+r}\right) & =\widehat{%
\mathcal{N}}_{a}\left( x_{j+r}\right) =\left[ \mathcal{N}_{a},x_{j+r}\right]
=\left[ \mathcal{N}_{a},\frac{i}{\sqrt{2}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger
}-a_{j}\right) \right] =\frac{i}{\sqrt{2}}\left( \left( a_{j}^{\dagger
}+a_{j}\right) \right) =x_{j},
\end{align}%
which shows that $\left\{ \left. a_{j}^{\dagger },a_{j}\right\vert 1\leq
j\leq \infty \right\} $ are eigen operator amplitudes of $\widehat{\mathcal{N%
}_{a}}$ with eigenvalues $\left\{ 1,-1\right\} $ respectively$.$ This is a
particular example of $\mathcal{F}_{1}$ being an invariant subspace of $%
O\left( 2r,\mathbb{C}\right) $ as the Gauge groups are abelian subgroups of $%
O\left( 2r,\mathbb{C}\right) ,$ ($\mathcal{F}_{1}$ is the direct sum of two
isomorphic subspaces that are invariant to $Gl\left( r,\mathbb{C}\right) ,$%
which can realized as inner automorphisms of $\mathcal{F}$ generated by $%
\mathcal{F}_{2}.$ The subspace $\mathcal{F}_{1}$ is invariant to $Gl\left(
2r,\mathbb{C}\right) ,$ but this group cannot be represented as inner
automorphisms of $\mathcal{F}$ generated by $\mathcal{F}_{\left( 2\right)
}). $ The preceding also shows that 
\begin{align}
\left[ i\mathcal{N}_{a},a_{j}^{\dagger }\right] & =ia_{j}^{\dagger } \\
\left[ i\mathcal{N}_{a},x_{j}\right] & =\left[ i\mathcal{N}_{a},\frac{1}{%
\sqrt{2}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger }+a_{j}\right) \right] =\frac{i}{\sqrt{2}}%
\left( a_{j}^{\dagger }-a_{j}\right) =x_{j+r} \\
\left[ i\mathcal{N}_{a},a_{j}\right] & =-ia_{j} \\
\left[ i\mathcal{N}_{a},x_{j+r}\right] & =\left[ i\mathcal{N}_{a},\frac{i}{%
\sqrt{2}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger }-a_{j}\right) \right] =-\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}%
\left( \left( a_{j}^{\dagger }+a_{j}\right) \right) =-x_{j}.
\end{align}%
The action of the Gauge group and its generator, (the Number operator $%
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}$), on $\mathcal{F}$ is thus given by 
\begin{align}
\widehat{u}_{N_{a}}\left( \theta \right) \left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq
N}a_{j}^{\dagger }\right) & =\prod_{1\leq j\leq N}\widehat{u_{1}}\left(
\theta \right) \left( a_{j}^{\dagger }\right) \\
\left. -i\frac{d}{d\theta }\widehat{u}_{N_{a}}\left( \theta \right)
\right\vert _{\theta =0}\left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq N}a_{j}^{\dagger }\right)
& =\left[ \mathcal{N}_{a},\prod_{1\leq j\leq N}a_{j}^{\dagger }\right] \\
\left. -i\frac{d}{d\theta }\prod_{1\leq j\leq N}\left( \widehat{u_{1}}\left(
\theta \right) \left( a_{j}^{\dagger }\right) \right) \right\vert _{t=0}& =%
\left[ \mathcal{N}_{a},a_{1}^{\dagger }\right] \left( \prod_{2\leq j\leq
N}a_{j}^{\dagger }\right) +a_{1}^{\dagger }\left[ \mathcal{N}%
_{a},a_{2}^{\dagger }\right] \left( \prod_{3\leq j\leq N}a_{j}^{\dagger
}\right) + \\
& \cdots +\left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq p}a_{j}^{\dagger }\right) \left[ 
\mathcal{N}_{a},a_{p+1}^{\dagger }\right] \left( \prod_{p+2\leq j\leq
N}a_{j}^{\dagger }\right) +\cdots +\left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq
N-1}a_{j}^{\dagger }\right) \left[ \mathcal{N}_{a},a_{N}^{\dagger }\right] \\
& =N\left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq N}a_{j}^{\dagger }\right) =N\left( 2^{-\frac{N%
}{2}}\prod_{1\leq j\leq N}\left( x_{j}+ix_{j+r}\right) \right) ,
\end{align}%
as in general any element of $\mathcal{F}$ can be expressed as strong limits
of polynomials formed from the mononomials 
\begin{equation}
\prod_{0\leq j\leq N_{p}}\widehat{u_{1}}\left( \theta \right) \left(
a_{j}^{\dagger }\right) \prod_{0\leq j\leq N_{h}}\widehat{u_{1}}\left(
\theta \right) \left( a_{j}\right) ;0\leq N_{p},N_{h}\leq \infty ,
\end{equation}%
where 
\begin{equation}
a_{0}^{\dagger }=a_{0}=I_{\mathcal{F}}.
\end{equation}%
The general action of any Number operator $\widehat{\mathcal{N}}$ is given
by considering the EOM produced by the Gauge group 
\begin{equation}
-i\frac{dA\left( \theta \right) }{d\theta }=\left[ \mathcal{N},A\left(
\theta \right) \right] =\widehat{\mathcal{N}}\left( A\left( \theta \right)
\right) ,
\end{equation}%
that gives for basis elements of $\mathcal{F}$ 
\begin{align}
& \left. \frac{d}{d\theta }\left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{p}}\widehat{u_{1}}%
\left( \theta \right) \left( a_{j}^{\dagger }\right) \prod_{1\leq j\leq
N_{h}}\widehat{u_{1}}\left( \theta \right) \left( a_{j}\right) \right)
\right\vert _{\theta =0}=\left( N_{p}\prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{p}}a_{j}^{\dagger
}\right) \left( -N_{h}\prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{h}}a_{j}\right) \\
& =\left( N_{p}-N_{h}\right) \prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{p}}a_{j}^{\dagger
}\prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{h}}a_{j}=\left( N_{p}-N_{h}\right) 2^{-\frac{N_{p}}{2}%
}\prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{p}}\left( x_{j}+ix_{j+r}\right) 2^{-\frac{N_{h}}{2}%
}\prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{h}}\left( x_{j}-ix_{j+r}\right) ,
\end{align}%
the last term can be simplified by noting 
\begin{align}
& 2^{-\frac{N_{p}+N_{h}}{2}}\prod_{1\leq j_{p}\leq N_{p}}\left(
x_{j_{p}}+ix_{j_{p}+r}\right) \prod_{1\leq j_{h}\leq N_{h}}\left(
x_{j_{h}}-ix_{j_{h}+r}\right) =2^{-\frac{N_{p}+N_{h}}{2}}\prod_{\substack{ %
1\leq j_{p}\leq N_{p}  \\ 1\leq j_{h}\leq N_{h}}}\left(
x_{j_{p}}+ix_{j_{p}+r}\right) \left( x_{j_{h}}-ix_{j_{h}+r}\right) \\
& =2^{-\frac{N_{p}+N_{h}}{2}}\prod_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{p}\leq N_{p}  \\ %
1\leq j_{h}\leq N_{h}}}\left(
x_{j_{p}}x_{j_{h}}+x_{j_{p}+r}x_{j_{h}+r}+i\left(
x_{j_{p}+r}x_{j_{h}}-x_{j_{p}}x_{j_{h}+r}\right) \right) ,
\end{align}%
giving 
\begin{align}
& \widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}\left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{p}}a_{j}^{\dagger
}\prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{h}}a_{j}\right) =\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}\left( 2^{-%
\frac{N_{p}+N_{h}}{2}}\prod_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{p}\leq N_{p}  \\ 1\leq
j_{h}\leq N_{h}}}\left( x_{j_{p}}x_{j_{h}}+x_{j_{p}+r}x_{j_{h}+r}+i\left(
x_{j_{p}+r}x_{j_{h}}-x_{j_{p}}x_{j_{h}+r}\right) \right) \right) \\
& =\left( N_{p}-N_{h}\right) \left( 2^{-\frac{N_{p}+N_{h}}{2}}\prod 
_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{p}\leq N_{p}  \\ 1\leq j_{h}\leq N_{h}}}\left(
x_{j_{p}}x_{j_{h}}+x_{j_{p}+r}x_{j_{h}+r}+i\left(
x_{j_{p}+r}x_{j_{h}}-x_{j_{p}}x_{j_{h}+r}\right) \right) \right) \\
& =\left( N_{p}-N_{h}\right) \left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{p}}a_{j}^{\dagger
}\prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{h}}a_{j}\right) .
\end{align}%
Thus 
\begin{equation}
\left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{p}}a_{j}^{\dagger }\prod_{1\leq j\leq
N_{h}}a_{j}\right) =\left( 2^{-\frac{N_{p}+N_{h}}{2}}\prod_{\substack{ 1\leq
j_{p}\leq N_{p}  \\ 1\leq j_{h}\leq N_{h}}}\left(
x_{j_{p}}x_{j_{h}}+x_{j_{p}+r}x_{j_{h}+r}+i\left(
x_{j_{p}+r}x_{j_{h}}-x_{j_{p}}x_{j_{h}+r}\right) \right) \right)
\end{equation}%
are eigen operators of $\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a},$which allows one to
decompose $\mathcal{F}$ as the direct sum of the eigen vector spaces 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{F}=\bigoplus\limits_{-\infty \leq N_{a}\leq \infty }\mathcal{F}_{%
\left[ N_{a}\right] },
\end{equation}%
where 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}\mathcal{F}_{\left[ N_{a}\right] }=N_{a}\mathcal{F}%
_{\left[ N_{a}\right] },
\end{equation}%
i.e. $\mathcal{F}_{\left[ N_{a}\right] }$ are invariant, (eigen), subspaces
of $\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}.$ The subspace $\mathcal{F}_{\left[ 0\right] }=%
\mathcal{F}_{e}$ is the even subalgebra of Gauge invariant operators. The
subspaces $N_{a}\neq 0$ are not in general invariant to $SO\left( 2r,\mathbb{%
R}\right) ,SO\left( 2r+1,\mathbb{R}\right) ,$ but are invariant to $U\left(
r,\mathbb{R}\right) .$

In general 
\begin{equation}
\sum_{1\leq j\leq \infty }a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{j}\neq \sum_{1\leq j\leq \infty
}b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{j}
\end{equation}%
even though both $\left\{ a_{j}^{\dagger },a_{j}\right\} $ and $\left\{
b_{j}^{\dagger },b_{j}\right\} $ satisfy the CAR. These correspond to
different complexifications of $\mathcal{F}_{1\mathbb{R}}$ and thus
representations of $\mathcal{F}$ (or in more precise notation $\mathcal{A}%
_{CAR}),$ as representations depend on states. Note that $\mathcal{F}$
usually denotes the standard Fock representation, which is based on a
particular Hilbert space $\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}=\wedge \left( \mathcal{H}%
^{1}\right) .$

\paragraph{Dispersion Theorem}

\begin{theorem}
$Av=\left( v^{\dagger }Av\right) v\Leftrightarrow v^{\dagger }A^{2}v=\left(
v^{\dagger }Av\right) ^{2},$ where $v^{\dagger }v=1$
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$Av=\alpha v\Rightarrow A^{2}v=\alpha ^{2}v\Rightarrow v^{\dagger
}A^{2}v=\left( v^{\dagger }Av\right) ^{2}$

$v^{\dagger }A^{2}v=\left( v^{\dagger }Av\right) ^{2}\Rightarrow v^{\dagger
}\left( A-\left( v^{\dagger }Av\right) I\right) ^{2}v=0$ as this equals

$v^{\dagger }\left( A^{2}-2A\left( v^{\dagger }Av\right) +\left( v^{\dagger
}Av\right) ^{2}I\right) v=0=v^{\dagger }A^{2}v-\left( v^{\dagger }Av\right)
^{2}$ and $\left( \left( A-\left( v^{\dagger }Av\right) I\right) v\right)
^{\dagger }\left( \left( A-\left( v^{\dagger }Av\right) I\right) v\right) =0$
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\Rightarrow \left( A-\left( v^{\dagger }Av\right) I\right) v=0\Rightarrow
Av=\left( v^{\dagger }Av\right) v$ QED.
\end{proof}
\end{theorem}

By using this theorem one obtains for gauge invariant $Q$ 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}\left( Q\right) =NQ\Leftrightarrow\left\langle
\left\langle Q^{\dagger}\widehat{\mathcal{N}}^{2}\left( Q\right)
\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0}=\left\langle \left\langle Q^{\dagger }%
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}\left( Q\right) \right\rangle \right\rangle
_{0}^{2}=N^{2},
\end{equation}
which is equivalent to%
\begin{align}
\left\langle \left\langle \widehat{\mathcal{N}}\left( Q\right) Q^{\dagger
}\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0} & =N \\
\left\langle \left\langle \widehat{\mathcal{N}}\left( QQ^{\dagger}\right)
\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0} & =0.
\end{align}

Gauge properties of general operators can be deduced from 
\begin{align}
& \frac{\left\langle \left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{p}}a_{j}^{\dagger
}\prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{h}}a_{j}\right) ^{\dagger }\widehat{\mathcal{N}}%
\left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{p}}a_{j}^{\dagger }\prod_{1\leq j\leq
N_{h}}a_{j}\right) \right\rangle _{0}}{\left\langle \left( \prod_{1\leq
j\leq N_{p}}a_{j}^{\dagger }\prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{h}}a_{j}\right) ^{\dagger
}\left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{p}}a_{j}^{\dagger }\prod_{1\leq j\leq
N_{h}}a_{j}\right) \right\rangle _{0}} \\
& =\frac{\left\langle \widehat{\mathcal{N}}\left( \left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq
N_{p}}a_{j}^{\dagger }\prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{h}}a_{j}\right) \right) \left(
\prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{p}}a_{j}^{\dagger }\prod_{1\leq j\leq
N_{h}}a_{j}\right) ^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{0}}{\left\langle \left(
\prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{p}}a_{j}^{\dagger }\prod_{1\leq j\leq
N_{h}}a_{j}\right) \left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{p}}a_{j}^{\dagger
}\prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{h}}a_{j}\right) ^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{0}} \\
& =\left( N_{p}-N_{h}\right) I_{\mathcal{F}}
\end{align}

\section{One Electron Example}

Consider the one particle Hilbert space $\mathcal{H}^{1},$ the Fock space
that it generates $\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\equiv \bigwedge \left( \mathcal{%
H}^{1}\right) $ and the Fock algebra $\mathcal{F}\equiv \mathcal{B}\left(
\bigwedge \left( \mathcal{H}^{1}\right) \right) \equiv \mathcal{B}\left( 
\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) .$ Notice $\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}$ is
itself an exterior algebra. Then 
\begin{align}
\mathcal{H}^{1}& \cong \limfunc{linspan}\left\{ \left. a_{j}^{\dagger
}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq r\right\} \\
\overline{\mathcal{H}}^{1}& \cong \limfunc{linspan}\left\{ \left.
a_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq r\right\}
\end{align}%
as 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}^{1d}\cong \overline{\mathcal{H}}^{1}
\end{equation}%
and 
\begin{eqnarray}
a_{j}^{\dagger } &=&\digamma _{1}\left( \varphi _{j}\right) \\
a_{j} &=&\digamma _{2}\left( \overline{\varphi _{j}}\right) ,
\end{eqnarray}%
where 
\begin{equation}
\digamma _{1}:\mathcal{H}^{1}\rightarrow \mathcal{B}\left( \bigwedge \left( 
\mathcal{H}^{1}\right) \right)
\end{equation}%
and 
\begin{equation}
\digamma _{2}:\overline{\mathcal{H}}^{1}\rightarrow \mathcal{B}\left(
\bigwedge \left( \mathcal{H}^{1}\right) \right)
\end{equation}%
are $\mathbb{C}$-linear injections.

We can introduce the notation 
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{F}_{1}=\mathfrak{F}_{10}\oplus \mathfrak{F}_{01}\cong \mathcal{H}%
^{1}\oplus \overline{\mathcal{H}}^{1}
\end{equation}%
and%
\begin{align}
\mathbf{a}& =\mathfrak{F}_{01}=\limfunc{linspan}\left\{ \left.
a_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq r\right\} \\
\mathbf{a}^{\dagger }& =\mathfrak{F}_{10}=\limfunc{linspan}\left\{ \left.
a_{j}^{\dagger }\right\vert 1\leq j\leq r\right\} .
\end{align}%
This gives%
\begin{align}
\mathcal{F}_{n}& =\dbigoplus\limits_{\substack{ p+q=n  \\ 1\leq p,q\leq r}}%
\mathcal{F}_{pq} \\
\mathcal{F}& =\bigoplus\limits_{1\leq n\leq 2r}\mathcal{F}_{n}.
\end{align}%
The subspaces $\left\{ \left. \mathcal{F}_{n}\right\vert 1\leq n\leq
2r\right\} $ and $\left\{ \left. \mathfrak{F}_{pq}\right\vert 1\leq p,q\leq
r\right\} $ are not traceless in contrast to the subspaces $\left\{ \left. 
\mathcal{F}_{n}\right\vert 1\leq n\leq 2r\right\} .$

The subspaces $\left\{ \left. \mathcal{F}_{n}\right\vert 1\leq n\leq
2r\right\} $ are invariant to $O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}\right) ,$while the
subspaces $\left\{ \left. \mathcal{F}_{\left[ N_{a}\right] }\right\vert
-2r\leq N_{a}\leq 2r\right\} $ 
\begin{align}
\mathcal{F}_{\left[ N_{a}\right] }& =\dbigoplus\limits_{\substack{ p-q=N_{a} 
\\ 0\leq p,q\leq r}}\mathcal{F}_{pq} \\
\mathcal{F}& =\bigoplus\limits_{-2r\leq N_{a}\leq 2r}\mathcal{F}_{\left[
N_{a}\right] }
\end{align}%
are not, though they are invariant to $U\left( r\right) $ and are in general
reducible eigen spaces of $\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}.$ Irreducible eigen
spaces associated with the eigenvalue $N_{a}$ are $\left\{ \left. \mathcal{F}%
_{pq}\right\vert p-q=N_{a},0\leq p,q\leq r\right\} .$

Let us consider a one particle Hamiltonian that has finite trace i.e $\in 
\mathcal{B}_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}^{1}\right) $ 
\begin{equation}
H=\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}E_{j}\left\vert \varphi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle
\varphi _{j}\right\vert
\end{equation}%
and its representation in the Fock algebra $\mathcal{F}$ 
\begin{equation}
\Gamma \left( H\right) =\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}E_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{j},
\end{equation}%
where 
\begin{equation}
a_{j}^{\dagger }=\Gamma \left( \left\vert \varphi _{j}\right\rangle
\left\langle \phi \right\vert \right) =a^{\dagger }\left( \varphi
_{j}\right) .
\end{equation}%
$\lceil $One has the following relationships

\begin{enumerate}
\item 
\begin{align}
\left( \sum_{1\leq j\leq r}E_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{j}\right) a_{k}&
=0\Leftrightarrow a_{k}^{\dagger }\left( \sum_{1\leq j\leq
r}E_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{j}\right) =0, \\
\left( \sum_{1\leq j\leq r}E_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{j}\right) a_{k}^{\dagger
}& =\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}E_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }\left( \delta
_{jk}-a_{k}^{\dagger }a_{j}\right) =\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}E_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger
}\delta _{jk}=E_{k}a_{k}^{\dagger }, \\
a_{k}\left( \sum_{1\leq j\leq r}E_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{j}\right) &
=E_{k}a_{k},
\end{align}

\item 
\begin{gather}
\left[ \left( \sum_{1\leq j\leq r}E_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{j}\right)
,a_{k}^{\dagger }\right] =\left( \sum_{1\leq j\leq r}E_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger
}a_{j}\right) a_{k}^{\dagger }-a_{k}^{\dagger }\left( \sum_{1\leq j\leq
r}E_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{j}\right) =E_{k}a_{k}^{\dagger }, \\
\Updownarrow \\
a_{k}\left( \sum_{1\leq j\leq r}E_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{j}\right) -\left(
\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}E_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{j}\right) a_{k}=\left[
a_{k},\left( \sum_{1\leq j\leq r}E_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{j}\right) \right]
=E_{k}a_{k}, \\
\Updownarrow \\
\left( \sum_{1\leq j\leq r}E_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{j}\right)
a_{k}-a_{k}\left( \sum_{1\leq j\leq r}E_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{j}\right) = 
\left[ \left( \sum_{1\leq j\leq r}E_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{j}\right) ,a_{k}%
\right] =-E_{k}a_{k}.
\end{gather}%
$\rfloor $
\end{enumerate}

In the case $r<\infty $ the tracial state $\limfunc{tr}$ on $\mathcal{F}$ is
faithful and produces the GNS\ representation $\Pi _{\limfunc{tr}}\left( 
\mathcal{F}\right) \subseteq \mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{B}_{2}\left( 
\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right) $ on $\Phi _{\limfunc{tr}}\left( 
\mathcal{F}\right) \equiv \mathcal{F}_{HS}\equiv \mathcal{B}_{2}\left( 
\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) ,$ in particular 
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{\limfunc{tr}}\left( \mathcal{F}_{1}\right) =\limfunc{linspan}\left\{
\left. \Phi _{\limfunc{tr}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger }\right) ,\Phi _{\limfunc{tr%
}}\left( a_{j}\right) \right\vert 1\leq j\leq r\right\} .
\end{equation}%
In this representation 
\begin{align}
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\limfunc{tr}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger }\right)
\right\vert \Pi _{\limfunc{tr}}\left( \widehat{H}\right) \Phi _{\limfunc{tr}%
}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger }\right) \right\rangle & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j}%
\widehat{H}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger }\right) \right\} =E_{j}\limfunc{tr}\left\{
a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }\right\} =\frac{E_{j}}{2} \\
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\limfunc{tr}}\left( a_{j}\right) \right\vert \Pi
_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( \widehat{H}\right) \Phi _{\limfunc{tr}}\left(
a_{j}\right) \right\rangle & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j}^{\dagger }\widehat{H}%
\left( a_{j}\right) \right\} =-E_{j}\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j}^{\dagger
}a_{j}\right\} =-\frac{E_{j}}{2}
\end{align}%
\begin{align}
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\limfunc{tr}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger }\right)
\right\vert \Pi _{\limfunc{tr}}\left( H\right) \Phi _{\limfunc{tr}}\left(
a_{j}^{\dagger }\right) \right\rangle & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j}H\left(
a_{j}^{\dagger }\right) \right\} =E_{j}\limfunc{tr}\left\{
a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }\right\} =\frac{E_{j}}{2} \\
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\limfunc{tr}}\left( a_{j}\right) \right\vert \Pi
_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( H\right) \Phi _{\limfunc{tr}}\left( a_{j}\right)
\right\rangle & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j}^{\dagger }H\left( a_{j}\right)
\right\} =0,
\end{align}%
which shows that 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{\limfunc{tr}}\left( \widehat{H}\right) \neq \Pi _{\limfunc{tr}}\left(
H\right) .
\end{equation}%
$($Check against \ref{rep check}$)$ It will be shown that in a vacuum
representation this is not true and in fact 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{vac}\left( \widehat{H}\right) =\Pi _{vac}\left( H\right) .
\end{equation}

\begin{remark}
The Von Neumann algebra $\Pi _{\limfunc{tr}}\left( \mathcal{F}\right)
^{\prime \prime }$ is a types II$_{1}$ Von Neumann algebra when $r=\infty $.
\end{remark}

\section{Vacuum States}

The vacuum state $f_{\left\vert \phi \right\rangle \left\langle \phi
\right\vert }\left( \cdot \right) =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \cdot \left\vert \phi
\right\rangle \left\langle \phi \right\vert \right\} $ on $\mathcal{F}$
gives 
\begin{align}
& \mathcal{H}^{1}\cong \limfunc{linspan}\left\{ \left. \Phi _{\left\vert
\phi \right\rangle \left\langle \phi \right\vert }\left( a_{j}^{\dagger
}\right) \right\vert 1\leq j\leq r\right\} =\Phi _{\left\vert \phi
\right\rangle \left\langle \phi \right\vert }\left( \mathcal{F}_{10}\right)
\\
& \Phi _{\left\vert \phi \right\rangle \left\langle \phi \right\vert }\left( 
\mathcal{F}_{01}\right) =0 \\
& \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}=\Phi _{\left\vert \phi \right\rangle
\left\langle \phi \right\vert }\left( \mathcal{F}\right) =\wedge \left( 
\mathcal{H}^{1}\right) .
\end{align}%
A (normal) vacuum state is characterized by 
\begin{align}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ D_{vac}\right\} & =1 \\
D_{vac}& \geq 0 \\
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}\left( D_{vac}\right) & =0 \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \mathcal{N}_{a}D_{vac}\right\} & =0,
\end{align}%
where $\mathcal{N}_{a}$ is the number operator corresponding to the non self
adjoint basis $\left\{ \left. a_{j},a_{j}^{\dagger }\right\vert 1\leq j\leq
r\right\} $ and 
\begin{equation}
\Gamma _{a}\left( D_{vac}\right) =I_{\mathcal{F}}.
\end{equation}%
$\lceil $A basic condition for $f_{\left\vert \phi \left( g\right)
\right\rangle \left\langle \phi \left( g\right) \right\vert }\left( \cdot
\right) $ being a vacuum state is the zero grade condition 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \phi \left( g\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \phi \left(
g\right) \right\vert \cdot \left\vert \phi \left( g\right) \right\rangle
\left\langle \phi \left( g\right) \right\vert =\left\vert \phi \left(
g\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \phi \left( g\right) \right\vert
\end{equation}%
and 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \phi \left( g\right) \right\rangle \wedge \left\vert \phi \left(
g\right) \right\rangle =\left\vert \phi \left( g\right) \right\rangle ,
\end{equation}%
\underline{\emph{but this is only true in the GNS representation produced by
the dressed vacuums}}\emph{, the representation we have defined the dressed
vacuums in is the one produced by the bare vacuum. }The bare vacuum $%
\left\vert \phi \right\rangle $ is obtained when $g=0$ i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \phi \right\rangle =\exp \left\{ 0\right\} \left\vert \phi
\right\rangle .
\end{equation}

$\emph{GRADE}$ \emph{ZERO} \emph{PROPERTIES OF THE VACUUM VECTOR STATE ARE
ONLY REALIZED IN THE GNS REPRESENTATION OF A VACUUM STATE }$\rfloor $

\begin{remark}
One can observe that, $\limfunc{tr},$ \emph{is not a vacuum state}.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
Normal states are pure in Type $I_{r}$ Von Neumann algebras if 
\begin{equation}
D=D^{2}=D^{\dagger }.
\end{equation}
\end{remark}

The action of the representation of the "annihilators" on the image of the
identity (the generating state, (but not necessarily a vacuum state), for
any representation) is 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{f}\left( a_{j}\right) \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) =\Phi
_{f}\left( a_{j}\right)
\end{equation}%
giving in the case of vacuum representations 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j}\right) \right\vert \Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j}\right) \right\rangle =f_{a}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger
}a_{j}\right) =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{j}D_{f_{a}}\right\} ,
\end{equation}%
showing that if 
\begin{equation}
f\left( a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{j}\right) =0
\end{equation}%
then 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{f}\left( a_{j}\right) \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) =0.
\end{equation}%
Thus a state $f$ is a vacuum state \emph{and produces a vacuum vector }$\Phi
_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $\emph{\ via the GNS construction} if
there exists a splitting of $\mathcal{F}_{1}$ into 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{F}_{1}=\mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\oplus \mathbf{a}
\end{equation}%
such that 
\begin{align}
& \mathbf{a\subset }\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f}\right\} \\
& \mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\cap \ker \left\{ \Phi _{f}\right\} =\phi .
\end{align}%
(Note that $\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f}\right\} $ is a left ideal containing the
two sided ideal $\tbigwedge \mathbf{a}$)

\begin{theorem}
\begin{enumerate}
\item In any even dimensional Clifford Alebra the vector subspace, $\mathcal{%
F}_{1}$ (grade 1 elements), can always be split into a direct sum 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{F}_{1}=\mathbf{b}^{\dagger }\oplus \mathbf{b}
\end{equation}%
of non self adjoint subspaces of elements that are adjoints of each other
and satisfy the CAR.

\item Each subspace decomposition $\mathbf{b}^{\dagger }\oplus \mathbf{b}$
is left invariant by a representation $\widehat{\mathcal{R}_{b}}$ $\left(
U\left( r\right) \right) $ of $U\left( r\right) .$

\item The decompositions are not unique and are related to each other by
transformations belonging to $\widehat{\mathcal{R}_{b}}\left( \left. O\left(
2r,\mathbb{R}\right) \right/ U\left( r\right) \right) $ i.e. nontrivial
cannonical transformations$.$
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{lemma}
Any element $A\in \mathcal{A}\equiv \mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{A}%
_{HS}\right) $ can be expanded as 
\begin{equation}
A=\tsum\limits_{0\leq p,q\leq r}\tsum\limits_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots
<j_{p}\leq r  \\ 1\leq k_{1}<\cdots <k_{qp}\leq r}}A_{j_{1}\cdots
j_{p}k_{1}\cdots k_{q}}\tprod\limits_{0\leq l\leq p}b_{j_{l}}^{\dagger
}\tprod\limits_{0\leq m\leq q}b_{k_{m}}
\end{equation}%
in the strong operator topology induced by $\mathcal{A}_{HS}$ and the left
regular representation i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\lim\limits_{j\rightarrow \infty }\left\{ \mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{
X^{\dagger }\left( A-A_{j}\right) ^{\dagger }\left( A-A_{j}\right) X\right\}
\right\} \forall X=0\equiv s-\lim\limits_{j}A_{j}=A.
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}

\begin{definition}
$f$ $\in \mathcal{S}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ is a vacuum state wrt a
decomposition $\mathbf{b}^{\dagger }\oplus \mathbf{b}$ $\limfunc{Iff}$ 
\begin{equation}
f\left( \widehat{\mathcal{R}_{b}}\left( \widehat{u}\left( A\right) \right)
\right) =f\left( A\right) \,\forall A\in \mathcal{A}
\end{equation}%
and is denoted by $f_{b},$ where $\widehat{\mathcal{R}_{b}}$ is a
representation of $U\left( r\right) .$
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}
$\left\{ b_{1},\cdots ,b_{r}\right\} $ generates a left ideal, $\mathcal{L}%
_{b},$ in $\mathcal{A}.$
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
\begin{equation}
A\left( \tprod\limits_{1\leq k\leq n}b_{j_{k}}\right) \in \mathcal{L}%
_{b};\,1\leq j_{k}\leq r,\,\forall A\in \mathcal{A}.
\end{equation}
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}
The left ideal $\mathcal{L}_{b}$ is the kernal, $\ker \left\{ \Phi
_{f_{b}}\right\} $ of the GNS\ map $\Phi _{f_{b}}$ i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{b}=\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f_{b}}\right\} .
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
\begin{eqnarray}
f_{b}\left( A\left( \tprod\limits_{1\leq k\leq n}b_{j_{k}}\right) ^{\dagger
}A\left( \tprod\limits_{1\leq k\leq n}b_{j_{k}}\right) \right)
&=&f_{b}\left( \left( \tprod\limits_{1\leq k\leq n}b_{j_{k}}\right)
^{\dagger }A^{\dagger }A\left( \tprod\limits_{1\leq k\leq n}b_{j_{k}}\right)
\right) =0 \\
\,1 &\leq &j_{k}\leq r,\,\forall A\in \mathcal{A},
\end{eqnarray}%
as 
\begin{equation}
A=\tsum\limits_{0\leq p,q\leq r}\tsum\limits_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots
<j_{p}\leq r  \\ 1\leq k_{1}<\cdots <k_{qp}\leq r}}A_{j_{1}\cdots
j_{p}k_{1}\cdots k_{q}}\tprod\limits_{0\leq l\leq p}b_{j_{l}}^{\dagger
}\tprod\limits_{0\leq m\leq q}b_{k_{m}}.
\end{equation}
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
If $\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $ is a vacuum vector
belonging to the Fock space $\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( \mathcal{F}\right) $ then
it is orthogonal to all other vectors in $\mathcal{H}_{f_{a}}$ as 
\begin{equation}
f_{a}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }\right)
=\left\langle \left. \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right\vert
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }\right)
\right\rangle =0;1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq r,1\leq n\leq r,
\end{equation}%
this follows directly from 
\begin{equation}
a_{j_{1}}\cdots a_{j_{n}}\in \ker \left\{ \Phi _{f_{a}}\right\} ;1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq r,1\leq n\leq r
\end{equation}%
and that $\left\{ a_{1},\cdots ,a_{r}\right\} $ generate a left ideal.
\end{remark}

States that are vacuum states produce generalized quasi free Fock
representations (or Fock representations) that are not all unitarily
equivalent. Not all states are vacuum states and these produce non Fock
representations of the CAR.

$\limfunc{tr}$ $\equiv I_{\mathcal{F}}$ is not a vacuum state. This can be
seen by considering the values of $\limfunc{tr}$ \emph{on any} split non
self adjoint basis that satisfy the CAR which are given by 
\begin{align}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{k}\right\} & =\left\langle
\left\langle a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{k}\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0}=\frac{1}{2%
}\left\langle \left\langle \left( x_{j}-ix_{j+r}\right) \left(
x_{k}+ix_{k+r}\right) \right\rangle \right\rangle _{0}=\frac{1}{2}%
\left\langle \left\langle
x_{j}x_{k}-ix_{j+r}x_{k}+ix_{j}x_{k+r}+x_{j+r}x_{k+r}\right\rangle
\right\rangle _{0} \\
& =\frac{1}{2}\left\langle \left\langle
x_{j}x_{k}-ix_{j+r}x_{k}+ix_{j}x_{k+r}+x_{j+r}x_{k+r}\right\rangle
\right\rangle _{0}=\frac{1}{2}\delta _{jk}=\limfunc{tr}\left\{
a_{k}a_{j}^{\dagger }\right\} \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{k}a_{j}^{\dagger }\right\} & =\left\langle
\left\langle a_{k}a_{j}^{\dagger }\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0}=\limfunc{%
tr}\left\{ \delta _{jk}-a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{k}\right\} =\delta _{jk}-\limfunc{%
tr}\left\{ a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{k}\right\} \\
& \Rightarrow \left\{ 
\begin{array}{c}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{k}a_{j}^{\dagger }\right\} =-\limfunc{tr}\left\{
a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{k}\right\} ;j\neq k \\ 
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }\right\} =1-\limfunc{tr}\left\{
a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{j}\right\} ;j=k%
\end{array}%
\right. \\
& \Rightarrow \limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }\right\} =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{j}\right\} =\frac{1}{2} \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j}a_{k}\right\} & =-\limfunc{tr}\left\{
a_{k}a_{j}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{k}a_{j}\right\} \Rightarrow 
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j}a_{k}\right\} =0
\end{align}%
Thus 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{0}=\left\langle
a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{j}\right\rangle _{0}=\frac{1}{2}\neq 0
\end{equation}%
and hence 
\begin{equation}
\left\{ a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger },a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{j}\right\} \notin
\bigoplus\limits_{1\leq n\leq 2r}\mathcal{F}_{n}
\end{equation}%
as they contain components belonging to $\mathcal{F}_{0}$.

The values on the associated (unnormalized) self adjoint bases are then 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ x_{j}x_{k}\right\} =\delta _{jk}\left\langle
\left\langle x_{j}^{2}\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0}=\frac{1}{2}\delta
_{jk};1\leq j\leq 2r.
\end{equation}%
This shows that $\limfunc{tr}$ is \emph{NOT }a vacuum state. Hence the
representation $\Pi _{\limfunc{tr}}$ is \emph{NOT }a Fock representation and 
\emph{in fact generates a type II}$_{1}$ Von Neumann algebra. In contrast
Fock representations produce \emph{type I}$_{r}$ Von Neumann algebras. These
observations show that the Von Neumann algebra generated by the left regular 
$c^{\ast }$-algebra representation of the CAR/Clifford algebra induced by
the central state $\limfunc{tr}$ that defines its $c^{\ast }$-algebra norm,
can thus be viewed as a type II$_{1}$ $w^{\ast }$-algebra/Von Neumann
algebra.

\paragraph{Splitting of $\mathcal{F}_{1}$ into non self adjoint subspaces
and Number Opeators.}

The number operators $\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}$ based on $\left\{ \left.
a_{j}^{\dagger},a_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq r\right\} $ split $\mathcal{F}%
_{1}$ into eigenspaces corresponding to the eigenvalues $+1$ and $-1$ i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{F}_{1}=\mathbf{a}^{\dagger}\oplus\mathbf{a}
\end{equation}
and have the properties%
\begin{align}
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) & =\left[ \mathcal{N}%
_{a},a_{j}^{\dagger}\right] =\left[ \sum_{1\leq k\leq
r}a_{k}^{\dagger}a_{k},a_{j}^{\dagger}\right] =a_{j}^{\dagger} \\
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}\left( a_{j}\right) & =\left[ \mathcal{N}_{a},a_{j}%
\right] =\left[ \sum_{1\leq k\leq r}a_{k}^{\dagger}a_{k},a_{j}^{\dagger}%
\right] =-a_{j} \\
\left\langle \left. a_{k}\right\vert \widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}\left(
a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle & =\left\langle a_{k}^{\dagger }%
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle
_{0}=\left\langle \left. a_{k}\right\vert a_{j}^{\dagger}\right\rangle =%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{k}^{\dagger}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right\} =-\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{k}^{\dagger}\right\} \Rightarrow\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ a_{k}^{\dagger}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right\} =0 \\
\left\langle \left. a_{k}^{\dagger}\right\vert \widehat{\mathcal{N}}%
_{a}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle & =\left\langle a_{k}^{{}}%
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle
_{0}=\left\langle \left. a_{k}^{\dagger}\right\vert a_{j}^{\dagger
}\right\rangle =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{k}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right\} =\left\{ 
\begin{array}{c}
0\text{ if }k\neq j \\ 
\frac{1}{2}\text{ if }k=j%
\end{array}
\right. \\
\left\langle \left. a_{k}^{{}}\right\vert \widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}\left(
a_{j}^{{}}\right) \right\rangle & =\left\langle a_{k}^{\dagger }\widehat{%
\mathcal{N}}_{a}\left( a_{j}^{{}}\right) \right\rangle _{0}=\left\langle
\left. a_{k}^{{}}\right\vert -a_{j}^{{}}\right\rangle =-\limfunc{tr}\left\{
a_{k}^{\dagger}a_{j}^{{}}\right\} =\left\{ 
\begin{array}{c}
0\text{ if }k\neq j \\ 
-\frac{1}{2}\text{ if }k=j%
\end{array}
\right. \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{k}^{\dagger}a_{k}^{{}}+a_{k}a_{k}^{\dagger }\right\}
& =\frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{2}=1 \\
\dim\left\{ \mathbf{a}^{\dagger}\right\} & =\dim\left\{ \mathbf{a}\right\}
=r.
\end{align}

One also has the real linear conjugation maps%
\begin{align}
\left. \sigma _{a}\right\vert _{\mathcal{F}_{1}}& :\mathbf{a}^{\dagger
}\rightarrow \mathbf{a} \\
\left. \sigma _{a}\right\vert _{\mathcal{F}_{1}}& :\mathbf{a}\rightarrow 
\mathbf{a}^{\dagger }
\end{align}%
and 
\begin{align}
\dagger & :\mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\rightarrow \mathbf{a} \\
\dagger & :\mathbf{a}\rightarrow \mathbf{a}^{\dagger }.
\end{align}%
We can express $\left. \sigma _{a}\right\vert _{\mathcal{F}_{1}}$ as 
\begin{equation}
\left. \sigma _{a}\right\vert _{\mathcal{F}_{1}}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}\left\{
\left\vert a_{j}^{{}}\right) _{\mathcal{F}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger
}\right\vert +\left\vert a_{j}^{\dagger }\right) _{\mathcal{F}}\left(
a_{j}^{{}}\right\vert \right\} .
\end{equation}

$\left\{ a_{j}^{\dagger},a_{j}\right\} $ are partial isometries with
associated projectors $\left\{ n_{aj},h_{aj}\right\} $ as 
\begin{equation}
n_{aj}=a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j};h_{aj}=a_{j}a_{j}^{%
\dagger};n_{aj}^{2}=n_{aj};h_{aj}^{2}=h_{aj}.
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j}+a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}=I_{\mathcal{F}}=n_{aj}+h_{aj}.
\end{equation}

\begin{theorem}
$\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}\left( Q\right) =N_{a}Q\Longleftrightarrow
Q=\left\langle Q\right\rangle _{N_{a}}\in\mathcal{F}_{\left[ N_{a}\right]
};-\infty\leq N_{a}\leq\infty$
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $\widehat{\mathcal{N}}\left( Q\right) =mQ=m\sum\limits_{-\infty\leq
N_{a}\leq\infty}Q_{\left[ N_{a}\right] }=\sum\limits_{-\infty\leq
N_{a}\leq\infty}N_{a}Q_{\left[ N_{a}\right] }$ then $m=N_{a}$ and $\widehat{%
\mathcal{N}}\left( Q\right) =\widehat{\mathcal{N}}\left( Q_{\left[ N_{a}%
\right] }\right) =N_{a}Q_{\left[ N_{a}\right] }$

If $Q=Q_{\left[ N_{a}\right] }$ then $\widehat{\mathcal{N}}\left( Q_{\left[
N_{a}\right] }\right) =N_{a}Q_{\left[ N_{a}\right] }.$
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$QQ^{\dagger }$ and $Q^{\dagger }Q\in \mathcal{F}_{\left[ 0\right] }=%
\mathcal{F}_{e}.$
\end{corollary}

\paragraph{Constructing Vacuum States}

Recall from the preceeding $f_{b}$ is a vacuum state Iff%
\begin{equation}
f_{b}\left( \widehat{u}\left( A\right) \right) =f_{b}\left( A\right)
\,\,\forall \widehat{u}\in \mathcal{R}_{b}\left( U\left( r\right) \right)
,\forall A\in \mathcal{A}.
\end{equation}

This can be expressed using the representation, $\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{A}%
}\left( O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}\right) \right) $, of $O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}%
\right) $ as inner $c^{\ast }$-algebra automorphisms of the CAR/Clifford
algebra $\mathcal{A}$ by 
\begin{equation}
f_{b}\left( A\right) =f_{a}\left( \widehat{o}\left( A\right) \right)
=f_{a}\left( o^{\dagger }Ao\right) ,\text{ }\widehat{o}\in \limfunc{Inn}%
U\left( \mathcal{A}\right) \Leftrightarrow o\in U\left( 2^{r}\right) ,
\label{vac_orbit}
\end{equation}%
where $\widehat{o}\in \mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{A}}\left( O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}%
\right) \right) .$ Thus all vacuum states lie on the same orbit of $\mathcal{%
R}_{\mathcal{A}}\left( O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}\right) \right) $ in $\mathcal{A}%
.$ As 
\begin{equation}
O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}\right) =\left( \left. O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}\right)
\right/ U\left( r\right) \right) U\left( r\right)
\end{equation}%
and vacuum states are invariant to $U\left( r\right) $ this can be
reexpressed as 
\begin{equation}
f_{b}\left( A\right) =f_{a}\left( \widehat{\mathfrak{c}}\left( A\right)
\right) ,
\end{equation}%
where $\widehat{\mathfrak{c}}\in $ $\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{A}}\left( \left(
\left. O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}\right) \right/ U\left( r\right) \right) U\left(
r\right) \right) $, 
\begin{equation}
\text{ }\widehat{o}=\widehat{\mathfrak{c}}\widehat{u}
\end{equation}%
and $\widehat{u}\in \mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{A}}\left( U\left( r\right) \right) 
$.

$\lceil $If $f_{b}$ is a vacuum state then $\exists \left\{ \left.
b_{j}^{\dagger },b_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq r\right\} $ such that%
\begin{align}
f_{b}\left( n_{bj}\right) & =f_{b}\left( b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{j}\right)
=f_{b}\left( b_{j}^{\dagger }\right) =f_{b}\left( b_{j}\right) =0 \\
f_{b}\left( h_{bj}\right) & =f_{b}\left( b_{j}b_{j}^{\dagger }\right)
=1=f_{b}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) ;1\leq j\leq r
\end{align}%
and%
\begin{align}
n_{bj}D_{f_{b}}& =0;\text{ }h_{bj}D_{f_{b}}=D_{f_{b}} \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ n_{bj}D_{f_{b}}\right\} & =0;\limfunc{tr}\left\{
h_{bj}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =1;1\leq j\leq r \\
\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f_{b}}\right\} & =\mathcal{F\times }\limfunc{ideal}%
\left\{ \left. b_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq r\right\} .
\end{align}%
Vacuum states are $\widehat{\mathcal{N}_{b}}$ guage invariant, thus
correspond to different complexifications of $\mathcal{F}_{1\mathbb{R}}$ and
satisfy the orthonormality relationships

\begin{eqnarray}
f_{b}\left( \prod_{1\leq \alpha \leq N_{h}}b_{k_{\alpha }}\prod_{1\leq \beta
\leq N_{p}}b_{j_{\beta }}^{\dagger }\right) &=&\delta _{k_{1}\cdots
k_{N_{h}}j_{1}\cdots j_{N_{p}}} \\
&=&\func{Tr}\left\{ \prod_{1\leq \alpha \leq N_{h}}\Pi _{f_{b}}\left(
b_{k_{\alpha }}\right) \prod_{1\leq \beta \leq N_{p}}\Pi _{f_{b}}\left(
b_{j_{\beta }}^{\dagger }\right) \left\vert \Phi _{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A%
}}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\vert \right\} \\
&=&\left\langle \Phi _{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \left\vert
\prod_{1\leq \alpha \leq N_{h}}\Pi _{f_{b}}\left( b_{k_{\alpha }}\right)
\prod_{1\leq \beta \leq N_{p}}\Pi _{f_{b}}\left( b_{j_{\beta }}^{\dagger
}\right) \right. \Phi _{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle .
\end{eqnarray}%
$\rfloor $

Utilizing the representation 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{A}}\left( O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}\right) \right) =\exp
\left\{ \sum_{1\leq j<k\leq r}\left( \gamma _{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger
}a_{k}^{\dagger }-\overline{\gamma }_{jk}a_{k}a_{j}\right) \right\} \exp
\left\{ i\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq r}\lambda _{jk}\left( \gamma \right)
a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{k}\right\} =\widehat{\mathfrak{c}}\left( \gamma \right) 
\widehat{u}\left( \gamma \right)
\end{equation}%
then one can generate the orbit of vacuum states $\left\{ f_{b}\right\} $
from the vacuum state $f_{a}$ by 
\begin{equation}
f_{b}\left( A\right) =\mathcal{N}\left( \gamma \right) ^{2}f_{a}\left( \exp
\left\{ \sum_{1\leq j<k\leq r}\left( \gamma _{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger
}a_{k}^{\dagger }-\overline{\gamma }_{jk}a_{k}a_{j}\right) \right\}
^{\dagger }A\exp \left\{ \sum_{1\leq j<k\leq r}\left( \gamma
_{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{k}^{\dagger }-\overline{\gamma }_{jk}a_{k}a_{j}%
\right) \right\} \right) ,
\end{equation}%
which can often be expressed as 
\begin{equation}
f_{b}\left( A\right) =\mathcal{N}\left( \mathbf{g}\right) ^{2}f_{a}\left(
\exp \left\{ \tsum\limits_{1\leq j<k\leq r}g_{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger
}a_{k}^{\dagger }\right\} ^{\dagger }A\exp \left\{ \tsum\limits_{1\leq
j<k\leq r}g_{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{k}^{\dagger }\right\} \right) .
\end{equation}

\begin{remark}
One can consider $f_{a}$ to be the "\emph{naked}" or "\emph{free}" vacuum
state and $\left\{ f_{b}\right\} $ to be "\emph{dressed}" or "\emph{quasi
free}" vacuum states. Within $\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ the
vector $\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) $ is the naked vacuum
vector and $\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( \widehat{\mathfrak{c}}\left( \gamma \right) 
\mathcal{A}\widehat{\mathfrak{c}}\left( \gamma \right) ^{\dagger }\right) $
dressed vacuum vectors, while within $\Phi _{f_{b}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) 
$ the vector $\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) $ is the naked
vacuum and $\Phi _{f_{b}}\left( \widehat{\mathfrak{c}}\left( \gamma \right) 
\mathcal{A}\widehat{\mathfrak{c}}\left( \gamma \right) ^{\dagger }\right) $
are dressed vacuum vectors.
\end{remark}

Some relationships between expectation values and GNS\ representations are
given by\qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad
\qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad
\qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad
\qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad
\qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad
\qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad
\qquad\ 
\begin{eqnarray}
f_{b}\left( A\right) &=&f_{a}\left( \exp \left\{ \tsum\limits_{1\leq j<k\leq
r}g_{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{k}^{\dagger }\right\} ^{\dagger }A\exp \left\{
\tsum\limits_{1\leq j<k\leq r}g_{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{k}^{\dagger }\right\}
\right) =\left\langle \Phi _{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \left\vert
\Pi _{f_{b}}\left( A\right) \right. \Phi _{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\rangle \\
&=&\left\langle \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \left\vert \Pi
_{f_{a}}\left( \exp \left\{ \tsum\limits_{1\leq j<k\leq
r}g_{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{k}^{\dagger }\right\} \right) \Pi _{f_{a}}\left(
A\right) \Pi _{f_{a}}\left( \exp \left\{ \tsum\limits_{1\leq j<k\leq
r}g_{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{k}^{\dagger }\right\} \right) \right. \Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle ,
\end{eqnarray}%
\begin{eqnarray}
f_{b}\left( A\right) &=&f_{a}\left( \exp \left\{ \tsum\limits_{1\leq j<k\leq
r}g_{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{k}^{\dagger }\right\} ^{\dagger }A\exp \left\{
\tsum\limits_{1\leq j<k\leq r}g_{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{k}^{\dagger }\right\}
\right) =\left\langle \Phi _{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \left\vert
\Pi _{f_{b}}\left( A\right) \right. \Phi _{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\rangle \\
&=&\left\langle \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \left\vert \Pi
_{f_{a}}\left( \exp \left\{ \tsum\limits_{1\leq j<k\leq
r}g_{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{k}^{\dagger }\right\} \right) \Pi _{f_{a}}\left(
A\right) \Pi _{f_{a}}\left( \exp \left\{ \tsum\limits_{1\leq j<k\leq
r}g_{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{k}^{\dagger }\right\} \right) \right. \Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle ,
\end{eqnarray}%
and%
\begin{eqnarray}
&&\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{f_{b}}\left( A\right) \left\vert \Phi
_{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi
_{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \right\} \\
&=&\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{f_{a}}\left( \exp \left\{ \tsum\limits_{1\leq
j<k\leq r}g_{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{k}^{\dagger }\right\} \right) ^{\dagger
}\Pi _{f_{a}}\left( A\right) \Pi _{f_{a}}\left( \exp \left\{
\tsum\limits_{1\leq j<k\leq r}g_{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{k}^{\dagger }\right\}
\right) \left\vert \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle
\left\langle \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \right\}
\\
&=&\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{f_{a}}\left( A\right) \Pi _{f_{a}}\left( \exp
\left\{ \tsum\limits_{1\leq j<k\leq r}g_{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{k}^{\dagger
}\right\} \right) \left\vert \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\vert \Pi _{f_{a}}\left( \exp \left\{ \tsum\limits_{1\leq j<k\leq
r}g_{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{k}^{\dagger }\right\} \right) ^{\dagger }\right\}
\end{eqnarray}%
where $\left\{ a_{j}^{\dagger },a_{j}\right\} $ refer to standard fermion
particles.

\begin{theorem}
The GNS representation, $\Pi _{f_{a}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ of $%
\mathcal{A}$ induced by a vacuum state $f_{a}$ is produced by the Hilbert
space 
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) =\overline{\limfunc{linspan}}\left\{
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) ,\left\{ \left. \Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{m}}^{\dagger }\right)
\right\vert 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{m}\leq r,1\leq m\leq r\right\} \right\}
\equiv \mathcal{H}_{f_{a}}=\tbigwedge \left( \mathcal{H}_{f_{a}}^{1}\right) ,
\end{equation}%
where 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}_{f_{a}}^{1}=\overline{\limfunc{linspan}}\left\{ \left. \Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) \right\vert 1\leq j_{1}\leq
r\right\} =\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\right)
\end{equation}%
and is a Fock representation.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}
$\Phi _{f_{a}}$ is an exterior algebra isomorphism 
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f_{a}}:\tbigwedge \left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\right) \rightarrow
\tbigwedge \left( \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\right) \right)
\equiv \tbigwedge \left( \mathcal{H}_{f_{a}}^{1}\right) .
\end{equation}

\begin{proof}
The map $\Phi _{f_{a}}$ is injective 
\begin{equation}
\ker \left\{ \left. \overset{}{\Phi _{f_{a}}}\right\vert _{\tbigwedge \left( 
\mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\right) }\right\} =0,
\end{equation}%
surjective 
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( \tbigwedge \left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\right) \right)
=\tbigwedge \left( \mathcal{H}_{f_{a}}^{1}\right)
\end{equation}
and is a homomorphism 
\begin{equation*}
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{m}}^{\dagger }\right)
=\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge _{\mathcal{A}}\cdots \wedge
_{\mathcal{A}}a_{j_{m}}^{\dagger }\right) =\Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) \wedge _{\mathcal{A}}\cdots \wedge _{\mathcal{A}%
}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{m}}^{\dagger }\right) .
\end{equation*}
\end{proof}
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
The map $\Phi _{f_{a}}$is not injective on $\mathcal{A}$ as 
\begin{equation}
\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f_{a}}\right\} =\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{a}}\neq 0,
\end{equation}%
where $\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{a}}$ is the left ideal generated by $\mathbf{a.}$
\end{remark}

\begin{notation}
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) =\tbigwedge \left( \mathcal{H}%
_{f_{a}}^{1}\right)
\end{equation}%
and 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{f_{a}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) =\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}%
_{f_{a}}\right) \equiv \mathcal{B}\left( \tbigwedge \left( \mathcal{H}%
_{f_{a}}^{1}\right) \right) \equiv \mathcal{B}\left( \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( 
\mathcal{A}\right) \right) .
\end{equation}
\end{notation}

\paragraph{Naked Vacuum}

The vacuum vector $\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) $ corresponds
to $\left\vert \phi _{a}\right\rangle \in \tbigwedge \left( \mathcal{H}%
_{f_{a}}\right) $ as 
\begin{gather}
\Pi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{m}}^{\dagger }\right)
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) =\Pi _{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{m}}^{\dagger }\right) \left\vert \phi
_{a}\right\rangle =\left\vert \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
a_{j_{m}}^{\dagger }\right) \right\rangle  \notag \\
\Pi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{k_{1}}\cdots a_{k_{n}}\right) \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) =\Pi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{k_{1}}\cdots a_{k_{n}}\right)
\left\vert \phi _{a}\right\rangle =0 \\
1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{m}\leq r,1\leq k_{1}<\cdots <k_{n}\leq r;1\leq
m,n\leq r
\end{gather}%
and 
\begin{eqnarray}
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \wedge _{\Phi _{f_{a}}}\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{m}}^{\dagger }\right)
&=&\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \wedge \Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge \cdots \wedge a_{j_{m}}^{\dagger }\right) \\
&=&\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\wedge a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge
\cdots \wedge a_{j_{m}}^{\dagger }\right) =\Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge \cdots \wedge a_{j_{m}}^{\dagger }\right) \\
&\updownarrow & \\
\left\vert \phi _{a}\right\rangle \wedge \left\vert \varphi _{j_{1}}\wedge
\cdots \wedge \varphi _{j_{m}}\right\rangle &=&\left\vert \phi _{a}\wedge
\varphi _{j_{1}}\wedge \cdots \wedge \varphi _{j_{m}}\right\rangle
=\left\vert \varphi _{j_{1}}\wedge \cdots \wedge \varphi
_{j_{m}}\right\rangle .
\end{eqnarray}%
Thus $\left\vert \phi _{a}\right\rangle \equiv \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) $ is a grade zero element of $\tbigwedge \left( \mathcal{%
H}_{f_{a}}^{1}\right) .$

The state $\Phi _{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) $ might correspond to
a state in $\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ i.e. via a vector space
isomorphism, where both $f_{a}$ and $f_{b}$ are vacuum states, \emph{BUT NOT
NECESSARILY BY} an unitary exterior algebra isomorphism.

\begin{enumerate}
\item The dressed states 
\begin{equation}
\prod_{1\leq \beta \leq N_{p}}b_{j_{\beta }}^{\dagger }\left\vert \overset{}{%
\phi _{b}}\right\rangle \left\langle \overset{}{\phi _{b}}\right\vert \left(
\prod_{1\leq \beta \leq N_{p}}b_{j_{\beta }}\right) ^{\dagger }
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
=\prod_{1\leq \beta \leq N_{p}}b_{j_{\beta }}^{\dagger }\exp \left\{
\tsum\limits_{1\leq j<k\leq r}g_{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{k}^{\dagger }\right\}
\left\vert \overset{}{\phi _{a}}\right\rangle \left\langle \overset{}{\phi
_{a}}\right\vert \exp \left\{ \tsum\limits_{1\leq j<k\leq
r}g_{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{k}^{\dagger }\right\} ^{\dagger }\left(
\prod_{1\leq \beta \leq N_{p}}b_{j_{\beta }}^{\dagger }\right) ^{\dagger },
\end{equation}%
are also vacuum states, where 
\begin{equation}
b_{l}\exp \left\{ \tsum\limits_{1\leq j<k\leq r}g_{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger
}a_{k}^{\dagger }\right\} \left\vert \overset{}{\phi _{a}}\right\rangle
=0;1\leq j\leq r
\end{equation}%
for some $g$.

\item The states 
\begin{equation}
\prod_{1\leq \beta \leq N_{p}}c_{j_{\beta }}^{\dagger }\exp \left\{
\tsum\limits_{1\leq j<k\leq r}g_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{k}^{\dagger }\right\}
\left\vert \overset{}{\phi _{b}}\right\rangle \left\langle \overset{}{\phi
_{b}}\right\vert \exp \left\{ \tsum\limits_{1\leq j<k\leq
r}g_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{k}^{\dagger }\right\} ^{\dagger }\left(
\prod_{1\leq \beta \leq N_{p}}c_{j_{\beta }}^{\dagger }\right) ^{\dagger },
\end{equation}%
are also vacuum states where 
\begin{equation}
c_{l}\exp \left\{ \tsum\limits_{1\leq j<k\leq r}g_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger
}b_{k}^{\dagger }\right\} \left\vert \overset{}{\phi _{b}}\right\rangle
=0;1\leq j\leq r,
\end{equation}%
$\lceil $which can be further expanded to give%
\begin{equation}
c_{l}\exp \left\{ \tsum\limits_{1\leq j<k\leq r}g_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger
}b_{k}^{\dagger }\right\} \exp \left\{ \tsum\limits_{1\leq j<k\leq
r}g_{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{k}^{\dagger }\right\} \left\vert \overset{}{\phi
_{a}}\right\rangle =0;1\leq j\leq r
\end{equation}%
$\rfloor $
\end{enumerate}

and $\left\{ \left. c_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq r\right\} $ are general
Bogoliubon annihilators.

$\left\vert \overset{}{\phi _{b}}\right\rangle $ are $O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}%
\right) $ coherent states i.e. 
\begin{eqnarray}
\left\vert \overset{}{\phi _{b}}\right\rangle &=&\exp \left\{
\tsum\limits_{1\leq j<k\leq r}z_{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{k}^{\dagger }\right\}
\exp \left\{ \tsum\limits_{1\leq j,k\leq r}h_{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger
}a_{k}\right\} \exp \left\{ \tsum\limits_{1\leq j<k\leq r}\overline{z}%
_{jk}a_{j}a_{k}\right\} \left\vert \overset{}{\phi _{a}}\right\rangle \\
&=&\exp \left\{ \tsum\limits_{1\leq j<k\leq r}z_{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger
}a_{k}^{\dagger }\right\} \left\vert \overset{}{\phi _{a}}\right\rangle .
\end{eqnarray}%
$\rfloor $

\paragraph{GNS representations produced by vacuum (quasi free) states}

The representation space is 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}_{f_{a}}=\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) =\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( \tbigwedge \mathcal{A}_{1}\right) =\Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
\tbigwedge \mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\right) =\tbigwedge \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( 
\mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\right)
\end{equation}%
and carries the representation 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{f_{a}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) =\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}%
_{f_{a}}\right)
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{f_{a}}\right) =\limfunc{linspan}\left\{ 
\begin{array}{c}
\left. \left\vert \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger }\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{k_{q}}^{\dagger }\right) \right\vert
\right\vert \\ 
1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{p}\leq r,1\leq k_{1}<\cdots <k_{q}\leq r,0\leq
p,q\leq r.%
\end{array}%
\right\} ,
\end{equation}%
where 
\begin{equation}
\left\{ \left. \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger }\right) \right\vert 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{p}\leq
r,0\leq p\leq r\right\}
\end{equation}%
is a conb for $\mathcal{H}_{f_{a}}$ i.e. 
\begin{align}
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }\right) \right\vert \Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{k_{m}}^{\dagger }\right) \right\rangle &
=f_{a}\left( a_{j_{n}}\cdots a_{j_{1}}a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
a_{k_{m}}^{\dagger }\right) \\
& =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j_{n}}\cdots a_{j_{1}}a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
a_{k_{m}}^{\dagger }D_{f_{a}}\right\} =\delta _{j_{1}\cdots j_{p}k_{1}\cdots
k_{q}} \\
1& \leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{p}\leq r,1\leq k_{1}<\cdots <k_{q}\leq r,0\leq
p,q\leq r.
\end{align}%
Different normal vaccum states in $\mathcal{A}$ are related by%
\begin{align}
D_{f_{a}}& =\mathcal{N}\left( \prod_{1\leq \beta \leq N_{p}}b_{j_{\beta
}}^{\dagger }\right) \exp \left\{ \tsum\limits_{1\leq j<k\leq
r}g_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{k}^{\dagger }\right\} D_{f_{b}}\exp \left\{
\tsum\limits_{1\leq j<k\leq r}g_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{k}^{\dagger }\right\}
^{\dagger }\left( \prod_{1\leq \beta \leq N_{p}}b_{j_{\beta }}^{\dagger
}\right) ^{\dagger } \\
\dprod\limits_{1\leq l\leq r}h_{ai}& =\left( \prod_{1\leq \beta \leq
N_{p}}b_{j_{\beta }}^{\dagger }\right) \exp \left\{ \tsum\limits_{1\leq
j<k\leq r}g_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{k}^{\dagger }\right\}
\dprod\limits_{1\leq l\leq r}h_{bi}\exp \left\{ \tsum\limits_{1\leq j<k\leq
r}g_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{k}^{\dagger }\right\} ^{\dagger }\left(
\prod_{1\leq \beta \leq N_{p}}b_{j_{\beta }}^{\dagger }\right) ^{\dagger } \\
& =Q_{\phi _{a}}Q_{\phi _{a}}^{\dagger }
\end{align}%
where 
\begin{align}
h_{ai}& =a_{l}a_{l}^{\dagger };1\leq l\leq r \\
h_{bi}& =b_{l}b_{l}^{\dagger };1\leq l\leq r \\
Q_{\phi _{a}}& =\left( -1\right) ^{r}\left( \prod_{1\leq \beta \leq
N_{p}}b_{j_{\beta }}^{\dagger }\right) \exp \left\{ \tsum\limits_{1\leq
j<k\leq r}g_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger }b_{k}^{\dagger }\right\}
\dprod\limits_{1\leq l\leq r}b_{i}.
\end{align}%
In the GNS\ representation $\Pi _{f_{a}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ 
\begin{equation*}
A_{j}^{\dagger }=\left\vert \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger }\right)
\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right)
\right\vert +\sum_{1\leq p\leq \infty }\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{p}\leq
\infty }\left\vert \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}\cdots a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger }\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger }\right)
\right\vert
\end{equation*}%
giving%
\begin{align}
A_{j}^{\dagger }\left\vert \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
a_{k_{q}}^{\dagger }\right) \right\rangle & =\left\vert \Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j}^{\dagger }\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{F}}\right) \right\vert \left\vert \Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{k_{q}}^{\dagger }\right) \right\rangle \\
& +\sum_{1\leq p\leq \infty }\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{p}\leq \infty
}\left\vert \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger }\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger }\right) \right\vert
\left\vert \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{k_{q}}^{\dagger
}\right) \right\rangle \\
& =0+\sum_{1\leq p\leq \infty }\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{p}\leq \infty
}\left\vert \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger }\right) \right\rangle \delta _{j_{1}\cdots
j_{p}k_{1}\cdots k_{q}} \\
& =\left\vert \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
a_{k_{q}}^{\dagger }\right) \right\rangle ,
\end{align}%
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
A_{j}^{\dagger }\left\vert \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
a_{k_{q}}^{\dagger }\right) \right\rangle =\Pi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger
}\right) \left\vert \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
a_{k_{q}}^{\dagger }\right) \right\rangle .
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Splitting of $\mathcal{F}_{1}$ into self adjoint subspaces}

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{F}_{1}=\mathbf{x}_{1}+_{\mathbb{C}}\mathbf{x}_{2}
\end{equation}
Is it is possible to have states $f$ that have the property 
\begin{equation}
\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f}\right\} \supset\mathbf{x}_{2}
\end{equation}
or 
\begin{equation}
\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f}\right\} \supset\mathbf{x}_{1}?
\end{equation}
If this was true then 
\begin{equation}
f\left( x_{1j}^{2}\right) =0
\end{equation}
or 
\begin{equation}
f\left( x_{2j}^{2}\right) =0
\end{equation}
but 
\begin{equation}
x_{1j}^{2}=x_{2j}^{2}=\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{F}},
\end{equation}
thus $f$ cannot be a state.

\subsubsection{Exterior Algebra Generated by the GNS\ Construction}

The GNS\ Hilbert space generated by the state $f$ 
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) =\limfunc{linspan}\left\{ \left. \Phi
_{f}\left( A_{j}\right) \right\vert 1\leq j\leq 2^{2r}\right\} ,
\end{equation}%
where $\left\{ \left. A_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq 2^{2r}\right\} $ is a
basis for $\mathcal{F}$ and has a metric matrix if $f$ is a normal state
given by 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{f}\left( A_{j}\right) \right\vert \Phi _{f}\left(
A_{k}\right) \right\rangle =f\left( A_{j}^{\dagger }A_{k}\right) =\limfunc{tr%
}\left\{ A_{j}^{\dagger }A_{k}D_{f}\right\} ;1\leq j,k\leq 2^{2r}.
\end{equation}

$\mathcal{A}_{1}$ generates $\mathcal{A}$ via the exterior algebra
construction 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}=\tbigwedge \left( \mathcal{A}_{1}\right)
\end{equation}%
and thus $\Phi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ by 
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) =\Phi _{f}\left( \tbigwedge \mathcal{A}%
_{1}\right) .
\end{equation}%
It is important to examine whether $\Phi _{f}$ is an exterior algebra map
(i.e. an exterior algebra homomorphism) i.e. 
\begin{align}
& \Phi _{f}\left( \tbigwedge\nolimits_{\mathcal{A}}\mathcal{A}_{1}\right) 
\overset{?}{=}\tbigwedge\nolimits_{\Phi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) }\Phi
_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}_{1}\right) \\
\Phi _{f}\left( A\wedge _{\mathcal{A}}B\right) & =\Phi _{f}\left( A\right)
\wedge _{\Phi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) }\Phi _{f}\left( B\right) \\
\Phi _{f}\left( \alpha A+\beta B\right) & =\alpha \Phi _{f}\left( A\right)
+\beta \Phi _{f}\left( B\right) \, \\
\forall A,B& \in \mathcal{A},\alpha ,\beta \in \mathbb{C}.
\end{align}%
Greub shows(chapter 5, second edition) that for any well defined exterior
multiplication in $\mathcal{A}$ if $\Phi _{f}$ is linear and\emph{\ is} a
exterior algebra homomorphism, $\mathcal{F}\rightarrow \Phi _{f}\left( 
\mathcal{F}\right) ,$ i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f}\left( A\wedge _{\mathcal{A}}B\right) =\Phi _{f}\left( A\right)
\wedge _{\Phi _{f}\left( \mathcal{F}\right) }\Phi _{f}\left( B\right)
\,\forall A,B\in \mathcal{A},
\end{equation}%
\emph{it is} an exterior algebra map. However one must determine whether $%
\Phi _{f}$ is a exterior algebra homomorphism i.e. whether $\ker \left\{
\Phi _{f}\right\} $ \emph{is a }$2$\emph{-sided exterior algebra ideal wrt "}%
$\wedge _{\mathcal{A}}"$\emph{\ in }$\mathcal{A}.$

\begin{theorem}
If $f$ is a \emph{vacuum state} $\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f}\right\} $ \emph{is
NOT a }$2$\emph{-sided ideal wrt "}$\wedge _{\mathcal{F}}"$\emph{\ in }$%
\mathcal{A}$
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The kernal of the linear map $\Phi _{f}$ is given by 
\begin{equation}
\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f}\right\} =\left\{ B\in \mathcal{A}\left\vert \limfunc{%
tr}\left\{ B^{\dagger }BD_{f}\right\} =0\right. \right\}
\end{equation}%
and 
\begin{equation}
\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f}\right\} ^{\bot }=\left\{ B\in \mathcal{A}\left\vert 
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ B^{\dagger }BD_{f}\right\} \neq 0\right. \right\} .
\end{equation}%
Both $\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f}\right\} $ and $\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f}\right\}
^{\bot }$ are subspaces of $\mathcal{A}$ and as a vector space 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}=\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f}\right\} \oplus _{\mathbb{C}}\ker \left\{
\Phi _{f}\right\} ^{\bot }.
\end{equation}%
The kernal of a vacuum state $f$ is given by 
\begin{equation}
\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f}\right\} =\left( \ker \left\{ \Phi _{f}\right\} ^{\bot
}\right) ^{\bot },
\end{equation}%
where 
\begin{equation}
\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f}\right\} ^{\bot }=\Phi _{f}\left( \tbigwedge \left( 
\mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\right) \right) =\left\{ \left. a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}\cdots a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger }\right\vert 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{p}\leq
r,0\leq p\leq r\right\} .
\end{equation}
Exterior multiplication in $\mathcal{A}$ is given by 
\begin{equation}
A\wedge _{\mathcal{A}}B=\sum_{0\leq p,q\leq 2r}\left\langle
A_{p}B_{q}\right\rangle _{q+p}=\sum_{0\leq p,q\leq 2r}A_{p}\wedge _{\mathcal{%
A}}B_{q}=\sum_{0\leq p,q\leq 2r}\left( -1\right) ^{pq}B_{q}\wedge _{\mathcal{%
A}}A_{p}.
\end{equation}%
Consider the exterior products of the grade $1$ elements (vectors) $\left\{
\left. a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger },a_{j_{2}}\right\vert 1\leq j_{1},j_{2}\leq
r\right\} $
\end{proof}

\begin{enumerate}
\item In general 
\begin{align}
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge _{\mathcal{A}}a_{j_{2}}& =\left\langle
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{2}}\right\rangle _{2}=\frac{1}{2}\left\langle
\left( x_{j_{1}}-ix_{j_{1}+r}\right) \left( x_{j_{2}}+ix_{j_{2}+r}\right)
\right\rangle _{2} \\
& =\frac{1}{2}\left\langle \left( \left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}+x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}+r}\right) +i\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}+r}-x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}}\right) \right) \right\rangle _{2}
\\
& =\frac{1}{2}\left( \left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}+x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}+r}\right)
+i\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}+r}-x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}}\right) \right) .
\end{align}%
and 
\begin{align}
a_{j_{2}}\wedge _{\mathcal{A}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }& =\left\langle
a_{j_{2}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{2}=\frac{1}{2}\left\langle
\left( x_{j_{2}}+ix_{j_{2}+r}\right) \left( x_{j_{1}}-ix_{j_{1}+r}\right)
\right\rangle _{2} \\
& =\frac{1}{2}\left\langle \left( \left(
x_{j_{2}}x_{j_{1}}+x_{j_{2}+r}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) +i\left(
x_{j_{2}}x_{j_{1}+r}-x_{j_{2}+r}x_{j_{1}}\right) \right) \right\rangle _{2}
\\
& =\frac{1}{2}\left( \left( x_{j_{2}}x_{j_{1}}+x_{j_{2}+r}x_{j_{1}+r}\right)
+i\left( x_{j_{2}}x_{j_{1}+r}-x_{j_{2}+r}x_{j_{1}}\right) \right) .
\end{align}

\item If $j_{1}\neq j_{2}$ 
\begin{eqnarray}
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge _{\mathcal{A}}a_{j_{2}} &=&a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}a_{j_{2}} \\
a_{j_{2}}\wedge _{\mathcal{A}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }
&=&a_{j_{2}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger } \\
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge _{\mathcal{A}}a_{j_{2}} &=&-a_{j_{2}}\wedge _{%
\mathcal{A}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\Leftrightarrow a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}a_{j_{2}}=-a_{j_{2}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }.
\end{eqnarray}

\item If $j_{1}=j_{2}$

\begin{enumerate}
\item 
\begin{align}
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge _{\mathcal{A}}a_{j_{1}}& =\left\langle
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{1}}\right\rangle _{2}=ix_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}=-\frac{%
1}{2}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }+a_{j_{1}}\right) \left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}-a_{j_{1}}\right) \\
& =-\frac{1}{2}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}+a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }-a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}a_{j_{1}}+a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}\right) \\
& =-\frac{1}{2}\left( a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }-a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}a_{j_{1}}\right) =-\frac{1}{2}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}-2a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}a_{j_{1}}\right) =a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{1}}-\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{A}}
\end{align}%
and

\item 
\begin{align}
a_{j_{1}}& \wedge _{\mathcal{A}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }=\left\langle
a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{2}=-ix_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}}=-%
\frac{1}{2}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }-a_{j_{1}}\right) \left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }+a_{j_{1}}\right) \\
& =-\frac{1}{2}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}+a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{1}}-a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}+a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}\right) \\
& =-\frac{1}{2}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}a_{j_{1}}-a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) =-\frac{1}{2}\left(
2a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{1}}-I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) =\frac{1}{2}I_{%
\mathcal{A}}-a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{1}}=-a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge _{%
\mathcal{A}}a_{j_{1}}.
\end{align}
\end{enumerate}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{proof}
Neither $a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge _{\mathcal{A}}a_{j_{1}}$ nor $%
a_{j_{1}}\wedge _{\mathcal{A}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }$ belong to $\ker \left\{
\Phi _{f}\right\} $ when $a_{j_{1}}\in \ker \left\{ \Phi _{f}\right\} $ as 
\begin{eqnarray}
\Phi _{f}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge _{\mathcal{A}}a_{j_{1}}\right)
&=&-\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{A}} \\
\Phi _{f}\left( a_{j_{1}}\wedge _{\mathcal{A}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) &=&%
\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{A}}
\end{eqnarray}%
although 
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f}\left( a_{j_{1}}\right) =0.
\end{equation}%
$.\blacksquare $
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}
If $f$ is a \emph{faithful state } $\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f}\right\} $ \emph{%
is a }$2$\emph{-sided ideal wrt "}$\wedge _{\mathcal{F}}"$\emph{\ in }$%
\mathcal{A}$

\begin{proof}
As 
\begin{equation}
\ker \left\{ \Phi _{f}\right\} =0,
\end{equation}%
the kernal is trivially closed to exterior multiplication in $\mathcal{A}.$
\end{proof}
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}
If $f$ is a \emph{faithful state then }$\Phi _{f}$ is an exterior algebra
isomorphism 
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f}:\mathcal{A}\rightarrow \bigwedge \left( \mathcal{H}_{f}^{1}\right)
.
\end{equation}
\end{corollary}

\paragraph{Splitting Basis of $\mathcal{A}_{1}$}

Self adjoint, $\left\{ \mathbf{x_{1},x}_{2}\right\} ,$ and non self adjoint, 
$\left\{ \mathbf{a}^{\dagger },\mathbf{a}\right\} ,$ splitting bases of $%
\mathcal{A}_{1}$ generate $\mathcal{A}$ and $\Phi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}%
\right) $ and have the following property 
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) =\bigwedge \Phi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}%
_{1}\right) =\bigwedge \left( \Phi _{f}\left( \mathbf{x}_{1}\oplus _{\mathbb{%
C}}\mathbf{x}_{2}\cong \mathbf{x}_{1}+_{\mathbb{C}}\mathbf{x}_{2}\right)
\right) =\bigwedge \Phi _{f}\left( \mathbf{x}_{1}\right) \otimes \bigwedge
\Phi _{f}\left( \mathbf{x}_{2}\right) =\mathcal{H}_{f}
\end{equation}%
and%
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) =\bigwedge \Phi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}%
_{1}\right) =\bigwedge \left( \Phi _{f}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger }+_{%
\mathbb{C}}\mathbf{a\cong a}^{\dagger }\oplus _{\mathbb{C}}\mathbf{a}\right)
\right) =\bigwedge \Phi _{f}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\right) \otimes
\bigwedge \Phi _{f}\left( \mathbf{a}\right) =\mathcal{H}_{f}
\end{equation}%
for all states $f$.

\emph{For non self adjoint splitting bases and vacuum states }$f_{a}$ \emph{%
one has the important identities} 
\begin{equation}
\bigwedge \left( \Phi _{f}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger }+_{\mathbb{C}}\mathbf{%
a\cong a}^{\dagger }\oplus _{\mathbb{C}}\mathbf{a}\right) \right) =\bigwedge
\Phi _{f}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\right) \otimes \bigwedge \Phi
_{f}\left( \mathbf{a}\right) =\bigwedge \left( \Phi _{f}\left( \mathbf{a}%
^{\dagger }\right) \right) =\Phi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) =\mathcal{H}%
_{f},
\end{equation}%
Thus to emphasize this result \emph{if }$f_{a}$\emph{\ is a vacuum state
corresponding to} $\left\{ \mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\right\} $ 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}_{f_{a}}=\bigwedge \left( \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger
}\right) \right) =\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( \bigwedge \left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger
}\right) \right) .
\end{equation}

\begin{remark}
\begin{equation}
\bigwedge \Phi _{f}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\right) \otimes \bigwedge
\Phi _{f}\left( \mathbf{a}\right) =\left\{ \left( \Phi _{f}\left( \bigwedge
\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\right) \right) ,\Phi _{f}\left( \bigwedge
\left( \mathbf{a}\right) \right) \right) \right\} =\left\{ \left( \Phi
_{f}\left( \bigwedge \left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\right) \right) ,0\right)
\right\} \cong \Phi _{f}\left( \bigwedge \left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\right)
\right) .
\end{equation}
\end{remark}

The metric matrix for the basis $\left\{ \left. \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{F}}\right) ,\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }\right) \right\vert 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq
r,1\leq n\leq r\right\} $ of $\bigwedge \left( \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( \mathbf{a}%
^{\dagger }\right) \right) $ for the vacuum state $f_{a}$ is given by 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }\right) \right\vert \Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{k_{m}}^{\dagger }\right) \right\rangle
=f_{a}\left( a_{j_{n}}\cdots a_{j_{1}}a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
a_{k_{m}}^{\dagger }\right) =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j_{n}}\cdots
a_{j_{1}}a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{k_{m}}^{\dagger }D_{f_{a}}\right\}
\end{equation}%
and%
\begin{align}
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right\vert
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }\right)
\right\rangle & =f_{a}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger
}\right) =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger
}D_{f_{a}}\right\} =0 \\
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right\vert
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right\rangle & =1,
\end{align}%
where 
\begin{align}
1& \leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq r,1\leq n\leq r \\
1& \leq k_{1}<\cdots <k_{m}\leq r,1\leq m\leq r.
\end{align}%
$\lceil $%
\begin{align}
& \left\langle \left. \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }a_{k_{1}}\cdots a_{k_{m}}\right) \right\vert \Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger
}a_{q_{1}}\cdots a_{q_{m}}\right) \right\rangle =f_{a}\left(
a_{k_{m}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{n}}\cdots
a_{j_{1}}a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger }a_{q_{1}}\cdots
a_{q_{m}}\right) \\
& =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{k_{m}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger
}a_{j_{n}}\cdots a_{j_{1}}a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger
}a_{q_{1}}\cdots a_{q_{m}}D_{f_{a}}\right\} =\left\langle \left. \phi
_{a}\right\vert a_{k_{m}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger
}a_{j_{n}}\cdots a_{j_{1}}a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger
}a_{q_{1}}\cdots a_{q_{m}}\phi _{a}\right\rangle =0
\end{align}%
\begin{align}
& \left\langle \left. \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{k_{1}}\cdots
a_{k_{m}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }\right) \right\vert
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger
}a_{q_{1}}\cdots a_{q_{m}}\right) \right\rangle =f\left( a_{j_{n}}\cdots
a_{j_{1}}a_{k_{m}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger
}\cdots a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger }a_{q_{1}}\cdots a_{q_{m}}\right) \\
& =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j_{n}}\cdots a_{j_{1}}a_{k_{m}}^{\dagger }\cdots
a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger
}a_{q_{1}}\cdots a_{q_{m}}D_{f_{a}}\right\} =\left\langle \left. \phi
_{a}\right\vert a_{j_{n}}\cdots a_{j_{1}}a_{k_{m}}^{\dagger }\cdots
a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger
}a_{q_{1}}\cdots a_{q_{m}}\phi _{a}\right\rangle =0
\end{align}%
\begin{align}
& \left\langle \left. \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{k_{1}}\cdots
a_{k_{m}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }\right) \right\vert
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{q_{1}}\cdots a_{q_{m}}a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger }\right) \right\rangle =f_{a}\left( a_{k_{1}}\cdots
a_{k_{m}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }a_{q_{1}}\cdots
a_{q_{m}}a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger }\right) \\
& =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j_{n}}\cdots a_{j_{1}}a_{k_{m}}^{\dagger }\cdots
a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{q_{1}}\cdots a_{q_{m}}a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger }D_{f}\right\} =\left\langle \left. \phi _{a}\right\vert
a_{j_{n}}\cdots a_{j_{1}}a_{k_{m}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger
}a_{q_{1}}\cdots a_{q_{m}}a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger
}\phi _{a}\right\rangle \overset{\text{in general}}{\neq }0
\end{align}%
$\rfloor $

If $f_{b}$ is a \emph{vacuum state} then exists some complete orthonormal
basis $\left\{ \left. \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) ,\Phi
_{f}\left( b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }\right)
\right\vert 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq r,1\leq n\leq r\right\} $ of $%
\mathcal{H}_{f_{b}}$\emph{. }

\paragraph{Exterior Products}

We review again some properties of the exterior product in $\mathcal{A}:$

Recall the CAR gives%
\begin{align}
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{1}}+a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }& =\frac{1}{2}%
\left( x_{j_{1}}-ix_{j_{1}+r}\right) \left( x_{j_{1}}+ix_{j_{1}+r}\right) +%
\frac{1}{2}\left( x_{j_{1}}+ix_{j_{1}+r}\right) \left(
x_{j_{1}}-ix_{j_{1}+r}\right) \\
& =\frac{1}{2}\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}}+ix_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}-ix_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}}+x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}+r}+x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}}-ix_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}+ix_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}}+x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}+r}\right)
\\
& =x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}}+x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}+r}=I_{\mathcal{F}}
\end{align}%
and 
\begin{align}
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{2}}+a_{j_{2}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }& =\frac{1}{2}%
\left( x_{j_{1}}-ix_{j_{1}+r}\right) \left( x_{j_{2}}+ix_{j_{2}+r}\right) +%
\frac{1}{2}\left( x_{j_{2}}+ix_{j_{2}+r}\right) \left(
x_{j_{1}}-ix_{j_{1}+r}\right) \\
& =\frac{1}{2}\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}+ix_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}+r}-ix_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}}+x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}+r}+x_{j_{2}}x_{j_{1}}-ix_{j_{2}}x_{j_{1}+r}+ix_{j_{2}+r}x_{j_{1}}+x_{j_{2}+r}x_{j_{1}+r}\right)
\\
& =0
\end{align}%
as it should.

\subparagraph{Clifford Wedge Products}

\begin{enumerate}
\item The Clifford wedge product$\mathcal{\ }$ $a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge
a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }$ is given by 
\begin{align}
& a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }=\left\langle
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{2}=\frac{1}{2}%
\left\langle \left( x_{j_{1}}-ix_{j_{1}+r}\right) \left(
x_{j_{2}}-ix_{j_{2}+r}\right) \right\rangle _{2} \\
& =\left\{ 
\begin{array}{c}
\frac{1}{2}\left( \left\langle x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}}\right\rangle
_{2}-\left\langle x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}+r}\right\rangle _{2}-i\left(
\left\langle x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right\rangle _{2}+\left\langle
x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}}\right\rangle _{2}\right) \right) =0\text{ if }%
j_{1}=j_{2} \\ 
\frac{1}{2}\left( \left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}-x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}+r}\right)
-i\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}+r}+x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}}\right) \right) \text{ }%
\neq 0\text{ if }j_{1}\neq j_{2},%
\end{array}%
\right. ,
\end{align}

\item which also gives $a_{j_{1}}\wedge a_{j_{2}}$ via the adjoint operation 
\begin{align}
& a_{j_{1}}\wedge a_{j_{2}}=\left\langle a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{2}}\right\rangle
_{2}=\frac{1}{2}\left\langle \left( x_{j_{1}}+ix_{j_{1}+r}\right) \left(
x_{j_{2}}+ix_{j_{2}+r}\right) \right\rangle _{2} \\
& =\left\{ 
\begin{array}{c}
\frac{1}{2}\left( \left\langle x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}+r}\right\rangle
_{2}-\left\langle x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}}\right\rangle _{2}++i\left( \left\langle
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right\rangle _{2}+\left\langle
x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}}\right\rangle _{2}\right) \right) =0\text{ if }%
j_{1}=j_{2} \\ 
\frac{1}{2}\left( \left( x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}+r}-x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}\right)
+i\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}+r}+x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}}\right) \right) \text{ }%
\neq 0\text{ if }j_{1}\neq j_{2},%
\end{array}%
\right. ,
\end{align}%
where 
\begin{equation}
1\leq j_{1},j_{2}\leq r.
\end{equation}

\item This shows that 
\begin{align}
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }& =-a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger
}\wedge a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }=a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }\quad 
\text{ }j_{1}\neq j_{2} \\
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }& =0
\end{align}%
and%
\begin{align}
a_{j_{1}}\wedge a_{j_{2}}& =-a_{j_{2}}\wedge
a_{j_{1}}=a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{2}}\quad \text{ }j_{1}\neq j_{2} \\
a_{j_{1}}\wedge a_{j_{1}}& =0.
\end{align}

\item The wedge product of $a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }$ and $a_{j_{2}}$ is given
by 
\begin{align}
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge a_{j_{2}}& =\left\langle a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}a_{j_{2}}\right\rangle _{2}=\frac{1}{2}\left\langle \left(
x_{j_{1}}-ix_{j_{1}+r}\right) \left( x_{j_{2}}+ix_{j_{2}+r}\right)
\right\rangle _{2} \\
& =\frac{1}{2}\left( \left( \left\langle x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}\right\rangle
_{2}+\left\langle x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}+r}\right\rangle _{2}\right) +i\left(
\left\langle x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}+r}\right\rangle _{2}-\left\langle
x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}}\right\rangle _{2}\right) \right) \\
& =\frac{1}{2}\left( \left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}+x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}+r}\right)
+i\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}+r}-x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}}\right) \right) \text{ },
\end{align}%
thus

\begin{enumerate}
\item if $j_{1}\neq j_{2}$%
\begin{equation}
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge a_{j_{2}}=\frac{1}{2}\left( \left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}+x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}+r}\right) +i\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}+r}-x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}}\right) \right) =a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}a_{j_{2}}
\end{equation}%
and 
\begin{equation}
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge a_{j_{2}}=-a_{j_{2}}\wedge a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }%
\text{ as }a_{j_{2}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }=-a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{2}}.
\end{equation}

\item If $j_{1}=j_{2}$ 
\begin{eqnarray}
&&\frac{1}{2}\left( \left( \left\langle x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}}\right\rangle
_{2}+\left\langle x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}+r}\right\rangle _{2}\right) +i\left(
\left\langle x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right\rangle _{2}-\left\langle
x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}}\right\rangle _{2}\right) \right) =\frac{i}{2}\left(
\left\langle x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right\rangle _{2}-\left\langle
x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}}\right\rangle _{2}\right) \\
&=&\frac{i}{2}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}-x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}}\right)
=ix_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r},
\end{eqnarray}%
i.e%
\begin{equation}
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge a_{j_{1}}=ix_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}
\end{equation}%
and $a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge a_{j_{1}}$ is self adjoint, i.e. $\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge a_{j_{1}}\right) ^{\dagger }=\left(
ix_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) ^{\dagger
}=-ix_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}}=ix_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}=a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge
a_{j_{1}}.$

\item One can observe that 
\begin{eqnarray}
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{1}} &=&\frac{1}{2}\left(
x_{j_{1}}-ix_{j_{1}+r}\right) \left( x_{j_{1}}+ix_{j_{1}+r}\right) =\frac{1}{%
2}\left( \left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}}+x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) +i\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}-x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}}\right) \right) \\
&=&\frac{1}{2}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}-2ix_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) =\frac{1}{%
2}I_{\mathcal{F}}-ix_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}
\end{eqnarray}%
thus 
\begin{equation}
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge a_{j_{1}}\neq a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{1}}.
\end{equation}%
$.$
\end{enumerate}

\item A slightly different way of showing this is

\begin{enumerate}
\item if $j_{1}\neq j_{2}$ 
\begin{align}
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge a_{j_{2}}& =\left\langle a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}a_{j_{2}}\right\rangle _{2}=\frac{1}{2}\left\langle \left(
x_{j_{1}}-ix_{j_{1}+r}\right) \left( x_{j_{2}}+ix_{j_{2}+r}\right)
\right\rangle _{2} \\
& =\frac{1}{2}\left\langle \left( \left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}+x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}+r}\right) +i\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}+r}-x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}}\right) \right) \right\rangle _{2}
\\
& =\frac{1}{2}\left( \left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}+x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}+r}\right)
+i\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}+r}-x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}}\right) \right)
=a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{2}},
\end{align}

\item If $j_{1}=j_{2}$ 
\begin{align}
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge a_{j_{1}}& =ix_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}=-\frac{1}{2}%
\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }+a_{j_{1}}\right) \left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}-a_{j_{1}}\right) \\
& =-\frac{1}{2}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}+a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }-a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}a_{j_{1}}+a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}\right) \\
& =-\frac{1}{2}\left( a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }-a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}a_{j_{1}}\right) =-\frac{1}{2}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}-2a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}a_{j_{1}}\right) =a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{1}}-\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{F}}
\end{align}%
and

\item 
\begin{align}
a_{j_{1}}& \wedge a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }=\left\langle
a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{2}=\frac{1}{2}\left\langle
\left( x_{j_{1}}+ix_{j_{1}+r}\right) \left( x_{j_{1}}-ix_{j_{1}+r}\right)
\right\rangle _{2} \\
& =\frac{1}{2}\left( \left\langle x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}}\right\rangle
_{2}+\left\langle x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}+r}\right\rangle _{2}+i\left(
\left\langle x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}}\right\rangle _{2}-\left\langle
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right\rangle _{2}\right) \right) =-ix_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}%
\text{ }.
\end{align}%
Hence%
\begin{equation}
a_{j_{1}}\wedge a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}=-ix_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}=-a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge a_{j_{1}}.
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate}
\end{enumerate}

The Clifford/Geometric product of $a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }$ and $a_{j_{2}}$ 
\begin{equation*}
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{2}}=\frac{1}{2}\left(
x_{j_{1}}-ix_{j_{1}+r}\right) \left( x_{j_{2}}+ix_{j_{2}+r}\right) =\frac{1}{%
2}\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}+ix_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}+r}-ix_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}}+x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}+r}\right) ,
\end{equation*}%
give in particular 
\begin{align}
n_{j_{1}}& =a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{1}}=\frac{1}{2}\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}}+x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}+r}+i\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}+r}-x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}}\right) \right) \\
& =\frac{1}{2}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}+x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}+r}+i\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}-x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}}\right) \right) \\
& =\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{F}}+ix_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}
\end{align}%
which shows that 
\begin{equation}
h_{j_{1}}=a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }=\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{F}%
}-ix_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}.
\end{equation}

\paragraph{GNS Maps Generated by Vacuum States}

The action of $\Phi _{f_{a}}$ on the self adjoint basis is 
\begin{align}
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\right) & =\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }+a_{j_{1}}\right) =\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) \\
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}+r}\right) & =\frac{i}{\sqrt{2}}\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }-a_{j_{1}}\right) =\frac{i}{\sqrt{2}}\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) .
\end{align}%
Thus one can construct a real map 
\begin{equation}
\Upsilon _{f_{a}}:\mathbf{x}_{1}\oplus _{\mathbb{R}}\mathbf{x}%
_{2}\rightarrow \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\right)
\end{equation}%
by 
\begin{equation}
\Upsilon _{f_{a}}\left( \sum_{1\leq j_{1}\leq r}\left\{
c_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}}+c_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}+r}\right\} \right) =\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}%
\sum_{1\leq j_{1}\leq r}\left( c_{j_{1}}+ic_{j_{1}+r}\right) \Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) .
\end{equation}

$\lceil $The CAR gives 
\begin{gather}
a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{j}=I_{\mathcal{A}}-a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger } \\
\Pi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}-a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }\right) \Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) =\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I\right) -\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }\right) =0 \\
\Longleftrightarrow \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) =\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }\right) ,
\end{gather}%
which is consistent with%
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }\right) \right\rangle =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }\left\vert \phi _{a}\right\rangle \left\langle
\phi _{a}\right\vert \right\} =1.
\end{equation}%
One can double check that 
\begin{equation}
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{1}}\in \ker \left\{ \Phi _{f_{a}}\right\}
\end{equation}%
by considering%
\begin{align}
& \left\langle \left. \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}a_{j_{1}}\right) \right\vert \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}a_{j_{1}}\right) \right\rangle \\
& =\left\langle \left. \frac{1}{2}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
+i\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) \right\vert \frac{1}{2}%
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) +i\Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) \right\rangle \\
& =\left\langle \left. \frac{1}{2}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\vert \frac{1}{2}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle +\left\langle \left. \frac{1}{2}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert i\Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) \right\rangle \\
& +\left\langle i\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) \left\vert 
\frac{1}{2}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right. \right\rangle
+\left\langle \left. i\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right)
\right\vert i\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) \right\rangle \\
& =\frac{1}{4}+\frac{i}{2}\limfunc{tr}\left\{ x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\left\vert
\phi _{a}\right\rangle \left\langle \phi _{a}\right\vert \right\} +\frac{i}{2%
}\limfunc{tr}\left\{ x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}}\left\vert \phi _{a}\right\rangle
\left\langle \phi _{a}\right\vert \right\} -\limfunc{tr}\left\{
x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\left\vert \phi _{a}\right\rangle
\left\langle \phi _{a}\right\vert \right\} \\
& =\frac{1}{4}-\frac{1}{4}=0,
\end{align}%
thus%
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{1}}\right) =\frac{1}{2}\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) +i\Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) =0
\end{equation}%
and 
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) =-2i\Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) ,
\end{equation}%
This again shows that 
\begin{align}
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) & =\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}-a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{1}}\right) =\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) , \\
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) & =-\frac{1}{2i}\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \\
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge a_{j_{1}}\right) & =i\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) =-\frac{1}{2}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \neq \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right)
\wedge \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}\right) .
\end{align}%
It is interesting to note that this also shows that 
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}-a_{j_{2}}a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }\right) =0.
\end{equation}%
As $a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{1}}\in \ker \Phi _{f_{a}}$%
\begin{align}
& \left\langle \left. \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}a_{j_{1}}\right) \right\vert \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}a_{j_{1}}\right) \right\rangle \\
& =\left\langle \left. \frac{1}{2}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
+i\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) \right\vert \frac{1}{2}%
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) +i\Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) \right\rangle \\
& =\left\langle \left. \frac{1}{2}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\vert \frac{1}{2}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle +\left\langle \left. \frac{1}{2}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert i\Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) \right\rangle \\
& +\left\langle i\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) \left\vert 
\frac{1}{2}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right. \right\rangle
+\left\langle \left. i\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right)
\right\vert i\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) \right\rangle \\
& =\frac{1}{4}+\frac{i}{2}\limfunc{tr}\left\{ x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\left\vert
\phi _{a}\right\rangle \left\langle \phi _{a}\right\vert \right\} +\frac{i}{2%
}\limfunc{tr}\left\{ x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}}\left\vert \phi _{a}\right\rangle
\left\langle \phi _{a}\right\vert \right\} -\limfunc{tr}\left\{
x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\left\vert \phi _{a}\right\rangle
\left\langle \phi _{a}\right\vert \right\} \\
& =\frac{1}{4}-\frac{1}{4}=0,
\end{align}%
thus%
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{1}}\right) =\frac{1}{2}\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) +i\Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) =0
\end{equation}%
and 
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) =-2i\Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) .
\end{equation}%
This again shows that%
\begin{equation}
a_{j_{1}}\wedge a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}=-ix_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}=-a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge a_{j_{1}}.
\end{equation}%
and%
\begin{align}
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) & =\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}-a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{1}}\right) =\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) , \\
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) & =-\frac{1}{2i}\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \\
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge a_{j_{1}}\right) & =i\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) =-\frac{1}{2}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \neq \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right)
\wedge \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}\right) .
\end{align}%
It is interesting to note that this also shows that 
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}-a_{j_{2}}a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }\right) =0\,\forall j_{1},j_{2}.
\end{equation}%
$\rfloor \rfloor $

We now prove that $\Phi _{f_{a}}$ is NOT an exterior algebra map in the
context of self adjoint bases:

\begin{theorem}
$\Phi _{f_{a}}$ is NOT an exterior algebra map
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
In the following we show $\Phi _{f_{a}}$ is a homomorphism for all cases
\end{proof}

\begin{enumerate}
\item For second order products 
\begin{align}
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\wedge x_{j_{1}}\right) & =\frac{1}{2}\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( \left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }+a_{j_{1}}\right) \wedge \left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }+a_{j_{1}}\right) \right) =\frac{1}{2}\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}+a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge a_{j_{1}}+a_{j_{1}}\wedge a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}+a_{j_{1}}\wedge a_{j_{1}}\right) \\
& =\frac{1}{2}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge
a_{j_{1}}+a_{j_{1}}\wedge a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) =\frac{1}{2}\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( ix_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}-ix_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) =0.
\end{align}

\item For $j_{1}\neq j_{2}$ 
\begin{align}
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\wedge x_{j_{2}}\right) & =\Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}\right) \text{ } \\
& =\frac{1}{2}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( \left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}+a_{j_{1}}\right) \left( a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }+a_{j_{2}}\right) \right) =%
\frac{1}{2}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger
}+a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }+a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}a_{j_{2}}+a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{2}}\right) \\
& =\frac{1}{2}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger
}\right) =\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right)
\wedge \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }\right)
=\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\right) \wedge \Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
x_{j_{2}}\right) ,
\end{align}

and 
\begin{align}
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\wedge x_{j_{2}+r}\right) & =\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}+r}\right) \\
& =\frac{i}{2}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( \left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}+a_{j_{1}}\right) \left( a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }-a_{j_{2}}\right) \right) =%
\frac{i}{2}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger
}-a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }+a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}a_{j_{2}}-a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{2}}\right) \\
& =\frac{i}{2}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger
}\right) =\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right)
\wedge \frac{i}{\sqrt{2}}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }\right)
=\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\right) \wedge \Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
x_{j_{2}+r}\right) .
\end{align}

\item For $j_{1}=j_{2}$
\end{enumerate}

\begin{proof}
as 
\begin{equation}
x_{j_{1}}\wedge x_{j_{1}+r}=x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}
\end{equation}%
one has 
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\wedge x_{j_{1}+r}\right) =\Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) .
\end{equation}%
Expanding the LHS and RHS one obtains 
\begin{align}
& \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\wedge x_{j_{1}+r}\right) =\frac{i}{2}\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( \left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }+a_{j_{1}}\right) \wedge \left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }-a_{j_{1}}\right) \right) =\frac{i}{2}\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}-a_{j_{1}}\wedge a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }+a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge
a_{j_{1}}-a_{j_{1}}\wedge a_{j_{1}}\right) \\
& =\frac{i}{2}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge
a_{j_{1}}-a_{j_{1}}\wedge a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) =i\Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge a_{j_{1}}\right) =i\Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) \wedge \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}\right) =0
\end{align}%
and%
\begin{align}
& \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) =\frac{i}{2}\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( \left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }+a_{j_{1}}\right) \left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }-a_{j_{1}}\right) \right) =\frac{i}{2}\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}+a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }-a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}a_{j_{1}}-a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}\right) \\
& =\frac{i}{2}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}-a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{1}}\right) =\frac{i}{2}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }-\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}-a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{%
\dagger }\right) \right) \\
& =\frac{i}{2}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }-I_{\mathcal{F%
}}\right) =\frac{i}{2}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}-a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}a_{j_{1}}-I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) =0.
\end{align}%
Hence 
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\right) \wedge \Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
x_{j_{2}+r}\right) =\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}\right) \wedge \frac{i}{\sqrt{2}}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}\right) =0,
\end{equation}%
hence it is \emph{NOT }homomorphic on these bases elements i.e. in the case $%
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}$ the GNS$\ $linear map, $\Phi _{f_{a}},$ \emph{fails to
be} an exerior algebra map.$\blacksquare $
\end{proof}

We recall that the action of $\Phi _{f_{a}}$ on the non self adjoint basis
for $j_{1}\neq j_{2}$ is 
\begin{align}
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge a_{j_{2}}\right) & =\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{2}}\right) =\frac{1}{2}\left( \Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}\right) +\Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}+r}\right) +i\Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}+r}\right) -i\Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}}\right) \right) \\
& =\frac{1}{2}\left( \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}\right) -\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}\right) +\Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}\right) -\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}\right)
\right) =0
\end{align}%
Hence 
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge a_{j_{2}}\right) =\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) \wedge \Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{2}}\right) =0,
\end{equation}%
which also shows that%
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}\wedge a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }\right) =\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) \wedge \Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{2}}\right) =\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }\right)
=-\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{1}}\right) =0,j_{1}\neq
j_{2}.
\end{equation}%
When $j_{1}=j_{2}$ 
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{1}}\right) =\frac{1}{2}\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) +i\Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) =0\Rightarrow \Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) =-\frac{1}{2i}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right)
\end{equation}%
showing that 
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge a_{j_{1}}\right) =i\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) =-\frac{1}{2}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \,\emph{\neq \,}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) \wedge \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}\right) =0
\end{equation}%
and 
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}\wedge a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) =-i\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) =\frac{1}{2}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \,\emph{\neq \,}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}\right)
\wedge \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) =0.
\end{equation}%
\emph{Thus again showing }$\Phi _{f_{a}}$ \emph{induced by a vacuum state} $%
f_{a}$ \emph{is NOT an exterior algebra map on }$\mathcal{A}$ \emph{only on }%
$\tbigwedge \left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\right) .$ \emph{Note that this is
not true for states in general.}

\begin{remark}
Note that according to the definition of wedge product in $\mathcal{A}$ 
\begin{equation*}
I_{\mathcal{A}}\wedge A=A\,\forall A\in \mathcal{A}
\end{equation*}%
thus $I_{\mathcal{A}}$ is the identity wrt the exterior algebra structure of 
$\mathcal{A}.$
\end{remark}

\emph{In summary} \emph{a vacuum state }$f$ \emph{induces} \emph{the map }$%
\Phi _{f}$\emph{\ which is an exterior algebra isomorphism from }$\bigwedge
\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\right) $ to $\bigwedge \Phi _{f}\left( \mathbf{a%
}^{\dagger }\right) $ as it\emph{\ }is $1-1$ and onto as a vector space map 
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f}:\bigwedge \left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\right) \rightarrow
\bigwedge \Phi _{f}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\right) \text{ i.e. }\Phi
_{f}\left( \bigwedge \left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\right) \right) =\bigwedge
\Phi _{f}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\right)
\end{equation}%
and as an algebra map 
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f}\left( \bigwedge \left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\right) \right)
=\bigwedge \Phi _{f}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\right) .
\end{equation}%
\emph{The vector }$\Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) $ acts as the
identity in $\Phi _{f}\left( \bigwedge \left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\right)
\right) $ i.e. \emph{\ } 
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \wedge \Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger }\right) =\Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
I_{\mathcal{A}}\wedge a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger }\right)
=\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger }\right)
,1\leq j_{1}<\cdots j_{p}\leq r,1\leq p\leq r,
\end{equation}%
i.e. $\Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) $ \emph{is the vacuum vector}
for $\Phi _{f}\left( \bigwedge \left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\right) \right) .$

The representation of the $c^{\ast }$-algebra $\mathcal{A}$ given by the map 
$\Pi _{f_{a}}$ 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{f_{a}}:\mathcal{A}\rightarrow \Pi _{f_{a}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right)
\subseteq \mathcal{B}\left( \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) \right) =%
\mathcal{B}\left( \bigwedge \left( \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger
}\right) \right) \right) .
\end{equation}%
produces a Fock representation, $\mathcal{F},$ as 
\begin{align}
\Pi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger }\right) \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) & =\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger }\right) \\
\Pi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j}\right) \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) &
=\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j}\right) =0
\end{align}%
showing that $\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) $ acts as a
traditional generating vacuum vector state, while also acting as a grade
zero vector. All normal form operators 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{f_{a}}\left( \tsum_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1},\cdots ,j_{n}\leq r  \\ %
1\leq k_{1},\cdots ,k_{m}\leq r}}\alpha _{j_{1}\cdots j_{n}k_{1}\cdots
k_{m}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }a_{k_{1}}\cdots
a_{k_{m}}\right)
\end{equation}%
annihilate $\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) $ (as they should if $%
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) $ is a vacuum vector for $\Pi
_{f_{a}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $)$,$ but are in general non zero on other
vectors in $\mathcal{H}_{f_{a}}.$

We now compare the operator wedge product defined by $\bigwedge \left( \Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\right) \right) $ in $\mathcal{B}\left(
\bigwedge \left( \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\right) \right)
\right) $ by a vacuum state $f_{a}$ to the $\boxtimes $ product in $\mathcal{%
F}$ and determine whether $\Pi _{f_{a}}$ is an algebra map i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{f_{a}}\left( A\wedge B\right) \overset{?}{=}\Pi _{f_{a}}\left(
A\right) \boxtimes \Pi _{f_{a}}\left( B\right) ,
\end{equation}%
where the exterior products are the ones defined in $\mathcal{A}$ and $%
\mathcal{B}\left( \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( \mathcal{F}\right) \right) =\mathcal{B}%
\left( \bigwedge \left( \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( \mathcal{A}_{1}\right) \right)
\right) =\mathcal{B}\left( \bigwedge \left( \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( \mathbf{a}%
^{\dagger }\right) \right) \right) .$

The definition of the operator exterior product in $\mathcal{B}\left(
\bigwedge \left( \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\right) \right)
\right) $ is 
\begin{align}
& \left( \Pi _{f_{a}}\left( A_{1}\right) \boxtimes \cdots \boxtimes \Pi
_{f_{a}}\left( A_{n}\right) \right) \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}\cdots a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }\right) =\left( \Pi _{f_{a}}\left( A_{1}\right)
\boxtimes \cdots \boxtimes \Pi _{f_{a}}\left( A_{n}\right) \right) \Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) \wedge \cdots \wedge \Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }\right) \\
& =\frac{1}{n!}\sum_{\sigma \in S_{n}}\Pi _{f_{a}}\left( A_{\sigma \left(
1\right) }\right) \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) \wedge
\cdots \wedge \Pi _{f_{a}}\left( A_{\sigma \left( n\right) }\right) \Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }\right) =\frac{1}{n!}\sum_{\sigma \in
S_{n}}\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( A_{\sigma \left( 1\right) }a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}\right) \wedge \cdots \wedge \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( A_{\sigma \left( n\right)
}a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }\right)
\end{align}%
$\lceil $%
\begin{align}
& \left( \Pi _{f_{a}}\left( A_{1}\right) \boxtimes \Pi _{f_{a}}\left(
A_{2}\right) \right) \left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right)
\wedge \Phi _{f}\left( a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }\right) \right\rangle =\frac{1}{2!%
}\sum_{\sigma \in S_{2}}\Pi _{f_{a}}\left( A_{\sigma \left( 1\right)
}\right) \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) \wedge \Pi
_{f_{a}}\left( A_{\sigma \left( 2\right) }\right) \Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }\right) \\
& =\frac{1}{2}\left( \Pi _{f_{a}}\left( A_{1}\right) \Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) \wedge \Pi _{f_{a}}\left( A_{2}\right) \Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }\right) +\Pi _{f_{a}}\left( A_{1}\right)
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) \wedge \Pi _{f_{a}}\left(
A_{2}\right) \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }\right) \right) \\
& =\frac{1}{2}\left( \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( A_{1}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right)
\wedge \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( A_{2}a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }\right) +\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( A_{2}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) \wedge \Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
A_{1}a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }\right) \right)
\end{align}%
$\rfloor $

We have shown that for vacuum states 
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }\right) =\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }\right) =\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) \wedge \Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }\right)
\end{equation}%
but it is not true that%
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) =\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}\wedge a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) =\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}\right) \wedge \Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right)
\end{equation}%
i.e 
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}\wedge a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) \neq \Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}\right) \wedge \Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) .
\end{equation}%
Hence 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}\wedge a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) \neq \Pi
_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}\right) \boxtimes \Pi _{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right)
\end{equation}%
and $\Pi _{f_{a}}$ is \emph{NOT }an \emph{algebra} isomorphism from $%
\mathcal{A}$ to $\Pi _{f_{a}}\left( \mathcal{F}\right) $ wrt the $\wedge $
product in $\mathcal{A}$ and the operator $\boxtimes $ product in $\Pi
_{f_{a}}\left( \mathcal{F}\right) .$

The following relationships can be easily observed%
\begin{eqnarray}
\Pi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }\right) \Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) &=&\Pi _{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) \Pi _{f}\left( a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }\right) \Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) =\Pi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}\wedge a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }\right) \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) \\
&=&\Pi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) \boxtimes \Pi
_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }\right) \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) =\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }\right)
\end{eqnarray}%
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) \Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }\right) =\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }\right) =\Pi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }\right) \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) =\Pi
_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) \boxtimes \Pi _{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }\right) \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right)
\end{equation}%
\begin{eqnarray}
\Pi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\wedge a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }\right)
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( A\right) &=&\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}\wedge a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }\wedge A\right) =\Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }A\right) \\
&=&\Pi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) \boxtimes \Pi
_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }\right) \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( A\right)
\quad \forall A\in \bigwedge \left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger }\right) ,
\end{eqnarray}%
so $\Pi _{f_{a}}$ is a homomorphism on $\bigwedge \left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger
}\right) .$

The exterior product in $\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( \mathcal{F}\right) $ has to be
defined as 
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) \wedge \Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }\right) =\frac{1}{2}\left( \Phi _{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) \otimes \Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger
}\right) -\Phi _{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }\right) \otimes \Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) \right) ,
\end{equation}%
which can be accomplished by isomorphisms.

$\lceil$%
\begin{align}
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \otimes\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) & =\left( \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right)
,\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) \right) \\
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \wedge\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) & =\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) \sim%
\frac{1}{2}\left( \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \otimes
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) -\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) \otimes\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right)
\right) .
\end{align}
$\rfloor$

\subsection{\emph{Partial }Isometries in c$^{\ast }$and w$^{\ast }$-Algebras}

If $QQ^{\dagger }$ is an orthogonal projector (self adjoint idempotent) then
both $Q$ and $Q^{\dagger }$ are a partial isometries and $Q^{\dagger }Q$ is
also an orthogonal projector, moreover this is equivalent to 
\begin{equation}
Q^{\dagger }QQ^{\dagger }=Q^{\dagger }\Leftrightarrow QQ^{\dagger }Q=Q,
\end{equation}%
which can be thought of as a projections from the left or eigenvalue
equations 
\begin{align}
\left( QQ^{\dagger }\right) Q& =Q \\
\left( Q^{\dagger }Q\right) Q^{\dagger }& =Q^{\dagger },
\end{align}%
thus $Q$ and $Q^{\dagger }$ are eigenvectors of eigenvalue $1$ of $%
QQ^{\dagger }$ and $Q^{\dagger }Q$ respectively.

These operations could also be written as acting from the right i.e. 
\begin{align}
Q\left( Q^{\dagger }Q\right) & =Q \\
Q^{\dagger }\left( QQ^{\dagger }\right) & =Q^{\dagger }.
\end{align}%
Observe that $\left\{ \left. \eta Q,\eta Q^{\dagger }\right\vert \eta \in 
\mathbb{C}\right\} $ are also eigenvectors with the eigenvalue $1$. These
equations can also be generalized to 
\begin{align}
\left( QQ^{\dagger }\right) QA& =QA \\
\left( Q^{\dagger }Q\right) Q^{\dagger }A& =Q^{\dagger }A
\end{align}%
for any $A\in \mathcal{A}.$

The projectors $QQ^{\dagger }$ and $Q^{\dagger }Q$ are said to be \emph{%
equivalent} and this defines an \emph{equivalence relationship} on the set
of orthogonal projectors (self adjoint idempotents) contained in $\mathcal{A}
$.

\paragraph{GNS and Ket Bra Operators when}

$f_{QQ^{\dagger }}$ is a normal state \emph{generated} by $QQ^{\dagger }$
even if it is not a projector as long as $Q$ is normalized 
\begin{equation}
f\left( A\right) =\left\langle \left. Q\right\vert AQ\right\rangle _{%
\mathcal{A}}=\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q^{\dagger }AQ\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
AQQ^{\dagger }\right\} =\left\langle \left\langle AQQ^{\dagger
}\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0}\forall A\in \mathcal{A},
\end{equation}%
where $Q\in \mathcal{A}.$ If $f$ is a normal state 
\begin{equation}
f\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) =1
\end{equation}%
showing that $Q$ and $Q^{\dagger }$ are must be \emph{normalized} i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q^{\dagger }Q\right\} =\left\langle \left. Q\right\vert
AQ\right\rangle _{\mathcal{A}}=\limfunc{tr}\left\{ QQ^{\dagger }\right\}
=\left\langle \left. Q^{\dagger }\right\vert AQ^{\dagger }\right\rangle _{%
\mathcal{A}}=1.
\end{equation}

$\lceil $A normal state $f_{QQ^{\dagger }}$ is pure $\limfunc{Iff}$ $%
QQ^{\dagger }$ is an \emph{atomic orthogonal projector} (self adjoint,
idempotent, rank $1$) i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\left( QQ^{\dagger }\right) ^{2}=QQ^{\dagger }QQ^{\dagger }=QQ^{\dagger }
\end{equation}%
and ?????????? 
\begin{equation}
\overline{\sigma \left( I_{\mathcal{A}}-QQ^{\dagger }\right) }\subset
\end{equation}%
depends on the type of the trace and VnA$\rfloor $

In order to give meaning to the term projector one can consider the special
case of the left regular representation of $\mathcal{A}$ on $\mathcal{A},$
which is given by the GNS\ representation $\Pi _{\limfunc{tr}},$where 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{\limfunc{tr}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) \subseteq \mathcal{B}\left(
\Phi _{\limfunc{tr}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) \right) .
\end{equation}

The eigenvalues of projectors are $\left\{ 0,1\right\} $, but observe that
such projectors do not have to be $1$- dimensional (the dimension
corresponds to the multiplicity of the eigenvalue $1$) e.g. $%
h_{j}=a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}$ and $n_{j}=a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j}$ can be infinite
dimensional projectors.

$\lceil $One can observe that $\left\{ a_{j}^{\dagger },a_{j}\right\} $ are
partial isometries but they are not normalized in the CAR/Clifford algebra $%
\mathcal{A}$ as 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{j}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }\right\} =\frac{1}{2}
\end{equation}%
The normalized operators $\left\{ \sqrt{2}a_{j}^{\dagger },\sqrt{2}%
a_{j}\right\} $ are not partial isometries as 
\begin{equation}
\left( \left( \sqrt{2}a_{j}^{\dagger }\right) \left( \sqrt{2}a_{j}\right)
\right) ^{2}=\left( 2a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{j}\right) ^{2}=4a_{j}^{\dagger
}a_{j}\neq 2a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{j}.
\end{equation}%
We can characterize the normalized solutions of 
\begin{equation}
a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }A=\lambda A,
\end{equation}%
we know there is at least one, namely $\frac{a_{j}}{\sqrt{\left( \limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }\right\} \right) }}.$

The projectors $a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }$ thus project onto the normalized
amplitudes $\frac{a_{j}}{\sqrt{\left( \limfunc{tr}\left\{
a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }\right\} \right) }}$ i.e.%
\begin{equation}
a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }\left( \frac{a_{j}}{\sqrt{\left( \limfunc{tr}\left\{
a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }\right\} \right) }}\right) =\frac{a_{j}}{\sqrt{\left( 
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }\right\} \right) }},
\end{equation}%
the induced operators $\widehat{a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }}\in \mathcal{B}\left( 
\mathcal{A}\right) $ are also orthogonal projecters 
\begin{align}
\widehat{a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }}\left( \frac{a_{j}}{\sqrt{\left( \limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }\right\} \right) }}\right) & =\left[
a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger },\frac{a_{j}}{\sqrt{\left( \limfunc{tr}\left\{
a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }\right\} \right) }}\right] \\
& =a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }\frac{a_{j}}{\sqrt{\left( \limfunc{tr}\left\{
a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }\right\} \right) }}-\frac{a_{j}}{\sqrt{\left( \limfunc{%
tr}\left\{ a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }\right\} \right) }}a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }=%
\frac{a_{j}}{\sqrt{\left( \limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }\right\}
\right) }}.
\end{align}%
The vectors 
\begin{equation}
\left\{ \frac{a_{j}}{\sqrt{\left( \limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger
}\right\} \right) }},\frac{a_{k}}{\sqrt{\left( \limfunc{tr}\left\{
a_{k}a_{k}^{\dagger }\right\} \right) }}\right\} ,\quad j\neq k
\end{equation}%
are orthogonal as%
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j}^{\dagger }a_{k}\right\} =0,\quad j\neq k.
\end{equation}%
This is consistent with $\widehat{a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger }}$ being a self
adjoint operator.$\rfloor $

One can consider the following cases:

\begin{enumerate}
\item The properties of normal states i.e. normalized positive operators $%
QQ^{\dagger }\in \mathcal{A}$ (both pure and mixed) in GNS representations
generated by normal pure states $Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger }$%
\begin{align}
\left\langle \left. Q\right\vert AQ\right\rangle _{\limfunc{tr}}& =\limfunc{%
tr}\left\{ AQQ^{\dagger }\right\} ;\limfunc{tr}\left\{ QQ^{\dagger }\right\}
=1 \\
f\left( A\right) & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ AQ_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger }\right\}
=\left\langle \left. \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right\vert \Pi
_{f}\left( A\right) \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right\rangle _{f}
\\
f\left( QQ^{\dagger }\right) & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ QQ^{\dagger
}Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger }\right\} =\left\langle \left. \Phi _{f}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{F}}\right) \right\vert \Pi _{f}\left( Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger }\right)
\Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right\rangle =\left\langle \left.
\Phi _{f}\left( Q^{\dagger }\right) \right\vert \Phi _{f}\left( Q^{\dagger
}\right) \right\rangle _{f}.
\end{align}%
Hence for a general normal state 
\begin{equation}
f\left( Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger }\right) =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger }Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger }\right\} =\left\langle \left. \Phi
_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right\vert \Pi _{f}\left(
Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger }\right) \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right)
\right\rangle _{f}=\left\langle \left. \Phi _{f}\left( Q_{f}^{\dagger
}\right) \right\vert \Phi _{f}\left( Q_{f}^{\dagger }\right) \right\rangle
_{f}
\end{equation}

\item The properties of normal pure states i.e. partial isometries of $%
\mathcal{A}$ in GNS representations generated by the same normal pure states%
\begin{align}
\left\langle \left. Q_{f}\right\vert AQ_{f}\right\rangle _{\limfunc{tr}}& =%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ AQ_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger }\right\} ;\limfunc{tr}\left\{
Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger }\right\} =1 \\
f\left( A\right) & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ AQ_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger }\right\}
=\left\langle \left. \Phi _{f}\left( I\right) \right\vert \Pi _{f}\left(
A\right) \Phi _{f}\left( I\right) \right\rangle _{f} \\
f\left( Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger }\right) & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger }Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger }\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger }\right\} =\left\langle \left. \Phi _{f}\left(
Q_{f}^{\dagger }\right) \right\vert \Phi _{f}\left( Q_{f}^{\dagger }\right)
\right\rangle _{f}=1
\end{align}

\item The properties of normal pure states $QQ^{\dagger }$ i.e. partial
isometries $Q$ of $\mathcal{A}$ in GNS representations generated by the
trace state%
\begin{align}
\left\langle \left. Q\right\vert AQ\right\rangle _{\limfunc{tr}}& =\limfunc{%
tr}\left\{ AQQ^{\dagger }\right\} ;\limfunc{tr}\left\{ QQ^{\dagger }\right\}
=1 \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ A\right\} & =\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\limfunc{tr}%
}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right\vert \Phi _{\limfunc{tr}}\left(
A\right) \right\rangle _{\limfunc{tr}} \\
\limfunc{tr}\left( QQ^{\dagger }\right) & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ QQ^{\dagger
}QQ^{\dagger }\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ QQ^{\dagger }\right\}
=\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\limfunc{tr}}\left( Q^{\dagger }\right)
\right\vert \Phi _{\limfunc{tr}}\left( Q^{\dagger }\right) \right\rangle _{%
\limfunc{tr}}=1
\end{align}

\item 
\begin{align}
QQ^{\dagger }& =I_{\mathcal{A}}+\sum_{1\leq p\leq 2r}\sum_{1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots <j_{p}\leq 2r}\xi _{j_{1}\cdots j_{p}}x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}
\\
Q^{\dagger }Q& =I_{\mathcal{A}}+\sum_{1\leq p\leq 2r}\sum_{1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots <j_{p}\leq 2r}\eta _{j_{1}\cdots j_{p}}x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}
\end{align}%
In general 
\begin{equation}
QQ^{\dagger }+Q^{\dagger }Q\leq I_{\mathcal{A}}.
\end{equation}%
If $Q$ was unitary 
\begin{equation}
QQ^{\dagger }=Q^{\dagger }Q=I_{\mathcal{A}}
\end{equation}%
If $P$ is an orthogonal projector then 
\begin{equation}
Q=I_{\mathcal{F}}-P
\end{equation}%
is also an orthogonal projector as 
\begin{align}
Q& =Q^{\dagger } \\
Q^{2}& =\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}-P\right) ^{2}=I_{\mathcal{A}}-2P+P=I_{%
\mathcal{A}}-P.
\end{align}%
If the projector $P$ can be expanded as 
\begin{equation}
P=I_{\mathcal{A}}+\sum_{1\leq p\leq 2r}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{p}\leq
2r}\zeta _{j_{1}\cdots j_{p}}x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}},
\end{equation}%
then 
\begin{equation}
Q=I_{\mathcal{A}}-P=\sum_{1\leq p\leq 2r}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{p}\leq
2r}\zeta _{j_{1}\cdots j_{p}}x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}},
\end{equation}%
thus 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \sum_{1\leq p\leq 2r}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{p}\leq
2r}\zeta _{j_{1}\cdots j_{p}}x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ Q\right\} =0.
\end{equation}%
This shows that if $QQ^{\dagger }$ is a pure state then $I_{\mathcal{A}%
}-QQ^{\dagger }$ \emph{is not a state. }
\end{enumerate}

\begin{remark}
The GNS\ representation gives 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{f}\left( QQ^{\dagger }\right) =\Pi _{f}\left( Q\right) \Pi _{f}\left(
Q^{\dagger }\right) =\Pi _{f}\left( Q\right) \Pi _{f}\left( Q\right)
^{\dagger },
\end{equation}%
as $\Pi _{f}$ is c$^{\text{*}}$-algebra mapping so $\Pi _{f}\left(
Q^{\dagger }\right) =\Pi _{f}\left( Q\right) ^{\dagger }$.
\end{remark}

If $\left\{ Q,Q^{\dagger }\right\} $ are partial isometries the
representations of the projection eigenvalue equations for \emph{any state} $%
f$ are 
\begin{align}
\Pi _{f}\left( QQ^{\dagger }\right) \left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( Q\right)
\right\rangle & =\Pi _{f}\left( Q\right) \Pi _{f}\left( Q\right) ^{\dagger
}\left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( Q\right) \right\rangle =\Phi _{f}\left( Q\right)
\\
\Pi _{f}\left( Q^{\dagger }Q\right) \left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( Q^{\dagger
}\right) \right\rangle & =\Pi _{f}\left( Q\right) ^{\dagger }\Pi _{f}\left(
Q\right) \left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( Q^{\dagger }\right) \right\rangle =\Phi
_{f}\left( Q^{\dagger }\right) .
\end{align}%
The vector states $\left\{ \Phi _{f}\left( Q^{\dagger }\right) ,\Phi
_{f}\left( Q\right) \right\} $ also satisfy the eigenvalue equations 
\begin{align}
\left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( Q\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi
_{f}\left( Q\right) \right\vert \left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( Q\right)
\right\rangle _{f}& =\Phi _{f}\left( Q\right) \limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q^{\dagger
}QQ_{f}^{\dagger }Q_{f}\right\} =\left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( Q\right)
\right\rangle \\
\left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( Q^{\dagger }\right) \right\rangle \left\langle
\Phi _{f}\left( Q^{\dagger }\right) \right\vert \left\vert \Phi _{f}\left(
Q^{\dagger }\right) \right\rangle _{f}& =\Phi _{f}\left( Q^{\dagger }\right) 
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ QQ^{\dagger }Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger }\right\} =\left\vert
\Phi _{f}\left( Q^{\dagger }\right) \right\rangle ,
\end{align}%
where normalized vectors $\left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( Q\right) \right\rangle $
are defined by 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( Q\right) \right\rangle =\frac{1}{\sqrt{\limfunc{tr%
}\left\{ QQ^{\dagger }Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger }\right\} }}\Phi _{f}\left(
Q\right) .
\end{equation}%
If $\Pi _{f}$ is a faithful representation then one can consider 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{f}^{-1}\left( \left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( Q^{\dagger }\right)
\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{f}\left( Q^{\dagger }\right) \right\vert
\right) ,\Pi _{f}^{-1}\left( \left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( Q\right)
\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{f}\left( Q\right) \right\vert \right) :%
\mathcal{F}\rightarrow \mathcal{F}.
\end{equation}

The GNS\ construction associated to the trace state and partial isometries
give the eigenvalue equations

\begin{align}
\Pi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( QQ^{\dagger}\right) \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left(
Q\right) & =\Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( QQ^{\dagger}Q\right) =\Phi_{\limfunc{tr%
}}\left( Q\right) \\
\Pi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( Q^{\dagger}Q\right) \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left(
Q^{\dagger}\right) & =\Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( Q^{\dagger
}QQ^{\dagger}\right) =\Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( Q^{\dagger}\right)
\end{align}
while the corresponding vector state eigenvalue equations are 
\begin{align}
\left\vert \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( Q\right) \right\rangle \left\langle
\Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( Q\right) \right\vert \left\vert \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}%
}\left( Q\right) \right\rangle & =\Phi _{\limfunc{tr}}\left( Q\right) 
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q^{\dagger }Q\right\} =\left\vert \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}%
}\left( Q\right) \right\rangle \\
\left\vert \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( Q^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle
\left\langle \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( Q^{\dagger}\right) \right\vert
\left\vert \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( Q^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle &
=\Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( Q^{\dagger}\right) \limfunc{tr}\left\{
QQ^{\dagger}\right\} =\left\vert \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left(
Q^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle .
\end{align}
The dimension of the idempotent $QQ^{\dagger}$ in the Trace representation
is given by 
\begin{equation}
\dim_{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ QQ^{\dagger}\right\} =\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi_{%
\limfunc{tr}}\left( QQ^{\dagger}\right) \right\} =\sum_{1\leq
j\leq\infty}\left\langle \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( A_{j}\right) \left. \Pi_{%
\limfunc{tr}}\left( QQ^{\dagger}\right) \right\vert \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}%
}\left( A_{j}\right) \right\rangle _{\limfunc{tr}}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq\infty}%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q^{\dagger}QA_{j}A_{j}^{\dagger}\right\} \in\mathbb{N},
\end{equation}
where $\left\{ \left. \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( A_{j}\right) \right\vert
1\leq j\leq\infty\right\} $ is a conb for $\mathcal{H}_{\limfunc{tr}}.$ In
general for any normal state $f$ and the GNS\ representation $\Pi_{f}$ 
\begin{equation}
\dim_{f}\left\{ QQ^{\dagger}\right\} =\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{f}\left(
QQ^{\dagger}\right) \right\} =\sum_{1\leq j\leq\infty }\left\langle
\Phi_{f}\left( A_{j}\right) \left. \Pi_{f}\left( QQ^{\dagger}\right)
\right\vert \Phi_{f}\left( A_{j}\right) \right\rangle _{f}=\sum_{1\leq
j\leq\infty}\limfunc{tr}\left\{
Q^{\dagger}QA_{j}A_{j}^{\dagger}Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger}\right\} \in\mathbb{N},
\end{equation}
where $\left\{ \left. \Phi_{f}\left( A_{j}\right) \right\vert 1\leq
j\leq\infty\right\} $ is a conb for $\mathcal{H}_{f}.$

If 
\begin{equation}
\dim _{f}\left\{ QQ^{\dagger }\right\} =\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{f}\left(
QQ^{\dagger }\right) \right\} =1
\end{equation}%
and $Q$ is a partial isometry then 
\begin{equation*}
\Pi _{f}\left( QQ^{\dagger }\right) =\frac{\left\vert \Phi _{f}\left(
Q\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{f}\left( Q\right) \right\vert }{%
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{f}\left( Q\right) \right\vert \Phi _{f}\left(
Q\right) \right\rangle _{f}}.
\end{equation*}%
$\lceil $ For any state $f$ 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Phi
_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{f}=1
\end{equation}%
\begin{align}
\left( \left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle
\left\langle \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \right)
^{2}& =\left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle
\left\langle \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \\
\func{Tr}\left\{ \left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\vert \right\} & =\left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\vert =1 \\
\func{Tr}\left\{ \mathrm{A}\left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\vert \right\} & =\func{Tr}\left\{ \mathrm{AD}_{f}\right\}
=\left\langle \left. \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert 
\mathrm{A}\Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{f};\mathrm{A%
}\in \mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{f}\right) ,
\end{align}%
where 
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{D}_{f}=\left\vert \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\vert .
\end{equation}%
If 
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{A}=\Pi _{f}\left( A\right) ,
\end{equation}%
i.e. lies in $\Pi _{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) ,$ and $f$ is a normal state
then 
\begin{eqnarray}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \mathrm{AD}_{f}\right\} &=&\left\langle \left. \Phi
_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \mathrm{A}\Phi _{f}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{f}=\limfunc{tr}\left\{ AD_{f}\right\} =%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ AQ_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger }\right\} \\
&=&\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q_{f}^{\dagger }AQ_{f}\right\} =\left\langle \left.
Q_{f}\right\vert AQ_{f}\right\rangle _{\mathcal{A}}=\left\langle
\left\langle Q_{f}^{\dagger }AQ_{f}\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0}.
\end{eqnarray}

One should observe that 
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{D}_{f}=\left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert
\end{equation}
might or might not belong to $\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) ,$ if it does%
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{f}^{-1}\left( \left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert
\right) \equiv\Pi_{f}^{-1}\left( \mathrm{D}_{f}\right)
\end{equation}
is a self adjoint idempotent belonging to $\mathcal{A}$ and 
\begin{align}
& \Pi_{f}^{-1}\left( \left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert
\right) \Pi_{f}^{-1}\left( \left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert
\right) =\Pi_{f}^{-1}\left( \left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert
\left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle
\Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \right) \\
& =\Pi_{f}^{-1}\left( \left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert
\right)
\end{align}
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{f}^{-1}\left( \mathrm{D}_{f}\right) \Pi_{f}^{-1}\left( \mathrm{D}%
_{f}\right) =\Pi_{f}^{-1}\left( \mathrm{D}_{f}\mathrm{D}_{f}\right)
=\Pi_{f}^{-1}\left( \mathrm{D}_{f}\right) .
\end{equation}
Under these circumstances, i.e. $\Pi_{f}^{-1}\left( \mathrm{D}_{f}\right) $
and $\Pi_{f}^{-1}\left( \mathrm{A}\right) $ exist, then 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ A\Pi_{f}^{-1}\left( \mathrm{D}_{f}\right) \right\} =%
\func{Tr}\left\{ \mathrm{AD}_{f}\right\}
\end{equation}
as%
\begin{equation}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \mathrm{AD}_{f}\right\} =\left\langle \left. \Phi_{f}\left(
I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \mathrm{A}\Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\rangle _{f}=\limfunc{tr}\left\{ AD_{f}\right\} .
\end{equation}

Note that $\Pi_{f}^{-1}$ exists in general if $\Pi_{f}$ is a faithful
representation, i.e. $\ker\left\{ \Pi_{f}\right\} =0,$which corresponds to $%
\Pi_{f}$ being injective. If $\Pi_{f}$ is not surjective on $\mathcal{B}%
\left( \mathcal{H}_{f}\right) $ then $\Pi_{f}^{-1}$ does not exist on all of 
$\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{f}\right) $ only $\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}%
\right) .\rfloor$

\paragraph{Vacuum States}

\begin{lemma}
If $f_{b}$ is a normal vacuum state given by 
\begin{equation}
f_{b}\left( A\right) =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ AQ_{f_{b}}Q_{f_{b}}^{\dagger
}\right\} \quad \forall A\in \mathcal{A}
\end{equation}%
then 
\begin{equation}
Q_{f_{b}}^{\dagger }=\zeta _{0}I_{\mathcal{A}}+\sum_{1\leq n\leq
2r}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq 2r}\zeta _{j_{1}\cdots
j_{p}}b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }.
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}

Consider normal vacuum states (Quasi-Free) $f_{b}$ then 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}_{f_{b}}=\mathbb{C}\text{-}\limfunc{linspan}\left\{ \left. \Phi
_{f_{b}}\left( b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }\right)
\right\vert 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq r;0\leq n\leq r\right\} .
\end{equation}

The Fock representation $\Pi _{f_{b}}$ is faithful if 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{f_{b}}\left( A\right) \Phi _{f_{b}}\left( b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }\right) =0\Rightarrow A=0;1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq
r;0\leq n\leq r,
\end{equation}%
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f_{b}}\left( Ab_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }\right)
=0\Rightarrow A=0;1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq r;0\leq n\leq r.
\end{equation}%
Let 
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{f_{b}}\left( Ab_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }\right)
=0;1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq r;0\leq n\leq r.
\end{equation}%
then 
\begin{align}
& \left\langle \Phi _{f_{b}}\left( Ab_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }\right) \left. \Phi _{f_{b}}\left( Ab_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}\cdots b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }\right) \right\vert \right\rangle _{f_{b}}=%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{j_{n}}\cdots b_{j_{1}}A^{\dagger }Ab_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}\cdots b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }Q_{f_{b}}Q_{f_{b}}^{\dagger }\right\} \\
& =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q_{f_{b}}^{\dagger }b_{j_{n}}\cdots
b_{j_{1}}A^{\dagger }Ab_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger
}Q_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left( Ab_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }Q_{f_{b}}\right) ^{\dagger }Ab_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }Q_{f_{b}}\right\} =0,
\end{align}%
and%
\begin{equation}
A\left( b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }Q_{f_{b}}\right) =0.
\end{equation}%
As $Q_{f_{b}}\in \ker \left\{ \Phi _{f_{b}}\right\} $ this is always true.

The trace in $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{f_{b}}\right) $ is given by 
\begin{align}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{f_{b}}\left( A\right) \right\} & =\sum_{0\leq n\leq
r}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq r}f_{b}\left( Ab_{j_{n}}\cdots
b_{j_{1}}b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }\right) \\
& =\sum_{0\leq n\leq r}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq r}f_{b}\left(
Ah_{j_{n}}\cdots h_{j_{1}}\right) =\sum_{0\leq n\leq r}\sum_{1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq r}\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Ab_{j_{n}}\cdots
b_{j_{1}}b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger
}Q_{f_{b}}Q_{f_{b}}^{\dagger }\right\} .
\end{align}%
If the representation $\Pi _{f_{b}}$ does not contain any finite dimensional
projectors then 
\begin{equation}
\sum_{0\leq n\leq r}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots <j_{n}\leq r}f_{b}\left(
Q^{\dagger }Qb_{j_{n}}\cdots b_{j_{1}}b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }\right) =\infty
\end{equation}%
for any partial isometry $Q.$This shows that vacuum representations (Quasi
Free) must be of certain types.

\bigskip\ Any normal state must have the form $QQ^{\dagger },$ where $Q$ and 
$Q^{\dagger }$ are normalized\emph{.}

\begin{lemma}
\emph{Operator amplitudes producing vacuum states }are of the form\emph{\ }%
\begin{equation}
Q=\alpha _{0}I_{\mathcal{F}}+\sum_{1\leq n\leq r}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots
j_{n}\leq r}\alpha _{j_{1}\cdots j_{n}}b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }
\end{equation}%
for some cannonical generating set $\left\{ b_{1}^{\dagger },\cdots
,b_{r}^{\dagger }\right\} $ of $\mathcal{A}.$
\end{lemma}

The kernel and the orthogonal compliment of the kernels of the partial
isometries are given by 
\begin{align}
\ker \left\{ Q\right\} & =\left\{ \left. A\right\vert QA=0;A\in \mathcal{A}%
\right\} \\
\ker \left\{ Q^{\dagger }\right\} & =\left\{ \left. A\right\vert Q^{\dagger
}A=0;A\in \mathcal{A}\right\} .
\end{align}%
and 
\begin{align}
\ker \left\{ Q\right\} ^{\bot }& =\left\{ \left. Q\right\vert \limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ Q^{\dagger }B\right\} =0;Q\in \mathcal{A};\forall B\in \ker \left\{
Q\right\} \right\} \equiv \text{initial subspace} \\
\ker \left\{ Q^{\dagger }\right\} ^{\bot }& =\left\{ \left. Q\right\vert 
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q^{\dagger }B\right\} =0;Q\in \mathcal{A};\forall B\in
\ker \left\{ Q^{\dagger }\right\} \right\} \equiv \text{final subspace}.
\end{align}

The relationship between these subspaces is given by%
\begin{eqnarray}
\forall B &\in &\ker \left\{ Q\right\} \Rightarrow \left\langle \left.
Q\right\vert QB\right\rangle _{\mathcal{A}}=\left\langle \left. Q^{\dagger
}Q\right\vert B\right\rangle _{\mathcal{A}}=0\,\forall Q\in \mathcal{A}%
\Rightarrow Q^{\dagger }Q\in \ker \left\{ Q\right\} ^{\bot } \\
&\Leftrightarrow &\limfunc{Range}\left\{ Q^{\dagger }\right\} =\ker \left\{
Q\right\} ^{\bot }
\end{eqnarray}%
\begin{eqnarray}
\forall B &\in &\ker \left\{ Q^{\dagger }\right\} \Rightarrow \left\langle
\left. Q\right\vert Q^{\dagger }B\right\rangle _{\mathcal{A}}=\left\langle
\left. QQ\right\vert B\right\rangle _{\mathcal{A}}=0\,\forall Q\in \mathcal{A%
}\Rightarrow QQ\in \ker \left\{ Q^{\dagger }\right\} ^{\bot } \\
&\Leftrightarrow &\limfunc{Range}\left\{ Q\right\} =\ker \left\{ Q^{\dagger
}\right\} ^{\bot }.
\end{eqnarray}

$Q$ is an isometry on $\ker\left\{ Q\right\} ^{\bot}$, while $Q^{\dagger}$
is an isometry on $\ker\left\{ Q^{\dagger}\right\} ^{\bot}$, as

\begin{align}
\left\langle \left. QA\right\vert QA\right\rangle _{\mathcal{A}}&
=\left\langle \left. Q^{\dagger }QA\right\vert A\right\rangle _{\mathcal{A}%
}=\left\langle \left. A\right\vert A\right\rangle _{\mathcal{A}} \\
Q^{\dagger }Q& =P_{\limfunc{Range}\left\{ Q\right\} }=P_{\ker \left\{
Q^{\dagger }\right\} }^{\bot },
\end{align}%
where 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{Range}\left\{ Q\right\} =\left\{ \left. QA\right\vert \forall A\in 
\mathcal{A}\right\}
\end{equation}%
and%
\begin{align}
\left\langle \left. Q^{\dagger }A\right\vert Q^{\dagger }A\right\rangle _{%
\mathcal{A}}& =\left\langle \left. QQ^{\dagger }A\right\vert A\right\rangle
_{\mathcal{A}}=\left\langle \left. A\right\vert A\right\rangle _{\mathcal{A}}
\\
QQ^{\dagger }& =P_{\limfunc{Range}\left\{ Q^{\dagger }\right\} }=P_{\ker
\left\{ Q\right\} }^{\bot }.
\end{align}%
$\lceil $%
\begin{align}
a& =\alpha _{0}I_{\mathcal{A}}+\alpha _{1}b_{1} \\
& aa^{\dagger }=\left( \alpha _{0}I_{\mathcal{A}}+\alpha _{1}b_{1}\right)
\left( \overline{\alpha }_{0}I_{\mathcal{A}}+\overline{\alpha _{1}}%
b_{1}^{\dagger }\right) =\left\vert \alpha _{0}\right\vert ^{2}I_{\mathcal{A}%
}+\alpha _{0}\overline{\alpha _{1}}b_{1}^{\dagger }+\overline{\alpha }%
_{0}\alpha _{1}b_{1}+\left\vert \alpha _{1}\right\vert
^{2}b_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger } \\
