\section{Enveloping von Neumann algebra of Clifford algebra \label%
{Enveloping algebra}}

As explained in the book "Spinors in Hilbert Space" by Plymen and Robinson,
if $V$ is a complex (separable) Hilbert space with a real structure, and $%
Cl(V)$ the corresponding Clifford algebra, there is a unique completion $%
Cl[V]$ of this algebra to a $c^{\ast }$-algebra: Any $\ast $-representation
of $Cl(V)$ on a Hilbert space will induce that same $c^{\ast }$-norm on it,
and the corresponding closure is the Clifford $c^{\ast }$-algebra $Cl[V]$.

The situation is different for von-Neumann algebras. If 
\begin{equation}
\Pi :Cl(V)\rightarrow B(H)
\end{equation}%
is a $\ast $-representation, then the Von-Neumann-closure 
\begin{equation}
\left[ \Pi \right] \left( Cl(V)\right) =\Pi (Cl(V))\prime \prime
\end{equation}%
will depend drastically on $\Pi $. For example, if $\Pi $ is the left
regular representation, then $\left[ \Pi \right] \left( Cl(V)\right) $ a
hyperfinite $II_{1}$ factor, if $\left[ \Pi \right] \left( Cl(V)\right) $ is
a Fock representation, then it is type $I_{\infty }$, and there are other
settings where we obtain type $III$ factors.

However, there is another canonical von-Neumann algebra containing $Cl(V)$,
namely the universal enveloping algebra, coming from the case where $\Pi $
is the universal representation of $Cl[V]$. Alternatively, it is the double
dual of $Cl[V]$.

\begin{remark}
Every von Neumann completion is a quotient of the universal enveloping
algebra. It's as far from being a factor as you can get.
\end{remark}

Any $w^{\ast }$ closed ideal of a von Neumann algebra $M$ has the form $pM$
for some central projection $p$. The quotient $M/pM$ is isomorphic to $%
(I-p)M $. Thus the image of $M$ under any normal (i.e., $w^{\ast }$
continuous) $\ast $-homeomorphism is a von Neumann algebra.

\section{Filtered and Graded Algebras \label{Filtered}}

\subsection{Filtered Algebra}

A filtered algebra is a generalization of the notion of a graded algebra. A
filtered algebra over the field $K$ is an algebra $(A,\cdot )$ over $K$ that
has an increasing sequence 
\begin{equation}
\{0\}\subseteq F_{0}\subseteq F_{1}\subseteq \cdots \subseteq F_{i}\subseteq
\cdots \subseteq A
\end{equation}%
of subspaces of A such that%
\begin{equation}
A=\tbigcup_{i\in \mathbb{N}}F_{i}
\end{equation}%
and that is compatible with the multiplication in the following sense:%
\begin{equation}
\forall m,n\in \mathbb{N},F_{m}\cdot F_{n}\subseteq F_{n+m}.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Associated graded algebra}

In general there is the following construction that produces a graded
algebra out of a filtered algebra.

If $A$ is a filtered algebra then the associated graded algebra $G(A)$ is
defined as follows:

\begin{enumerate}
\item As a vector space%
\begin{equation}
G(A)=\mathfrak{\tbigoplus\limits_{n\in \mathbb{N}}}G_{n}
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate}

where,%
\begin{equation}
G_{0}=F_{0},
\end{equation}

and%
\begin{equation}
\forall n>0,G_{n}=F_{n}/F_{n-1},
\end{equation}

the multiplication is defined by%
\begin{equation}
(x+F_{n-1})(y+F_{m-1})=x\cdot y+F_{n+m-1}
\end{equation}

for all $x\in F_{n}$ and $y\in F_{m}$. (More precisely, the multiplication
map 
\begin{equation}
G(A)\times \ G(A)\rightarrow G(A)
\end{equation}%
is combined from the maps%
\begin{eqnarray}
(F_{n}/F_{n}-1)\times \ (F_{m}/F_{m-1}) &\rightarrow &F_{n+m}/F_{n+m-1}, \\
(x+F_{n-1},y+F_{m-1}) &\mapsto &x\cdot y+F_{n+m-1}
\end{eqnarray}%
for all $n\geq 0$ and $m\geq 0$. The multiplication is well-defined and
endows $G(A)$ with the structure of a graded algebra, with gradation $%
\{G_{n}\},n\in \mathbb{N}$. Furthermore if $A$ is associative then so is $%
G(A)$. Also if $A$ is unital, such that the unit lies in $F_{0}$, then $G(A)$
will be unital as well.

As algebras $A$ and $G(A)$ are distinct (with the exception of the trivial
case that $A$ is graded) but as vector spaces they are isomorphic. (One can
prove by induction that%
\begin{equation}
G(A)=\mathfrak{\tbigoplus\limits_{0\leq i\leq n}}G_{i}
\end{equation}%
is isomorphic to $F_{n}$ as vector spaces).

\begin{example}
Any graded algebra graded by $\mathbb{N}$ , for example 
\begin{equation}
A=\tbigoplus\limits_{n\in \mathbb{N}}A_{n},
\end{equation}
has a filtration given by 
\begin{equation}
F_{n}=\mathfrak{\tbigoplus\limits_{0\leq i\leq n}}A_{i}.
\end{equation}
\end{example}

\begin{example}
An example of a filtered algebra is the Clifford algebra $\limfunc{Cliff}%
(V,q)$ of a vector space $V$ endowed with a quadratic form $q$. The
associated graded algebra is $\U{22c0} V$, the exterior algebra of $V$.
\end{example}

\section{Unitary Equivalence}

\begin{theorem}
Kadison: Given a state $f$ of $\mathcal{A}$, let $\Pi _{1}$,$\Pi _{2}$ be $%
\ast $-representations of $\mathcal{A}$ on Hilbert spaces $\mathcal{H}_{1}$, 
$\mathcal{H}_{2}$ respectively each with normed \emph{cyclic} vectors $v_{1}$
$\in $ $\mathcal{H}_{1}$, $v_{2}$ $\in $ $\mathcal{H}_{2}$ i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}_{1}=\left\{ \left. \Pi _{1}\left( A\right) v_{1}\right\vert A\in 
\mathcal{A}\right\}
\end{equation}%
and 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}_{2}=\left\{ \left. \Pi _{2}\left( A\right) v_{2}\right\vert A\in 
\mathcal{A}\right\} ,
\end{equation}%
such that
\end{theorem}

\begin{equation}
f\left( A\right) =\left\langle \left. v_{1}\right\vert \Pi _{1}\left(
A\right) v_{1}\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{1}}=\left\langle \left.
v_{2}\right\vert \Pi _{2}\left( A\right) v_{2}\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}%
_{2}}.
\end{equation}%
Then $\Pi _{1},\Pi _{2}$ are unitarily equivalent $\ast $-representations
i.e. there is a unitary operator $U$ from $\mathcal{H}_{1}\rightarrow $ $%
\mathcal{H}_{2}$ such that $\Pi _{2}\left( A\right) $ $=U\Pi _{1}\left(
A\right) U^{\dagger }$ for all $A$ in $\mathcal{A}$. The operator $U$
implements the unitary equivalence maps $\Pi _{1}\left( A\right) v_{1}$ to $%
\Pi _{2}\left( A\right) v_{2}$ for all $A$ in $\mathcal{A}$.

\bigskip

Consider the direct sum 
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{H}_{\mathbb{C}}=\mathfrak{H}\oplus _{\mathbb{C}}\mathfrak{H}
\end{equation}%
with norm%
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert \left( v_{1},v_{2}\right) \right\Vert _{\mathfrak{H}_{\mathbb{C}%
}}=\sqrt{\left\langle \left. \left( v_{1},v_{2}\right) \right\vert \left(
v_{1},v_{2}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathfrak{H}_{\mathbb{C}}}}=\sqrt{\left(
\left\langle \left. v_{1}\right\vert v_{1}\right\rangle _{\mathfrak{H}%
}+\left\langle \left. v_{2}\right\vert v_{2}\right\rangle _{\mathfrak{H}%
}\right) }
\end{equation}%
and inner product%
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. \left( v_{1},v_{2}\right) \right\vert \left(
w_{1},w_{2}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathfrak{H}_{\mathbb{C}}}=\left\langle
\left. v_{1}\right\vert w_{1}\right\rangle _{\mathfrak{H}}+\left\langle
\left. v_{2}\right\vert w_{2}\right\rangle _{\mathfrak{H}}.
\end{equation}%
Consider the transformations $T,T_{1},T_{2}\in \mathcal{B}\left( \mathfrak{H}%
\oplus _{\mathbb{C}}\mathfrak{H},\mathfrak{H}\right) $ 
\begin{equation}
T:\mathfrak{H}\rightarrow \mathfrak{H}\oplus _{\mathbb{C}}\mathfrak{H=H}_{%
\mathbb{C}}
\end{equation}%
defined by 
\begin{eqnarray}
T\left( v\right) &=&\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left( v,v\right) \\
T_{1}\left( v\right) &=&\left( v,0\right) \\
T_{2}\left( v\right) &=&\left( 0,v\right)
\end{eqnarray}%
They are isometries 
\begin{eqnarray}
\left\langle \left. Tv\right\vert Tv\right\rangle _{\mathfrak{H}_{\mathbb{C}%
}} &=&\left\langle \left. \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left( v,v\right) \right\vert 
\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left( v,v\right) \right\rangle _{\mathfrak{H}_{\mathbb{C}%
}}=\frac{1}{2}\left( \left\langle \left. v\right\vert v\right\rangle _{%
\mathfrak{H}}+\left\langle \left. v\right\vert v\right\rangle _{\mathfrak{H}%
}\right) =\left\langle \left. v\right\vert v\right\rangle _{\mathfrak{H}} \\
\left\langle \left. T_{1}v\right\vert T_{1}v\right\rangle _{\mathfrak{H}_{%
\mathbb{C}}} &=&\left\langle \left. \left( v,0\right) \right\vert \left(
v,0\right) \right\rangle _{\mathfrak{H}_{\mathbb{C}}}=\left\langle \left.
v\right\vert v\right\rangle _{\mathfrak{H}}=\left\langle \left. v\right\vert
v\right\rangle _{\mathfrak{H}} \\
\left\langle \left. T_{2}v\right\vert T_{2}v\right\rangle _{\mathfrak{H}_{%
\mathbb{C}}} &=&\left\langle \left. \left( 0,v\right) \right\vert \left(
0,v\right) \right\rangle _{\mathfrak{H}_{\mathbb{C}}}=\left\langle \left.
v\right\vert v\right\rangle _{\mathfrak{H}}=\left\langle \left. v\right\vert
v\right\rangle _{\mathfrak{H}}
\end{eqnarray}%
They are injective 
\begin{eqnarray}
T\left( v-w\right) &=&0\Leftrightarrow v=w \\
T_{1}\left( v-w\right) &=&0\Leftrightarrow v=w \\
T_{2}\left( v-w\right) &=&0\Leftrightarrow v=w
\end{eqnarray}%
but clealy not surjective%
\begin{eqnarray}
\limfunc{Ran}\left( T\right) &\subset &\mathfrak{H}_{\mathbb{C}} \\
\limfunc{Ran}\left( T_{1}\right) &\subset &\mathfrak{H}_{\mathbb{C}} \\
\limfunc{Ran}\left( T_{2}\right) &\subset &\mathfrak{H}_{\mathbb{C}}
\end{eqnarray}%
so an inverses 
\begin{equation}
T^{-1},T_{1}^{-1},T_{2}^{-1}:\mathfrak{H}\oplus _{\mathbb{C}}\mathfrak{%
H\rightarrow H}
\end{equation}%
do not exist.

Consider representations 
\begin{eqnarray}
\Pi _{1}\left( A\right) v &\rightarrow &\Pi _{2T}\left( A\right) \frac{1}{%
\sqrt{2}}\left( v,v\right) =\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left( \Pi _{1}\left( A\right)
v,\Pi _{1}\left( A\right) v\right) \\
\Pi _{1}\left( A\right) v &\rightarrow &\Pi _{2T_{1}}\left( A\right) \frac{1%
}{\sqrt{2}}\left( v,v\right) =\left( \Pi _{1}\left( A\right) v,0\right) \\
\Pi _{1}\left( A\right) v &\rightarrow &\Pi _{2T_{2}}\left( A\right) \frac{1%
}{\sqrt{2}}\left( v,v\right) =\left( 0,\Pi _{1}\left( A\right) v\right)
\end{eqnarray}%
then 
\begin{equation}
\Pi _{2T}=\Pi _{1}\left( A\right) \oplus _{\mathbb{C}}\Pi _{1}\left(
A\right) .
\end{equation}

\begin{lemma}
Let $(\Pi ,\mathcal{H})$ be a unitary representation of a group $G$. Then
the following conditions are equivalent.

\begin{enumerate}
\item The representation $\Pi $ is irreducible.

\item The only $G$-equivariant orthogonal projections in $\mathcal{H}$ are $%
0 $ and $I$.

\item The representation $\Pi $ is not unitarily equivalent to the direct
sum of two unitary representations
\end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}
Let $(\Pi ,\mathcal{H})$ be a unitary representation for $G$. Then $\Pi $ is
irreducible if and only if%
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{End}\left\{ G(\mathcal{H})\right\} =\mathbb{C}I.
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}

\begin{remark}
Reducible representations of $c^{\ast }$-algebras are characterized as
follows:

\begin{enumerate}
\item A -representation $\Pi $ of a $c^{\ast }$-algebra $\mathcal{A}$ on a
Hilbert space $\mathcal{H}$ is irreducible if, and only if, there is no
closed subspace $K$ different from $\mathcal{H}$ and $\{0\}$ which is
invariant under all operators $\Pi (x)$ with $x\in \mathcal{A}$ .
\end{enumerate}
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
\begin{enumerate}
\item The spectrum of a $c^{\ast }$-algebra $\mathcal{A}$, denoted $\widehat{%
\mathcal{A}}$, is the set of unitary equivalence classes of irreducible $%
\ast $-representations of $\mathcal{A}$ .

\item The primitive spectrum of $\mathcal{A}$ is the set of primitive ideals
of $\mathcal{A}$, where a primitive ideal is the kernel of a non-zero
irreducible -representation .

\item The topology of $\widehat{\mathcal{A}}$ can be defined in several
equivalent ways.

\begin{enumerate}
\item The hull-kernel topology is one such topology, which is defined in
terms of the primitive spectrum of $\mathcal{A}$ .
\end{enumerate}

\item The Gelfand-Naimark-Segal construction is another way to characterize
irreducible representations of $c^{\ast }$-algebras .
\end{enumerate}
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
A *-representation of a $c^{\ast }$-algebras $\mathcal{A}$ on a Hilbert
space $\mathcal{H}$ is a mapping \U{3c0} from $\mathcal{A}$ into the algebra
of bounded operators on $\mathcal{H}$ such that

\begin{enumerate}
\item $\Pi $is a ring homomorphism which carries involution on $\mathcal{A}$
into involution on operators

\item $\Pi $ is nondegenerate, that is the space of vectors $\Pi (x)\U{3be} $
is dense as $x$ ranges through $\mathcal{A}$ and $\U{3be} $ ranges through $%
\mathcal{H}$.

\item Note that if $\mathcal{A}$ has an identity, nondegeneracy means
exactly $\Pi $ is unit-preserving, i.e. $\Pi $ maps the identity of $%
\mathcal{A}$ to the identity operator on $\mathcal{H}$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
A state on a $c^{\ast }$-algebra $\mathcal{A}$ is a positive linear
functional $f$ of norm $1$. If $\mathcal{A}$ has a multiplicative unit
element this condition is equivalent to $f(1)=1.$
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
For a representation $\Pi $ of a $c^{\ast }$-algebra $\mathcal{A}$ on a
Hilbert space $\mathcal{H}$, an element $\U{3be} $ is called a cyclic vector
if the set of vectors $\Pi (x)\U{3be} $ is norm dense in $\mathcal{H}$, in
which case $\Pi $ is called a cyclic representation. Any non-zero vector of
an irreducible representation is cyclic. However, non-zero vectors in a
general cyclic representation may fail to be cyclic.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}
Given a state $\U{3c1} $ of $\mathcal{A}$, let $\Pi ,\Pi ^{\prime }$ be $%
\ast $-representations of $\mathcal{A}$ on Hilbert spaces $\mathcal{H},%
\mathcal{H\prime }$ respectively each with unit norm cyclic vectors $\U{3be}
\in \mathcal{H},\U{3be} \prime \in \mathcal{H}\prime $ such that 
\begin{equation}
\U{3c1} \left( a\right) =\left\langle \left. \Pi \left( a\right) \U{3be}
\right\vert \Pi \left( a\right) \U{3be} \right\rangle =\left\langle \left.
\Pi ^{\prime }\left( a\right) \U{3be} \prime \right\vert \Pi ^{\prime
}\left( a\right) \U{3be} \prime \right\rangle \quad \forall a\in \mathcal{A}
\end{equation}%
then $\Pi ,\Pi ^{\prime }$ are unitarily equivalent $\ast $-representations
i.e. there is a unitary operator $U$ from $\mathcal{H}$ to $\mathcal{H}%
\prime $ such that $\Pi ^{\prime }(a)$ $=U\Pi (a)U^{\ast }$ for all a in $%
\mathcal{A}$. The operator $U$ that implements the unitary equivalence maps $%
\Pi \left( a\right) \U{3be} $ to $\Pi ^{\prime }\left( a\right) \U{3be}
\prime $ for all $a$ in $\mathcal{A}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}
Let $\mathcal{A}$ be a $c^{\ast }$-algebra . If $\Pi $ is a $\ast $%
-representation of $\mathcal{A}$ on the Hilbert space $\mathcal{H}$ with
unit norm cyclic vector $\U{3be} $, then $\Pi $ is irreducible if and only
if the corresponding state $f$ is an extreme point of the convex set of
positive linear functionals on $\mathcal{A}$ of norm $\leq 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
To prove this result one notes first that a representation is irreducible if
and only if the commutant of $\Pi (\mathcal{A})$, denoted by $\Pi (\mathcal{A%
})^{\prime }$, consists of scalar multiples of the identity.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
\emph{Stinespring's dilation theorem}, also called \emph{Stinespring's
factorization theorem}, named after \emph{W. Forrest Stinespring}, is a
result from operator theory that represents any \underline{\emph{completely}}%
\emph{\ positive} map on a $c^{\ast }$-algebra $\mathcal{A}$ as a
composition of two completely positive maps each of which has a special form:

\begin{enumerate}
\item A $\ast $-representation of $\mathcal{A}$ on some auxiliary Hilbert
space $K$ followed byAn operator map of the form $T\mapsto V^{\ast }TV$.

\item Stinespring's theorem is a structure theorem from a $c^{\ast }$%
-algebra into the algebra of bounded operators on a Hilbert space. \emph{%
Completely positive maps} are shown to be simple modifications of $\ast $%
-representations, or sometimes called $\ast $-homomorphisms.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{theorem}
Let $\mathcal{A}$ be a unital $c^{\ast }$-algebra on a Hilbert space $%
\mathcal{H}$, and $\mathcal{B(H)}$ be the bounded operators on $\mathcal{H}$%
. For every completely positive 
\begin{equation}
\U{3a6} :\mathcal{A\rightarrow B(H)}
\end{equation}%
there exists a Hilbert space $\mathcal{K}$ and a unital $\ast $-homomorphism%
\begin{equation}
\U{3a6} :\mathcal{A\rightarrow B(K)}
\end{equation}%
such that 
\begin{equation}
\U{3a6} \left( a\right) =V^{\prime }\U{3a6} \left( a\right) V,
\end{equation}%
where $V\in \mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{K},\mathcal{H}\right) .$ Furthermore,
we have%
\begin{equation}
\U{3a6} \left( I\right) =\left\Vert V\right\Vert ^{2}.
\end{equation}%
Informally, one can say that every completely positive map $\U{3a6} $ can be
"lifted" up to a map of the form
\end{theorem}
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
The converse of the theorem is trivially true. So Stinespring's result
classifies completely positive maps.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
The Gelfand--Naimark--Segal (GNS) construction is as follows. Let $\mathcal{H%
}$ in Stinespring's theorem be $1$-dimensional, i.e. the complex numbers. So 
$\U{3a6} $ now is a positive linear functional on $\mathcal{A}$. If we
assume $f$ is a state, that is, $f$ has norm $1$, then the isometry $V\in 
\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{K},\mathcal{H}\right) $ is determined for some $%
x_{i}\in K$ of unit norm. So%
\begin{equation}
V\Phi \left( I\right) =\xi
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
f\left( a\right) =V^{\ast }\Pi \left( a\right) V=\left\langle \left. V^{\ast
}\Pi \left( a\right) V\U{3a6} \left( I\right) \right\vert \U{3a6} \left(
I\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}}=\left\langle \left. \Pi \left(
a\right) V\U{3a6} \left( I\right) \right\vert V\U{3a6} \left( I\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{K}}=\left\langle \left. \Pi \left( a\right) \xi
\right\vert \xi \right\rangle _{\mathcal{K}}
\end{equation}%
and we have recovered the GNS representation of states. This is one way to
see that completely positive maps, rather than merely positive ones, are the
true generalizations of positive functionals and we have recovered the GNS
representation of states.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
A linear positive functional on a C*-algebra is absolutely continuous with
respect to another such functional (called a reference functional) if it is
zero on any positive element on which the reference positive functional is
zero.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
This leads to a noncommutative generalization of the Radon--Nikodym theorem.
The usual density operator of states on the matrix algebras with respect to
the standard trace is nothing but the Radon--Nikodym derivative when the
reference functional is chosen to be trace. Belavkin introduced the notion
of complete absolute continuity of one completely positive map with respect
to another (reference) map and proved an operator variant of the
noncommutative Radon--Nikodym theorem for completely positive maps.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
A particular case of this theorem corresponding to a tracial completely
positive reference map on the matrix algebras leads to the Choi operator as
a Radon--Nikodym derivative of a CP map with respect to the standard trace
(see Choi's Theorem).
\end{remark}

\end{document}
